    "chunk_text": "Year one was a good year for stability: low inflation, falling rates, record revenue and a booming market. Year two has reversed most of those gains, and the oil shock explains only part of it.\n\nA budget surplus is earned in two ways: by raising more or by spending less. This Government has done both, and the second is the problem.\n\nThe pattern runs through the line ministries. A Right to Information disclosure from July 2025 showed that only 19% of the Health Ministry\u2019s Rs. 18 billion medicine allocation had been spent at mid-year. For new hospital buildings, the figure was 10%. Twenty-five health construction projects, some begun in 2014, still need Rs. 76 billion to finish.\n\nMeanwhile, the money keeps coming in. In the first half of 2026, tax revenue rose 26%. The Government ran an overall budget surplus of Rs. 9.5 billion, against a deficit of Rs. 406 billion a year earlier. A country still rebuilding from Cyclone Ditwah, with hospitals unfinished and roads washed away, posted a surplus because it could not spend what Parliament had approved.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s entire GDP, $ 108.8 billion in 2025, is less than two months of Walmart\u2019s global sales. An economy this small cannot tax its way to prosperity. Growth must come from investment, exports and productivity.\n\nThe signals there are mixed at best. Foreign direct investment reached $ 1.06 billion in 2025, a genuine improvement. But in the first half of 2026 it slowed to about $ 450 million, against a full-year target of $ 1.5 billion. Exports did better: $ 17.25 billion of goods and services in 2025, and up 8% in the first half of 2026. Tourism tells a harder story. Arrivals hit a record 2.36 million in 2025, but earnings rose only 1.6%, so each visitor spent about 12% less. In 2026, arrivals are slightly down on last year."
