    "chunk_text": "An economy is sustained by what its people save and invest, not by what they borrow to consume. Here the trend runs the wrong way. In its December 2025 review, the IMF projected national savings falling from 28.7% of GDP in 2024 to 23.2% in 2026, and national investment from 27.0% to 23.6%. The State saves by not spending its investment budget, while households borrow to stand still. That is not a recipe for growth.\n\nAgriculture shrank 2.3% in the second quarter of 2026, after growing 2.5% a year earlier. Rice production fell 15.1%, marine fishing 10.1% and tea 1.9%. In April, the Government had to allow emergency imports of Samba and Keeri Samba after the Maha harvest fell short. Farm employment fell by 174,000 in a year. Cyclone Ditwah destroyed an estimated $ 814 million of agricultural assets, and a bag of urea now costs Rs. 9,900 to Rs. 10,200. A country this fertile should not be importing its staple.\n\nBefore the election, Anura Kumara Dissanayake promised to cut electricity bills by a third. Tariffs fell 20% in January 2025, then rose 15% in June 2025, well before any war. The Ceylon Electricity Board ended 2025 with a Rs. 38.7 billion loss. In February 2026, still before the Gulf erupted, it asked for a 13.56% increase, and from April tariffs rose by 8% to 14%. The war came on top, opening a Rs. 38 billion hole for mid-2026, which the regulator kept off 95% of consumers with a Rs. 15 billion subsidy. Low-quality coal, a procurement failure, cut output at Norochcholai. Most of the electricity problem was made at home. A third off was the promise, and voters remember it."
