    "md5": "7064feceb07dac50b4931fe4b4c39332",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Tourism must focus on value, not volume\n\nOur tourism industry needs to reconsider how it measures success. For too long, the sector\u2019s performance has been judged primarily by the number of international arrivals, with ambitious annual targets becoming the centrepiece of tourism policy. Yet, the latest figures offer a timely reminder that arrivals alone do not tell the full story,\u00a0 and setting such arbitrary numbers just sets the stage for failure while the true story may be different.\n\nAccording to Central Bank data, tourism earnings during the first nine months of 2026 declined by 9.2%, falling to $ 2.25 billion\u00a0 from $ 2.47 billion during the corresponding period last year. International tourist arrivals also registered a marginal decline of 1.8%, with around 1.7 million visitors arriving during the period.\n\nAt this rate, the industry is unlikely to achieve its ambitious target of 2.6 million tourist arrivals for the year. But perhaps that should not be the principal concern. The more important question is why the country is earning less from the visitors it attracts.\n\nA significant proportion of those entering Sri Lanka on foreign passports are former Sri Lankan nationals, who have migrated overseas and are returning to visit family and friends. While their contribution to the economy is undoubtedly valuable, their travel patterns and spending behaviour may differ considerably from those of conventional leisure tourists. Consequently, treating every arrival as an equivalent tourism success risks creating a misleading picture of the industry\u2019s actual performance.\n\nWe must also recognise that its tourism product has a finite carrying capacity. More visitors do not necessarily translate into greater prosperity. Beyond a certain threshold, increased arrivals can place pressure on infrastructure, natural resources, cultural sites and the very experiences that make the country attractive. Sustainability must therefore be central to tourism planning."
  },
