    "chunk_size_bytes": 1710,
    "chunk_text": "We must also recognise that its tourism product has a finite carrying capacity. More visitors do not necessarily translate into greater prosperity. Beyond a certain threshold, increased arrivals can place pressure on infrastructure, natural resources, cultural sites and the very experiences that make the country attractive. Sustainability must therefore be central to tourism planning.\n\nConnectivity must become a national priority. Better air links, increased flight frequencies and stronger regional connections can expand access to Sri Lanka, while encouraging higher-spending travellers to consider the country. Tourism promotion cannot succeed independently of an efficient aviation strategy.\n\nThe industry should also place greater emphasis on Meetings, Incentives, Conferences and Exhibitions (MICE) tourism, alongside high-end leisure travel. Business events, international conferences and corporate gatherings can generate substantial economic activity,\n\nwhile supporting hotels, restaurants, transport operators and other service providers. Similarly, attracting premium travellers, who stay longer and spend more can deliver greater returns without placing excessive pressure on destinations.\n\nThere is also a need for pragmatism when considering the country\u2019s tourism offering. Sri Lanka should be willing to examine opportunities to attract high-spending visitors from South Asia, including through a more open discussion on regulated entertainment and gaming facilities. Rather than allowing historical sensitivities to prevent a policy debate, the Government should objectively assess the potential economic benefits, regulatory safeguards and reputational implications of such developments."
