    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "The base is narrow. Of the 2,547,322 employees in the Inland Revenue Department\u2019s employer returns for 2024/25, about 75.6% had no tax deducted, which is consistent with earnings below the taxable threshold. On my earlier estimate, drawing on IRD and Labour Force Survey data, the system reliably reaches only around a quarter of the employed population. For 2024/25, only 25% of individual income tax returns were filed on time.\n\nThe IRD\u2019s APIT data for 2024/25 show how concentrated the load already is. Of the 2.55 million employees on employer returns, 1.92 million (75.6%) had no tax deducted. Their average gross pay was about Rs. 528,000 a year, or roughly Rs. 44,000 a month, which fits earnings below the threshold. The 622,419 on whom tax was deducted paid about Rs. 308,000 each on average. At the top, the 83,397 employees earning above Rs. 3.7 million (3.3% of the total) accounted for Rs. 150.5 billion, or 78.4% of all APIT, an average of about Rs. 1.8 million each. The table also shows how steeply the average tax rises between income ranges. It describes employees covered by completed employer returns for 2024/25, under the earlier Rs. 1.2 million relief, so it is a guide to the distribution of the load and not a basis for costing any change.\n\nThe balance between corporate and non-corporate taxpayers is the other side of the same question. The IRD\u2019s 2025 data show that corporate taxpayers contributed Rs. 669.6 billion, or 59% of income tax collections, up from 57% in 2024. Non-corporate taxpayers contributed Rs. 461.7 billion, or 41%, down from 43%. Corporate collections grew by about 15% over the year, against about 4.5% for the non-corporate group. The corporate figure includes corporate income tax, dividend tax and remittance tax, while the non-corporate figure includes individual and partnership income tax, APIT , AIT and withholding taxes. The split is therefore by type of tax, not strictly by type of taxpayer."
