    "chunk_text": "Faced with escalating pressure and the threat of costly public proxy contests, CEO Marc Benioff and Salesforce\u2019s leadership responded with swift corporate transformations. Salesforce initiated sweeping workforce reductions, cutting 10% of its staff and downsizing its real estate footprint to dramatically curb operating expenses. To demonstrate stronger corporate governance, the company refreshed its Board of directors, appointed ValueAct CEO Mason Morfit to the Board, and disbanded its dedicated strategic M&A committee to restrict further buyout sprees. Furthermore, Salesforce launched aggressive share buyback programs and eventually paid out its first-ever quarterly dividend, funneling billions of dollars directly back to shareholders. The collaborative restructuring paid off rapidly as Salesforce reported surging operating margins and robust free cash flow, driving a sharp rally in its share price. Satisfied with the swift operational pivot and improved financial performance, firms like Elliott withdrew their proposed Board slates. For me, this event stands as a textbook example of how concentrated activist pressure can rapidly force a tech giant to transition from a growth-at-all-cost mindset into a high-margin, disciplined profit engine.\n\nAt its core, shareholder activism exists on a delicate balance of risks and rewards. Shareholder activism is now a mainstream, year-round force reshaping global corporate governance. Activist campaigns are surging, targeting companies across all sectors and size categories. Shareholder activism in Sri Lanka remains in a nascent, evolving stage compared to western markets\n\nTo survive and thrive in this climate, corporate Boards must stop being reactive. Modern executives must perform rigorous \u201cactivist\u201d audits on their own operational portfolios, proactively engage in open, ongoing dialogue with top shareholders, and articulate clear, long-term value-creation narratives."
