    "chunk_text": "Activism in Sri Lanka currently centres on minority protections during mergers, capital restructurings, and transparency issues. Recently, minority shareholders of Associated Motor Finance PLC (AMF) pushed for minority safeguards during the company\u2019s mandatory amalgamation into L B Finance PLC. After the company offered Rs. 55 per share to buy out minority investors and delist from the Colombo Stock Exchange, dissenting shareholders rejected the cash consideration as inadequate. Exercising statutory rights under Section 93 of the Companies Act No. 7 of 2007, minority investors formally demanded that the Board purchase their shares at a fair and reasonable value. Despite these objections, LB Finance finalised the merger, resulting in AMF\u2019s share cancellation and license revocation.\n\nThe above events underscore how Sri Lankan minority investors are leveraging statutory buyout mechanics and regulatory appeals to demand price fairness and transparency during corporate restructurings.\n\nA common misconception about shareholder activism is that it targets broken or fundamentally bad businesses"
