    "chunk_size_bytes": 1648,
    "chunk_text": "The above events underscore how Sri Lankan minority investors are leveraging statutory buyout mechanics and regulatory appeals to demand price fairness and transparency during corporate restructurings.\n\nA common misconception about shareholder activism is that it targets broken or fundamentally bad businesses\n\nA quiet reckoning approaches Sri Lankan Boardrooms. The days of treating capital as a passive commodity and Board seats as comfortable sanctuaries are coming to an end. For decades, legacy conglomerates and entrenched management teams have shielded underperforming asset bases behind voting blocs and historical prestige. But as economic pressures intensify and institutional oversight sharpens, corporate Sri Lanka must look inward and confront a hard truth: complacency is no longer an option.\n\nWhen a company consistently fails to sweat its real estate, optimise its Balance Sheet, or unlock the true potential of its operations, it invites corporate predators and the sharp scrutiny of shareholder activism. Investors are waking up, recognising that persistent value gaps are not inevitable market conditions, but the direct result of operational inertia and unexamined strategy. Boards can no longer hide behind vague promises of long-term vision while current capital lies stagnant. To survive this impending shift, Sri Lankan Boards must undergo a soul-searching transformation. They must perform ruthless self-audits, adopt the mindset of an activist against their own inefficiencies, and prioritise genuine value creation over comfortable precedent. The market is opening, and accountability is demanding its seat at the table."
