    "chunk_size_bytes": 1310,
    "chunk_text": "The days of treating capital as a passive commodity and board seats as comfortable sanctuaries are coming to an end. Sri Lankan boards must undergo a soul-searching transformation. They must perform ruthless self-audits, adopt the mindset of an activist against their own inefficiencies, and prioritise genuine value creation over comfortable precedent. The market is opening, and accountability is demanding its seat at the table\n\nIn this light, there is a need for Sri Lanka\u2019s corporates to be proactive in preparing for and managing the increasing involvement of activist shareholders. I suggest that Boards have a bi-annual agenda item to discuss governance, strategy, and operating issues which are likely to attract the attention of activists. Independent directors can be asked to think like activists and prepare a note for Board discussion. As observed by Peter Drucker; \u201cThe best way to predict the future is to create it.\u201d Rather than treating activism as an unexpected crisis, forward-thinking directors can identify, up front, the key issues which are likely to create shareholder disappointment. By incorporating this review into the regular Board calendar, Boards can transform a reactive defense into a disciplined mechanism for continuous self-improvement and shareholder value creation."
