    "chunk_text": "To translate these focus areas into concrete outcomes, each bi-annual meeting must generate specific action items. The Board should mandate benchmarking of total shareholder return and price-to-book performance against domestic and regional peers over multi-year horizons. Independent perception audits must be conducted prior to these meetings to catch early signs of shareholder discontent. Additionally, directors should periodically conduct brief tabletop simulation exercises to assess response readiness across legal, regulatory, and public relations channels. By rigorously examining these core areas twice a year, Sri Lankan Boards can effectively eliminate the vulnerabilities that attract activist attention while building enduring trust with their shareholder base.\n\nActivism is expanding everywhere. It is on the rise. The best strategic move any Board or CEO can make today is simple. Conduct ruthless critiques of your own business regularly and fix the flaws before someone else points them out for you. Confronting investor pressure is not an exercise in defending the status quo. It is an opportunity to prove that leadership possesses a sharper, more effective roadmap for growth. Corporate leaders in Sri Lanka can no longer afford passive governance. Survival and success depend on preemptive self-evaluation: uncovering internal vulnerabilities, fixing systemic inefficiencies early, and clearly communicating why the current strategy remains the absolute best vehicle for sustained economic returns. Be braver in describing the future. Spend less time describing the past through boring annual reports. Start thinking like activists before they think about you.\n\n(The author is currently a Leadership Coach, Mentor and Consultant and boasts over 50 years of experience in very senior positions in the corporate world \u2013 local and overseas. ww.ronniepeiris.com)"
