    "md5": "4d55e29efac30b0823aa8ecfe2f0c80b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "Rubber supply squeeze pushes prices higher\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s rubber manufacturers increasingly turned to imported lower-grade ribbed smoked sheet (RSS) rubber in October, as the wet weather disrupted the domestic tapping and tightened the raw material supplies, while the volumes offered at the Colombo Rubber Auction fell by more than a third from a year earlier.\n\nForbes & Walker Commodity Brokers said the domestic supply constraints supported the higher prices across the latex crepe grades, with the RSS availability further restricted by the diversion of available latex towards centrifuged latex production.\n\nTotal rubber offerings at the Colombo auctions declined 34.1 percent year-on-year (YoY) to 291,869 kilogrammes in October, from 442,859 kilogrammes in the corresponding month of 2025. The decline reflects the reduced availability through the auction channel rather than a measure of the change in national production.\n\nThe tightening supply comes against a backdrop of rising export receipts. The rubber and rubber-based product export earnings increased 33.68 percent YoY to US $ 99.31 million in September, taking the earnings for the first nine months of 2026 to US $ 773.88 million, up 8.44 percent. According to the broker, the persistent rainfall disrupted tapping across key rubber-growing regions during October, with the wet days increasing after the middle of the month.\n\nThe RSS supplies were particularly limited. Just 2,844 kilogrammes were offered across the month\u2019s four auctions, with no offerings at the final two sales. Grades RSS2 to RSS5 remained unquoted throughout October, while RSS1 was quoted only at the first two auctions.\n\n\u201cWith the domestic raw material availability remaining tight, the manufacturers increasingly relied on imported lower-grade RSS to meet their production requirements,\u201d the report said."
