    "chunk_text": "What is the relevance of these claims about falling from a bicycle and assault during a clash? Do the police attempt to attribute the death of the youth to those incidents? However, the very action by the police clearly negates those attempts. It is common knowledge that hardly a man would be able to walk into a police station or at least to his home with three broken ribs and\n\nPolice admitted the man to Homagama hospital on the day after he was arrested. It also negates the validity of the claims about\u00a0 falling from the bicycle and assaulted during a clash.\n\nEventually, as social media mounted its criticism the OIC of the Crimes Division was arrested on October 28 and remanded till November 12 in connection with the incident.\n\nIn the second incident, villagers of Beddegama area in Kobeigane apprehended a 41-year-old man who was suspected of multiple burglaries in the area tied him to a tree, and assaulted him on October 25 \u2013 on the same day Homagama man was arrested. Kobeigane Police took the injured suspect into custody and held him at the station without immediately providing medical treatment. Although he was eventually hospitalised, he succumbed to his injuries the following day. Two police sergeants and five residents who initially apprehended the deceased and handed him over to police have also been arrested by Kobeigane Police following the death in custody.\n\nAlthough assaulting the suspects - leave alone torturing - in police custody is illegal, it is common knowledge that it is a norm in police stations. Conversely. even the most innocent law breaker would not voluntarily reveal or accept his culpability for an offence, unless some sort of pressure is exerted on him. Suspects in high-profile cases\u00a0 reportedly refuse to reveal the passwords of their computers which are believed to contain information crucial to national security. Needless to say, this a tricky issue for police officers."
