    "chunk_text": "Opposition parties, civil society organisations and professional bodies scrambled for a candidate, rather like prize-hunters at an Avurudu festival. Two obstacles stood in the way. One was finding someone trustworthy enough to relinquish power after winning an office of near-limitless authority. The other was finding a figure who could match the incumbent\u2019s popularity and charisma. Sobitha Thero\u2019s tireless efforts to resolve the constitutional crisis, abolish the Executive Presidency and secure a lasting devolution settlement had made him the favourite of many in opposition ranks for the role of \u201cCommon Candidate\u201d.\n\nSovereignty should be understood in the same way, as a relationship between the State and its people. Independence from external control means little if citizens have no meaningful voice in how they are governed, or if public institutions answer to political masters. Real sovereignty means power exercised within constitutional limits, with institutions serving the public rather than those who command them.\n\nExperience has shown that a change of faces alone cannot deliver good governance while the structures of power remain poorly restrained. The task is to build a constitutional order that protects democratic freedoms, strengthens institutional accountability and leaves future generations a country governed by principles rather than by the unchecked authority of individuals.\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power must honour its manifesto pledge to introduce such a Constitution. This is a responsibility owed to the people, not merely an election promise."
