    "md5": "3b511d4dfe331c2ac340314368c2f74f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "This is not just a problem of a single writer, nor is it the first time this issue has happened. It is part of a systematic suppression directed against Tamil books and Tamil writers. Whenever a book is written by Tamil writers like us, the State machinery approaches it with the prejudice that it should be against the State and the armed forces.\n\nIn this context, even though the Human Rights Commission recommended the immediate release of my books, the government has not released them.\n\nQWe are aware that detaining books is not the first obstacle your freedom of expression has encountered. Can you recall previous incidents where you were questioned by the authorities about your creative work?\n\nSurveillance and interrogations have been present ever since I started writing. Initially, I was threatened through indirect inquiries. Later, in 2024, the Terrorism Investigation Division in Kilinochchi conducted an inquiry based on whether the novel Paiyankaravathi (Terrorist) was written with the aim of reviving the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE). It was a matter of suspecting and questioning our stories and the characters within them. They inquired whether the characters appearing in the novel were real people and where they were. I informed the authorities that they were fictional characters created in writing based on actual real-life events. Although such interrogations created pressure on a writer, they also gave me the drive to keep writing. In fact, it was during the days when that investigation took place that I was writing my subsequent novel, Cyanide.\n\nQYou and most Tamil-speaking writers publish your work in India, yet almost all literary awards in Colombo for the best Tamil literary works only consider books published in Sri Lanka. How do you interpret this issue?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
