    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "519485003975155a6f135eeba33396eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "Sinhala readers who read my novel \u2018Nadugal\u2019 understood with great pain and emotional resonance the feelings of a Tamil mother and a Tamil brother yearning for the memory of a freedom fighter who died in the war. At the same time, ethnic discrimination, oppression, and threats still prevail. I still have strong hope that one day, the Sinhala people will fully understand and acknowledge the political struggle of the Tamil people.\n\nAs both a poet and a novelist, I am compelled to handle both of these forms through their unique structures and impacts. Poetry is like a flash of lightning; it instantly awakens emotions and creates a deep impact in the reader\u2019s heart. A novel is like a river; it is capable of detailing and patiently constructing the many layers of society, life, history, and human psychology.\n\nPoems and novels are powerful in two different dimensions. Poetry serves as a voice against oppression and as a medium that instantly records the pain of the oppressed. Only a novel can comprehensively record the deep roots of politics, the multifaceted contradictions of society, and the scars of history across time and space. It is only through the form of a novel that we can describe across a broad canvas\u2014through multiple characters\u2014how human beings are shattered and how they survive in an oppressive society. The two are not alternatives to one another, but rather complement each other.\n\nQLooking ahead, what can readers expect from your forthcoming book, and what future literary paths you are hoping to explore?\n\nRight now, I am writing a novel titled Kuzhandhai Veeran (Child Hero). It is the story of a young boy who was severely affected by the war. Additionally, my short story collection titled Yaazh Sumandha Siruvan (The Boy Who Carried the Yaazh) is scheduled to be released at the end of this year. This collection also mostly features stories about women and children affected by the war."
  },
