    "md5": "516370556f62eedee960ae2429b60abd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, LAUGFS Terminals Ltd., a wholly owned subsidiary of LAUGFS Gas, has sought three months of temporary permission to use its Hambantota terminal for domestic LPG supplies. The US$65 million facility, which commenced operations in 2019, has a storage capacity of 30,000 metric tonnes and is Sri Lanka's largest LPG receiving, storage and entrep\u00f4t terminal.\n\nThe company said the COVID-19 pandemic, Sri Lanka's economic and political crisis, foreign exchange shortages and sovereign credit rating downgrades had affected its ability to maintain the required re-export levels, resulting in restrictions on domestic supplies through the terminal.\n\nLAUGFS said it was working with the BOI, Ministry of Finance, Sri Lanka Customs and the Department of Imports and Exports Control to complete its transition out of Hub status and establish the necessary operating arrangements, with applicable duties and taxes payable.\n\nThe company further said the BOI Chairman had informed it yesterday that, following instructions from President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, he had written to the Secretary to the Treasury requesting that the necessary approvals be facilitated within one week. However, the approvals remain pending.\n\nAs an additional measure to strengthen supply security, LAUGFS Gas has also called a fresh tender for direct LPG deliveries to Colombo, scheduled to commence in January 2027.\n\nThe company also clarified that neither LAUGFS Gas PLC nor LAUGFS Terminals Ltd. is itself designated by the United States Department of the Treasury's Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC).\n\nLAUGFS Gas said it appreciated the patience of its customers and business partners and would provide further updates as shipment arrivals and regulatory approvals were confirmed."
