    "md5": "7a4501870f20002e7156cc4e12f04b21",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Americans head to polls in midterm elections seen as referendum on Trump\n\nThe Guardian - Americans are heading to the polls today in US midterm elections widely seen as a referendum on Donald Trump\u2019s second presidency and a chance for opponents to rein in his untrammeled power.\n\nDemocrats and Republicans have scrambled in recent days to deliver their final pitches to voters, with control of the US Senate and House of Representatives \u2013 both currently held by the Republicans \u2013 at stake.\n\nThe campaign has been overshadowed by Trump, who has staged several dozen rallies across the country while urging his make America great again (Maga) base to vote for Republican candidates and pretend his name was on the ballot.\n\nThe party holding the White House typically loses congressional seats at midterm elections and Democrats are hoping Trump\u2019s nose-diving popularity can be exploited to generate a \u201cblue wave\u201d groundswell of popular opposition that will win them the Senate and the House.\n\nThey are seeking to wrest control over both chambers of Congress to put a brake on Trump\u2019s presidential power, which has gone largely unchecked as he has launched a fierce immigration crackdown involving mass arrests and deportations, waged war on Iran, imposed tariffs on foreign imports, and embarked on vainglorious building projects, including a White House ballroom.\n\nOpinion surveys have suggested the Democrats are well-positioned to win the House. The party faces a tougher task to regain control of the Senate, although it has targeted several Republican-held seats including Ohio, Texas and Maine.\n\nA final set of polls from the New York Times and Siena University, published on Sunday, put Democratic candidates ahead in five of seven key battleground contests \u2013 including Texas and Kansas, where voters have not sent a Democrat to the Senate since 1988 and 1932 respectively."
  },
