    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "A final set of polls from the New York Times and Siena University, published on Sunday, put Democratic candidates ahead in five of seven key battleground contests \u2013 including Texas and Kansas, where voters have not sent a Democrat to the Senate since 1988 and 1932 respectively.\n\nStevens was widely condemned by fellow Democrats over the move, which followed a feud with the El-Sayed campaign over an outside group\u2019s campaign ad that blamed the high cost of living on US support for Israel. Chuck Schumer, the Senate minority leader, described her endorsement of Rogers as \u201ca despicable betrayal\u201d.\n\n\u201cWhatever they\u2019re trying to distract you, it\u2019s because they\u2019re picking your pocket,\u201d El-Sayed told hundreds of supporters in Royal Oak, Michigan on Monday.\n\nThe extraordinary rupture took places days after the party faced damaging headlines in Alaska, where it was reported that the Democratic senate candidate\u2019s campaign was behind an effort to recruit a challenger with almost the same name as her rival, the Republican senator Dan Sullivan. (The Democratic candidate, Mary Peltola, has denied any knowledge of such a scheme on the part of her campaign.)\n\nBefore the report of the Peltola campaign\u2019s involvement, the Department of Justice (DoJ) filed a lawsuit to block the certification of Alaska\u2019s Senate results in the event of a Republican defeat, citing potential \u201cvoter confusion\u201d over the presence of two Dan Sullivans on the ballot. The lawsuit has since been dismissed by a judge.\n\nTrump has put himself center stage by crisscrossing the country to deliver a succession of speeches for Republican candidate, despite opinion polls showing his approval ratings falling to record lows of between 33 and 37%."
  },
