    "chunk_size_bytes": 2026,
    "chunk_text": "JAV to protest today near Parliament against PTA, PSTA, OCA laws\n\nSays if these laws are enacted, the people will only be able to open their mouths to eat or to yawn\n\nDemanding the repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), the proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and the Online Safety Act (OSA), which it says threaten the democratic rights of ordinary citizens, the Jana Aragala Vyaparaya(JAV-People\u2019s Struggle Movement),will stage a protest near Parliament today (4).\n\nThe demonstration, scheduled to begin at 12 noon at the Parliament Roundabout, is expected to bring together trade unions, political activists and others opposed to the legislation.\n\nAddressing a press conference in Nugegoda yesterday, JAV\u2019s National Executive Committee member Vasantha Mudalige said the Government had temporarily shelved plans to introduce the PSTA following strong protests by the public.\n\nHe said the Government had initially intended to table and pass the proposed legislation in Parliament on November 4 and 5, but public resistance had forced it to reconsider.\n\n\u201cWe believe it is a positive step that the Government has, to some extent, done by responding to people\u2019s voices against those laws. However, we urge the Government to repeal the PTA, just as it should withdraw the PSTA. The Online Safety Act must also be repealed,\u201d he said.\n\nMudalige warned that the proposed laws could be used to suppress dissent and silence citizens opposing Government policies.\n\n\u201cIf this legislation is enacted, the people will only be able to open their mouths to eat or to yawn,\u201d he said.\n\nHe accused the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Government of abandoning the democratic principles it advocated during the 2022 public uprising, which challenged political corruption and economic mismanagement.\n\nMudalige said the Government\u2019s economic policies, centred on the International Monetary Fund (IMF) program, had imposed a heavy burden on ordinary citizens through taxation and rising living costs."
