    "md5": "308701671914e404c1e8e4dc4305730d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "New UN Women report calls for stronger action to advance women\u2019s political leadership in SL\n\nUN Women Head of Office Ramaaya Salgado (right) presents the \u201cBeyond Numbers\u201d report to Mass Media Deputy Minister\n\nUN Women yesterday launched a new report \u201cBeyond Numbers\u201d examining the impact and lessons of Sri Lanka\u2019s 25% local government quota, bringing together government representatives, political actors, women leaders, civil society, development partners and the United Nations to discuss the future of women\u2019s political participation and leadership in Sri Lanka.\n\nLaunched at a strategic dialogue convened by UN Women Sri Lanka, the report brought together government representatives, political actors, women leaders, civil society, development partners, academia and the United Nations to reflect on the future of women\u2019s political participation and leadership in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe launch comes at an important moment for the country. Recent elections have brought notable gains in women\u2019s representation and visibility in political office, including an increase in the number of women elected to Parliament. While these developments signal progress, women continue to face barriers at multiple stages of political participation, including party gatekeeping, limited access to campaign resources and political networks, unequal opportunities for leadership within party structures, harassment and violence, and persistent social norms that question women\u2019s legitimacy as political leaders.\n\nThe report highlights the significance of the 25% quota for women\u2019s seats in local government, which increased women\u2019s representation in local authorities from approximately 2% to nearly 23%. Beyond increasing numbers, the quota provides important lessons on what enables women to enter political institutions, the extent to which greater representation translates into influence, and where structural barriers continue to impede women\u2019s leadership and participation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-3ab10379",
