    "md5": "772de6e57194d4aaae286d5f25ce890c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "The report highlights the significance of the 25% quota for women\u2019s seats in local government, which increased women\u2019s representation in local authorities from approximately 2% to nearly 23%. Beyond increasing numbers, the quota provides important lessons on what enables women to enter political institutions, the extent to which greater representation translates into influence, and where structural barriers continue to impede women\u2019s leadership and participation.\n\nDiscussions during the dialogue explored the relationship between representation, influence and political leadership; the implications of the quota experience for future reforms at provincial and national levels; and the institutional, political and social changes needed to strengthen pathways for women\u2019s leadership. Participants also examined issues related to nomination processes, campaign financing, leadership progression, safety, accountability and the broader enabling environment for women in politics.\n\nThe dialogue further underscored the importance of ensuring that efforts to strengthen political participation are inclusive and responsive to the experiences of women facing multiple and intersecting forms of exclusion, including women from minority communities, women with disabilities, young women, rural women, women affected by conflict, and women outside established political networks.\n\nThe event forms part of UN Women\u2019s ongoing support to evidence-based policymaking, inclusive governance and women\u2019s leadership in Sri Lanka. Findings from the report and recommendations emerging from the dialogue will contribute to future policy engagement, advocacy, programming and partnerships aimed at advancing women\u2019s political participation and leadership, and ensuring that increased representation translates into meaningful influence and lasting change."
  },
