    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s service crisis: When poor service becomes an economic\u00a0problem",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Sri-Lanka-s-service-crisis-When-poor-service-becomes-an-economicproblem/4-799604",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793749892.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-93e20520-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0093a4c472c410c9b5e4dd6da2f1d1c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1700,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s service crisis: When poor service becomes an economic\u00a0problem\n\nSri Lankan hospitality was once unmatched. Warmth, attentiveness, and generosity were qualities associated not only with our tourism industry but with the country itself. Increasingly, however, the experience of service in Sri Lanka tells a different story.\n\nAcross restaurants, retail, hospitality and even professional services, poor service is becoming difficult to ignore. Long waits, indifferent attitudes, limited product knowledge, poor communication and an apparent reluctance to go beyond the minimum are increasingly commonplace. While it is tempting to dismiss this as an \u201cattitude problem\u201d, doing so overlooks a more important question: what has happened to the environment in which Sri Lankans work?\n\nSri Lanka has undergone an extraordinary succession of shocks: decades of conflict, the Easter attacks, COVID-19 and, most recently, the 2022 economic crisis. These experiences do not disappear simply because inflation stabilises or GDP begins growing again.\n\nPerhaps, therefore, some of what we interpret as apathy is exhaustion.\n\nThere is an underlying pessimism that appears to have settled across parts of society. When employees feel financially insecure, see limited possibilities for advancement, and watch peers leave for opportunities overseas \u2014 expecting consistently enthusiastic service without addressing these underlying conditions is unrealistic.\n\nService improves when there are incentives to provide it. Competitive markets create consequences and where competition is limited, employment is disconnected from performance. Organisations tolerate mediocrity and these incentives weaken."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-93e20520",
    "num": "dailyftlk-93e20520",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s service crisis: When poor service becomes an economic\u00a0problem",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Sri-Lanka-s-service-crisis-When-poor-service-becomes-an-economicproblem/4-799604",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793749892.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-93e20520-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c4d29fab91690723aad2aacfd63dea36",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "Service improves when there are incentives to provide it. Competitive markets create consequences and where competition is limited, employment is disconnected from performance. Organisations tolerate mediocrity and these incentives weaken.\n\nThese are also questions of corporate governance. How businesses manage performance, reward employees and invest in human capital ultimately determines their productivity and competitiveness. Strong governance requires accountability at every level, including management\u2019s responsibility to create the conditions in which employees can perform.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s skills problem predates the economic crisis. Much of the country\u2019s workforce was historically shaped by a colonial education and employment system designed to produce disciplined administrators and service workers who could meet established standards \u2013 rather than innovators and independent problem-solvers. That model may once have served the needs of the British administration and the economy of its time, but it is increasingly outdated in a world that demands creativity, initiative, and adaptability. An education system that has changed too slowly, combined with institutional cultures shaped by hierarchy, entitlement and limited competition, has allowed these habits to become deeply embedded.\n\nThere is also a more fundamental question: do employees know what excellent service looks like? Customer service is itself a skill. Anticipating needs, communicating professionally, resolving complaints, understanding products and creating an experience require training and exposure. Businesses cannot demand \u201cbetter attitudes\u201d without investing in developing them.\n\nSri Lanka may also need to confront a politically uncomfortable question: does the country have enough people willing and able to fill the jobs its economy requires?"
  },
