    "md5": "df1b7acca80fe90021e02a506a0d2372",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1785,
    "chunk_text": "There is also a more fundamental question: do employees know what excellent service looks like? Customer service is itself a skill. Anticipating needs, communicating professionally, resolving complaints, understanding products and creating an experience require training and exposure. Businesses cannot demand \u201cbetter attitudes\u201d without investing in developing them.\n\nSri Lanka may also need to confront a politically uncomfortable question: does the country have enough people willing and able to fill the jobs its economy requires?\n\nForeign labour should not substitute for improving wages and conditions for Sri Lankan workers. However, selective labour migration could alleviate genuine shortages, introduce new skills and, importantly, increase competition within parts of the labour market.\n\nSri Lanka increasingly depends on services to generate employment, tourism receipts, investment and economic growth. The World Bank identifies tourism and services as important contributors to the country\u2019s recent recovery. A country seeking higher-value tourists and foreign investment cannot accept declining service standards as inevitable.\n\nThe experience of Singapore demonstrates what sustained investment in service quality, skills and institutional standards can achieve. Over the past decade, Singapore has continued to strengthen its position as a global services hub, particularly in finance, logistics, professional services, tourism and digital industries. Singapore\u2019s progress has not been based simply on demanding better attitudes from workers. It has involved systematic investment in education, technical and vocational training, productivity, digitalisation and service standards, supported by institutions such as SkillsFuture and the Singapore Tourism Board."
  },
