    "chunk_size_bytes": 1827,
    "chunk_text": "The experience of Singapore demonstrates what sustained investment in service quality, skills and institutional standards can achieve. Over the past decade, Singapore has continued to strengthen its position as a global services hub, particularly in finance, logistics, professional services, tourism and digital industries. Singapore\u2019s progress has not been based simply on demanding better attitudes from workers. It has involved systematic investment in education, technical and vocational training, productivity, digitalisation and service standards, supported by institutions such as SkillsFuture and the Singapore Tourism Board.\n\nNeither example is directly transferable to Sri Lanka. Singapore has far greater fiscal capacity and a highly centralised system, while India benefits from a much larger domestic market and a substantial technology workforce. But both demonstrate that service quality and service-sector growth are not accidental. They emerge when countries combine skills development, competition, infrastructure, professional management, technology, and credible standards.\n\nThe solution therefore requires action from both Government and business: modernise vocational and soft-skills education; professionalise service careers; reward excellence; facilitate carefully regulated foreign labour where genuine shortages exist; and create more competitive markets in which consumers can hold businesses accountable.\n\nMost importantly, businesses must invest again in their people. This is where the social dimension of ESG becomes particularly relevant. Fair remuneration, employee well-being, skills development and opportunities for advancement are fundamental to building a productive and motivated workforce. Service quality cannot sustainably exceed the quality of the employment experience behind it."
  },
