    "md5": "a844b03f2f980f00e92469980023b025",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "Why Sri Lanka needs serious reset of its tourism strategy\n\nTWC Holdings Chairman Thilan Wijesinghe speaks at the THINC Tourism Conference\n\nOnly 6% of Sri Lanka\u2019s graded resort rooms internationally branded vs. at least 33% in Maldives, Vietnam, Mauritius\n\nSays Govt. quoted Rs. 20 m per acre on 99-year leases in 2012 when freehold East Coast land sold at Rs. 5.5 m\n\nProposes destination-level zoning, tax on idle beachfront land, viability-gap funding for domestic flights, curbs on high-rise beach apartments\n\nPitches international luxury train on Colombo-Badulla line and F1 street race in Colombo as moonshots\n\nFollowing is the speech by TWC Holdings Chairman Thilan Wijesinghe on the subject \u2018Tourism Uncensored\u2019 at the THINC Tourism Conference on 15 October, 2026. Drawing on 20 years in small luxury tourism, starting with Ceylon Tea Trails, Wijesinghe, who is also a Director of Ventive Hospitality (India), Resplendent Ceylon and Tea Trails, sets out what Sri Lanka got right and wrong in tourism. He faults successive governments for arrival targets without strategy, mispriced State land and lax coastal zoning, and calls for a strategic reset through five policy reforms and two \u2018moonshot\u2019 projects to move the country up the tourism value chain.\n\nAs the subject assigned to me is Tourism Uncensored, I intend to speak candidly about the lessons I have learnt over the past 20 years while pursuing my passion for small luxury tourism in Sri Lanka. I will speak also about what we got right, what we got wrong, and what I believe we must do differently if Sri Lanka is to realise its tourism potential.\n\nIn 2004, I undertook a seven-day road trip around Sri Lanka with Robert Hecker, the Managing Director of Horwath, a well-known hotel consultancy firm. During that journey, I realised that Sri Lanka had received what may be one of the greatest destination taglines in history as far back as 1293, when Marco Polo described it as \u201cthe finest island of its size in the world\u201d."
  },
