    "md5": "2fcd387ef86519efa6a300c5b0a1019a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1941,
    "chunk_text": "In 2004, I undertook a seven-day road trip around Sri Lanka with Robert Hecker, the Managing Director of Horwath, a well-known hotel consultancy firm. During that journey, I realised that Sri Lanka had received what may be one of the greatest destination taglines in history as far back as 1293, when Marco Polo described it as \u201cthe finest island of its size in the world\u201d.\n\nWhat made the concept unique was that we did not need to build a conventional resort on one large, contiguous piece of land. Instead, we restored five plantation bungalows spread across an area of approximately 50 square kilometres and operate them as one integrated resort. We developed what we called a mothership-and-satellite concept: several individual properties, each with its own identity, brought together under one hospitality operation.\n\nWhen Tea Trails opened in stages in 2006 and 2007, we encountered what I can only describe as a perfect storm. First, through my own naivety and inexperience, I had not estimated a pre-opening and marketing budget. Second, Sri Lanka\u2019s conflict intensified during 2006 and 2007, creating an extremely difficult environment in which to launch a luxury tourism product.\n\nOur saving grace came from an unexpected source. In 2006, the tourist board had entered into a public-relations contract with Batey, the Singapore-based PR agency known for its work on major tourism and the \u201cSingapore Girl\u201d campaigns.\n\nI approached the then Chairman of the Tourist Board and requested permission to invite foreign journalists visiting Sri Lanka through this programme to experience Tea Trails. The resulting articles by these journalists were extraordinarily positive. International publications featured the resort as \u201cThe world\u2019s best place to pop the question to your partner\u201d, \u201cTop five colonial holidays in the world\u201d, \u201cBest all-inclusive hotel in the world\u201d and \u201cThe top 10 world's greatest trips\u201d."
  },
