    "md5": "cc28056410ddaba8af62c45cd289cba9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1606,
    "chunk_text": "I approached the then Chairman of the Tourist Board and requested permission to invite foreign journalists visiting Sri Lanka through this programme to experience Tea Trails. The resulting articles by these journalists were extraordinarily positive. International publications featured the resort as \u201cThe world\u2019s best place to pop the question to your partner\u201d, \u201cTop five colonial holidays in the world\u201d, \u201cBest all-inclusive hotel in the world\u201d and \u201cThe top 10 world's greatest trips\u201d.\n\nThe lesson is simple: strategic marketing and public relations are investments in enhancing the economic value of a tourism product or destination. The Government must therefore recognise that there is a substantial cost to not investing in strategic destination marketing.\n\nLesson Two: Design and storytelling determine what a resort can charge\n\nMy second lesson came soon after the end of the conflict. Around 2010, I became a shareholder in a 100-acre property on Sri Lanka\u2019s East Coast.\n\nI asked the CEO of a prominent ground-handling company a straightforward question: \u201cIf I were to build a hotel here, what room rate could I expect?\u201d His answer was that we might achieve between $ 80 and $ 100 per night. I then showed him photographs of a resort in Indonesia named Nihi Watu, in a remote location that was difficult to access. The resort\u2019s design and concept offered a very different experience from a conventional beach hotel. When he saw the photographs, his assessment changed immediately. He believed that a resort of this nature could potentially command $ 200 to $ 250 per night."
  },
