    "chunk_size_bytes": 1668,
    "chunk_text": "I asked the CEO of a prominent ground-handling company a straightforward question: \u201cIf I were to build a hotel here, what room rate could I expect?\u201d His answer was that we might achieve between $ 80 and $ 100 per night. I then showed him photographs of a resort in Indonesia named Nihi Watu, in a remote location that was difficult to access. The resort\u2019s design and concept offered a very different experience from a conventional beach hotel. When he saw the photographs, his assessment changed immediately. He believed that a resort of this nature could potentially command $ 200 to $ 250 per night.\n\nLesson Three: Building hotels is not the same as building a destination\n\nMy third lesson came from Pasikuda on the East Coast. Following the end of the conflict, the Government invested in infrastructure, including roads, electricity and water, to encourage private investment in hotels.\n\nHowever, what emerged was a cluster of hotels ranging from five-star to two-star properties, without a coherent approach to destination planning, design, positioning or the overall visitor experience. There was little sense of place and limited coordination between the individual developments. Today, some properties in Pasikuda face serious financial difficulties, and others have reportedly been offered for sale, some at distressed prices.\n\nPasikuda was another lesson learnt: Sri Lanka has followed the simplistic assumption that if we build enough hotels, the tourists will come without always asking whether the product being built is appropriate for its location, whether it has a clear market position, and whether the destination as a whole will be competitive"
  },
  {
