    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7c001ba0ce2c24abfd75cf19188a0d6a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1566,
    "chunk_text": "Pasikuda was another lesson learnt: Sri Lanka has followed the simplistic assumption that if we build enough hotels, the tourists will come without always asking whether the product being built is appropriate for its location, whether it has a clear market position, and whether the destination as a whole will be competitive\n\nAnother problem was the approach to pricing land for tourism development. In 2012, when my partners were acquiring freehold land on the East Coast at approximately Rs. 5.5 million per acre, the Government was quoting approximately Rs. 20 million per acre for 99-year leases on comparable East Coast lands \u2013 an almost 400% premium to the value of freehold land at the time!\n\nThis matters particularly in Sri Lanka, where the State owns a substantial share of the land. Beachfront land suitable for tourism is a scarce economic resource. Government owned land should be allocated and priced in a manner that encourages the highest sustainable economic value from its use. When land is priced beyond what a high-quality resort can economically support, investors respond by reducing their development footprint, compromising the concept, or abandoning the investment altogether.\n\nPolicy mistake: Doing away with height restriction on beach properties\n\nAnother concern was the relaxation of height restrictions that had traditionally limited construction in coastal areas to the height of a coconut tree. Sri Lanka subsequently saw the growth of apartment developments on beachfront properties, marketed for short-term tourist accommodation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-3902def9",
    "num": "dailyftlk-3902def9",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "Why Sri Lanka needs serious reset of its tourism strategy",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Why-Sri-Lanka-needs-serious-reset-of-its-tourism-strategy/14-799605",
