    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8a7d77f6d7da7f1871bc77215ebc7d9c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "International brands can bring distribution networks, loyalty programmes, global marketing, operating systems and access to customers who might otherwise not consider a destination. They can also help local investors benchmark their products against international standards.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tourism earnings have not consistently reflected the extraordinary potential of its natural and cultural assets. We need to understand why, and then address the underlying causes. The strategic question remains: are we doing enough to attract investors and operators capable of helping us compete in the world\u2019s most valuable tourism segments?\n\nSo, where do we go from here? My answer is that we need to reflect, reassess and re-strategise. Let me emphasise that strategy does not mean commissioning yet another master plan, of which there are many. The issue is whether our plans have been translated into coherent policies, investment decisions and measurable results.\n\nHere are five \u201cuncensored\u201d proposals I would like to propose.\n\n(1)Introduce destination-level zoning that determines what we want each tourism area to become. We should not repeat on the East Coast the mistakes that have occurred in many parts of the South Coast.\n\n(2)Introduce viability-gap funding for domestic aviation. I have been advocating this for several years, particularly in relation to facilitating access to the North and East.\n\n(3)Address the underutilisation of scarce beachfront land. This is perhaps my most controversial proposal. We should consider a carefully designed land tax or other fiscal mechanism for suitable beachfront land that remains undeveloped for extended periods. The objective should be to discourage speculative landholding that prevents productive use of a scarce resource.\n\n(4)Restrict high-rise beachfront apartment developments to designated zones. High-rise developments may be appropriate in selected urban zones. It is not necessarily appropriate everywhere along our coastline."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
