    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1f26525ebd5fc3693ebbb1d6c584f0bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "(4)Restrict high-rise beachfront apartment developments to designated zones. High-rise developments may be appropriate in selected urban zones. It is not necessarily appropriate everywhere along our coastline.\n\nBeyond these reforms, I believe Sri Lanka needs to think bigger. We should identify one or two ambitious projects capable of changing how the world perceives the country. I have personally explored two such possibilities.\n\n1.An internationally branded luxury train. Imagine attracting an internationally recognised luxury rail operator, potentially a brand such as Orient Express, to develop a world-class train experience in Sri Lanka. The Colombo-Badulla rail track is possibly one of the world\u2019s most picturesque train journeys rising up to over 6000 feet above sea level and is the highest track in the world in the 5\u00bd foot gauge. We are also probably the only country in the world where in under a day you can take a train to all 8 world heritage sites in Sri Lanka. What is stopping such an investment \u2013 safety. While no one has got killed in recent times in Sri Lanka due to train accidents, the railway records show many derailments caused by poor maintenance.\n\n2.My second moonshot is even more ambitious: to explore the possibility of bringing Formula 1 to Colombo following the successful example of Singapore. F1 wants to be in South Asia, but was discontinued in India after 2013 following commercial, tax and regulatory difficulties. We have a perfect track in Colombo stretching from Port City to Galle Face and Baladaksha Mawatha. We also have superior logistics. This would undoubtedly be a complex undertaking, requiring substantial investment, Government coordination and a long-term commercial commitment. But we should at least explore the opportunity, considering there is potential investment interest from India."
  },
