    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "2.My second moonshot is even more ambitious: to explore the possibility of bringing Formula 1 to Colombo following the successful example of Singapore. F1 wants to be in South Asia, but was discontinued in India after 2013 following commercial, tax and regulatory difficulties. We have a perfect track in Colombo stretching from Port City to Galle Face and Baladaksha Mawatha. We also have superior logistics. This would undoubtedly be a complex undertaking, requiring substantial investment, Government coordination and a long-term commercial commitment. But we should at least explore the opportunity, considering there is potential investment interest from India.\n\nThe underlying point is that the value of a luxury experience increasingly depends on its rarity. The world\u2019s most discerning travellers are prepared to pay a premium for properties that offer something they cannot easily find elsewhere. This principle has influenced my own work over the past several years, including the investments I pursue, the projects on which my company advises, and the boards on which I serve.\n\nSri Lanka has a significant opportunity in this segment. It has been 27 years since the Aman brand established a presence in Sri Lanka. We should ask why the country has not attracted more internationally recognised ultra-luxury resorts in the intervening period.\n\nI am however pleased to say that management agreements have recently been signed for a Ritz-Carlton Reserve resort project in Sri Lanka, which is proceeding through its preliminary approvals. There is also interest from other international hospitality brands, supported by investments from India and the Middle East. These developments are encouraging. They suggest that, despite the policy missteps of the past, a new wave of international investments and brands could still reshape Sri Lanka\u2019s resort landscape."
  },
