    "chunk_text": "Let us give credit where it is due. Foreign direct investment (FDI) reached approximately $1.06 billion in 2025, the first time inflows have crossed the billion-dollar mark, and a rise of roughly 72% on 2024. Revenue collection has been restored. The Public Debt Management Act, No. 33 of 2024, has placed debt reporting on a transparent statutory footing. Our position on the corruption perceptions ranking improved from 121st to 107th in a single year. These are real achievements, and they belong to the country, not to any one party.\n\nThe clock runs against the recipient, never against the State\n\nConsider what the mid-sized investor, the person who actually makes up most of our transactions, encounters on arrival. To open an Inward Investment Account (IIA), through which capital must legally enter the country, original physical documents must be couriered or hand-delivered from abroad. There is no uniform procedure across licensed banks, with each institution applying its own internal processes. More importantly, the Central Bank has not published prescribed guidelines setting out the mandatory disclosures required from investors or specifying maximum processing timelines. Consequently, while the investor is bound by strict compliance requirements, neither the banks nor the regulator is subject to a corresponding standard of procedural certainty or timeliness. Unless the Central Bank addresses these gaps by introducing uniform procedures, clear disclosure requirements and prescribed processing timelines, the framework remains deficient in both prudence and substance."
