    "chunk_text": "The same pattern runs through the courts. A litigant who misses a prescribed period may lose a case before it is heard. Meanwhile 1.13 million cases are pending across 293 courts. The rules of procedure are strict with the party and silent on the delay. Contract enforcement is now slow enough that every investor prices it into every deal, and we pay that premium, whether or not we ever see the dispute.\n\nWe have spent decades debating tax holidays and concessions. But the marginal investor in Sri Lanka is not a multinational deciding between continents; it is a firm placing a few million dollars and borrowing against a construction schedule. For an investor, an approval that may take three months or eighteen months is not merely an inconvenience; it is an unfinanceable risk. Predictability of process matters more than the generosity of the incentive, just as, in labour law, predictability of cost matters more than the level of cost. That is precisely why the absence of prescribed timelines in any of our guidelines or handbooks cannot be justified: the framework must provide foreign investors with a predictable timeframe within which approvals will be processed.\n\nThere is also a question of fairness that goes beyond economics. A State that demands punctuality from its people while exempting itself teaches a corrosive lesson: that rules are for the governed, not for those who govern. Investors notice that. So do our own professionals: skilled workers now account for 76.5% of departures for foreign employment. We are losing the very educated workforce we advertise."
