    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "There is also a question of fairness that goes beyond economics. A State that demands punctuality from its people while exempting itself teaches a corrosive lesson: that rules are for the governed, not for those who govern. Investors notice that. So do our own professionals: skilled workers now account for 76.5% of departures for foreign employment. We are losing the very educated workforce we advertise.\n\nFDI also has a quality that borrowed money does not. It does not have to be repaid on a fixed schedule, it brings skills and export markets with it, and it creates the jobs that may persuade our professionals to build their careers at home. As heavy debt service resumes in 2028, a country that has diversified its sources of capital will face those repayments from strength. A country that has not will be tempted back into the same cycle that brought us to crisis.\n\nThe Government must give greater credence to attracting foreign direct investment, improving Sri Lanka\u2019s competitiveness as an investment destination and establishing a predictable, prudent and investor-friendly tax policy. Revenue mobilisation is important, but it cannot be the sole measure of economic success.\n\nThis is precisely why the timeliness of the State matters so much. Investors do not compare Sri Lanka with its own past; they compare it with Thailand, Vietnam, and Malaysia, each competing for the same capital. Those jurisdictions offer investors something we too often do not: certainty about how long the State will take to say yes or no. Every month an approval sits unanswered is a month of financing costs, a delayed construction start and, increasingly, a decision to invest elsewhere. Investors can price risk; what they cannot price is silence."
