    "chunk_size_bytes": 1892,
    "chunk_text": "This is precisely why the timeliness of the State matters so much. Investors do not compare Sri Lanka with its own past; they compare it with Thailand, Vietnam, and Malaysia, each competing for the same capital. Those jurisdictions offer investors something we too often do not: certainty about how long the State will take to say yes or no. Every month an approval sits unanswered is a month of financing costs, a delayed construction start and, increasingly, a decision to invest elsewhere. Investors can price risk; what they cannot price is silence.\n\nOur natural advantages are real: a location on the main east-west shipping lanes, an extraordinary tourism endowment and a literate population that could make us a regional centre for higher education. But advantages that cannot be unlocked on time are advantages in name only. A State that answers promptly is the bridge between stability and growth.\n\nUnless we are able to address the existing tax structure, Sri Lanka may no longer be a cost-competitive destination when labour costs are considered alongside the prevailing tax burden. Countries such as India and Bangladesh benefit from relatively lower labour costs, partly due to their large labour force and population. Consequently, when both taxation and labour costs are taken into account, Sri Lanka may no longer offer a significant cost advantage.\n\nThe remedy is neither complicated nor expensive, and most of it requires no new Act of Parliament.\n\nFirst, gazette maximum determination periods for every category of investment approval, and attach a rule of deemed approval: if an agency fails to respond within the period, the application is approved unless refused in writing with reasons. A published timeline without consequence is an aspiration. Deemed approval turns it into a commitment. This can be done by regulation under existing investment law within six months."
