    "md5": "c1eac574867b73d7a6edca9844673863",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "South Asia\u2019s demographic shift is an opportunity\u2014but only if the region acts now\n\nOne in five workers worldwide lives in South Asia, where 1.17 billion people are in their prime working years. This represents an enormous economic opportunity. A workforce of this scale can fuel economic growth, create wealth, and drive innovation. South Asia has already reaped some benefits from this demographic dividend: Since 2000, the growing share of working-age adults has contributed to gains in GDP growth and labor productivity.\n\nBut this demographic advantage will not last forever. South Asia is aging faster and at lower income levels, than comparable emerging market and developing economies. By 2049, people aged 65 and older are projected to account for 14% of the region\u2019s population, marking the region\u2019s transition from an aging to an aged society.\n\nThis shift presents an urgent challenge, but also an opportunity. If South Asia acts now, it can capture more of the economic benefits of its large working-age population while preparing for a future in which more people live longer.\n\nOnly 60% of South Asia\u2019s working-age population is employed, while 88% of employed workers hold informal jobs that pay less, offer limited protections and benefits, and make no contributions to the taxes that fund public services. A large working-age population alone cannot deliver a demographic dividend. People need opportunities to put their skills to productive use and they need to remain healthy enough to do so.\n\nThat means making it easier for micro, small, and medium enterprises to access finance and grow, streamlining regulations that hold back businesses, and improving education and training so workers have the skills employers need. It also means removing the barriers that keep women from entering and remaining in the workforce, and addressing the rising burden of chronic disease which is a growing issue for working-age people."
  },
