    "md5": "a25b864a7404307884e3cbb44746f83c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1773,
    "chunk_text": "That means making it easier for micro, small, and medium enterprises to access finance and grow, streamlining regulations that hold back businesses, and improving education and training so workers have the skills employers need. It also means removing the barriers that keep women from entering and remaining in the workforce, and addressing the rising burden of chronic disease which is a growing issue for working-age people.\n\nGovernments can help people remain healthier longer by shifting health systems toward prevention and primary care rather than relying on hospital care. Employers and policymakers can expand mid- and late-career training and create phased pathways to retirement, enabling people to work for as long as they want. And formal long-term care systems, supported by trained and skilled care workers, can improve quality of life for older people while easing the burden on families.\n\nOver time, a healthier older population, supported by the right institutions and opportunities, could generate a second demographic dividend. They could also support a silver economy: Older people are workers, consumers, savers and contributors to their communities. Their needs and preferences can create markets for better housing, health services, care, and financial products.\n\nStronger social protection and financial markets will be essential to turning longer lives into greater economic security and opportunity. Only 17% of South Asia\u2019s working-age population contributes to a pension scheme, and just 10% of older South Asians receive pension benefits\u2014the lowest coverage globally. Pension and financial systems need to protect workers\u2019 savings, expand basic coverage for those who need it, and help channel greater savings into productive investment."
