    "chunk_size_bytes": 1592,
    "chunk_text": "Cost of living crisis: The system destroyer?\n\nThere is nothing to quarrel about the agonising fact that rising cost of living (C-O-L) is a pandemic that has propelled anti-Government protests and street riots right across the Americas, Europe and Asia; and it is this issue that is going to decide the outcome of US Midterm elections.\n\nMany of the protestors and rioters in the EU for example are adolescents who are increasingly concerned about rising price levels, which make college and higher education unaffordable to them. Given the rapid spread of robotic technology and AI in both private and public sectors, the Generation Z\u2019s prospects of gaining employment are becoming increasingly grim. Will these riots and protests ultimately end with a wave of Luddism?\n\nC-O-L is the ultimate economic thermometer which measures peoples\u2019 happiness or discontent in any economy or society. Prices of consumables rise and fall depending on the stock of supply available in the market. There is no denying that the current pandemic is the consequence of supply constraints driven by two main forces: wars and climate change. The first is recent and the second is much older. But both are beyond the control of monetary authorities and their pet remedy to bring down C-O-L by monitoring the rate of inflation to counter it by raising interest rate and urging Governments to cut public spending is a futile exercise, which will eventually reduce investment, increase unemployment and worsen poverty level.\n\nThe post-Cold War economic order, hailed by Fukuyama as\u00a0 \u2018The End of History and The"
