    "chunk_text": "Consumers are not to question how and why but to go and buy, and buy what? Whatever the market dishes out through its aggressive marketing techniques. Hedonistic adaptation of consumer tastes and availability of credit facilities has made consumers and especially the young ones to buy what they do not need with money they do not have, perhaps to keep up with the Joneses or to impress someone whom they do not like. In short, the market creates hedonistic wants and sells them as needs.\n\nThe cost-of living crisis is not going to disappear soon but is destined to get worse and may force the long-awaited system change.\n\nThe AKD/NPP Government came to power with the promise of system change. But so far that change has been confined only to areas of governance and judicial administration. As far as the economy is concerned, the IMF continues to determine the terms and direction of management.\u00a0 Perhaps the need to bring stability to an economy which was on the brink of bankruptcy and burdened with heavy foreign debt could justify the pragmatic option of going along with the IMF program.\u00a0 That seemed to have been a popular choice when the Government took control of the Government. As a result, the economy was not only stabilised but even recorded a respectable 4% growth rate last year.\u00a0 But the pains of that growth had been unequally shared, and it was the lower-income groups that had made the most sacrifice.\n\nThe Government knows very well that the IMF is not a friend of the poor but of the rich and the so-called enterprising. It is these groups that set the prices in the market and ordinary consumers have no choice but to pay or go without buying the products. It is this stratum of society that feels most the pain of increasing C-O-L, and their numbers are swelling."
