    "chunk_size_bytes": 1741,
    "chunk_text": "Between NPP\u2019s authoritarian agenda and Opposition\u2019s racist battle-plans\n\nIn May 2019, the Hasalaka police arrested 47-year-old M.R. Mazahima, because they couldn\u2019t tell the difference between a ship\u2019s wheel and the Dhammachakka (Wheel of Dhamma). Mazahima was accused of wearing a dress offensive to Buddhist feelings and detained under the ICCRP. The dress was sent to the Department of Buddhist Affairs for identification.\n\nIn July 2025, the Supreme Court ruled that Mazahima\u2019s arrest and detention were wrong and ordered the former OIC of the Hasalaka police to pay her compensation.\n\nIf the proposed Organised Crime Prevention Bill goes through, that same police will be given a freehand to arrest any citizen for wearing an \u2018organised crime\u2019 kind of dress or tattoo.\n\nWhoever drafted the Organised Crime Prevention Bill seems to have made liberal use of the cut-and-paste method. For instance, Section 5(2) of Singapore\u2019s Organised Crime Act of 2015, which deals with evidentiary factors for determining the guilt of an accused, states in clause c, \u201cwhether that person adopts the style of dress, handsigns, language or tattoos used by known members of the group\u201d (https://sso.agc.gov.sg/Act/OCA2015?ProvIds=P12-.) Section 4(2) of Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed bill, which deals with evidentiary factors for determining the guilt of an accused, states in clause c, \u201cwhether the person adopts the style of dress, hand signs, language or tattoos used by known members of organised criminal groups\u201d (https://documents.gov.lk/api/content-file-proxy?file=/bill-content/PL%2015938%20%5BS%5D%20Crime%20Act_1791287837.pdf). Almost word-to-word, including the misspelling handsigns (it should be two words or a hyphenated word)."
  },
