    "chunk_text": "Whoever drafted the Organised Crime Prevention Bill seems to have made liberal use of the cut-and-paste method. For instance, Section 5(2) of Singapore\u2019s Organised Crime Act of 2015, which deals with evidentiary factors for determining the guilt of an accused, states in clause c, \u201cwhether that person adopts the style of dress, handsigns, language or tattoos used by known members of the group\u201d (https://sso.agc.gov.sg/Act/OCA2015?ProvIds=P12-.) Section 4(2) of Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed bill, which deals with evidentiary factors for determining the guilt of an accused, states in clause c, \u201cwhether the person adopts the style of dress, hand signs, language or tattoos used by known members of organised criminal groups\u201d (https://documents.gov.lk/api/content-file-proxy?file=/bill-content/PL%2015938%20%5BS%5D%20Crime%20Act_1791287837.pdf). Almost word-to-word, including the misspelling handsigns (it should be two words or a hyphenated word).\n\nWhat tattoos, hand signals, language, and/or style of dress do Lankan organised crime groups sport? What kind of tattoos, hand signals, language, and/or style of dress would the police be instructed to look out for in a suspect? Or would the police be given a freehand to act according to whatever preconceived notions they harbour? In the absence of clear and specific guidelines, what is to prevent the police from giving into ignorance and prejudices and persecuting citizens (especially young persons with unorthodox appearances), the same way the ICCPR Act was used to persecute Mazahima?\n\nIn its election manifesto, the NPP promised to reform the Online Safety Act by removing its anti-democratic clauses. That promise is yet to be honoured.\n\nIn its election manifesto, the NPP promised to abolish the PTA. That promise was shattered with the Protection of the State from Terrorism Act."
