    "chunk_size_bytes": 1483,
    "chunk_text": "In its election manifesto, the NPP promised to reform the Online Safety Act by removing its anti-democratic clauses. That promise is yet to be honoured.\n\nIn its election manifesto, the NPP promised to abolish the PTA. That promise was shattered with the Protection of the State from Terrorism Act.\n\nThe Organised Crime Prevention Bill was drafted with no public consultation. In consequence, it contains provisions way more draconian and open to abuse than even the Protecting the State from Terrorism Act. For instance, under the PSTA, a suspect can be detained only for 48 hours before being arraigned while under the Organised Crime Prevention Bill, a suspect can be detained for 72 hours before being arraigned.\n\nImagine what some members of the police would do to a suspect in those 72 hours, especially since the Organised Crime Prevention Bill also allows confessions, if they are made before a police official of or above the rank of assistant superintendent of police (ASP).\n\nThis in a country where the IGP was accused by the AG\u2019s Department of turning police officers into a \u2018paramilitary force\u2019 and running \u201ca criminal network, allegedly exploiting police officers under his command for unlawful activities...\u201d That same Deshabandu Tennakoan was a Superintendent of Police (one rank higher than an ASP) when he ordered three suspects to be stripped, thrashed them with a \u2018three-wheeler belt\u2019, and forced them to apply Siddhalepa balm on their own genitalia."
