    "chunk_text": "This in a country where the IGP was accused by the AG\u2019s Department of turning police officers into a \u2018paramilitary force\u2019 and running \u201ca criminal network, allegedly exploiting police officers under his command for unlawful activities...\u201d That same Deshabandu Tennakoan was a Superintendent of Police (one rank higher than an ASP) when he ordered three suspects to be stripped, thrashed them with a \u2018three-wheeler belt\u2019, and forced them to apply Siddhalepa balm on their own genitalia.\n\nTwo fundamental rights judgements made last week remind us that unlawful arrests and custodial abuses are not isolated issues but \u2018symptoms of institutional failures,\u2019 as the Supreme Court put it.\n\nOn 30 October 2026, the Supreme Court ruled that the Kuliyapitiya police violated the fundamental rights of a jewellery shop owner. The police came into his house on 13 June 2022, accused him of buying gold from a suspect, handcuffed him, made him kneel, and assaulted him in front of his wife and children. They forced him to handover his vehicle and assaulted him some more, demanding gold. CCTV cameras in the shop showed him handing over gold to the officers. When he was eventually produced before a JMO, he was threatened by Inspector Manohara to deny any assault (https://dailyfc.com/supreme-court-slams-police-for-brutal-assault-extortion-orders-compensation-for-fundamental-rights-violations/).\n\nOn 30 October 2026, the Supreme Court ruled that the Dompe police violated the fundamental rights of a woman. In October 2019, she went to the police station with a lunch packet for her husband who had been arrested by the STF on allegations of brewing illicit liquor. She too was arrested and assaulted (https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Supreme-Court-rules-police-constable-violated-womans-fundamental-rights-through-assault/108-353909)."
  },
