    "chunk_size_bytes": 1718,
    "chunk_text": "On 30 October 2026, the Supreme Court ruled that the Dompe police violated the fundamental rights of a woman. In October 2019, she went to the police station with a lunch packet for her husband who had been arrested by the STF on allegations of brewing illicit liquor. She too was arrested and assaulted (https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Supreme-Court-rules-police-constable-violated-womans-fundamental-rights-through-assault/108-353909).\n\nTwo days before the custodial death at the Homagama police station, Gampaha Magistrate Bandula Gunaratne requested the Court of Appeal to file contempt charges against the IGP for ignoring a court order. The police had charged a man with possessing drugs, based on false information allegedly provided by officers of the Gampaha Police Anti-Corruption Unit. The Magistrate then ordered the IGP to issue a circular regarding arrests on drug charges. Five months on, the IGP continued to ignore the court order. \u201cIf the police treat a court order in this manner, what can be said about everything else in this country?\u201d the Magistrate asked in open court (https://srilankamirror.com/news/gampaha-magistrate-initiates-contempt-of-court-action-against-igp/).\n\nWhen guardians of the law turn into serial lawbreakers, it indicates not the presence of a few bad apples but a systemic issue. Instead of understanding the problem and dealing with it, the NPP Government is intent on bringing in a new law which would confer on this same police an unprecedented level of impunity. The police, whose chief officer might face contempt of court charges soon, will soon be able to justify any custodial crime by painting every victim in the lurid colours of an organised criminal."
