    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "When guardians of the law turn into serial lawbreakers, it indicates not the presence of a few bad apples but a systemic issue. Instead of understanding the problem and dealing with it, the NPP Government is intent on bringing in a new law which would confer on this same police an unprecedented level of impunity. The police, whose chief officer might face contempt of court charges soon, will soon be able to justify any custodial crime by painting every victim in the lurid colours of an organised criminal.\n\nIn Kalama Sutta, the Buddha explains to the Kalamas the importance of doing good in this life irrespective of whether they believe in an afterlife or not. This is excellent advice for any Government. Most governments believe they will never lose power. The NPP is no exception. Even so, it would make sense to follow the Buddha\u2019s advice and not enact repressive laws which could and would be used against them in case they lose power, someday.\n\nLast month, a group of monks led by that self-proclaimed Arhat Umandawe Samanthabadra made a clay figurine of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, stuck poison thorns into it, and consigned it to the custody of folkloric demon Mahasona for punishment.\n\nSpeaking a few days later, President Dissanayake mentioned the incident and wondered about the mentality of those who engage in such practices.\n\nIt is reasonable to assume that none of the monks who engaged in a practice condemned by the Buddha as an animal art believe in Mahasona. The whole sordid spectacle was aimed at inciting supernatural fear in the public mind and using it to weaken the Government. If, for instance, some natural calamity occurs in the coming months, the monks will depict it as supernatural punishment of the Government for jailing Galagoda-Atte Gnanasara.\n\nAddle Sinhala-Buddhist minds and use the resulting confusion to come to power.\n\nLike Udaya Gammanpila\u2019s baseless diatribe about an evil conspiracy to destroy Ruwanweliseya."
