    "md5": "1936b575fc0c7c0151d3a08a70e3b2e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "A flight may be asked to divert if the credibility of a threat \"is of a certain level\", Mason added, and in some cases the onboard crew may not even be advised on what the ground operators were dealing with.\n\nIn Rome, sources at Fiumicino airport told the BBC police bomb disposal experts carried out standard security protocols to ensure the safety of all passengers and crew.\n\nOne plane which landed in Murcia, southern Spain - a Ryanair flight coming from Manchester - was also found not to have any explosives on board, Spanish authorities said.\n\nOne passenger later said she was unaware of the bomb threat until after the plane landed.\n\n\"We weren't told anything during the flight,\" Sarah told BBC Radio 5 Live. \"The plane stopped quite suddenly and then we were kept on the runway for around 30 minutes.\"\n\n\"Then the fire engines turned up, the bomb squad turned up and we were asked to evacuate,\" she continues.\n\nSarah said the plane had not been on the ground a minute before it was \"surrounded by fire engines and police and a bomb squad\". All the passengers were then escorted by police, leaving all their belongings.\n\n\"We were only later told that it was an actual threat, a bomb threat to the plane itself,\" she added.\n\nTwo flights heading to Warsaw were also affected, including one from London Heathrow.\n\nThe Heathrow flight was originally meant to land at the Polish capital's main airport, Chopin, but was diverted instead to the smaller Modlin airport, briefly forcing its closure.\n\nA Scandinavian Airlines flight from Copenhagen to Warsaw-Chopin landed as planned.\n\nLater on Tuesday, Bulgarian Transport Minister Georgi Peev said there were bomb threats against two planes travelling to the country's capital Sofia.\n\nHe told the state-owned Bulgarian News Agency (BTA) the flights were from the northern Italian city of Bergamo and Ireland's capital Dublin.\n\nThe flights into Sofia were not diverted and no explosives were found when the planes were checked, Peev said."
