    "md5": "758f136157becbd10e3af93e2ed2b351",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "He told the state-owned Bulgarian News Agency (BTA) the flights were from the northern Italian city of Bergamo and Ireland's capital Dublin.\n\nThe flights into Sofia were not diverted and no explosives were found when the planes were checked, Peev said.\n\n\"Standard procedures for such incidents were immediately activated, and an operational headquarters comprising representatives of various institutions was convened,\" the statement said.\n\nThe border police added that the same email also reported a \"similar threat against aircraft arriving from Dublin\".\n\nIn a separate development, a bomb alert triggered the evacuation of 1,537 passengers and crew from an Israel-bound cruise ship in Greece, media reports from both countries say.\n\nInitial checks of the Panama-flagged Crown Iris vessel currently docked at a port on the Aegean island of Syros found no explosives, they said citing the Greek Coast Guard, and the ship was due to resume its journey.\n\nTuesday's aircraft incidents come a day after a Wizz Air flight from London Luton made an emergency landing in Budapest, escorted by a Hungarian fighter jet, after a bomb threat.\n\nWizz Air said later that security checks had been made and nothing was found.\n\nBBC Verify has consulted aviation safety experts about what happens on board a plane when a bomb threat is issued.\n\nMarco Chan, a former pilot for Cathay Pacific Airways and Hong Kong Airlines who is now a senior lecturer in aviation operations at Kingston University, said a bomb threat is considered \"genuine until it is resolved, even though most turn out to be hoaxes\".\n\nChan said crews will \"quietly search\" toilets, galleys and storage areas and will not touch a suspicious item unless it has to be taken to an area on the aircraft designated as the least risky bomb location.\n\nThe aircraft's captain will have multiple factors to assess, according to former airline pilot Terry Tozer, an aviation safety commentator."
