    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "While several television networks projected that Democrats would eventually win a House majority, most of the chamber's most tightly contested races had yet to be called.\n\nSo far, Democrats have picked up four seats from Republicans, meaning they can afford to lose one of their existing seats and still take control of the 435-member chamber.\n\nIn Florida, Republican Senator Ashley Moody fended off Democrat Angie Nixon but was ahead by only about 3 percentage points in a state Trump carried by 13 points in 2024, according to a vote count compiled by Reuters and USA Today, further evidence that even solidly Republican states were shifting dramatically toward Democrats.\n\nDemocrats held onto three House districts in Florida that Republican lawmakers had redrawn earlier this year, all of which backed Trump by at least 9 percentage points. The fate of the House overall remained uncertain, with the majority of races still uncalled.\n\nMidterm elections are typically viewed as a referendum on the president, and that is especially true for Trump, who dominates attention like no other politician. Trump himself told his supporters to \"pretend I'm on the ballot\" and held dozens of rallies in the closing weeks.\n\nAccording to exit polls conducted by SSRS on behalf of a consortium of US media outlets including major television networks and the Associated Press, half of voters nationwide said they saw the 2026 midterm elections as an opportunity to rebuke the president, and three-quarters said they are \"angry\" or \"dissatisfied\" with the way the country is headed.\n\nIn addition, 70 per cent of voters surveyed said they want Congress to provide more checks and balances on the president.\n\nHistory is against Republicans: The sitting president's party has lost ground in Congress in all but two midterms since World War Two. Trump's approval rating is hovering near his career lows, with voters frustrated by war with Iran, higher fuel prices and wider affordability concerns."
  },
