    "md5": "2ce91608da76e56014d38c2bf2440770",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "PUCSL proposes 1.46% electricity tariff increase for fourth quarter\n\nColombo, Nov. 4 (Daily Mirror) - The Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka (PUCSL) has proposed a 1.46% increase in electricity tariffs for the fourth quarter of 2026, following a preliminary review of cost and revenue forecasts submitted by electricity sector licensees.\n\nThe proposal will be discussed at a stakeholder consultation scheduled to be held at the Bandaranaike Memorial International Conference Hall (BMICH) in Colombo tomorrow (November 5).\n\nAccording to the PUCSL, the electricity sector's total forecasted cost of supply for the October-December quarter stands at Rs. 157.3 billion, while the expected revenue from electricity sales is Rs. 154 billion. This includes a revenue deficit of Rs. 1.1 billion brought forward from the previous period.\n\nBased on submissions by the National System Operator (NSO) and Electricity Distribution Lanka (Pvt) Ltd. (EDL), the required revenue increase was initially estimated at 2.88%.\n\nHowever, following its preliminary assessment, the PUCSL has reduced the proposed increase to 1.46% by removing an increase in generation capacity costs attributed to Electricity Generation Lanka (Pvt) Ltd. (EGL) and revising the estimated additional costs arising from the use of substandard coal during the second quarter of 2026.\n\nThe NSO has forecast electricity demand of 4,796 million units for the fourth quarter. Major hydro power is expected to contribute 32.34% of generation, while coal power is expected to account for 25.68%. Other renewable energy sources are forecast to contribute 28.81%.\n\nThe forecast also includes 8.94% from furnace oil, 3.65% from naphtha and 0.58% from diesel.\n\nThe estimated generation cost submitted by the NSO is Rs. 117.29 billion, while the energy cost component alone has been estimated at Rs. 98.3 billion."
  },
