    "chunk_text": "The forecast also includes 8.94% from furnace oil, 3.65% from naphtha and 0.58% from diesel.\n\nThe estimated generation cost submitted by the NSO is Rs. 117.29 billion, while the energy cost component alone has been estimated at Rs. 98.3 billion.\n\nThe Commission has also raised concerns over fuel pricing arrangements. It noted that EGL has yet to enter into a Fuel Supply Agreement, while transparent pricing provisions have not been included in the agreements covering the Westcoast and Sobadhanavi power plants. As a result, liquid fuel generation cost forecasts remain exposed to pricing assumptions made by the Ceylon Petroleum Corporation.\n\nMeanwhile, the NSO has projected generation capacity costs of Rs. 19 billion for the quarter, an increase of Rs. 1.1 billion compared with the Commission-approved costs for the third quarter. Of this increase, Rs. 700 million is attributed to EGL plants, which operate without Power Purchase Agreements.\n\nThe NSO has also included Rs. 703 million as capacity costs for battery energy storage systems, anticipating the phased connection of 160 MW of battery storage capacity.\n\nAnother issue under review is EDL's request for Rs. 2.1 billion in additional costs outside the multi-year tariff framework. Of this amount, Rs. 1.1 billion relates to loan repayments under the Supporting Electricity Supply Reliability Improvement Project (SESRIP).\n\nThe PUCSL has excluded this amount from its preliminary assessment, stating that costs relating to capital work in progress should be recovered through tariffs only after the relevant assets are completed and capitalised.\n\nThe Commission has also considered the revenue position for the second quarter of 2026, which is relevant to the current tariff review under the tariff methodology. The NSO reported a provisional revenue deficit of Rs. 1.1 billion after removing Rs. 2.9 billion in estimated additional costs linked to substandard coal usage during that period."
