    "description": "Sri Lanka needs to save its garment industries",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Sri-Lanka-needs-to-save-its-garment-industries/172-354106",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793770052.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-d425fb6f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6712661602a3d73ea46bf25fc5203878",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka needs to save its garment industries\n\nSri Lanka has spent decades building a garment industry that is now one of its biggest economic strengths. It has earned the confidence of leading clothing brands, created hundreds of thousands of jobs and brought in billions in foreign exchange. Yet manufacturers are finding it increasingly difficult to operate here.\n\nHigh operating costs, taxes, electricity bills and labour shortages are making Sri Lanka a less attractive manufacturing destination. Neighbouring countries are competing for investment. Sri Lanka risks losing industries it spent years building. Why should an investor stay when another country offers lower costs, a larger workforce and a more predictable business environment?\n\nApparel remains Sri Lanka\u2019s largest export industry, generating around US$5 billion annually and providing employment to hundreds of thousands. Behind these figures are families whose livelihoods depend on factories remaining open. Losing this industry would mean losing jobs, foreign exchange, buyers, expertise and decades of investment.\n\nRecent developments should concern the Government. NEXT Manufacturing closed its Katunayake factory in 2025, affecting around 1,400 employees, citing high operating costs. In 2026, MAS Holdings announced production at its Methliya facility in Thulhiriya would cease, affecting approximately 2,200 employees. Production would move elsewhere and the site be repurposed for fabric manufacturing. This was a restructuring, not a complete withdrawal. Nevertheless, it raises questions about Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to retain production.\n\nSri Lanka cannot assume investors will remain loyal because they have operated here for decades. Business decisions are driven by costs, efficiency and profitability. Bangladesh, Vietnam, India and Cambodia compete for orders with competitive costs, skilled workers and incentives. If Sri Lanka becomes too expensive, manufacturers and buyers have alternatives."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
