    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9b9d7af950479afb7c4069cfecfdcd81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1642,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka cannot assume investors will remain loyal because they have operated here for decades. Business decisions are driven by costs, efficiency and profitability. Bangladesh, Vietnam, India and Cambodia compete for orders with competitive costs, skilled workers and incentives. If Sri Lanka becomes too expensive, manufacturers and buyers have alternatives.\n\nThe answer is not simply to ask more people to join production lines. Workers need reasonable wages, safe workplaces, transport and opportunities to advance. The industry must challenge the perception that factory work offers few prospects. Training and promotion can make these jobs more attractive.\n\nEmployers cannot absorb every increase in costs without losing competitiveness. Protecting workers and keeping factories viable must go hand in hand. Taxation is another concern. Sri Lanka\u2019s standard corporate income tax rate is 30 per cent, while businesses face other levies and operating expenses. Although direct exports are zero-rated for VAT, the wider cost of doing business and imported inputs put pressure on manufacturers. Electricity and fuel costs matter too. Garment factories run machinery for long hours, and higher energy bills quickly erode margins. Manufacturers need predictable pricing and access to renewable energy. Sudden changes make it harder to plan orders and compete.\n\nThe Government should consider targeted incentives for genuine export manufacturers. These could include tax relief linked to retaining and creating jobs, investing in machinery, training workers and increasing exports. Support must have clear conditions and measurable results."
  },
