    "md5": "f95accd6d956efcf2dec5fb3d0100606",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 787,
    "chunk_text": "Kandy toilets are notorious\n\nPublic toilets in Kandy are few and far between \u2013 mostly being located in a cluster around the market. People \u2018caught short\u2019 up in D.S. Senanayake Street have a half mile to walk. When out shopping, people have to note closely the location of the nearest toilet to avoid being horribly embarrassed.\n\nOn arrival at the public toilets, shoppers are greeted by a stifling, choking smell of urine so thick and overwhelming that you can cut the air with a knife. But this must be ignored due to the pressing need in hand.\n\nThis unsanitary state of affairs is the result of many months of neglect by the authorities, who, one can suppose,\u00a0 are waiting for a sympathetic, generous benefactor who is willing to donate funds to make a fresh start, so to speak."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "PST and POC:  A recipe for abuse in their current form",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/PST-and-POC-A-recipe-for-abuse-in-their-current-form/172-354110",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793771655.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7a5103788e54a16e433ea29edf074537",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "PST and POC:  A recipe for abuse in their current form\n\nThis article is not meant to be yet another run-of-the-mill civil society protest over two bills: Protection of the State from Terrorism (PST), submitted to Parliament on October 6,\u00a0 and Prevention of Organised Crimes (POC), submitted on October 20. The PST\u00a0 is intended to replace the existing draconian Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), and POC is presented as an Act to give effect to the UN Convention against Transnational Organised Crime.\n\nThere is no gainsaying that Sri Lanka needs special criminal laws to combat terrorism and organised crime. The latter should be focused on the local underworld nexus with class A narcotics trafficking, which is the most consequential form of organised criminality in Sri Lanka.\n\nCrime scene at the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court where Ganemulle Sanjeewa was gunned down (Pic courtesy: Tamil Guardian)\n\nExisting legal provisions provide inadequate prosecutorial space to combat the local underworld or fight terrorism if the PTA is to be repealed without a replacement bill. The lacuna of the existing penal code provisions in fighting organised crime was on display when a large number of high-profile underworld kingpins fled the country immediately after being granted bail.\u00a0 Of twenty-five underworld criminals, held in Bossa detention centre and subsequently released on bail, 16 have already fled the country. The underworld has money to splash, and the majority of the escaped underworld members were duly represented by\u00a0 President\u2019s Counsels.\n\nAmong the high-profile figures are Mohammad Najim Mohammad Imaran alias Kanjipani Imran, who fled the country five days after being granted bail on December 20k 2022,\u00a0 Madushanka de Silva alias Podi Lassie, Ganemulle Sanjeewa, who was later gunned down after being repatriated, Kawuma and Army Sampath."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "PST and POC:  A recipe for abuse in their current form",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/PST-and-POC-A-recipe-for-abuse-in-their-current-form/172-354110",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793771655.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "da3469c9d2395d80405977871a5ead6b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1688,
    "chunk_text": "Among the high-profile figures are Mohammad Najim Mohammad Imaran alias Kanjipani Imran, who fled the country five days after being granted bail on December 20k 2022,\u00a0 Madushanka de Silva alias Podi Lassie, Ganemulle Sanjeewa, who was later gunned down after being repatriated, Kawuma and Army Sampath.\n\nMany civil society groups have opposed both bills. However, the question lies not in the necessity for either of the proposed Acts, but that they are overblown and a recipe for abuse. The POC has lumped together every imaginary offence, sufficiently covered under other penal code provisions. And the PST has introduced an overly broad and ambiguous definition of an act of terrorism, which could criminalise legitimate public protests and demonstrations.\n\nThe Bill states a terrorism offence as: Any person who intentionally or knowingly commits any act which causes a consequence specified in sub-section (2), with the purpose of:\u00a0 (A) Provoking a state of terror in the minds of the public or a group of persons or (B) unduly compelling a government or international organisation to do or to abstain from doing any act in order to advance a political or ideological objective, committing the offence of terrorism.\n\nHowever, the broad interpretation of (B) risks any public demonstration being termed as an act of terrorism, though the act specifically states that the fact that a person engages in a protest or industrial action or a humanitarian organisation providing humanitarian or medical assistance does not itself provide a sufficient basis for such an act to constitute a terrorism offence. Yet, this overblown and ambiguous definition carries an enhanced risk of misuse."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-760e31a6",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "PST and POC:  A recipe for abuse in their current form",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/PST-and-POC-A-recipe-for-abuse-in-their-current-form/172-354110",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793771655.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailymirrorlk-760e31a6-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ba7403c82a45de704d15ffbb92c4ed68",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "However, the broad interpretation of (B) risks any public demonstration being termed as an act of terrorism, though the act specifically states that the fact that a person engages in a protest or industrial action or a humanitarian organisation providing humanitarian or medical assistance does not itself provide a sufficient basis for such an act to constitute a terrorism offence. Yet, this overblown and ambiguous definition carries an enhanced risk of misuse.\n\nThe United States Federal Statutory Definition of terrorism defines acts of terrorism as activities involving acts dangerous to human life violating U.S. or state criminal laws, intended to intimidate/coerce a civilian population, influence government policy, or affect conduct via mass destruction, assassination, or kidnapping primarily within U.S. jurisdiction.\n\nAll politics is local, so are laws. Any Act introduced in Sri Lanka should take into account the previous incidents of grotesque abuses of PTA, which saw, among many other abuses, the trumped-up charges against journalist Tissanayagam and\u00a0 Lawyer Hajaaz Hizbullah, and resulted in the prolonged detention without trial of a number of individuals.\n\nSimilarly, the provisions that empower the military and coast guard to conduct searches and execute arrests without a warrant are a matter of concern, and should be exercised sparingly. Assuming they are only intended for extreme circumstances, they should also entail additional safeguards. However, as with the rest, one cannot rule out the prospect of such measures being normalised by a future government, as in the past.\u00a0 In a separate note, reorienting the Police Special Task Force into a specialised counter-terrorism and organised crime fighting unit could overcome some of these limitations."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
