    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ce21e2d7d503d8e982446ef07829e4b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "Similarly, the provisions that empower the military and coast guard to conduct searches and execute arrests without a warrant are a matter of concern, and should be exercised sparingly. Assuming they are only intended for extreme circumstances, they should also entail additional safeguards. However, as with the rest, one cannot rule out the prospect of such measures being normalised by a future government, as in the past.\u00a0 In a separate note, reorienting the Police Special Task Force into a specialised counter-terrorism and organised crime fighting unit could overcome some of these limitations.\n\nThe POC has in some ways caricatured itself into an absurdity. Its Schedule 1 lumps together 53 offences as diverse as discovery of antiques (treasure hunting), computer crimes, already covered by the Computer Crime Act of 2007,\u00a0 prohibition of import of fishes, mammals, and birds, etc., clearing forest land, gambling advertisements and gambling with underage individuals, patent infringements, mining without licence, harbouring a military deserter and wearing the dress of a soldier, sailor or airman.\n\nSimilarly, Clause 4(2)(c) stipulates that style of dress, language, hand signs, or tattoos could be used to infer membership in a criminal gang. Despite the denial by the Justice Minister, there is no guarantee that such provisions could lead to grotesque abuses.\n\nThe absurdities in the POC are largely due to it having apparently been implanted from\u00a0 El Salvador and Singapore. El Salvador has, until recently, been beset with organised transnational criminal gangs such as MS-13, which originated among Salvadoran migrant youth in Los Angeles in the 1980s, and Barrio 18 and many other small gangs. Those gangs possessed quasi-military capabilities and entrapped the state. It took Nayib Bukele, the president, to lock up 2 per cent of the adult population to rid the country of gangs."
  },
