    "chunk_size_bytes": 1825,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan universities slip in QS Asia rankings\n\nColombo, Nov. 4 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka's leading universities slid to their lowest positions in four years in the QS World University Rankings: Asia 2027, with the University of Colombo dropping 46 places to 364th, as regional rivals moved ahead while its own score barely changed, QS told Mirror Business.\n\nColombo nonetheless remained Sri Lanka's highest-ranked university for a fifth consecutive year, followed by the University of Peradeniya at 389th and the University of Sri Jayewardenepura at 398th. While the trio kept the country's presence in the top 400 at three for a fourth straight edition, each recorded its lowest placing over that period, with Colombo now 89 places below the 275th position it held in 2025.\n\nOverall, eight of Sri Lanka's 12 ranked universities declined this year, two improved and two held steady, leaving a net decline of 50 percent, as per data from London-based higher education analytics firm QS Quacquarelli Symonds. The University of Moratuwa was the strongest local performer, climbing 37 places to 517th, while the University of Kelaniya advanced from the 731-740 band to 687th, a gain of at least 44 places.\n\nQS Senior Vice President Ben Sowter credited Sri Lanka with holding its ground at the upper end of an expanded field.\n\n\"In a year when almost 200 universities have debuted in the rankings, Sri Lanka does well to maintain three universities among the top 400,\" he said.\n\nHowever, QS told Mirror Business that the 195 newcomers were not the main reason for the slide. Only two debutants were placed above Colombo or Sri Jayewardenepura, it pointed out, and they accounted for about two places of each university's fall.\n\n\"For these two institutions in particular, it is more a question of rivals moving ahead,\" QS noted."
