    "chunk_text": "However, QS told Mirror Business that the 195 newcomers were not the main reason for the slide. Only two debutants were placed above Colombo or Sri Jayewardenepura, it pointed out, and they accounted for about two places of each university's fall.\n\n\"For these two institutions in particular, it is more a question of rivals moving ahead,\" QS noted.\n\nEmployer Reputation, which carries a 20 percent weighting, was the main drag on the two universities, with Colombo's score in the indicator falling 2.8 points and Sri Jayewardenepura's 4.3 points even as most universities improved slightly. At 192nd, Colombo is now the only Sri Lankan university in Asia's top 200 for employer reputation, compared with five in 2022. QS read this as a sign that regional peers were building and maintaining links with employers at a faster pace.\n\n\"Sri Lankan institutions need to do more to build their reputation among industry stakeholders globally,\" it added.\n\nHowever, the average Employer Reputation score of Sri Lankan entries rose 3.3 points to 35.5, more than six times the regional gain of 0.5 points to 24.8. All nine universities outside the top three improved their scores, and four of them also climbed in rank. QS observed that this pointed to employers in the region rating Sri Lankan graduates more highly than a year earlier, while Colombo's 192nd place still keeps it within the top 12 percent in Asia for the indicator."
