    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Spanish transport minister resigns days after deadly train crash\n\nBBC - Spain's Transport Minister \u00d3scar Puente has resigned - six days after the high-speed train collision in which at least 69 people were killed and dozens injured.\n\nHe announced his decision in Congress, after giving evidence on last Wednesday's disaster near the town of Urda, south of the capital Madrid, in which a train smashed into the back of a stationary train.\n\n\"A transport minister in a country like Spain cannot remain in office after two accidents of this seriousness in less than a year,\" he said - a reference to January's crash on the same line which left 46 people dead.\n\nLast week's crash was one of the worst rail disasters in Europe's modern history, and Spain's worst crash since 2013.\n\nPuente announced his decision at the end of a lengthy congressional session on Tuesday evening in which he had outlined new rail safety measures and had been grilled by the opposition.\n\nHe also said: \"I am not only stepping down from my position as minister of transport, but... I am also withdrawing my name from the lists for the early legislative elections.\"\n\nPuente's position immediately came under scrutiny after the 28 October crash.\n\nThe main opposition, the conservative People's Party (PP) and the far-right Vox, were both calling for his resignation - as were some of the government's allies.\n\nThey argued that the minister should take responsibility for a high-speed rail system that many Spaniards now regard as unsafe and whose credibility even Socialist Prime Minister Pedro S\u00e1nchez admitted was \"broken\".\n\nThe opposition also criticised Puente for appearing to blame human error for the crash, claiming the minister was trying to deflect the blame from his own failings.\n\nBut, having said he was going to remain in his post just a few days ago, his resignation appears to have taken many by surprise."
  },
