    "chunk_size_bytes": 1896,
    "chunk_text": "The opposition also criticised Puente for appearing to blame human error for the crash, claiming the minister was trying to deflect the blame from his own failings.\n\nBut, having said he was going to remain in his post just a few days ago, his resignation appears to have taken many by surprise.\n\nMany observers see the accident as a massive electoral setback to the Socialist-led government that will make it impossible for S\u00e1nchez to defy the polls and win the election.\n\nPuente's predicament presented him and the prime minister with a dilemma - refusing to resign might look insensitive in the wake of the accident and stepping down could look like acknowledging he and the government were at fault.\n\nIn the end, Puente opted for the second option, insisting his decision had been driven by \"political dignity\".\n\nThe prime minister praised his colleague who had \"faced up to the situation, provided answers and solutions, and then stepped aside\".\n\n\"Out of dignity. Of his own accord. Out of respect for the victims,\" S\u00e1nchez added.\n\nMany Spaniards will now ask whether Puente's successors will manage to restore faith in the country's railway system.\n\nLast Wednesday's accident involved a train run by state-owned operator Renfe with 397 passengers on board and a stationary train run by private operator Iryo, which was carrying 147 passengers.\n\nA reported issue with the automatic signalling system - a computer failure - had forced all trains to halt on the affected stretch.\n\nLeaked audio recordings published in Spanish media soon after the crash revealed conversations between train drivers and a control centre just minutes before the disaster.\n\nThe three recordings of conversations between Madrid's control centre and two train drivers appear to show that the Renfe train was given clearance to move even though the other train had stopped on the track, about 25km (15 miles) ahead."
