    "chunk_text": "The three recordings of conversations between Madrid's control centre and two train drivers appear to show that the Renfe train was given clearance to move even though the other train had stopped on the track, about 25km (15 miles) ahead.\n\nAfter some exchanges, the control operator tells the driver: \"Continue your journey and then proceed under [Lateral Signalling Block], at normal speed with normal operation.\"\n\nThe driver replies: \"At normal speed?\" To which the controller answers: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe Lateral Signalling Block is a back-up system used when the automatic signalling or data transmission system fails.\n\nThe driver of the stationary Iryo train says in another audio recording that communication with the train-control system has been lost, and it is not moving.\n\nThe train \"is stationary in front of the entry signal at Urda\", the driver tells the control centre operator. After a series of exchanges, the driver is then told to \"resume the journey\" normally.\n\nThe Iryo driver then contacts the control centre again, according to a later recording, saying they cannot start the train. \"The train won't allow me to control the [security] system. That's why I am still stopped in front of the entry signal,\" they say.\n\nThe latter recording suggests that the controller had been notified that the Iryo train remained stationary as the Renfe train approached.\n\nIryo said its carriages were \"struck from behind\" by the other train."
