    "md5": "a445e1b9a512d7fd4678d577ab8a767d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1650,
    "chunk_text": "South Asia aging faster, Sri Lanka the fastest\n\nColombo, Nov. 4 (Daliy Mirror) - South Asia which once boasted of boosting growth with its rising working age population is now ageing fast with Sri Lanka bound to cross the aged threshold fastest, according to a World Bank report launched yesterday.\n\nThe report titled \u2018Demographic Change in South Asia: From Tailwinds to Headwinds for Development\u2019 highlights that Sri Lankans will age by 2023 whereas some Indian states such as Kerala have already crossed it.\n\nSouth Asia accounts for 1.66 billion people - 24 percent of the global population. The working age group stands at 1.17 billion and represents 22 percent of the world.\n\nDemographic tailwinds (the first demographic dividend) boosted growth over the past several decades as more adults entered their prime working years and had fewer dependents to support. Since 2000, demographic trends have added an estimated 0.4 percentage point a year to South Asia\u2019s gross domestic product (GDP) growth (0.5 percentage point for India). International evidence suggests that the rising share of the working-age population may also have raised annual labor productivity growth in the region by 0.4\u20131.1 percentage points over the same period, the report says.\n\nHowever, the region is not confronted with fading demographic tailwinds. Working-age population growth which averaged 2.2 percent a year between 1960 and 2023, is now projected to fall to 0.6 percent a year between 2024 and 2050. The resultant contribution of demographics to GDP growth is expected to shrink by three-quarters, to about 0.1 percentage point a year over the same period."
  },
