    "chunk_text": "However, the region is not confronted with fading demographic tailwinds. Working-age population growth which averaged 2.2 percent a year between 1960 and 2023, is now projected to fall to 0.6 percent a year between 2024 and 2050. The resultant contribution of demographics to GDP growth is expected to shrink by three-quarters, to about 0.1 percentage point a year over the same period.\n\nThe study finds that only 60 percent of South Asia\u2019s working-age population is employed, compared with 69 percent in other EMDEs (Emerging Market and Developing Economies).\n\n\u201cOnly 34 percent are employed in the more productive nonagricultural sectors, compared with 51 percent elsewhere. Among those employed, 88 percent are informally employed, a share higher than in any EMDE region and on par with Sub-Saharan Africa. Informal employees earn 45\u201384 percent less than formal employees in Bangladesh, India, Nepal, and Sri Lanka, representing a large loss of income and productivity,\u201d the report says."
