    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e4205fec2cae83f1aecd337fb8b1b3bc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1913,
    "chunk_text": "Will social media ban guarantee children\u2019s online safety?\n\nColombo, Nov. 4 (Daily Mirror) - Amidst increasing international discussion about restricting children's access to social media, including proposals to establish minimum age limits Sri Lanka needs to carefully consider age-appropriate access to digital platforms.\n\nAge restrictions may form part of the solution, but banning access alone cannot guarantee children's online safety, as children may bypass restrictions or move to less regulated platforms. Overly restrictive measures could also prevent them from accessing beneficial educational resources, health information and supportive communities, a child health expert has warned.\n\nCommenting on the importance of protecting children in an increasingly digital world, pediatrician Dr. Maheshaka Wijayawardena told the Daily Mirror that digital technology had become an integral part of children's lives, offering valuable opportunities for education, creativity, communication and social connection through smartphones, social media, online games and artificial intelligence (AI).\n\nHowever, alongside these benefits, children were increasingly exposed to risks that could affect their mental health, physical wellbeing, development and personal safety.\n\nHe stressed that online child protection should be recognised as a public health, child protection and children's rights priority in Sri Lanka.\n\nChildren may encounter cyberbullying, online harassment, inappropriate content, misinformation and online sexual exploitation. Some may also be manipulated by strangers through social media and gaming platforms, while repeated exposure to idealised images on social media could contribute to anxiety, low self-esteem and body image concerns.\n\nExcessive or poorly managed digital use could also interfere with sleep, physical activity, schoolwork, family relationships and face-to-face interactions."
  },
