    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4bcb8b22ea70b57801d46f2f7923fd41",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Children may encounter cyberbullying, online harassment, inappropriate content, misinformation and online sexual exploitation. Some may also be manipulated by strangers through social media and gaming platforms, while repeated exposure to idealised images on social media could contribute to anxiety, low self-esteem and body image concerns.\n\nExcessive or poorly managed digital use could also interfere with sleep, physical activity, schoolwork, family relationships and face-to-face interactions.\n\nArtificial intelligence has introduced further challenges, with children potentially receiving inaccurate health advice, disclosing personal information to AI chatbots or becoming victims of AI-generated content used for bullying and exploitation.\n\nHowever, Dr. Wijayawardena pointed out that digital technology was not inherently harmful, as its impact depended on a child's age, the content accessed, the nature of online interactions and how digital activities affected everyday life.\n\nOn the growing international debate over minimum age limits for social media, he said Sri Lanka should carefully consider policies that ensure age-appropriate access to digital platforms.\n\nHe cautioned that any age-based restrictions should be evidence-based, appropriate to children's developmental stages, enforceable and respectful of privacy. Digital platforms should also be designed with children's safety in mind rather than placing the entire responsibility on parents to manage online risks.\n\nParents and caregivers, meanwhile, have an important role in helping children develop healthy digital habits.\n\nHe said parents should begin conversations about online friendships, experiences and potential dangers at an early age, encouraging children to speak openly without fear of being blamed or punished.\n\nSetting reasonable limits on recreational screen time, while ensuring children have sufficient time for sleep, studies, exercise, outdoor play and family activities, was also important."
  },
