    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1bd8050ea03ba58fda0144a8142b29eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1687,
    "chunk_text": "He said parents should begin conversations about online friendships, experiences and potential dangers at an early age, encouraging children to speak openly without fear of being blamed or punished.\n\nSetting reasonable limits on recreational screen time, while ensuring children have sufficient time for sleep, studies, exercise, outdoor play and family activities, was also important.\n\nChildren should be taught to recognise cyberbullying, suspicious messages, inappropriate requests and attempts by strangers to gain their trust. More importantly, they should feel comfortable approaching a trusted adult whenever an online experience makes them uncomfortable or frightened.\n\nIf a child reports cyberbullying, grooming or another harmful online experience, parents should respond calmly, prioritise the child's safety and seek appropriate assistance. Blaming or automatically punishing children could discourage them from reporting similar incidents in the future.\n\nDr. Wijayawardena stressed that responsibility for online safety should not rest solely with children and their families, adding that schools, healthcare professionals and technology companies also had important roles to play.\n\nSchools should introduce age-appropriate digital literacy education to help children identify misinformation, protect personal information, respect others online and use AI responsibly.\n\nHealthcare professionals, particularly pediatricians, should also consider digital wellbeing when assessing children's sleep, behaviour, emotional health and development. Children experiencing significant distress, problematic digital use or online abuse should receive appropriate support and referrals."
  },
