    "md5": "f9db181d3754e6a232b95942dfefd220",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1843,
    "chunk_text": "Healthcare professionals, particularly pediatricians, should also consider digital wellbeing when assessing children's sleep, behaviour, emotional health and development. Children experiencing significant distress, problematic digital use or online abuse should receive appropriate support and referrals.\n\nChildren's personal information should not be exploited for targeted advertising or inappropriate profiling, he said.\n\nHe also called for a coordinated national strategy involving the Ministries of Health and Education, child protection authorities, law enforcement agencies, digital regulators and other relevant stakeholders.\n\nSuch a strategy should include parent education, professional training, effective reporting systems and research into the online experiences and health outcomes of Sri Lankan children.\n\nEmphasising the need to strike a balance between protecting children and allowing them to benefit from technology, Dr. Wijayawardena said online safety was not simply about reducing screen time or keeping children away from digital platforms, but ensuring they could participate in the digital world without being exposed to preventable harm.\n\nParents should guide rather than simply police their children's online activities, while schools should educate rather than merely prohibit access to technology. Healthcare professionals should identify problems early and provide support, while governments must establish appropriate safeguards and technology companies should be held accountable for the digital environments they create.\n\nEvery child deserves a digital environment that supports learning, creativity, healthy relationships and emotional wellbeing. Protecting children online, he emphasised, is a shared responsibility, and their safety must always come before commercial interests or technological convenience."
