    "md5": "be5a5095aeb62d8d1c73cb9a7ab878e0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1869,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cCoexistence is the only way forward than control\u201d \u2013  Dr. Ruchira Somaweera\n\nFor Dr. Ruchira \u2018Ru\u2019 Somaweera, zoologist, evolutionary biologist and herpetologist, getting up close and personal with wild species has been a childhood passion. Today, apart from his academic career, Dr. Somaweera dedicates much of his time to explore unknown territories and educate masses about different wildlife species, their habitats, and behavioural patterns and so on. Dr. Somaweera is also a National Geographic Explorer and the creator of \u2018Ru Goes Wild\u2019, a YouTube channel dedicated to wildlife education.\u00a0 In a candid interview with the Daily Mirror, Dr. Somaweera spoke about why he chose this particular path, the need for science-based conservation, knowledge gaps pertaining to crocodiles in Sri Lanka, unforgettable experiences in the wild and why coexistence is the only way forward.\n\nQ : How do you identify yourself -- as an evolutionary biologist or behavioural ecologist?\n\nI wear several hats. Academically, my field of research is behavioural ecology; basically understanding how animals adapt to a changing world, be it urbanisation, climate change, or invasive species. It\u2019s broadly looking at how animals are confronting challenges in a changing world and what we can do to help. Within that,\u00a0 I primarily focus on reptiles.\n\n\u201cSometimes I identify myself as a herpetologist but within herpetology,\u00a0 I look at behavioural ecology. Evolutionary biology is part of that \u2013 basically how things change overtime. There have been a few projects where I look at how things have changed overtime when it comes to impacts on animal behaviour. At the moment,\u00a0 I\u2019m not in a very strict research position,\u00a0 and my full time job is in environmental advisory. I\u2019m the national executive for WSP, which is one of the largest consultancies in the world."
