    "chunk_text": "\u201cSometimes I identify myself as a herpetologist but within herpetology,\u00a0 I look at behavioural ecology. Evolutionary biology is part of that \u2013 basically how things change overtime. There have been a few projects where I look at how things have changed overtime when it comes to impacts on animal behaviour. At the moment,\u00a0 I\u2019m not in a very strict research position,\u00a0 and my full time job is in environmental advisory. I\u2019m the national executive for WSP, which is one of the largest consultancies in the world.\n\nQ : Let\u2019s talk about science-based conservation. In the Sri Lankan context,\u00a0 do these work together or separately?\n\nThroughout the world there\u2019s a separation \u2013 it\u2019s not a Sri Lankan thing. For many governments conservation is not a prime focus. Especially in third world or developing countries, governments have bigger issues to tackle than wildlife conservation. This is understandable; people\u2019s safety, people feeding, livelihoods, and income are bigger problems than wildlife conservation. The focus of a system for wildlife conservation depends on financial stability and many other things.\n\n\u201cWe can\u2019t expect what Australia does to be done in Sri Lanka. They are very different systems altogether. In systems like Australia where I\u2019m heavily involved in, you have much more science integrated into conservation. A lot of conservation decisions are very much science-driven rather than political agendas. However, it happens around the world. I work in Asia-Pacific countries where it is even not so much as Sri Lanka. It\u2019s a spectrum. However,\u00a0 there\u2019s a huge need for decisions to be science-driven rather than political driven."
  },
