    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe can\u2019t expect what Australia does to be done in Sri Lanka. They are very different systems altogether. In systems like Australia where I\u2019m heavily involved in, you have much more science integrated into conservation. A lot of conservation decisions are very much science-driven rather than political agendas. However, it happens around the world. I work in Asia-Pacific countries where it is even not so much as Sri Lanka. It\u2019s a spectrum. However,\u00a0 there\u2019s a huge need for decisions to be science-driven rather than political driven.\n\n\u201cA lot of scientists don\u2019t communicate their science to end users and regulators. You publish a paper in a scientific journal and that\u2019s the end of it. They don\u2019t have the tools, needs and experience sometimes to take it to the people that matter. A lot of these regulators also don\u2019t have much of a science background either. They take decisions based on political or other agendas. There\u2019s that mismatch. It\u2019s an ongoing issue and Sri Lanka by no means is alone in that.\n\nQ : Based on your work with crocodiles, what do you think about the protection we have for crocodiles in Sri Lanka?"
