    "chunk_text": "\u201cA lot of scientists don\u2019t communicate their science to end users and regulators. You publish a paper in a scientific journal and that\u2019s the end of it. They don\u2019t have the tools, needs and experience sometimes to take it to the people that matter. A lot of these regulators also don\u2019t have much of a science background either. They take decisions based on political or other agendas. There\u2019s that mismatch. It\u2019s an ongoing issue and Sri Lanka by no means is alone in that.\n\nQ : Based on your work with crocodiles, what do you think about the protection we have for crocodiles in Sri Lanka?\n\nQ : Does that mean Sri Lanka doesn\u2019t have a human-crocodile conflict?\n\n\u201cI don\u2019t think Sri Lanka has a human-crocodile conflict. It\u2019s a mega predator and obviously it\u2019s going to eat you if you\u2019re in the wrong place. It\u2019s not a conflict. It\u2019s a mega predator feeding thinking that you are prey. That\u2019s not a conflict per se. But crocodile deaths are not uncommon in Sri Lanka. People kill them as a precaution, preventing future attacks, sometimes due to sheer misunderstanding. It\u2019s a predator sharing space. Drowning in nets is common, especially in tanks in the dry zone, but none of those are recorded.\n\n\u201cA crocodile attack can be deadly, and it\u2019s the same with shark attacks. Throughout the world, less than 10 people die from shark attacks. But when it happens it becomes\u00a0 big news. We don\u2019t need to over-exaggerate something that\u2019s not there. But the threat is real. The number of people dying from crocodile attacks is negligible when compared to how many people die from snake bites or mosquito-borne diseases, insect stings and so on. But there\u2019s a massive knowledge gap when it comes to a standardised survey over the years. Australia is leading in that space and so are some Asian countries such as Indonesia, Malaysia. We have done some haphazard surveys in the Nilwala river ecosystem and so on."
