    "chunk_text": "\u201cA crocodile attack can be deadly, and it\u2019s the same with shark attacks. Throughout the world, less than 10 people die from shark attacks. But when it happens it becomes\u00a0 big news. We don\u2019t need to over-exaggerate something that\u2019s not there. But the threat is real. The number of people dying from crocodile attacks is negligible when compared to how many people die from snake bites or mosquito-borne diseases, insect stings and so on. But there\u2019s a massive knowledge gap when it comes to a standardised survey over the years. Australia is leading in that space and so are some Asian countries such as Indonesia, Malaysia. We have done some haphazard surveys in the Nilwala river ecosystem and so on.\n\nQ : Certain reptile species such as the Dumbara horn lizard are vulnerable to wildlife trafficking. Is Sri Lanka becoming a hub for wildlife trafficking?\n\nI deny it. We need to put things in perspective. If we go down the path saying wildlife trafficking is a major threat to Sri Lankan wildlife, at least reptiles, there\u2019s no objective evidence whatsoever to prove that. In Indonesia, wildlife trafficking happens in thousands. But trafficking one or two animals \u2013 while it shouldn\u2019t have happened in the first place and animals bred in captivity are not a threat to a local species. It is very immature to think so. The threat definitely is there. Dumbara species and other endemic agamids such as Cophotis lizards have popped up. I have seen a lot of hype on this. But given the extent of wildlife trafficking in other parts of the world, this is a small scale thing. We have enough rules. Millions of people go in and out of this country. There\u2019s a possibility that before the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance was in place, there was no rule about taking animals back and forth. So there\u2019s a possibility that some of these animals are captive bred animals from things that were taken back in the past."
