    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3f67c120ff5ece94099c0132b04e4a2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Pakistan Lost $40 Billion Since Trade With India Was Halted: Report\n\nNDTV WORLD - Pakistan has suffered an estimated USD 40 billion in economic losses since trade with India was suspended in 2019 following the Pulwama terror attack and subsequent escalations, the head of a business advocacy group said Monday.\n\nChairman of the Pak-India Business Council Noor Muhammad Qasuri also urged the two neighbouring countries to restore commercial ties, saying it would benefit businesses on both sides and create opportunities for investment, tourism and regional cooperation.\n\nIn a statement, Qasuri said the suspension forced Pakistani businesses to import essential raw materials and other goods from distant markets, pushing up transportation and freight costs as well as delivery times.\n\nPakistan's pharmaceutical industry relied on raw materials available in India, he said, adding that its imports from the neighbouring country could generate annual savings of USD 2 billion to USD 3 billion.\n\nThe textile sector, meanwhile, incurred additional costs amid declining domestic cotton production. Textile mills had been forced to source cotton from Brazil, Egypt, China and the United States, resulting in an estimated additional expenditure of USD 5 billion, he added.\n\nQasuri further said Pakistan was also importing sugar, fertiliser, cement, petroleum products, iron and steel, vegetables and heavy machinery from distant markets despite India's substantial production capacity in these sectors.\n\nThe resumption of bilateral trade could lower transportation costs and ensure timely supplies, he said, pointing out that Indian oil refineries were geographically closer to Pakistan, making petroleum imports potentially cheaper and faster.\n\nAccess to Indian agricultural and industrial products could also help Pakistani businesses reduce production costs, he added."
