    "chunk_text": "The resumption of bilateral trade could lower transportation costs and ensure timely supplies, he said, pointing out that Indian oil refineries were geographically closer to Pakistan, making petroleum imports potentially cheaper and faster.\n\nAccess to Indian agricultural and industrial products could also help Pakistani businesses reduce production costs, he added.\n\nHe called for improved agricultural policies to revive domestic cotton production.\n\nThe restoration of trade would benefit businesses on both sides and create opportunities for investment, tourism and regional cooperation, he said.\n\nPakistan could also attract more Indian tourists, particularly those interested in visiting religious and historical sites as well as their ancestral homes and villages, he added.\n\nCiting regional economic cooperation, Qasuri referred to India's financial assistance to Sri Lanka during its economic crisis as an example of how neighbouring countries could support one another in difficult times.\n\nHe stressed that political differences should not prevent the two countries from exploring economic opportunities, arguing that outstanding disputes could be addressed through dialogue.\n\n\"Pakistan and India share historical, cultural and family ties, which could provide a foundation for improved relations,\" he said."
