    "language": "en",
    "md5": "49938c3698635a2cc91b2148da0f4672",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "The creation of Sri Lanka\u2019s first dedicated agricultural banking arm was another expression of this approach. By developing financial solutions specifically for the farmers and agri-businesses, the initiative sought to connect banking with one of the fundamental sectors of the national economy.\n\nToday, as CEO of Bank of Ceylon, Jayathilaka leads the institution at a time when the banking industry is undergoing perhaps its most profound transformation in generations.\n\nThe customers increasingly expect banking to be instantaneous, intuitive and available wherever they are.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, the launch of BOC Flex, the bank\u2019s internet and mobile banking solution, marked an important milestone in BOC\u2019s digital journey. Launched on March 21, 2025, Flex has rapidly expanded its customer base and transaction volumes. According to the figures cited in the bank\u2019s profile, the platform has reached approximately 2.5 million registered users, facilitating around Rs.300 billion in monthly transactions and approximately 400,000 transactions a day.\n\nYet, the significance of Flex is not simply measured by the transaction volumes.\n\nFor Jayathilaka, digital transformation represents a fundamental change in how a bank relates to its customers.\n\nThe objective is not to digitise the existing processes for their own sake but to understand how people live and manage their finances and then redesign banking around those realities.\n\nThat philosophy is increasingly shaping BOC\u2019s future direction, with the bank seeking to combine its traditional strength of trust and accessibility with technology, innovation and a more seamless digital customer experience."
  },
