    "chunk_text": "However, accounts of why Hewawasam left differ sharply. While some within the industry maintain that he stepped down on personal grounds, one source familiar with the matter pointed to friction with the ministry. \u201cFrom what I hear, there is a disagreement between him and the foreign minister\u2014a clash,\u201d the source told Mirror Business. Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism Minister Vijitha Herath oversees both the SLTPB and the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA). Attempts to reach Hewawasam for comment were unsuccessful.\n\nMeanwhile, the change in leadership comes at a delicate juncture for Sri Lanka\u2019s destination marketing. In August, the bureau launched a Rs.1.5 billion interim digital and public relations campaign across six primary source markets, intended to bridge the gap until a Rs.5 billion global branding campaign then slated for March 2027. Less than two weeks later, however, Tourism Deputy Minister Prof. Ruwan Ranasinghe confirmed that the global campaign would be pushed back by nearly a year, with the government opting to complete a World Bank-supported roadmap consultancy, led by Skift Inc. and MTI Consulting, before awarding the main campaign.\n\nThe bureau has also struggled to deploy its funds. It utilised only Rs.2.5 billion of the Rs.8 billion allocated for promotions in 2025, roughly 31 percent, a shortfall Hewawasam had previously attributed to procurement bottlenecks. Its leadership has been similarly unsettled, with 13 chairmen since 2007 and an average tenure of under 18 months, which places his two-year stint above the norm. (NF)"
