    "chunk_size_bytes": 2019,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI\u2019ll talk about the past, and I\u2019ll let you infer from that to the future,\u201d he remarked, noting that CAL\u2019s assets under management (AUM) had grown roughly 15 to 17 times over the past eight years, a compound annual growth rate of around 40 to 42 percent.\n\nThe briefing came a day after CAL disclosed to the bourse that its wholly owned Singapore arm, CAL Global Pte. Ltd., had inked a share purchase agreement for a 51 percent stake in AFC at approximately US$ 2.5 million.\n\nThe consideration implies a valuation of about US$ 4.9 million for the whole of AFC, or roughly 3.9 percent of its AUM of approximately US$ 125 million as at end-September. CAL applied to the Securities and Futures Commission of Hong Kong around a month ago and expects regulatory sign-off within two to three months, with Mannakkara insisting it is \u201ca matter of when, not if\u201d.\n\nHe described AFC as the \u201cplumbing\u201d needed to cut through the complexity that keeps global allocators out of frontier markets.\n\n\u201cAFC already has the infrastructure that allows global investors to easily take positions and easily channel their investments into frontier markets,\u201d he explained.\n\nThe combined entity will manage around US$ 1.2 billion, placing it among the largest frontier-focused managers, with AFC accounting for a little over a tenth. AFC Chief Executive Officer and Fund Manager Thomas Hugger welcomed the added heft, quipping that in fund management \u201cthe big guys get everything, and the small guys like us, we have to fight for the crumbs\u201d.\n\nAFC also brings a presence in Vietnam, Uzbekistan and Iraq. By Mannakkara\u2019s count, those markets add over US$ 900 billion in GDP to the roughly US$ 600 billion of Sri Lanka and Bangladesh, extending CAL\u2019s reach to frontier economies worth about US$ 1.5 trillion, half as large again as the \u201cover US$ 1 trillion\u201d footprint it had targeted.\n\nHowever, Mannakkara stressed that market access alone no longer commands a premium and that CAL\u2019s priority is product creation."
