    "chunk_text": "AFC also brings a presence in Vietnam, Uzbekistan and Iraq. By Mannakkara\u2019s count, those markets add over US$ 900 billion in GDP to the roughly US$ 600 billion of Sri Lanka and Bangladesh, extending CAL\u2019s reach to frontier economies worth about US$ 1.5 trillion, half as large again as the \u201cover US$ 1 trillion\u201d footprint it had targeted.\n\nHowever, Mannakkara stressed that market access alone no longer commands a premium and that CAL\u2019s priority is product creation.\n\nAFC currently invests only in listed equities, but its fund structure allows it to move into fixed income and unlisted equities.\n\nHe also contended that buying a manager with a 13-year track record carries less execution risk than the greenfield route CAL took in Bangladesh.\n\nMeanwhile, AFC\u2019s managers believe frontier markets have turned the corner after nearly a decade of foreign outflows. Desai put the cyclical bottom at end-2022, arguing that a run of crises had forced governments into reform. \u201cThe macroeconomic fundamentals, in my view, have never been stronger in our markets,\u201d he observed.\n\nMannakkara blamed geopolitics rather than domestic fundamentals for the recent dent in foreign investor confidence in Sri Lanka, while Desai noted that the local market has de-rated from around 15 times earnings before the crisis to about 9 times today, even as many listed companies and banks have returned to pre-crisis earnings.\n\n\u201cThe signal that we want to give is that frontier markets are an extraordinarily interesting place for global capital to be deployed. They\u2019re overlooked,\u201d Mannakkara concluded."
