    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "CBSL puts stability at centre of fintech ambitions\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s ambitions for a more digital financial system will have to advance within firm boundaries protecting the rupee and financial stability, the country\u2019s monetary watchdog stressed yesterday while reiterating that digital assets cannot be used for payments between residents under the existing legal framework.\n\nCentral Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe welcomed the opportunities offered by financial technology but stressed that innovation must safeguard public confidence and the country\u2019s monetary sovereignty.\n\nHis remarks added support for greater competition and new financial services with an explicit warning against allowing digital assets to displace the rupee in domestic transactions.\n\n\u201cUnder the current legal framework in Sri Lanka, digital assets, also called virtual assets, cannot be used for payments between residents,\u201d Weerasinghe said, addressing the Sri Lanka FinTech Festival in Colombo yesterday.\n\n\u201cThis position is important in preventing the substitution of the Sri Lankan rupee in domestic transactions and in safeguarding the country\u2019s monetary sovereignty.\u201d\n\nWhile acknowledging that digital assets could present opportunities, he said their development required clear regulatory frameworks, appropriate safeguards and careful consideration of the implications for the wider financial system.\n\nThe Governor\u2019s remarks reaffirmed the existing legal position rather than announcing a new restriction. He also stressed that regulation must evolve alongside technology while preserving its essential objectives.\n\n\u201cOur approach must be proportionate, risk-based and forward-looking, providing clarity to responsible innovators while maintaining firm safeguards against unacceptable risks,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Governor urged Sri Lanka to move beyond restoring macroeconomic stability and focus on raising productivity, attracting investment, creating employment and widening economic opportunities."
  },
