    "chunk_text": "\u201cOur approach must be proportionate, risk-based and forward-looking, providing clarity to responsible innovators while maintaining firm safeguards against unacceptable risks,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Governor urged Sri Lanka to move beyond restoring macroeconomic stability and focus on raising productivity, attracting investment, creating employment and widening economic opportunities.\n\n\u201cHowever, stability is not the destination; it is the foundation upon which sustainable growth must be built.\u201d\n\nHe identified financial technology as a means of strengthening the financial sector\u2019s core functions, from mobilising savings and directing capital towards productive investment to facilitating trade and widening participation in economic activity.\n\nLower transaction costs, better use of data and new business models could help small businesses expand, connect underserved communities to formal finance and improve the allocation of resources, he said.\n\n\u201cIn this sense, FinTech is not merely about modernising financial services; it is about enabling national economic development.\u201d\n\nWeerasinghe highlighted opportunities in open and interoperable financial services, digital identity, data-driven lending, cross-border payment connectivity and investment products.\n\nSri Lanka had already made progress through mobile and internet banking, digital payments, payment cards and applications, supported by national payment infrastructure, including LankaPay systems, he said. Digitalisation was also expanding access to capital markets and investment services.\n\nHowever, the Dr. Weerasinghe cautioned that these developments brought risks extending beyond individual institutions and products.\n\nCyberattacks, operational disruptions, fraud, data breaches, inadequate consumer disclosures and excessive leverage were among the vulnerabilities he identified, alongside the concentration of critical services among technology providers."
