    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Kandy named world\u2019s calmest city getaway, Colombo outranks Phuket and Bali\n\nKandy tops Tiny Away\u2019s 972-city index with score of 0.1, greenest of all cities studied\n\nKandy has emerged as the world\u2019s calmest city getaway for holidaymakers looking to escape crowds and bright lights, topping a 972-city index of environmental intensity. Colombo placed 64th, finishing ahead of regional favourites Phuket and Bali, according to research by cabin stays company Tiny Away.\n\nThe Overstimulation Index scores cities on nine environmental factors, namely population density, night-time brightness, PM2.5 air pollution, green space, urban heat, built-up land, humidity, summer heat and winter cold.\n\nThe first four carry the heaviest weighting, while the three climate measures account for just 9 percent of the total weighting. Each score is a percentile rank against the full sample, placing Kandy\u2019s 0.1 at the calmest extreme of the scale and Colombo\u2019s 6.6 within the calmest 7 percent of cities studied.\n\nKandy, a UNESCO World Heritage city and a fixture on the island\u2019s Cultural Triangle circuit, recorded the highest vegetation cover of all 972 cities at around 73 percent. Its night-time brightness was the eighth lowest in the study and less than a fifth of Colombo\u2019s, while built-up cover in the areas where residents live stood at just 11.5 percent, roughly a third of Tokyo\u2019s.\n\nThe hill capital\u2019s core was also 1.5\u00b0C cooler at night than the rural ring around it between June and August, the reverse of the urban heat island effect that left Colombo\u2019s core 0.8\u00b0C warmer and Tokyo\u2019s 2.6\u00b0C warmer than their surroundings. Tiny Away pointed to Kandy\u2019s vegetation, low built-up cover and dim night lights as the likely factors, though it cautioned that the satellite figure was best read as \u201crelative rather than precise\u201d."
  },
