    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "The hill capital\u2019s core was also 1.5\u00b0C cooler at night than the rural ring around it between June and August, the reverse of the urban heat island effect that left Colombo\u2019s core 0.8\u00b0C warmer and Tokyo\u2019s 2.6\u00b0C warmer than their surroundings. Tiny Away pointed to Kandy\u2019s vegetation, low built-up cover and dim night lights as the likely factors, though it cautioned that the satellite figure was best read as \u201crelative rather than precise\u201d.\n\nBoth cities were assessed on the Global Human Settlement Layer\u2019s wider urban footprint rather than on municipal limits.\n\nThat footprint spans 273 square kilometres and around 696,000 people for Kandy, and 1,206 square kilometres and some 4.7 million people for Colombo, with Kandy\u2019s footprint taking in a good deal of the greenery surrounding the city.\n\nColombo\u2019s population density of 7,680 people per square kilometre was more than twice Kandy\u2019s, yet the commercial capital\u2019s night skies were still darker than those of around 94 percent of cities in the sample. The index measures environmental load alone and does not factor in noise, traffic or crowding.\n\nAmong the region\u2019s best-known escapes, Colombo\u2019s 6.6 left it calmer than Mataram on Lombok (10.4), Phuket (15.2), Yogyakarta (15.8) and Bali\u2019s capital Denpasar (18.3). Meanwhile, Bangkok was the calmest of 11 major Asian city hubs at 55.0, ahead of Tokyo (64.7) and Singapore (68.3).\n\nThe rankings could hand the island\u2019s tourism promoters a timely pitch, as both cities have lost ground in visitor itineraries. As per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority\u2019s latest survey of departing tourists, the Temple of the Tooth in Kandy featured in 51.3 percent of itineraries in 2024/25, down from 64.9 percent in 2018/19, while Colombo slipped to 46.2 percent from 68.3 percent as Galle Fort climbed to the top."
