    "chunk_text": "The rankings could hand the island\u2019s tourism promoters a timely pitch, as both cities have lost ground in visitor itineraries. As per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority\u2019s latest survey of departing tourists, the Temple of the Tooth in Kandy featured in 51.3 percent of itineraries in 2024/25, down from 64.9 percent in 2018/19, while Colombo slipped to 46.2 percent from 68.3 percent as Galle Fort climbed to the top.\n\n\u201cPeople are increasingly choosing holidays based on how they want to feel, not just where they want to go. While many travellers love the energy of bustling city destinations, we\u2019re seeing growing demand for places that allow people to slow down, disconnect from constant stimulation and genuinely recharge,\u201d Tiny Away co-founder Jeff Yeo observed.\n\nThe study also showed how much a short trip out of a major hub can change the experience, with Ha Long scoring around a third of Hanoi\u2019s score. Sri Lanka goes a step further, pairing a comparatively calm gateway city with the world\u2019s calmest hill capital a few hours inland, a combination that lends itself naturally to slow-travel and wellness marketing.\n\nTiny Away offers nature-based tiny house stays designed around disconnecting from everyday pressures and reconnecting with the outdoors. The company operates across Australia and in Asian markets such as Malaysia and Japan. (NF)"
