    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "JKH triples shareholder profit\n\nLower tax charge and changing earnings mix boost bottom line\n\nPremier blue chip John Keells Holdings PLC reported a sharp rise in earnings attributable to shareholders in the September quarter, with profit more than tripling to Rs.5.17 billion and earnings per share rising to 29 cents from 9 cents.\n\nThe performance of JKH in the second quarter of FY2026/27 reflected stronger contributions from transportation, financial services and property, alongside a lower Group tax charge. However, the scale of shareholder profit growth also reflected a change in the composition of earnings, with a reduced contribution from its 50 percent-owned John Keells CG Auto business lowering the share allocated to minority shareholders.\n\nTotal Group profit after tax grew 39 percent to Rs.5.82 billion, while profit attributable to equity holders increased 213 percent. Earnings allocated to non-controlling interests declined 74 percent to Rs.653 million.\n\n\u201cThe higher flow through to attributable PAT primarily reflects the lower effective contribution from JKCG, in which the Group has an ownership of 50 percent,\u201d Chairperson and CEO Krishan Balendra said in a commentary accompanying the interim financial statements.\n\nThe improvement in shareholder returns came despite revenue declining 2 percent to Rs.142.09 billion and Group earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation remaining broadly unchanged at Rs.18.30 billion.\n\nPretax profit edged up 2 percent to Rs.7.95 billion. Excluding exchange movements, however, it declined by about 5 percent to Rs.7.61 billion.\n\nTransportation was the standout operational contributor, with EBITDA increasing 153 percent to Rs.4.89 billion, driven by bunkering business Lanka Marine Services and Colombo West International Terminal."
  },
