    "md5": "659e569a50cb831ad9f1f804d1cc3e4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Transportation was the standout operational contributor, with EBITDA increasing 153 percent to Rs.4.89 billion, driven by bunkering business Lanka Marine Services and Colombo West International Terminal.\n\nColombo West International Terminal handled 502,351 twenty-foot equivalent units during the quarter, operating beyond full utilisation of its first-phase capacity. The terminal remains on track for full operations in December 2026, providing a further source of capacity as commissioning progresses.\n\nThese gains helped offset a 53 percent decline in retail EBITDA to Rs.4.75 billion. Within retail, the combined contribution from John Keells CG Auto and John Keells Office Automation fell 70 percent to Rs.2.34 billion.\n\nThe decline was principally associated with the vehicle business, which faced an exceptionally strong comparative quarter following the resumption of imports and the release of pent-up demand.\n\nDemand was also affected by the temporary surcharge on vehicle duties and tighter vehicle financing limits introduced in May, while a higher proportion of lower-priced models weighed on the revenue mix.\n\nJKCG handed over more than 2,400 vehicles during the quarter and reported approximately 1,000 vehicles scheduled for delivery in the coming months.\n\nAt Keells supermarkets, stronger sales translated into only a marginal improvement in earnings as rising costs absorbed much of the benefit.\n\nSupermarket revenue increased 17 percent, with same-store sales growing 13 percent on higher customer footfall and basket values. EBITDA rose just 1 percent to Rs.2.40 billion, reflecting pressure from electricity and distribution costs.\n\nThe increase in basket values reflected higher selling prices, particularly across food products, as well as more units purchased per basket. This points to an improvement in purchasing activity, although the earnings outcome shows the challenge of protecting margins as operating costs rise."
  },
