    "md5": "a8731e1eafe24959d837369d08b6b4ec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "The increase in basket values reflected higher selling prices, particularly across food products, as well as more units purchased per basket. This points to an improvement in purchasing activity, although the earnings outcome shows the challenge of protecting margins as operating costs rise.\n\nConsumer foods delivered a stronger earnings increase, with EBITDA rising 17 percent to Rs.2 billion. Carbonated soft drink volumes increased 10 percent and confectionery volumes grew 7 percent, while convenience foods also improved.\n\nNevertheless, higher input, fuel and electricity costs continued to pressure margins, limiting the extent to which volume growth could flow through to profit.\n\nCity of Dreams Sri Lanka recorded a further improvement in operating performance, generating EBITDA of Rs.340 million against a loss of Rs.64 million a year earlier.\n\nThe integrated resort\u2019s pretax loss narrowed to Rs.1.19 billion from Rs.3.39 billion. However, the improvement included a Rs.896 million exchange gain, compared with an exchange loss of Rs.523 million in the corresponding quarter.\n\nDepreciation, amortisation and interest expenses totalled Rs.2.43 billion. Interest costs benefited from refinancing and the reduction of the syndicated loan balance to $150 million.\n\nHigher occupancy and average room rates supported hotel earnings, although electricity and other costs associated with scaling up operations affected momentum.\n\nJKH recognised fixed rental income from the casino during the quarter. Variable rental income will become applicable once the relevant performance threshold is reached, making this an additional earnings opportunity dependent on the casino\u2019s operating performance.\n\nDespite the improvement at City of Dreams, overall leisure EBITDA declined 10 percent to Rs.1.28 billion, with weaker contributions from Colombo hotels and Maldivian resorts partly offset by improved profitability at Sri Lankan resorts."
  },
