    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "Despite the improvement at City of Dreams, overall leisure EBITDA declined 10 percent to Rs.1.28 billion, with weaker contributions from Colombo hotels and Maldivian resorts partly offset by improved profitability at Sri Lankan resorts.\n\nForward bookings across Sri Lanka and the Maldives remained encouraging ahead of the winter season, although continued connectivity through regional transit hubs remains a factor in the outlook.\n\nFinancial services EBITDA rose 44 percent to Rs.3.62 billion, supported by a higher contribution from Nations Trust Bank. This included a one-off tax credit arising from the acquisition of HSBC Sri Lanka\u2019s retail banking business, partly offset by integration expenses.\n\nThe size of the credit\u2019s contribution was not separately quantified in the chairperson\u2019s review. Its presence means the financial services earnings increase includes a benefit that should be distinguished from recurring business growth.\n\nProperty EBITDA rose to Rs.1.11 billion from Rs.196 million, driven by profit recognition from residential and commercial sales at Cinnamon Life and residential sales at TRI-ZEN.\n\nAt Group level, a lower tax expense was a major factor behind the increase in after-tax earnings. The charge declined 41 percent to Rs.2.13 billion, compared with Rs.3.60 billion a year earlier.\n\nCurrency movements also supported reported pretax profit, with a net exchange gain of Rs.340 million replacing a loss of Rs.245 million. The gain primarily reflected rupee appreciation on foreign currency borrowings at City of Dreams, partly offset by losses on dollar cash holdings at the holding company.\n\nFor the first half, Group revenue increased 10 percent to Rs.283.74 billion and EBITDA grew 11 percent to Rs.34.65 billion. Pretax profit rose 10 percent to Rs.12.03 billion, while total profit after tax increased 34 percent to Rs.6.60 billion."
