    "md5": "0e31755144c8227c30593a7051682997",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "We are now waiting to count how many lies the President tells in his third Budget \u2013 Namal\n\nColombo, Nov. 4 (Daily Mirror) - SLPP National Organiser and MP Namal Rajapaksa today accused the Government of failing to deliver on its promises, saying the public was now waiting to see how many more promises the President would make in his third Budget.\n\nSpeaking in Parliament, Rajapaksa said the Government had initially promised to digitalise everything, starting with national identity cards, but the country was now witnessing queues for fuel, gas and even tins.\n\n\u201cWe came to power hearing promises about digitalising everything, starting with national identity cards. But today, fuel queues and gas queues have been joined by queues for tins. We are now waiting to count how many lies the President will tell in his third Budget,\u201d he said.\n\nWhile acknowledging the Government\u2019s decision to provide some relief on fuel prices through the Aswesuma programme, Rajapaksa questioned why the Government had failed to honour its earlier promise to sell fuel at the price at which it was unloaded from ships.\n\nHe said the President himself had made that promise and that ministers should explain its failure directly to him rather than telling the Opposition that it was impractical.\n\nRajapaksa also accused the Government of increasing taxes despite earlier assurances that VAT would be removed from essential items, including school supplies, medicines and food.\n\nHe claimed that taxes had been increased on vehicle imports, bank interest and consultancy services, including those in the IT sector, despite the Government\u2019s stated ambition to build an economy centred on information technology.\n\n\u201cThe Government talks about building a massive IT-driven economy, but at the same time it is imposing taxes on people in the IT sector who are struggling to earn a living without any meaningful assistance from the State,\u201d he said."
  },
