    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Government talks about building a massive IT-driven economy, but at the same time it is imposing taxes on people in the IT sector who are struggling to earn a living without any meaningful assistance from the State,\u201d he said.\n\nTurning to the rising cost of living, Rajapaksa said the reduction in fuel prices offered limited relief when the Government continued to impose higher taxes on fuel.\n\nHe claimed that fuel taxes had increased by around Rs. 30 compared with previous levels, while the prices of milk powder and other essential commodities continued to put pressure on household budgets.\n\n\u201cPeople\u2019s salaries are not increasing at the same pace as the cost of living. When Government ministers travel around the country, they may not see gas queues, but ordinary people in villages and towns are experiencing these difficulties every day,\u201d he said.\n\nRajapaksa questioned what concrete measures the Government had introduced over the past two-and-a-half years to control the cost of living, reduce the financial burden on schoolchildren\u2019s education and strengthen household economies.\n\nHe also raised concerns over inflation, claiming that it had exceeded eight per cent and warning that the situation could worsen further.\n\nReferring to the Government\u2019s response to the damage caused by Cyclone Ditwah, Rajapaksa accused the President of making misleading statements in Parliament, claiming that some affected families were still living in temporary shelters and that damaged roads and houses remained unrepaired.\n\nHe said the Government had spent two Budgets without delivering on its promises and questioned whether the third Budget would simply contain more unfulfilled assurances.\n\nRajapaksa also challenged the Government to identify new employment opportunities created through foreign investments attracted during its tenure."
