    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "The \"Snap, Crackle and Pop\" - made when milk is added to the puffed rice - has been a centrepiece of the brand's marketing since the 1950s.\n\nKellogg's insisted the snap, crackle and pop is \"still there, but consumers might notice that the sound may be quieter and not as instant as before\".\n\nWhen Scottish fizzy drink Irn Bru cut its sugar content in 2018, fans famously stockpiled crates of the old version.\n\nNo changes are being made to Rice Krispies products in the US and Canada.\n\nKellogg's said the recipe change in the UK would help \"maintain consistent quality\" as extreme weather takes a toll on crops and pushes up ingredient prices.\n\nExtreme weather in Spain in 2022 and 2023 in particular had reduced availability of the types of rice the company uses for its breakfast cereals, the company said\n\nRice Krispies, launched nearly a century ago, are sold around the world and come in variations such as vanilla and strawberry flavours in some markets alongside the widespread chocolate-flavoured Coco Pops version.\n\n\"We appreciate that even small changes to a much-loved product can feel noticeable and that's why we have approached this with a lot of care and consideration,\" Kellogg's said.\n\nThe firm said it appreciated the customer feedback and would \"continue to look at ways to make [its] products even better in the future\".\n\nThe cereals have already undergone some change, with reductions in salt content in the 1990s and lowered sugar in 2018. The company said Rice Krispies and Coco Pops will continue to meet UK food standards on levels of fat, salt and sugar in food.\n\nA backlash against processed foods in recent years, especially those low in fibre and high in sugar, has put cereals generally under the spotlight from healthy eating advocates.\n\nKellogg's stressed the cereals contain no artificial colours, flavours or sweeteners and the nutritional benefits in each bowl, such as fibre, Vitamin D and iron are staying the same."
