    "chunk_text": "A backlash against processed foods in recent years, especially those low in fibre and high in sugar, has put cereals generally under the spotlight from healthy eating advocates.\n\nKellogg's stressed the cereals contain no artificial colours, flavours or sweeteners and the nutritional benefits in each bowl, such as fibre, Vitamin D and iron are staying the same.\n\nPreviously packaging said the products \"may contain gluten\" because they were made in a factory handling other cereals and there was a risk of cross-contamination.\n\nKellogg's said it has not changed the recipe for any of its other cereals.\n\nMany parts of the world have been affected by droughts, floods and heatwaves this year due to the El Ni\u00f1o weather phenomenon. The weather event is expected to severely affect crop production from South East Asia in particular. The region produces more than a quarter of the world's rice.\n\nThe World Meteorological Agency expects the record-breaking El Ni\u00f1o will last until at least 2027.\n\nThat will intensify pressure on food manufacturing companies, already looking at ways to source reliable and affordable ingredients.\n\nThe original Kellogg company separated into two distinct firms in 2023.\n\nThe largest is Kellanova, which focuses on snacks, cereals, noodles and other food products like Eggo, Pringles and Pop-Tarts sold globally. It is now owned by Mars, which makes confectionery and pet food.\n\nThe other business, WK Kellogg's, operates in North America and has since been taken over by Italian chocolate giant Ferrero."
