    "md5": "c54c3d98aef956fd99fb267438966a03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "Tragedy of SLFP tradition\n\nRanil who was never elected President by the citizenry, was appointed to that post by Gotabaya Rajapaksa and endorsed by the SLPP\u2014reloading Chandrika\u2019s 2015 \u2018hybrid\u2019. Ranil\u2019s economic stabilisation/adjustment \u2018shock therapy\u2019 (2022-2024) further crunched the SLPP\u2019s base after Gotabaya\u2019s \u2018fertiliser shock\u2019 had shrunk it (2019-2022). SLPP voters shifted to the JVP-NPP\n\nThe phrase \u201cThe Children of \u201956\u201d coined by Mervyn de Silva in 1973 (Sri Lanka: The End of Welfare Politics, South Asian Review, UK, 1973), recorded as such in the published doctoral thesis of Prof. Gamini Samaranayake, was plagiarised and popularised in Sinhala by the founding \u2018philosopher\u2019 of Jathika Chinthanaya.\n\nI am literally a child of \u201956. That was the year Ceylon/Sri Lanka\u2019s two-party system was born with the replacement in power of the dominant postcolonial party the UNP, commencing the alternation in power of two democratic parties/coalitions, two decades before India passed that landmark of political development. 2026 is the 70th anniversary year of the birth of the island\u2019s two-party system.\n\nThe shocking success of the JVP, leaping from 3 seats and 3% to the Presidency and a 2/3rds majority in Parliament was possible only because of the crisis of the two-party system and the two main political traditions:\n\nThe SLFP had been the most successful political party in the country. Under two leaders, Chandrika Bandaranaike Kumaratunga and Mahinda Rajapaksa, it led the country for two decades uninterruptedly from 1994 to 2014.\n\nBefore 1994 and CBK, the SLFP led by Sirimavo Bandaranaike had been in the Opposition for 17 years.\n\nFrom the country\u2019s first-ever Government with a 2/3rds majority in Parliament (1970) to a 17-year exile from Government."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
