    "md5": "5f9e902570728f52db8ac5bd378a7e03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Before 1994 and CBK, the SLFP led by Sirimavo Bandaranaike had been in the Opposition for 17 years.\n\nFrom the country\u2019s first-ever Government with a 2/3rds majority in Parliament (1970) to a 17-year exile from Government.\n\nThe SLFP crashed to its worst defeat with the TULF scoring more seats in the 1977 parliament.\u00a0 The party\u2019s crisis was compounded by the dismal showing of its candidate Hector Kobbekaduwa at the Presidential election of October 1982 against incumbent JR Jayewardene.\n\nBut there was one option which if exercised could have mitigated the crisis of the UF coalition in the 1970s, narrowed the margin between the Jayewardene UNP and the SLFP candidate in October 1982, and shortened the SLFP\u2019s 17-year spell of defeat.\n\nThe SLFP had within its topmost ranks a figure who was seen simultaneously as a moderate democrat in economics and governance, but also a progressive who enjoyed the support of the LSSP- CPSL and the USSR. That was the SLFP\u2019s Deputy Leader and the UF Government\u2019s Deputy Prime Minister who acted for Madam Bandaranaike when she was overseas: Maithripala Senanayake of Anuradhapura, an electorally unbeaten political giant from the rural Sinhala heartland who had also studied Tamil and was married to a journalist who was an ethnic Tamil.\n\nHad Maithripala Senanayake been given a fair chance, it could have reduced the extent of (but not entirely avoided) the swing to the UNP in 1977 and given JR a run for his money in October 1982.\n\nAfter decades of marginalisation by the Ratwatte-Bandaranaikes, a bitterly disappointed Maithripala Senanayake finally defected to JR Jayewardene, rather like Philip Gunawardena did to Dudley Senanayake.\n\nIt took almost two decades from the 1977 debacle for the SLFP to win under Chandrika Bandaranaike Kumaratunga.\n\nNo President since JR Jayewardene had the chances and options of CBK as president but she consistently ignored them in favour of unrealistic, irrational, irresponsible experimentation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
