    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "Mahinda Rajapaksa had to contest with a divided SLFP, his old friend Anura Bandaranaike (and even Sarath Amunugama) against him. After Mahinda won, Mangala Samaraweera tried to stall his assumption of SLFP leadership. MR plugged the gap with the support of his family \u2018clan\u2019, the ultranationalist Hela Urumaya, and the JVP. Mahinda had been until then, an opponent of an SLFP-JVP alliance which he prophesied would siphon-off SLFP votes to the JVP someday.\n\nChandrika Kumaratunga\u2019s role in forming a coalition between a breakaway faction of the SLFP led by Maithripala Sirisena and the UNP against the MR Presidency would\u2019ve been politically legitimate in the aftermath of Bharatha Lakshman Premachandra\u2019s killing, except she forged a coalition which she knew from experience was unsustainable.\n\nCBK\u2019s experiment in political cohabitation with Ranil Wickremesinghe who was Prime Minister in 2001-2003, ended because she simply had to fire him, go for an election and defeat him. No cohabitation between SLFP and UNP was sustainable so long as Ranil, with his neoliberal and abjectly pro-Western views, was partner.\n\nAn SLFP-UNP bloc was sustainable only as a centrist coalition, feasible if the UNP was represented by a liberal-centrist whose views were compatible with the SLFP\u2019s. Chandrika could have insisted on Karu Jayasuriya as the dissident SLFP\u2019s partner. A Maithri-Karu convergence would have been sustainable.\n\nInstead, CBK visited the UNP headquarters Siri Kotha and stitched together a Sirisena-Wickremesinghe equation. In 2015 she put into practice the plan she had divulged in Washington DC in Autumn 2005.\n\nThe SLFP\u2019s base and (therefore) most of the SLFP parliamentary group couldn\u2019t be won over to the Yahapalanaya agenda driven by Ranil\u2019s UNP which didn\u2019t shift to the centre but remained on the neoliberal right.\n\n1. The SLFP shattered, with the overwhelming majority remaining loyal to Mahinda Rajapaksa and launching the SLPP."
