    "md5": "f3323e295617aa14d9e28b0f37a223db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "The SLFP\u2019s base and (therefore) most of the SLFP parliamentary group couldn\u2019t be won over to the Yahapalanaya agenda driven by Ranil\u2019s UNP which didn\u2019t shift to the centre but remained on the neoliberal right.\n\n1. The SLFP shattered, with the overwhelming majority remaining loyal to Mahinda Rajapaksa and launching the SLPP.\n\nDesperate to save the SLFP from terminal erosion by alignment with Ranil\u2019s UNP, President Sirisena re-linked in late-2018 with Mahinda Rajapaksa who had retained the bulk of the SLFP\u2019s vote-base. This \u201852-day experiment\u2019 was thwarted by the decision of the Supreme Court which predictably ruled-out a dissolution and snap election. In conversations from Moscow with Mahinda Rajapaksa and President Sirisena, I had cautioned that a snap election was highly improbable, and urged a non-binding Referendum instead.\n\nThe elated, delusional UNP thought that the next election was in the bag. The reality was that the UNP never emerged from the 20% trough of February 2018\u2014nor did or has its derivative/successor, the SJB.\n\nThe SLPP swept the board in 2019, but that led to another disaster because it was not Mahinda Rajapaksa who was candidate. Mahinda had blundered in attempting a third term, with a colluding CJ dismissing as unnecessary a referendum to change the constitutional two-term limit\u2014and lost the gamble (2015). The Yahapalanaya Government (2015-2019) then unfairly banned Mahinda from running for even a non-consecutive third term, which had it existed in Brazil would have prevented Lula being re-elected.\n\nThe necessity which arose with the gap left by the ban on MR\u2019s candidacy.\n\nThe shift in the balance of the SLFP and its partners, strengthening the ultranationalist element when Chandrika divided it in 2005 in her attempt to sabotage Mahinda.\n\nThe catastrophic Gotabaya Presidency was overthrown by the Sri Lankan citizens led by the university students and youth."
  },
