    "md5": "37a5910a9f69f1cd521baa35912ee942",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "Proletarian, virtuous Bonapartism\n\n\u201cUnlimited power in the hands of limited people always leads to cruelty\u2026 The state was created to protect us from evil... but it generates others of its own, still more powerful, and this time one-directional\u201d \u2014 Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, The Gulag Archipelago / The Red Wheel\n\nI voted for AKD. A profound sense of letdown compels this essay. In search of solace, I find myself turning to the old poets for refuge. It is a terrible thing when the dawn you so long-awaited deliver nothing but the same old midnight.\n\nI fervently hope that within the ranks of the 159 NPP parliamentarians, there remain at least a few who have read Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn\u2019s The Gulag Archipelago and The Red Wheel. It is in that hope that I recall his timeless warnings in this essay.\n\nThe titles of the two legislative masterpieces of the NPP Government, now awaiting debate and perhaps eventual passage, are eloquent.\n\nThe first is called the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill, and the second is the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill.\n\nThe term \"Terrorism\" famously defies definition. What, then, are organised crimes? And what are disorganised crimes?\n\nThose wise words of US Supreme Court Justice Felix Frankfurter immediately come to mind:\u00a0 \u201cAll our work, our whole life is a matter of semantics, because words are the tools with which we work, the material out of which laws are made, out of which the Constitution was written. Everything depends on our understanding of them.\"\n\nBoth bills have already generated furious scrutiny. Distinguished scholars, including those rare individuals who possess the unique privilege of first-hand experience with \"State Terror,\" have exposed the deep systemic dangers of these two drafts.\n\nI am no lawyer. I am no constitutional pundit either. But I do have a memory. I belong to the generation that witnessed our collective slide into the abyss of unreason following the passage of the original Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA)."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-05-dailyftlk-fbec5331",
