    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7f54774043b625ec7e46542da7789c93",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "Now, the Ministry of Justice has presented a parallel universe of legal wizardry. To the uninitiated, this looks like a flagrant betrayal. But that is because you do not understand the subtle, Orwellian dialectic of modern governance.\n\nIn this new dispensation, \"Good Governance\" is no longer measured by human rights charters or international legal certainty. \"Good\" is whatever the JVP Politburo decides is good on any given day!\n\nNowhere is this legislative sorcery more sickening than in the actual provisions of the new Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill.\n\nFor a nation that has spent nearly half a century suffocating under the old PTA, this new draft is an exercise in profound disillusionment.\n\nInstead of delivering the promised burial of counter-terrorism overreach, this bill replicates the fluid, all-encompassing definitions of \"terrorism\" that turn ordinary political expression into capital offenses.\n\nUnder these provisions, the state reserves the right to classify acts of public protest, labor strikes, or civil disobedience as acts of terrorism if they can be framed as \"disrupting essential services\" or \"coercing the Government\".\n\nThis is the foundational architecture of the garrison state. By leaving the definition of terror patently ambiguous, the bill systematically strips away the foundational due process guarantees that Sri Lankans have fought to preserve since the birth of our modern nation-state.\n\nI have been told by knowledgeable people that this new law grants law enforcement sweeping powers of executive detention, allowing suspects to be held for prolonged periods completely severed from the vital check of immediate judicial oversight.\n\nParallel to this is the jurisprudential innovation of the dress code and tattoo clause inside the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill."
  },
