    "md5": "5659ed3febf106d43c44c89d8f1073a8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "I have been told by knowledgeable people that this new law grants law enforcement sweeping powers of executive detention, allowing suspects to be held for prolonged periods completely severed from the vital check of immediate judicial oversight.\n\nParallel to this is the jurisprudential innovation of the dress code and tattoo clause inside the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill.\n\nSri Lankans will easily recall Naomi Coleman, the 37-year-old British mental health nurse who was arbitrarily arrested and deported simply for having a tattoo of the Buddha on her arm\u2014an act the Supreme Court later ruled had absolutely no basis in law.\n\nIf you sport a bizarre tattoo, use a pithy Sinhala expression that irritates a sensitive cop, or happen to accidentally join a Zoom meeting where someone holds a dissenting opinion, congratulations! You are now a potential asset of a transnational syndicate.\n\nThis law effectively criminalises identity and association rather than concrete criminal conduct, turning every eccentric citizen into a potential threat to the state.\n\nIt was the great Czech dissident V\u00e1clav Havel who warned us about the unique horror of the post-totalitarian state, where the system demands total, unquestioning conformity to its arbitrary codes.\n\nHavel observed that such a system forces the individual to \"live within a lie,\" compelling ordinary citizens to act out rituals of obedience just to survive.\n\nA lawyer friend tells me that these two bills effectively \"automate compliance\". Our original PTA famously converted the land into a permanent garrison state; these new bills threaten to convert the remaining civilian spaces into an open-air prison where state wardens judge your loyalty by the ink on your skin and the words in your mouth."
  },
