    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s Rs. 2.1 trillion tax arrears: Collect, resolve or remove\n\nSri Lanka has made significant progress in rebuilding government revenue following the economic crisis. Tax revenue increased from 7.3% of GDP in 2021 to 15.4% in 2025. Yet, behind this recovery lies an uncomfortable figure. Approximately Rs. 2.1 trillion is reported as taxes in default at the end of 2025, as per the findings published in IMF\u2019s revenue administration diagnostic report.\n\nThe obvious question is why the Government is not simply collecting this money? However, the Rs. 2.1 trillion should not be viewed as cash waiting to be collected. Approximately Rs. 1.6 trillion is under dispute, while IMF\u2019s report also raises concerns over the composition and collectability of older arrears.\n\nBefore embarking on another collection drive, the Government therefore needs to establish what the Rs. 2.1 trillion actually represents.\n\nThe Inland Revenue Department should undertake a comprehensive internal analysis of the arrears stock, categorising liabilities by age, tax type, dispute status, recovery action already initiated, and likelihood of recovery.\n\nParticular attention should be given to older debt. Section 161 of the Inland Revenue Act No. 24 of 2017 provides that: \u201cInstitution of action under this Chapter shall be commenced within five years of the date on which the taxpayer was in default.\u201d\n\nThis does not necessarily mean that every tax debt disappears automatically after five years. The critical question is whether the required recovery action was commenced within that statutory period. Nevertheless, where five years have elapsed without such action, the legal recoverability of those amounts needs to be established."
  },
