    "md5": "77ddb715f1c60e6e4589d6efab0a93e3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1683,
    "chunk_text": "This does not necessarily mean that every tax debt disappears automatically after five years. The critical question is whether the required recovery action was commenced within that statutory period. Nevertheless, where five years have elapsed without such action, the legal recoverability of those amounts needs to be established.\n\nThe objective is to arrive at a figure far more useful than Rs. 2.1 trillion: Sri Lanka\u2019s net collectible tax arrears.\n\nWith around three-quarters of reported defaults under dispute, Sri Lanka has a dispute-resolution problem as much as a collection problem.\n\nCases should be segmented according to value, age, complexity, and prospects of recovery. Large, long-standing cases should receive priority, while relatively straightforward computational and interpretational disputes should be fast-tracked.\n\nThere may also be scope for a pragmatic, time-bound settlement program.\n\nFor both the Government and affected taxpayers, long-standing disputed arrears have effectively become a sunk cost. The Government has not received the cash, while taxpayers continue to carry unpaid or disputed liabilities. Pursuing the full amount for another five or ten years may produce less value than securing a substantial portion today.\n\nThe Government could therefore explore a carefully structured, one-off settlement window. Under this arrangement, taxpayers would receive a defined concession or discount, potentially on accumulated penalties and interest, in return for withdrawing disputes and settling the agreed liability within a short period. Any broader compromise of disputed principal tax would, of course, require a clear legal basis and safeguards."
