    "md5": "fb70b4feb9942e8879672bf68800a434",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "The Government could therefore explore a carefully structured, one-off settlement window. Under this arrangement, taxpayers would receive a defined concession or discount, potentially on accumulated penalties and interest, in return for withdrawing disputes and settling the agreed liability within a short period. Any broader compromise of disputed principal tax would, of course, require a clear legal basis and safeguards.\n\nDone properly, this could produce a genuine win-win. Taxpayers could clear legacy liabilities and move forward, while the Government could convert ageing paper assessments into immediate cash. That cash could then support debt reduction, essential government expenditure, or productive economic activity rather than remaining trapped for years in disputes.\n\nInternational practice also demonstrates the value of distinguishing taxpayers who cannot pay from those who will not pay.\n\nAustralia permits payment arrangements for taxpayers experiencing financial difficulty, while retaining stronger recovery options where necessary. Compliance history and taxpayer engagement can influence the collection approach.\n\nCanada illustrates the potential of digital debt management. Its tax administration enables taxpayers to arrange payments electronically.\n\nNew Zealand similarly provides instalment arrangements for taxpayers unable to settle immediately and encourages early engagement where payment difficulties are anticipated.\n\nThe common principle is simple: intervene early, facilitate payment by viable taxpayers, and act decisively against deliberate defaulters.\n\nCleaning up historical arrears addresses only part of the problem. Sri Lanka must simultaneously prevent another stock of uncollectible debt from accumulating."
