    "md5": "af298e7fe6524b0f6e83ed15f6ff0ba3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s THC dispute: Regulating charges or regulating contracts?\n\nA transparent charging framework should protect cargo owners without weakening Colombo\u2019s shipping connectivity\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recurring dispute over terminal handling charges (THC) has again set maritime service providers against the trading community, and each side claims to speak for the national interest. The Sri Lanka Association of NVOCC Agents (SLANA) told its 9th AGM that THC is treated as a separate land-based cost in international markets and asked the Government to expedite a committee on the issue (Daily FT, 14 September 2026). The National Chamber of Exporters (NCE) has urged that Gazette No. 2334/26 be retained, warning that a separate THC would reopen the fragmented system of \u201cnearly 50\u201d charges that existed before 2013 (Hiru News, 16 October 2026). The Ceylon Freight & Logistics Association (CEYFFA) has since said at a press conference that Sri Lanka \u201ccould be losing\u201d more than $ 50 million a year in foreign exchange under the current framework (press report, 31 October 2026).\n\nThe task for Government is not to choose between shipping lines and cargo owners. It is to distinguish legitimate cost recovery from uncontracted charging, and commercial freedom from regulatory privilege.\n\nThe service providers\u2019 case deserves a hearing. Shipping lines, NVOCCs, consolidators and feeder operators argue that terminal handling is a land-side cost, distinct from sea carriage, and that in many markets it is quoted and invoiced separately. They point to rising terminal costs, the expense of repositioning empty containers and the thin margins of NVOCC and LCL operators. CEYFFA adds a foreign-exchange argument: that dollar payments abroad and dollar billing by terminals strain local logistics firms and the country\u2019s reserves."
  },
