    "md5": "b29c4e62c47fd2d5b32ddb55ee8cd7b3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "The service providers\u2019 case deserves a hearing. Shipping lines, NVOCCs, consolidators and feeder operators argue that terminal handling is a land-side cost, distinct from sea carriage, and that in many markets it is quoted and invoiced separately. They point to rising terminal costs, the expense of repositioning empty containers and the thin margins of NVOCC and LCL operators. CEYFFA adds a foreign-exchange argument: that dollar payments abroad and dollar billing by terminals strain local logistics firms and the country\u2019s reserves.\n\nThe two arguments answer different questions. The first asks whether the cost can be recovered at all. The second asks from whom it may be recovered, and with what disclosure. A rise in operating cost shows that a cost exists. It does not show that the cost must be billed separately to a Sri Lankan importer or exporter. Separating the questions makes the dispute far easier to resolve.\n\nThe all-inclusive freight regime began with Gazette Extraordinary No. 1842/16 of 27 December 2013, effective 6 January 2014, and was developed through Gazette No. 2041/10 of 17 October 2017. The 2022 amendments under Gazette No. 2302/24 proved controversial, and Gazette No. 2321/62 rescinded the 2017 regulations and the 2022 amendment in March 2023. The Minister then issued Gazette Extraordinary No. 2334/26 on 31 May 2023, under section 10 of the Licensing of Shipping Agents, Freight Forwarders, Non-Vessel Operating Common Carriers and Container Operators Act, No. 10 of 1972. Its key provisions are these:\n\nRegulation 3: charges covering carriage \u201cfrom the origin to destination\u201d are included in the all-inclusive freight and recovered \u201conly from the party who is contractually bound to pay\u201d it."
