    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "Two layers of rules decide who pays. The first is the sale contract. Incoterms 2020, published by the International Chamber of Commerce, decide which of buyer or seller must arrange and pay for carriage: under FOB the buyer, under CIF the seller. They are contractual terms and do not govern the contract of carriage itself (Maersk summary of Incoterms 2020). The second layer is the contract of carriage. The party that contracts with the line or NVOCC pays the freight, and in Sri Lanka the Gazette requires that freight to be all-inclusive and recoverable only from that party.\n\nOne area needs care. Under FCA, commonly cited practice places origin handling on the seller as part of delivering the goods on board. For a Sri Lankan exporter selling FCA, the foreign buyer is the carriage contracting party, so on my reading of Regulations 3 and 7 a licensed provider may not invoice the exporter anything beyond the all-inclusive freight. The Gazette therefore governs whom a licensed provider may invoice. It does not stop buyer and seller from agreeing between themselves how the cost is shared. DGMS should confirm this reading in writing, because exporters and providers currently argue past each other on exactly this point.\n\nPort-to-port freight is more than the vessel. Basic ocean freight \u201ccovers the basic cost of transporting a container \u2026 from origin to destination\u201d, derived from vessel cost, voyage distance and fixed costs, with fuel risk passed through a bunker adjustment factor and currency risk through a currency adjustment factor (Marine Insight). Behind every loaded box lies an empty one. Trade imbalances leave surplus empties in some places and shortages in others, and lines reposition them. Lines \u201ctypically absorb direct repositioning expenses as operational costs\u201d and recover them \u201cindirectly\u201d through freight rates and surcharges (Docshipper)."
