    "md5": "1de342ba19c243b958bbc8484349b857",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1574,
    "chunk_text": "Port-to-port freight is more than the vessel. Basic ocean freight \u201ccovers the basic cost of transporting a container \u2026 from origin to destination\u201d, derived from vessel cost, voyage distance and fixed costs, with fuel risk passed through a bunker adjustment factor and currency risk through a currency adjustment factor (Marine Insight). Behind every loaded box lies an empty one. Trade imbalances leave surplus empties in some places and shortages in others, and lines reposition them. Lines \u201ctypically absorb direct repositioning expenses as operational costs\u201d and recover them \u201cindirectly\u201d through freight rates and surcharges (Docshipper).\n\nThis explains why a label proves little. An illustrative quote of $ 500 ocean freight plus $ 150 in terminal charges is not inherently cheaper or dearer than an all-inclusive $650. The distinction matters only when a charge is added after the freight contract is concluded, or when the structure stops customers comparing quotations.\n\n5. The feeder layer: SOC containers, FIFO and direct billing\n\nColombo is mainly a transhipment hub: about 80 per cent of its January to October 2025 volume was transhipment (Maritime Gateway). Regional feeders connect it to neighbouring ports and carry many NVOCC and shipper-owned containers (SOC). Freight terms in that trade vary. Under Free In/Free Out (FIFO) terms, loading and discharging handling charges are not included in the rate; under Liner In/Liner Out (LILO) they are (SeaRates glossary). The terms show there is no single universal way of quoting terminal handling."
