    "chunk_text": "Colombo is mainly a transhipment hub: about 80 per cent of its January to October 2025 volume was transhipment (Maritime Gateway). Regional feeders connect it to neighbouring ports and carry many NVOCC and shipper-owned containers (SOC). Freight terms in that trade vary. Under Free In/Free Out (FIFO) terms, loading and discharging handling charges are not included in the rate; under Liner In/Liner Out (LILO) they are (SeaRates glossary). The terms show there is no single universal way of quoting terminal handling.\n\nThe key point is the one Figure 3 makes: the same terminal cost can sit inside two different contracts. Between an NVOCC and a feeder, both are commercial parties and the freight term allocates the handling cost. Between an importer or exporter and a line or NVOCC, the Gazette requires one all-inclusive freight recoverable only from the party contractually bound. Treating the two as identical would be wrong in either direction.\n\nThe Gazette\u2019s treatment of business-to-business feeder and SOC contracts needs clarification. Regulation 3 is broadly drafted, while Regulation 11 defines the regulated carriage by container yard and freight station movements. As I read it, there is no express carve-out for feeder-to-SOC FIFO contracts, so it would be premature to say that every separate port handling invoice between a feeder and an SOC operator is exempt. DGMS should state how the Gazette applies to those contracts without weakening protection against uncontracted charges on importers and exporters."
  },
