    "chunk_text": "The CEYFFA figure is reported as \u201ccould be losing\u201d more than $ 50 million a year. The report prints no calculation, period, container volumes or transaction data. Four points frame it. First, Figure 1 shows that the handling element of the freight ends with Sri Lankan terminal operators, which SLPA reportedly collects in dollars from shipping lines. What the carrier keeps is the ocean-freight element, earned abroad in every international shipment. Second, a complaint that terminals bill local firms in dollars concerns the currency of billing, a separate question that the all-inclusive freight rule does not address; Gazette 2334/26 names no terminal operators and its only currency provision is the rupee delivery order fee. Third, scale: Central Bank data reported by Maritime Gateway show about 1.10 million domestic TEU in January to October 2025, so $ 50 million spread across that volume is roughly $ 38 to $ 45 per TEU. That is a plausible order of magnitude, but a total divided by a volume is not a loss. Fourth, genuine leakage, when investigated, is quantified in accounts and transfers: Parliament was told in July 2026 of about $ 715 million remitted overseas through 105 shell companies and some 24,300 transfers (Daily FT). The claimants should publish their workings so regulators can reconcile them.\n\nThe Government should avoid blanket rescission of Gazette No. 2334/26 and instead:\n\n1. Review before reform. Have DGMS review the charging arrangements with shipping agents, vessel operators, NVOCCs, consolidators, feeders, SLPA, the terminal operators, exporters and importers, using anonymised freight contracts, port invoices and comparable regional tariffs.\n\n2. Publish a charge map. For each shipment type (import FCL, export FCL, LCL, feeder or SOC), set out in one page the contracting party, who invoices whom, in which currency and on what legal basis."
