    "md5": "b000905ff4238d33b7efe7f1710f7ceb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "1. Review before reform. Have DGMS review the charging arrangements with shipping agents, vessel operators, NVOCCs, consolidators, feeders, SLPA, the terminal operators, exporters and importers, using anonymised freight contracts, port invoices and comparable regional tariffs.\n\n2. Publish a charge map. For each shipment type (import FCL, export FCL, LCL, feeder or SOC), set out in one page the contracting party, who invoices whom, in which currency and on what legal basis.\n\n4. Settle the evidence on foreign exchange. Ask CEYFFA for its workings, and have the Central Bank, SLPA, Customs and DGMS reconcile them and publish the result.\n\n5. Review terminal billing conditions for NVOCC and feeder cargo, including direct billing and guarantee requirements, from a competition standpoint.\n\n6. Require any amendment to be precise. It should define its scope and safeguards, rest on the proper statutory authority and procedure, follow stakeholder consultation, and avoid creating a general right to recover charges from cargo owners merely because a provider can point to an underlying cost.\n\nThe THC dispute is not about whether port handling has a cost. It does. Nor is it only about whether the cost is collected locally or folded into ocean freight. The questions that matter are who contracted for the service, who is legally liable for the cost, whether the charge was transparently disclosed and whether its collection complies with the law. Sri Lanka needs a framework that protects traders against uncontracted charges while letting providers recover legitimate costs from parties that have lawfully agreed to bear them.\n\nColombo\u2019s competitiveness will not be decided by whether a charge is called THC, PHC or all-inclusive ocean freight. It will be decided by the total cost of moving cargo, the predictability of commercial obligations and the integrity of the regulatory framework.\n\nDaily FT, SLANA calls for urgent fix to Colombo terminal charges (14 September 2026)"
