    "chunk_size_bytes": 1790,
    "chunk_text": "The sectors expected to feature include agriculture and processed foods, palmyrah-based products, seafood, handloom and handicrafts, information technology, renewable energy, small industries, wellness products, traditional medicine, medical equipment, tourism and cultural industries.\n\nThe two-day forum will include seminars, discussions, an exhibition of products and services, presentations by businesses and direct business meetings.\n\nThe organisers aim to facilitate at least 50 to 75 business meetings and attract between 75 and 100 qualified buyers. These meetings will give Northern producers the opportunity to discuss product quality, pricing, supply capacity and export potential directly with buyers.\n\nGuidance will also be provided on international market information, overseas trade regulations, quality certifications, trade finance, risk management, transportation and supply-chain arrangements. As part of the preparations, workshops were held on 3 and 4 November.\n\nThe success of the forum should not be measured only by the number of organisations attending. Its long-term value will depend on the business agreements that follow, ongoing buyer relationships, stronger production capacity and sustainable export earnings.\n\nA tripartite agreement was signed at the meeting to advance an online initiative aimed at reducing the difficulties investors face when seeking approvals from multiple government departments.\n\nThe agreement was signed by Northern Province Governor N. Vethanayahan, Vavuniya University Vice-Chancellor Arputharajah and Management Club of Sri Lanka Project Chairman Kaushal Rajapaksa. The initiative is intended to coordinate investors\u2019 engagement with relevant departments and institutions, and make it easier to obtain the information they need."
