    "md5": "a51b4ac62083bf1932315e380f769f97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1962,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka FinTech Festival 2026 signals startup capital, virtual asset regulation and digital expansion\n\nSri Lanka will set up a sovereign-backed fund of funds for startups, develop a regulatory framework for virtual assets and virtual asset service providers, and expand telecom coverage to over 99% of the population, Deputy Minister of Digital Economy Eng. Eranga Weeraratne said.\n\nHe made the remarks at the recent Sri Lanka FinTech Festival 2026 in Colombo, where he outlined a broader push to accelerate digital adoption and position the country as a regional fintech hub.\n\nWeeraratne said Sri Lanka may be a few years behind other regional countries in digital payment and fintech adoption, but said the country has the necessary ingredients to move faster, including a strong IT talent pool, an established IT industry, a heavily banked population, high literacy and government commitment to digital transformation.\n\nHe said the Government\u2019s ambition is to move Sri Lanka from a cash-heavy society towards a cash-light, digitally enabled economy, while also helping local IT and fintech companies export solutions to global markets.\n\n\u201cWe are setting up a fund of funds, a sovereign fund, for investing in our startups, and we are inviting other international and local funds to contribute,\u201d he said.\n\nThe fund is expected to provide matching capital for startups, while the Government works with local and international fund managers to create the policies and documentation needed to connect Sri Lankan entrepreneurs with global capital.\n\nWeeraratne also said work is under way on regulations for virtual assets and virtual asset service providers, a move intended to give clarity to businesses and investors and allow local firms to innovate in the sector.\n\nOn digital payments, he said the Government will push QR-based payments through promotions, incentives and support for devices, aiming to shift both consumers and merchants away from cash."
  },
