    "chunk_text": "Weeraratne also said work is under way on regulations for virtual assets and virtual asset service providers, a move intended to give clarity to businesses and investors and allow local firms to innovate in the sector.\n\nOn digital payments, he said the Government will push QR-based payments through promotions, incentives and support for devices, aiming to shift both consumers and merchants away from cash.\n\n\u201cDigital payment histories can become a new form of collateral for SMEs,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Deputy Minister also said telecom coverage, currently around 94\u201395% of the population, will be expanded beyond 99% within the next two to three years, with investment and support from partners including the Asian Development Bank.\n\nTo build trust in digital services, the Government plans to establish a cybersecurity regulatory authority under forthcoming legislation and invest in advanced security operations centres to protect public and private digital infrastructure.\n\nWeeraratne said interoperability, open systems and low-code tools will be key to allowing new services to be built on national platforms and helping Sri Lankan fintech firms export solutions.\n\nHowever, Central Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe cautioned that virtual assets cannot be used for payments between residents under Sri Lanka\u2019s current legal framework.\n\n\u201cThis position is important in preventing the substitution of the Sri Lankan rupee in domestic transactions and in safeguarding the country\u2019s monetary sovereignty,\u201d he said.\n\nDelivering the keynote on \u2018The Future of FinTech,\u2019 Weerasinghe said Sri Lanka\u2019s post-crisis recovery has created the foundation for growth, but the next phase must focus on productivity, investment, employment and innovation.\n\n\u201cStability is not the destination; it is the foundation upon which sustainable growth must be built,\u201d he said."
  },
