    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking at the event, IFC South Asia Manager-Country Advisory and Economics Gregory Smith, emphasised that helping SMEs are central to Sri Lanka\u2019s economy, and helping them compete globally is key to unlocking Sri Lanka\u2019s growth, creating jobs and strengthening economic resilience.\n\nDelegation of the EU to Sri Lanka and the Maldives Head of Partnerships Virginie Lafleur-Tighe said, \u201cThe EU is proud to support the ExpoScaleUp program as part of our broader partnership with Sri Lanka under the ACSIIS regional program. This initiative reflects our shared commitment to strengthening the competitiveness of SMEs by helping them become more export-ready, more sustainable, and better equipped to meet evolving market standards. By linking private sector development with climate action, and by working closely with partners such as IFC and the EDB, we are contributing to a more resilient, inclusive, and future-ready export ecosystem in Sri Lanka.\u201d\n\nParticipating SMEs represented a diverse range of sectors, including spices and concentrates, processed food and beverages, coconut-based products, gifts and handicrafts, ICT/BPO, and herbal cosmetics.\n\nTen SMEs were recognised for the best Export Market Plans, highlighting their efforts to develop structured market-entry strategies and identify opportunities for sustainable export growth. Selected SMEs will have the opportunity to advance to ExpoConnect, the program\u2019s next stage, focused on market access, trade facilitation, and targeted matchmaking with international buyers.\n\nNearly 750 SMEs across Sri Lanka have benefited from the SLEEI program through regional awareness sessions and business clinics. The initiative highlights the commitment of the EDB, IFC and the European Union to helping Sri Lankan SMEs compete globally."
  },
