    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3ee0a52b764df7e630e5e79ff350e44b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1713,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cAs I have consistently tried to emphasise, climate change is no longer just a terrifying prospect, but a catastrophic reality which threatens our very way of life\u2026 And in each of our countries, for too many people, the opportunity to fulfil their own potential lies far beyond their reach.\n\nEchoing The King\u2019s remarks, Secretary-General Botchwey said the world today is \u201csorely in need of the Commonwealth spirit of always finding a way\u201d.\n\n\u201cTogether with our hosts, and our member countries, we have remade this CHOGM as a summit of action. Our goal is to build a new model of partnership and cooperation that is capable of responding to the scale of challenge we face; to avoid the mistakes of the past by making our collective interest the sum of our individual national interest. It is a success strategy that requires a mindset change, including discarding the old division of labour between states and business.\u201d\n\nShe said that strategy is already taking shape through new initiatives to mobilise investment, boost intra-Commonwealth trade, upskill young people and expand renewable energy.\n\nAddressing the Heads of Government, the Secretary-General said: \u201cOur people will judge this meeting by what it delivers for them. Each of you brings the authority to act on behalf of your country. Together, you can deliver for a third of humanity. I urge you to seize this chance to build resilience and shared prosperity together.\u201d\n\nPrime Minister of Antigua and Barbuda Gaston Browne described the meeting as a \u201cconsequential CHOGM\u201d, pointing to early results including US$200 million mobilised through the Nature Initiative and US$1.3 billion announced through the Global Eye Health Summit."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
