    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7afa77ce44556bcfc8c7458be8dcec1d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "Prime Minister of Antigua and Barbuda Gaston Browne described the meeting as a \u201cconsequential CHOGM\u201d, pointing to early results including US$200 million mobilised through the Nature Initiative and US$1.3 billion announced through the Global Eye Health Summit.\n\nDuring this summit, Heads of Government and business leaders are meeting in two dedicated sessions to mobilise finance for an over $52 billion pipeline of investment-ready projects from 28 developing Commonwealth countries.\n\nLeaders will also consider a new Commonwealth work programme to remove barriers to intra-Commonwealth trade; a common approach to sustainable debt and concessional finance that better reflects vulnerability; and a plan to carry the investment agenda forward in 2027.\n\nWith 60 per cent of Commonwealth citizens under the age of 30, leaders will discuss how to expand education, skills and entrepreneurship opportunities for young people. Discussions will also cover building democratic resilience, accelerating climate action and responding to the particular challenges facing small states.\n\nDeputy Prime Minister Mulipola Anarosa Ale-Molio\u2019o of Samoa, speaking as outgoing Commonwealth Chair-in-Office, highlighted the growing pressure of climate change on small and vulnerable states.\n\n\u201cThe world is already pushing past the critical 1.5\u00b0C degree threshold. Many nations and communities are facing permanent losses, but overshoot must not become an excuse for delayed admission or inaction, the Commonwealth must hear the calls from the recent Pacific Pre-COP31 discussions in Fiji and Tuvalu.\n\n\u201cAccessible and predictable climate finance is the bridge between ambition and action. No nation should have to borrow its way out of a crisis it did so little to create.\u201d\n\nThe CHOGM executive sessions will culminate in the Leaders\u2019 Retreat, which gives Heads of Government an opportunity to have frank discussions in an informal setting."
  },
