    "chunk_text": "Elon Musk\u2019s business empire braces for Democratic wave as Texas flips blue\n\nReuters - As Republicans suffered a landslide defeat and Texas elected its first Democratic governor in more than three decades, one of the biggest losers of the US midterms was not on the ballot: billionaire Elon Musk.\n\nThe world's richest man spent nearly half a billion \u200bdollars to back Republican candidates, opens new tab and keep Texas red - a state home to his automaker Tesla, rocket-to-AI company SpaceX and a planned chipmaking venture \u200cbetween the two.\n\nThose bets unraveled as Democrats seized both chambers of Congress and Gina Hinojosa defeated Greg Abbott in the Texas governor's race, in a stinging rebuke to US President Donald Trump, a close Musk ally whom the billionaire helped elect.\n\nMusk, who urged his followers on X to vote Republican as recently as Tuesday, now faces a resurgent Democratic Party that he has spent years antagonizing.\n\nThe GOP's largest donor even \u200bthreatened retaliation last month, opens new tab on his social media platform after a report said Democrats were considering probes into his companies if they won midterms.\n\nShares of SpaceX (SPCX.O), opens new tab \u200bfell 5.5%, while Tesla (TSLA.O), opens new tab was down 5.1% as both stocks lagged the broader market.\n\nOne of Musk's biggest challenges could be slower approvals for \u2060the data centers crucial to tapping the $28.5 trillion addressable market that SpaceX touted to investors ahead of its IPO.\n\nAnalysts and experts expect Democrats to tighten scrutiny of data-center projects \u200band push for construction moratoriums as local opposition grows over electricity costs.\n\n\"A Democratic sweep of Congress would put pressure on tech and potentially bring regulatory pressure on everything AI \u200band possibly slow down the wild, wild west growth figures that we see daily and often take for granted,\" said Jake Dollarhide, CEO of Longbow Asset Management."
