    "chunk_text": "\"A Democratic sweep of Congress would put pressure on tech and potentially bring regulatory pressure on everything AI \u200band possibly slow down the wild, wild west growth figures that we see daily and often take for granted,\" said Jake Dollarhide, CEO of Longbow Asset Management.\n\nMusk has also drawn \u200bscrutiny, opens new tab from Democratic lawmakers over restrictions on satellite communications used by Ukraine's military, mass federal job cuts under his watch at DOGE and the government contracts and subsidies \u200bflowing to his companies.\n\nOne of the lawmakers is Robert Garcia, the next House Oversight Committee chair, who had a blunt two-word message for Republicans and their allies on Tuesday evening: \"Lawyer up\n\nIn \u2060Texas, Musk will have to navigate an already strained relationship with Hinojosa. He will also face newly elected U.S. Senator James Talarico, against whom he spent heavily while backing Republican Ken Paxton.\n\nIncoming governor Hinojosa had sued to obtain emails between Musk and Abbott and accused the former governor of selling out the Lone Star state by offering favorable deals to the billionaire.\n\nShe has also dismissed Abbott's efforts to rein in data centers as political theater, calling instead for an immediate halt to new projects until lawmakers strengthened protections for \u200blocal communities, as well as an end \u200bto tax breaks.\n\nMusk himself appeared to \u2060make light of the hurdles. \"Data centers in space looking pretty appealing right now,\" he posted on X, opens new tab, alluding to SpaceX's ambitious plan to put data centers in orbit despite major challenges around cooling and repairs.\n\nThe company's AI business is centered around Colossus and Colossus II, \u200btwo of the world's largest AI data centers near Memphis. Renting their computing power to customers including Anthropic has sent \u200brevenue soaring and helped SpaceX \u2060post its first profit as a public company."
