    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "JAAF calls for evidence before Colombo Port charging rules are changed\n\nApparel industry says a US $ 50mn forex leakage claim needs to be tested and that exporters need cost certainty\n\nThe Joint Apparel Association Forum (JAAF) is calling on the authorities to test a reported claim that Sri Lanka \u201ccould be losing\u201d more than US $ 50 million a year in foreign exchange through the Colombo Port charging arrangements before any changes are made to the rules that the exporters rely on.\n\nThe claim was made by the Ceylon Freight and Logistics Association (CEYFFA), whose General Secretary Inam Caffoor raised it at a press conference, reported in the Sunday Island, on October 31, 2026.\n\nThe report gives no calculation, period, container volume or transaction data and its wording is \u201ccould\u201d, not \u201cis\u201d.\n\n\u201cThe apparel exporters ship every day and they plan their costs around the freight charges they are quoted,\u201d said JAAF Secretary General Yohan Lawrence.\n\n\u201cA figure of this size deserves scrutiny. But the rules that give the exporters predictable pricing should not be rewritten on a claim that has yet to be backed by evidence.\u201d\n\nFor the JAAF\u2019s members, the stakes are practical. Under Gazette No. 2334/26 of May 31, 2023, the terminal handling charges form part of all-inclusive freight, which can be recovered only from the party contractually bound to pay it and no other charges may be added for exporters. That framework gives the shippers a single, transparent price.\n\nThe National Chamber of Exporters has noted that before 2013, the importers and exporters faced nearly 50 separate charges and it has warned against going back to that. The timing adds to the concern. The apparel exports for January to September 2026 stood at US $ 3,588.46 million, a decline of 5.52 percent from the same period last year, even after September\u2019s 3.47 percent rise.\n\nn a year when the industry is working hard to hold on to orders, cost certainty and reliable logistics matter."
  },
  {
