    "md5": "4ee4d5fc773da2e2beee0bbfda9d93d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "The National Chamber of Exporters has noted that before 2013, the importers and exporters faced nearly 50 separate charges and it has warned against going back to that. The timing adds to the concern. The apparel exports for January to September 2026 stood at US $ 3,588.46 million, a decline of 5.52 percent from the same period last year, even after September\u2019s 3.47 percent rise.\n\nn a year when the industry is working hard to hold on to orders, cost certainty and reliable logistics matter.\n\nThe money paid to a Sri Lankan port or terminal operator is a foreign-exchange inflow, not a leak. The rest of the freight rate is the shipping line\u2019s payment for carrying cargo by sea, which is earned overseas on every international shipment. A legitimate freight payment to a foreign carrier is not automatically an unlawful outflow.\n\nA simple test of scale shows why the detail matters. Colombo handled about 1.10 million import and export containers (TEUs) between January and October 2025, so a US $ 50 million loss spread across that cargo would equal roughly US $ 38 to US $ 45 per TEU. That arithmetic proves nothing by itself but it shows that a credible estimate has to start from actual transactions.\n\nThe JAAF is asking for five questions to be answered: Who is losing the money? Who is receiving it? Which transactions are involved? What is the legal basis? How was the figure calculated?\n\nIt also urges the Director General of Merchant Shipping, Sri Lanka Ports Authority, Central Bank of Sri Lanka and Sri Lanka Customs to request the underlying evidence from the CEYFFA, carry out a formal examination and publish the findings.\n\nThe JAAF supports the CEYFFA\u2019s call for a consultative mechanism that includes all stakeholders and believes the exporters should have a seat at that table.\n\n\u201cIf a loss exists, we should find it and fix it,\u201d said Lawrence."
  },
