    "md5": "54d6c70a23d4c5e3d1da326ff2ba8959",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "Indian surge snaps 4-month slide in tourist arrivals\n\nSri Lanka welcomed 168,625 tourists in October, up 2.1 percent compared to the same month last year, recording its first year-on-year monthly growth since May and ending a four-month losing streak, as per the data from the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA).\n\nThe uptick follows successive declines of 9.9 percent in June, 1.7 percent in July, 3.3 percent in August and a marginal 0.3 percent in September, with the arrivals in the third week of October climbing 14.5 percent to 42,739.\n\nHowever, the recovery was driven almost entirely by India, which sent 62,211 holidaymakers, up 29.3 percent from 48,113, lifting its share of total arrivals to 36.9 percent, from 29.1 percent.\n\nThe 14,098 additional Indian arrivals were more than four times the overall gain of 3,432, while the arrivals from all other markets combined contracted 9.1 percent to 106,414.\n\nAmong the remaining top 10 markets, only Australia and China grew, by 7.7 percent and 3.0 percent, respectively. Russia, the island\u2019s third-largest source market last October, slid to sixth as the arrivals plunged 41.3 percent to 6,750, while Germany fell 17.7 percent and Bangladesh 18.0 percent.\n\nMeanwhile, the cumulative arrivals for the first 10 months stood at 1,862,304, down 1.5 percent compared to the same period last year, weighed down by the steep falls of 19.7 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April amid the West Asia war. India led with 499,625 arrivals, up 18.0 percent, followed by the United Kingdom (173,129, down 1.0 percent), China (123,740, up 10.0 percent), Germany (105,305, down 9.8 percent) and Australia (95,498, up 12.0 percent).\n\nAmong the top 20 markets, Japan was the fastest-growing after India, rising 16.8 percent to 36,266, followed by Pakistan, Australia, Israel and the Maldives, all up more than 10 percent."
