    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, the cumulative arrivals for the first 10 months stood at 1,862,304, down 1.5 percent compared to the same period last year, weighed down by the steep falls of 19.7 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April amid the West Asia war. India led with 499,625 arrivals, up 18.0 percent, followed by the United Kingdom (173,129, down 1.0 percent), China (123,740, up 10.0 percent), Germany (105,305, down 9.8 percent) and Australia (95,498, up 12.0 percent).\n\nAmong the top 20 markets, Japan was the fastest-growing after India, rising 16.8 percent to 36,266, followed by Pakistan, Australia, Israel and the Maldives, all up more than 10 percent.\n\nThe outlook for the peak winter season appears promising as air connectivity expands.\n\nBritish Airways resumed thrice-weekly London Gatwick-Colombo flights on October 24 after a decade-long absence, complementing Jetstar\u2019s thrice-weekly Melbourne-Colombo service launched in August and strengthening links to two of the island\u2019s top five markets.\n\nAzur Air resumed Russian charter flights to Mattala on November 2, after a one-year break, joining Belavia, Kazakhstan\u2019s SCAT Airlines and Uzbekistan\u2019s Centrum Air at the southern airport, while S7 Airlines and French Bee are due to commence services in December.\n\nEven so, reaching the SLTDA\u2019s revised conservative scenario of 2,500,220 arrivals would require 637,916 tourists in November and December, 35.2 percent more than 471,834 recorded in the same two months last year, while its lower-end scenario of 2,377,370 would need a growth of 9.2 percent."
