    "chunk_id": "2026-11-05-dailymirrorlk-baf31530-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e6f82747ce88665ed1914528ddc98756",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1713,
    "chunk_text": "IMF flags unequal liquor taxes as local duties nearly double while import excise stays frozen\n\nExcise on imported liquor has not changed since December 2019\n\nThe locally made liquor and imports are taxed on an unequal footing that the International Monetary Fund (IMF) has described as unusual, with the excise on the local liquor nearly doubling since 2019, while the excise on the imported spirits, wine and beer has stayed frozen, the official and industry data showed.\n\nIn a report published by the Finance Ministry on October 30, the IMF noted that the imported alcoholic beverages carry a lower fixed excise rate per litre than the domestic products.\n\n\u201cThis differential treatment is unusual, as the imported and domestically produced excise goods are typically subject to equivalent excise rates to protect revenue, support neutrality and reduce opportunities for arbitrage,\u201d the report stated.\n\nThe excise on the locally made spirits has risen to Rs.7,969 per litre of alcohol, from Rs.4,150 in 2019, while the duty on the local strong beer has climbed to Rs.6,015, from Rs.3,200 and on local wine to Rs.5,735, from Rs.3,200, as per the industry data. The increases came through near-annual revisions, the latest in January 2025.\n\nHowever, the excise on the imported liquor has not changed since Excise Notification No. 08/2019 of December 2019. That notification roughly tripled the duty on the imported whisky, rum and brandy to Rs.650 per bulk litre and set Rs.600 for gin and vodka, Rs.230 for wine and Rs.135 for beer.\u00a0 The customs import duties of Rs.2,750 per litre on spirits, Rs.440 on wine and Rs.550 on beer have also stayed unchanged since at least 2021, as per the Customs tariff guides."
  },
