    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "However, the excise on the imported liquor has not changed since Excise Notification No. 08/2019 of December 2019. That notification roughly tripled the duty on the imported whisky, rum and brandy to Rs.650 per bulk litre and set Rs.600 for gin and vodka, Rs.230 for wine and Rs.135 for beer.\u00a0 The customs import duties of Rs.2,750 per litre on spirits, Rs.440 on wine and Rs.550 on beer have also stayed unchanged since at least 2021, as per the Customs tariff guides.\n\nThe border charges have in fact eased since 2023. A 100 percent surcharge on the customs duty, which applied to the imported beer, wine and spirits in 2022 and early 2023, has been absent from the tariff schedule since 2024, effectively halving the customs duty on the imported liquor.\n\nOn a 750ml bottle of 40 percent whisky, the local excise now works out to about Rs.2,391, nearly five times the roughly Rs.488 in excise on an imported bottle. The imported bottle also pays about Rs.2,063 in customs duty, down from about Rs.4,125 while the surcharge applied.\n\nAccording to the industry data, a 750ml bottle of imported whisky now sells for about Rs.8,000, down from Rs.11,500 in 2022, while a local premium whisky has risen to about Rs.5,500, from Rs.3,850. That has narrowed the price gap between the two by about two-thirds.\n\nThe lapse of the surcharge and a firmer rupee help explain the fall. The currency depreciated 44.8 percent against the US dollar in 2022, before recovering to Rs.332.61 by end-October 2026, from around Rs.363 at end-2022, as per the Central Bank data, although it has weakened by 6.8 percent this year. The industry estimates nevertheless warn that the imported spirits such as whisky and gin could soon become cheaper than the locally made whisky."
