    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "The lapse of the surcharge and a firmer rupee help explain the fall. The currency depreciated 44.8 percent against the US dollar in 2022, before recovering to Rs.332.61 by end-October 2026, from around Rs.363 at end-2022, as per the Central Bank data, although it has weakened by 6.8 percent this year. The industry estimates nevertheless warn that the imported spirits such as whisky and gin could soon become cheaper than the locally made whisky.\n\nThe three levies accounted for about 17 percent of Rs.1,528 billion collected by Customs in the first seven months of 2026, as per the Treasury data.\n\nThe IMF urged the MTRS to assess bringing the imported and local alcohol onto a more neutral rate basis, while the MTRS objectives pledge a review of alcohol excise and appropriate indexation of specific rates.\n\nThe local manufacturers have pressed the Treasury since January to raise the import levies in line with the local excise increases, Mirror Business learns. Such a move would lift the excise on the imported whisky to about Rs.1,248 per bulk litre and on the imported beer to about Rs.254.\n\nMeanwhile, Sri Lanka Excise expects the licensed liquor production to return to around its 2022 high of 43.3 million absolute litres this year, crediting a reduction in the illegal products. The excise duty on liquor rose 27 percent to Rs.137.5 billion in the first half of 2026, from Rs.108.2 billion a year earlier, as per the Treasury data.\n\nThe IMF also noted that splitting responsibility between the Excise and Customs departs from common international practice, where \u201ccustoms generally administer excise on imports\u201d and proposed a time-bound review of a possible the customs and excise model. An industry expert similarly noted that the imported alcohol would ideally be taxed entirely through customs."
