    "chunk_size_bytes": 1666,
    "chunk_text": "US Midterm Polls: From historic firsts to meaningful governance\n\nThe 2026 US midterm polls held on November 3 produced a series of historic firsts across the country. Women and candidates in minorities broke longstanding barriers in races for governor and Congress flipping seats in Republican strongholds.\n\nKeisha Lance Bottoms became the first Black woman governor in US history defeating Republican businessman Rick Jackson in Georgia. Former US Interior Secretary Deb Haaland became the first Native American woman to secure a seat. In 2021, she made history as the first Native American woman to serve as a US cabinet secretary. In another historic first, Abdul El-Sayed won the Michigan State race becoming the first Muslim senator while Adam Hamawy became New Jersey\u2019s first Muslim member of Congress. Gina Hinojosa became the first Latina governor in Texas,\u00a0 defeating Republican incumbent Greg Abbott.\n\nDuring the 2024 Parliamentary Elections, Ambika Samuel became the first Tamil politician representing the Malaiyaha community to be elected to Parliament. National People\u2019s Power candidate Sugath Wasantha De Silva was elected as the country\u2019s first visually impaired Member of Parliament.\n\nIt would be interesting to have members of indigenous communities for instance being represented in Parliament. The 54th New Zealand Parliament included a record 33 M\u0101ori MPs. Ahead of their upcoming election on November 7, the spotlight is now focused on seven M\u0101ori electorates.\n\nIn India,\u00a0 out of 543 elected seats in Lokh Sabha, 47 seats are strictly reserved for candidates belonging to Scheduled Tribes under Article 330 of the Indian Constitution."
  },
