    "chunk_size_bytes": 1492,
    "chunk_text": "It would be interesting to have members of indigenous communities for instance being represented in Parliament. The 54th New Zealand Parliament included a record 33 M\u0101ori MPs. Ahead of their upcoming election on November 7, the spotlight is now focused on seven M\u0101ori electorates.\n\nIn India,\u00a0 out of 543 elected seats in Lokh Sabha, 47 seats are strictly reserved for candidates belonging to Scheduled Tribes under Article 330 of the Indian Constitution.\n\nEmbracing Sri Lanka\u2019s pluralistic identity fosters cross-cultural understanding, encouraging communities that rarely interact to,\u00a0 listen to, learn from, and respect one another\u2019s perspectives. Inclusivity also leads to better decision-making.\n\nThe 2024 Parliamentary Election saw a record high of 22 female MPs elected to Parliament. But even though it is a record high, this number remains well below the global average. As of August 1, the global average of women in parliaments is 27.4% across all chambers. Sri Lanka legally mandates a 25% quota for women in local government authorities under the Local Authorities Elections (Amendment) Act No. 16 of 2017. This increased female representation in local councils from about 2% to nearly 23%.\n\nBut some of the more difficult questions and challenges arise once these representatives are elected to Parliament. Women face structural challenges when working alongside their male counterparts. Women candidates complain that they are not given a space to speak during meetings."
