    "md5": "87b3e1804918ce1c777fdad6dea65182",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1501,
    "chunk_text": "ECT delay - a textbook case of stranded capital\n\nYour excellent analysis on \u201cWhy Colombo\u2019s East Container Terminal (ECT) Still Isn\u2019t Working\u201d exposes how poor design, fragmented procurement and weak governance turned a strategic national asset into stranded capital.\n\nThe facts are damning. The first phase of ECT was completed in mid-2015, yet after 11 years, it still does not operate as intended. The President himself revealed at the Economic Summit that it handles only 500,000 TEUs against a built capacity of 3.2 million TEUs. In other words, we use only 15% of what we built with public money.\n\nThe reason is almost comic if it were not tragic. Four tenders for straddle carriers failed. We are now on a fifth tender for 15 machines, and even after award,\u00a0 delivery takes 10-12 months. The promised date of\u00a0 December 14, 2026 is therefore only for partial operation, with full operation unlikely before July 2027. Meanwhile,\u00a0 the contractor claims Rs. 4,227 million for 548 days of delay caused by our own decisions -- money the SLPA, and ultimately the taxpayer, must pay.\n\nThe President\u2019s proposal to retain state ownership but give management to private shipping lines for efficiency is pragmatic. It is regrettable that port unions immediately oppose any private participation, even as the port bleeds revenue. National interest must prevail over vested interests.\n\nIf we cannot operate a terminal on the world\u2019s busiest shipping lane, how will we achieve export-led growth?"
