    "md5": "dcadf63d0d9837db61c36991b3f9f5e5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "UNP-SJB coalition efforts \u2013 going nowhere?\n\n(Left) Ranil Wickremesinghe (right) Sajith Premadasa -- Setting aside ego issues, the leaders of both sides have strong grounds to claim the right to this joint leadership\n\nThe hopes of a UNP-SJB coalition re-emerged when campaign work commenced for the local government election held in May last year. However, it was not pushed forward due to the stubbornness of the party leaders\n\nSome media houses headlined the story stating that a UNP-SJB alliance cannot guarantee electoral success, while others framed it as meaning an alliance \u2018alone\u2019 merely cannot guarantee victory\n\nFor nearly two years, supporters of both the United National Party (UNP) and the Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) have been eagerly anticipating the formation of an alliance between the two parties.\n\nIn this context,\u00a0 remarks made over the weekend by UNP Leader and former President Ranil Wickremesinghe regarding alliance-building would have undoubtedly left the supporters of both parties stunned and disappointed.\n\nHe stated that a United National Party (UNP) and Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) alliance does not spell victory and emphasised that to win the next election, they must win over the 8 million young generation voters known as Gen Z and Millennials. (Generations are commonly categorised based on their birth years. Accordingly, those born between 1981 and 1996 are called the Millennial generation, while those born between 1997 and 2012 are referred to as Gen Z.)\u00a0 Had he stopped there, the UNP and SJB supporters waiting for a coalition would not have been confused. It is a fact that simply uniting the two parties is insufficient and that winning over the youth is vital. Electoral victory would be a cakewalk if a party or an alliance gets at least 7 million out of 17.5 million registered votes. There is no deep political philosophy in it."
  },
