    "md5": "9372675d32232c172b8d2c3df7adee08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1616,
    "chunk_text": "He stated that a United National Party (UNP) and Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) alliance does not spell victory and emphasised that to win the next election, they must win over the 8 million young generation voters known as Gen Z and Millennials. (Generations are commonly categorised based on their birth years. Accordingly, those born between 1981 and 1996 are called the Millennial generation, while those born between 1997 and 2012 are referred to as Gen Z.)\u00a0 Had he stopped there, the UNP and SJB supporters waiting for a coalition would not have been confused. It is a fact that simply uniting the two parties is insufficient and that winning over the youth is vital. Electoral victory would be a cakewalk if a party or an alliance gets at least 7 million out of 17.5 million registered votes. There is no deep political philosophy in it.\n\nAlmost all print media outlets highlighted this point of his speech on Monday. They seemed to have viewed this as a death blow to coalition talks. Some media houses headlined the story stating that a UNP-SJB alliance cannot guarantee electoral success, while others framed it as meaning an alliance \u2018alone\u2019 merely cannot guarantee victory.\n\nIt is possible that the UNP dispatched this speech to all the media outlets under a uniform headline. If so, the question arises as to why the party wanted to emphasise this point. Furthermore, looking at Wickremesinghe\u2019s continuous focus on building up his own party rather than forming a coalition, it becomes clear that the UNP has either abandoned joining forces with the SJB or is no longer seriously bothered about it."
  },
