    "md5": "13c1da15eefd5b8fcb7fc687f34c7ea9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "It is possible that the UNP dispatched this speech to all the media outlets under a uniform headline. If so, the question arises as to why the party wanted to emphasise this point. Furthermore, looking at Wickremesinghe\u2019s continuous focus on building up his own party rather than forming a coalition, it becomes clear that the UNP has either abandoned joining forces with the SJB or is no longer seriously bothered about it.\n\nTherefore, Wickremesinghe is implicitly suggesting that public support still remains with the National People\u2019s Power (NPP). Also, by stating that victory is impossible without winning over the younger generation, he is subtly conceding that this demographic also firmly supports the NPP. This observation inadvertently boosts the morale of the ruling party.\n\nIn the Presidential Election held in September 2024, Anura Kumara Dissanayake who won the race secured 5,634,915 votes, Sajith Premadasa obtained 4,363,035 votes, and Ranil Wickremesinghe received 2,299,767 votes. It is evident that if Sajith and Ranil had contested under a common symbol, one of them would have been elected President. This is exactly why the idea of the two parties functioning together arose among their respective supporters right after the Presidential election.\n\nYet, the parties failed to unite even for the General Election held two months later in November 2024. In that election, the votes secured by the NPP under Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s leadership surged to 6,863,186. Consequently, by contesting independently, the NPP achieved the prestigious feat of winning a two-thirds majority without any alliance. Even if the UNP and SJB had formed a coalition to contest the General Election, the psychological impact of the Presidential Election results might still have carried the NPP to victory.\n\nHowever, following the crushing defeat suffered by both parties at the general election, the idea of building an alliance faded from the minds of their supporters for some time."
