    "chunk_size_bytes": 1594,
    "chunk_text": "Yet, the parties failed to unite even for the General Election held two months later in November 2024. In that election, the votes secured by the NPP under Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s leadership surged to 6,863,186. Consequently, by contesting independently, the NPP achieved the prestigious feat of winning a two-thirds majority without any alliance. Even if the UNP and SJB had formed a coalition to contest the General Election, the psychological impact of the Presidential Election results might still have carried the NPP to victory.\n\nHowever, following the crushing defeat suffered by both parties at the general election, the idea of building an alliance faded from the minds of their supporters for some time.\n\nMany arrangements were made to hold talks between the two factions, but both leaders showed very little interest on those occasions. At times, both parties even appointed committees to conduct negotiations, but we have no recollection of these committees proceeding past a single round of talks.\n\nThe primary obstacle here is the question of who will lead if the parties merge. Setting aside ego issues, the leaders of both sides have strong grounds to claim the right to this joint leadership. Ranil Wickremesinghe has been in politics for over five decades. He has served as the country\u2019s Prime Minister five times\u2014in 1994, 2001, 2015, 2018, and 2022. He also served as the President from 2022 to 2024. Therefore, it is not entirely unreasonable for Ranil to dislike operating under Sajith Premadasa, who functioned under his leadership in the UNP for nearly 20 years."
