    "chunk_text": "The primary obstacle here is the question of who will lead if the parties merge. Setting aside ego issues, the leaders of both sides have strong grounds to claim the right to this joint leadership. Ranil Wickremesinghe has been in politics for over five decades. He has served as the country\u2019s Prime Minister five times\u2014in 1994, 2001, 2015, 2018, and 2022. He also served as the President from 2022 to 2024. Therefore, it is not entirely unreasonable for Ranil to dislike operating under Sajith Premadasa, who functioned under his leadership in the UNP for nearly 20 years.\n\nOn October 27 last year, opposition parties held a rally in Nugegoda under the slogan \u201cMaha Jana Handa\u201d (The People\u2019s Voice), which the SJB did not attend. On March 11 this year, opposition factions met to formulate a common programme against the government, and the SJB stayed away from that as well. Since other opposition parties lack the public backing that the SJB enjoys, its leaders\u2014particularly Sajith Premadasa\u2014likely suspect that those parties are merely trying to piggyback on the SJB\u2019s popular support.\n\nTherefore, even if Ranil Wickremesinghe desires it, the SJB does not seem to align with him. Even if they do unite, the leadership dispute will certainly remain unresolved. In such a scenario, the UNP is entirely justified in choosing to chart its political journey alone."
