    "chunk_text": "From discovery to the patient Role of a Pharmacist in the journey of a medicine\n\nOn World Pharmacists Day 2026, the SLCPP urges the public to recognise the broad expertise of pharmacists.\n\nBy Dr. Uthpali Mannapperuma, Dr. Abarna Nadeshkumar and Dr. Savini Senadheera\n\nThe Sri Lanka College of Pharmacy Professionals (SLCPP) brings together pharmacists with postgraduate qualifications working across different areas of the profession. As we celebrated the World Pharmacists Day recently, the SLCPP wishes to take readers along the journey of a medicine - from its early discovery and development to its eventual use by patients by highlighting the many roles pharmacists play along the way.\n\nA pharmacist is much more than the person behind the pharmacy counter. Internationally, pharmacists are recognised as scientifically trained healthcare professionals with expertise that contributes to the safe and effective use of medicines.\n\nPharmacists work in many areas beyond the traditional roles that the public may be most familiar with. Some pharmacists build careers as pharmaceutical scientists, contributing to research, development, production and quality of medicines.\n\nTo a patient holding a tablet in the palm of their hand, it may simply be a small tablet taken once or twice a day. The journey of that tablet began years before it reached the patient. Along that journey, pharmacists contribute at many different stages, starting from discovering a potential medicine to developing and manufacturing it, checking its quality, contributing to its regulation, ensuring it reaches hospitals and pharmacies safely, and finally helping the patient to use it correctly."
