    "chunk_size_bytes": 1184,
    "chunk_text": "To a patient holding a tablet in the palm of their hand, it may simply be a small tablet taken once or twice a day. The journey of that tablet began years before it reached the patient. Along that journey, pharmacists contribute at many different stages, starting from discovering a potential medicine to developing and manufacturing it, checking its quality, contributing to its regulation, ensuring it reaches hospitals and pharmacies safely, and finally helping the patient to use it correctly.\n\nEven when research finds a new chemical compound that works, the next challenge is turning this promising compound into a form that can be used effectively and safely. The tablet that eventually reaches a patient\u2019s hand is not simply a chemical compound pressed into a desirable shape. Pharmacists help to decide whether the new chemical compound should become a tablet, capsule, syrup, injection, inhaler, cream or another type of product. The decision is shaped by considerations such as how quickly the medicine should be released, which other ingredients are needed to make the product stable and usable, and what type of packaging will protect it from the environment around it."
