    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "Even when research finds a new chemical compound that works, the next challenge is turning this promising compound into a form that can be used effectively and safely. The tablet that eventually reaches a patient\u2019s hand is not simply a chemical compound pressed into a desirable shape. Pharmacists help to decide whether the new chemical compound should become a tablet, capsule, syrup, injection, inhaler, cream or another type of product. The decision is shaped by considerations such as how quickly the medicine should be released, which other ingredients are needed to make the product stable and usable, and what type of packaging will protect it from the environment around it.\n\nThe next stage takes the medicine from development into large-scale manufacturing. Producing millions of tablets creates the challenge of being able to manufacture the same pharmaceutical product consistently to meet the required quality standards. Pharmacists working in the pharmaceutical industry who are involved in production, quality assurance, quality control testing and stability studies ensure that the medicine contains the medicinal active ingredient at the correct strength and continues to meet quality requirements throughout its shelf life.This is why \u201c100 mg\u201d on a medicine label is more than a printed number. It is a claim that must be supported by manufacturing controls and testing.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, the National Medicines Regulatory Authority (NMRA) provides regulatory oversight of medicines, including functions related to assessment, manufacturing oversight, and monitoring after medicines enter the market. Pharmacists working in regulatory settings apply their knowledge when technical information is evaluated, helping connect the scientific evidence provided about a medicine being assessed with the needs of the public in mind. Regulatory pharmacists safeguard medicines available to patients to ensure they meet required standards of quality, safety and effectiveness."
