    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka, the National Medicines Regulatory Authority (NMRA) provides regulatory oversight of medicines, including functions related to assessment, manufacturing oversight, and monitoring after medicines enter the market. Pharmacists working in regulatory settings apply their knowledge when technical information is evaluated, helping connect the scientific evidence provided about a medicine being assessed with the needs of the public in mind. Regulatory pharmacists safeguard medicines available to patients to ensure they meet required standards of quality, safety and effectiveness.\n\nEventually, the medicine reaches the pharmacy counter. Professional dispensing by a pharmacist involves more than selecting the correct medicine and handing it over. Pharmacists consider whether the medicines, doses and instructions in the prescriptions are appropriate for the individual patient based on factors such as age, allergies, other illnesses and other medicines being taken. Some patients may receive medicines from several clinics and sources. Pharmacists involved in clinical services can look at the patient\u2019s medicines, helping to identify possible interactions, duplicated treatment, unsuitable doses and other medicine-related problems.\n\nThe way a medicine is used matters too. If correct instructions are not provided, the medicine might not work as intended. An inhaler will not work properly if the patient does not know how to use it. Certain tablets should not be crushed because it changes the way the medicine is released.Pharmacists in hospitals, community pharmacies, industry and regulatory agencies contribute to correct use of medicines and also recognise and report suspected adverse reactions and other medicine-related problems."
