    "md5": "10fa50c68c03d16ffaebfea8b88d4e00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "Over half of Sri Lankans cannot independently use a computer\n\nColombo, Nov. 5 (Daily Mirror) - Nearly 62 per cent of Sri Lankans aged between five and 69 are unable to independently use a computer, despite the growing use of digital technology, the latest government survey has revealed.\n\nAccording to the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS), only 38.1 per cent of the population in this age group are computer literate.\n\nThe Computer Literacy Statistics Bulletin for the first six months of 2026 found that although only 38.1 per cent of Sri Lankans are computer literate, 72.5 per cent have digital literacy, suggesting that many people can independently use smartphones and other digital devices but not necessarily computers.\"\n\nThe survey revealed a slight gap between male and female computer literacy, with 38.9 per cent of males and 37.3 per cent of females able to use a computer independently.\n\nYoung people aged between 15 and 19 recorded the highest computer literacy rate at 75.2 per cent, followed by those in the 20\u201324 age group at 71.2 per cent. However, the rate dropped sharply among older people, with only 18 per cent of those aged 50\u201359 and 9.2 per cent of those aged 60\u201369 being computer literate.\n\nEducation was also found to have a significant influence on computer literacy. Those with G.C.E. Advanced Level qualifications or higher recorded the highest rate at 77.7 per cent, while only 1.5 per cent of those with no schooling were computer literate.\n\nThe survey also found that computer literacy was considerably higher among people who could read and write English, with 74.7 per cent of this group able to use a computer independently.\n\nMeanwhile, only 20.8 per cent of households in Sri Lanka owned a desktop or laptop computer, meaning that more than four out of every five households did not have either device.\n\nComputer ownership was highest in the urban sector at 36.2 per cent, compared to 18.6 per cent in rural areas and just 2.2 per cent in estate areas."
