    "md5": "66016e209dc35313f4b6b929ff266b18",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, only 20.8 per cent of households in Sri Lanka owned a desktop or laptop computer, meaning that more than four out of every five households did not have either device.\n\nComputer ownership was highest in the urban sector at 36.2 per cent, compared to 18.6 per cent in rural areas and just 2.2 per cent in estate areas.\n\nThe urban sector also recorded the highest computer literacy rate at 51.6 per cent, followed by the rural sector at 36.3 per cent. The estate sector recorded the lowest rate at 15.8 per cent.\n\nAmong the provinces, the Western Province had the highest computer literacy rate at 47.6 per cent, while the North Central Province recorded the lowest at 27.5 per cent.\n\nDespite the relatively low computer literacy rate, internet usage has continued to increase, with 63.9 per cent of the population aged between five and 69 using the internet at least once during the preceding 12 months. This was an increase from 60.4 per cent in 2025.\n\nPeople aged between 20 and 24 recorded the highest internet usage at 91.8 per cent, followed by those aged 25\u201329 at 90.6 per cent.\n\nHowever, only 21.9 per cent of the population used email during the same 12-month period.\n\nThe findings also highlighted the growing reliance on smartphones to access the internet and email. Smartphones accounted for 80.9 per cent of devices used for these purposes, while desktop and laptop computers accounted for 17.1 per cent.\n\nSchools and universities were identified as the main sources of computer knowledge, accounting for 63.5 per cent of computer-literate respondents. Self-learning was the second most common source at 41 per cent, followed by family members at 28.1 per cent.\n\nThe survey was conducted between January and June 2026, covering a nationally distributed sample of 12,500 households and individuals aged between five and 69."
