    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e400940703b9a90d202195fc6c451844",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "UN reports no accountability in 90% of journalist deaths\n\nSince 1993, more than 1,700 journalists have been killed while reporting, the UN says. UNESCO\u2019s most recent data puts the global \u201cimpunity rate\u201d in these cases at 85%\n\nAccording to the UN, in roughly nine out of every ten journalist killings worldwide, nobody is held accountable. The \u201cInternational Day to End Impunity for Crimes against Journalists\u201d, observed every year on November 2, is meant to stop such impunity and indifference on the part of the State and also Society.\n\nThe day commemorates the murder of two French radio journalists, Ghislaine Dupont and Claude Verlon, in Mali on November 2, 2013 when they were reporting for their station RFI.\n\nSince 1993, more than 1,700 journalists have been killed while reporting, the UN says. UNESCO\u2019s most recent data puts the global \u201cimpunity rate\u201d in these cases at 85%.\n\n\u201cKillings represent only the most extreme form of pressure against journalists. The International Day also draws attention to kidnapping, torture, harassment, and a growing wave of digital-sphere threats that media workers increasingly face, particularly women journalists,\u201d the UN points out.\n\nWhen attacks on journalists go unpunished, public-interest reporting is suppressed, including investigations into corruption, human rights abuses and crime.\n\nIn recent years, declining press freedom worldwide has exposed journalists to growing threats, including escalating attacks, judicial harassment through lawsuits, arbitrary detention, intimidation and killings, making independent journalism increasingly dangerous.\n\nWhile killings are the most extreme form of media censorship, journalists are also subjected to a variety of threats - ranging from kidnapping, torture and other physical attacks to harassment, particularly in the digital sphere."
