    "chunk_text": "While killings are the most extreme form of media censorship, journalists are also subjected to a variety of threats - ranging from kidnapping, torture and other physical attacks to harassment, particularly in the digital sphere.\n\nAccording to UNESCO\u2019s discussion paper, \u201cThe Chilling: Global Trends in Online Violence against Women Journalists,\u201d 73% of the women journalists surveyed said they had been threatened, intimidated and insulted online in connection with their work.\n\nThe Arab States had the highest number of murders of journalists, according to the 2026 UNESCO Director-General\u2019s Report on the \u201cSafety of Journalists and the Danger of Impunity.\u201d\u00a0 The Palestinian-American woman correspondent, Ms. Abu Akleh, was fatally shot in the head on May 11, 2022 by an Israeli soldier while wearing a \u201cPress\u201d vest as she covered an Israeli army operation in the West Bank town of Jenin. An investigation by Palestinian human rights organisation Al-Haq and research agency Forensic Architecture, found that she was directly targeted, despite repeated denials by Israeli authorities.\n\nHer killing prompted the Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ) to investigate Israel\u2019s killings of journalists over 22 years. In its 2023 report \u201cDeadly Pattern\u201d it said that from 2001 to 2022, the Israel Defence Forces (IDF) killed at least 20 journalists, with no accountability for any of the deaths.\n\nJavier Valdez Cardenas, a veteran Mexican investigative journalist and editor who reported on organised crime and corruption \u2013 despite threats and attacks against him and his colleagues, was shot\u00a0 \u00a012 times on May 15, 2017, near the editorial offices of Riodoce, the weekly he co-founded, in the Mexican city of Culiac\u00e1n. A Sinaloa drug cartel member ordered Valdez\u2019s murder over his reporting on a cartel turf war, prosecutors said."
