    "chunk_text": "Javier Valdez Cardenas, a veteran Mexican investigative journalist and editor who reported on organised crime and corruption \u2013 despite threats and attacks against him and his colleagues, was shot\u00a0 \u00a012 times on May 15, 2017, near the editorial offices of Riodoce, the weekly he co-founded, in the Mexican city of Culiac\u00e1n. A Sinaloa drug cartel member ordered Valdez\u2019s murder over his reporting on a cartel turf war, prosecutors said.\n\nAccording to CPJ, despite government\u2019s promises, unsolved journalist murders remain persistent in Pakistan. Since 1992, not a single journalist murder case in Pakistan has been met with full justice.\n\nZakir Husein Dahar, also known as Shan Dahar,\u00a0 a senior journalist with Abb Takk Television was shot on January 1, 2014, in Larkana district of Sindh province. At the time of his death, he was investigating the illegal resale of donated medicines. The sole suspect prosecuted in the case was acquitted in 2018. Two other suspects, Brohi and his brother Aamir, were declared absconders. Brohi was arrested this year.\n\nIn the evening of 18 December 2025, panic rippled through the newsrooms of The Daily Star and Prothom Alo, two of Bangladesh\u2019s most popular media outlets. Mobs armed with sticks and petrol torches forced their way past the gated buildings in Dhaka. They set fire to vehicles and pushed towards the main press buildings. Inside, journalists and support staff scrambled in the smoke and heat, trying to secure files and save equipment as the blaze grew.\n\nFor the first time in decades, the country\u2019s two largest newspapers were forced to stop printing. Outside, the crowd cheered. Inside, reporters huddled on the rooftop, fearing for their lives. Observers accused the interim government led by Dr. Muhammad Yunus\u00a0 of allowing it to happen."
