    "chunk_text": "For the first time in decades, the country\u2019s two largest newspapers were forced to stop printing. Outside, the crowd cheered. Inside, reporters huddled on the rooftop, fearing for their lives. Observers accused the interim government led by Dr. Muhammad Yunus\u00a0 of allowing it to happen.\n\nUnder the interim government led by Nobel Peace Prize laureate Muhammad Yunus, the threat was less from official censorship and more about mob intimidation. Journalists reported that if a newspaper published something unpopular, crowds quickly appeared with sticks, fire, or threats.\n\n351 journalists were harassed in their offices or on the street; 109 journalists beaten while reporting; 167 journalists stripped of press cards; More than 300 journalists were been banned from leaving the country; 140 journalists faced criminal cases, including murder.\n\nCriminal cases against journalists had shot up by 558% during the interim government days. Murder charges were particularly worrying. Many reporters were simply covering the summer 2024 protests where people were killed, largely by Hasina\u2019s security forces. But reporters who happened to be on the scene were blamed for those deaths.\n\nPresently, under the Tarique Rahman government, Bangladeshi journalists face 10-year jail terms for \u2018unverified\u2019 reporting under draft cybercrime law. In September 2026, the CPJ asked the government to immediately withdraw or substantially revise proposed amendments to the Cyber Protection Act that would criminalise reporting officials deem unverified, and grant authorities the power to block journalists\u2019 work and deregister news outlets."
