    "chunk_id": "2026-11-05-dailymirrorlk-d4001da3-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "026aaf7e0755ea18c2028d63be3178ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "Dignity at work: Confronting the harassment of women in Sri Lanka\u2019s workplaces\n\nRatifying an international convention an important step; protecting the women who keep our economy running now demands a law, a culture and the will to enforce both\n\nVery few stories ever reach a formal complaint, let alone a courtroom. The reasons are well known\n\nWomen fear losing their jobs, particularly in sectors where employment is precarious.\n\nSilence is often mistaken for the absence of a problem. It is nothing of the sort. Where reporting channels are weak, where the alleged harasser is a supervisor with power over pay and promotion, and where the culture of an institution tolerates such conduct, silence is the rational response of a person with everything to lose\n\nA criminal prosecution, with its high standard of proof and its long delays, is rarely the remedy a frightened employee needs\n\nEvery morning, hundreds of thousands of Sri Lankan women set out for work. They board crowded buses to garment factories in Katunayake and Biyagama, take their places at bank counters and hospital wards, teach in our schools, pluck tea on our estates and staff the offices of both the public and private sectors. They are, without exaggeration, the backbone of our economy. Yet, far too many of them carry an additional burden that their male colleagues seldom share, which is the fear, and too often the reality, of harassment at the very place where they go to earn an honest living.\n\nWorkplace harassment is not a minor matter of bad manners. It ranges from unwelcome remarks, persistent advances and humiliating \u2018jokes\u2019 to intimidation, demands for sexual favours in return for promotion or job security, and, at its worst, physical assault. Its consequences are serious as they range from lost confidence, anxiety and depression, ruined careers, and for many women, the decision simply to leave work altogether. When that happens, the loss is not hers alone; it is the nation\u2019s."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
