    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4165f804b103cebda53693d31ee9ca8d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1941,
    "chunk_text": "Workplace harassment is not a minor matter of bad manners. It ranges from unwelcome remarks, persistent advances and humiliating \u2018jokes\u2019 to intimidation, demands for sexual favours in return for promotion or job security, and, at its worst, physical assault. Its consequences are serious as they range from lost confidence, anxiety and depression, ruined careers, and for many women, the decision simply to leave work altogether. When that happens, the loss is not hers alone; it is the nation\u2019s.\n\nThis silence is often mistaken for the absence of a problem. It is nothing of the sort. Where reporting channels are weak, where the alleged harasser is a supervisor with power over pay and promotion, and where the culture of an institution tolerates such conduct, silence is the rational response of a person with everything to lose. Our task, as legislators, employers and citizens, is to change that calculation.\n\nIt would be wrong to say that Sri Lanka has done nothing. Section 345 of the Penal Code, as amended by Act No. 22 of 1995, made sexual harassment a criminal offence, including harassment committed in the workplace. Our Supreme Court has, in several fundamental rights cases, recognised that sexual harassment at work violates a woman\u2019s constitutional right to equality, and that employers, including the State, carry obligations to prevent it and to respond properly when it occurs. These are important foundations, and the judges who laid them deserve credit.\n\nEarlier this year, Sri Lanka also ratified the International Labour Organisation\u2019s (ILO\u2019s) Violence and Harassment Convention, 2019 (No. 190), the first international treaty dedicated to eliminating violence and harassment, including gender-based harassment, in the world of work. In doing so, the Government committed itself to introducing the domestic legal provisions needed to give the Convention effect. I welcome that ratification wholeheartedly."
  },
