    "md5": "b4f3ef6e342e182d849d06e5fa43bff5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1545,
    "chunk_text": "We need not look to distant shores for answers. Several of our Asian neighbours, with legal traditions and social pressures not unlike our own, have already travelled this road. Their experience offers both encouragement and caution.\n\nPakistan enacted its Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act in 2010 and, notably, established a Federal Ombudsperson to whom women may complain directly, alongside inquiry committees within organisations. In 2022, Parliament widened the law considerably, extending it to the informal sector, to domestic workers and to students, and broadening the definition of harassment to include discrimination based on gender, whether or not it is sexual in nature. An independent, specialised ombudsperson gives a woman an alternative to an internal process she may not trust; that is a model worth our close attention.\n\nThe Philippines has gone further still in defining employers\u2019 duties. Its Safe Spaces Act of 2019 requires employers to adopt a code of conduct prohibiting gender-based sexual harassment, developed in consultation with workers, and to establish an independent committee to investigate complaints. That committee must be headed by a woman, at least half of its members must be women, and it must resolve complaints within 10 days, with due process. The Act recognises that harassment may come from peers and even subordinates, not only from superiors, and that it may take place through text messages and email. Employers who fail in these duties may themselves be held liable."
  },
