    "chunk_size_bytes": 1584,
    "chunk_text": "The Philippines has gone further still in defining employers\u2019 duties. Its Safe Spaces Act of 2019 requires employers to adopt a code of conduct prohibiting gender-based sexual harassment, developed in consultation with workers, and to establish an independent committee to investigate complaints. That committee must be headed by a woman, at least half of its members must be women, and it must resolve complaints within 10 days, with due process. The Act recognises that harassment may come from peers and even subordinates, not only from superiors, and that it may take place through text messages and email. Employers who fail in these duties may themselves be held liable.\n\nElsewhere in the region, Japan has for many years required employers to take preventive measures against sexual harassment, extended that duty to so-called \u2018power harassment\u2019 through amendments passed in 2019, and, since October 2026, also obliges employers to protect staff from harassment by customers and to prevent the harassment of job applicants.\n\nSingapore\u2019s Workplace Fairness Act, passed in 2025 and to be enacted end of 2027, will require employers to put in place proper grievance-handling processes for harassment and discrimination, to keep complainants\u2019 identities confidential as far as possible and to refrain from any retaliation against those who come forward.\n\nAlso in South Asia, Bangladesh\u2019s High Court issued binding guidelines in 2009 requiring complaints committees in workplaces and educational institutions, an example of judicial leadership filling a legislative gap."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-05-dailymirrorlk-d4001da3",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d4001da3",
    "date_str": "2026-11-05",
    "description": "Dignity at work: Confronting the harassment of women in Sri Lanka\u2019s workplaces",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Dignity-at-work-Confronting-the-harassment-of-women-in-Sri-Lankas-workplaces/131-354232",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793860740.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-05-dailymirrorlk-d4001da3-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a2487f624c9a704ba10b1c3be2083d44",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Also in South Asia, Bangladesh\u2019s High Court issued binding guidelines in 2009 requiring complaints committees in workplaces and educational institutions, an example of judicial leadership filling a legislative gap.\n\nFirst, Parliament should enact a dedicated Workplace Anti-Harassment Act, aligned fully with Convention No. 190. Such a law should define harassment clearly, apply to all workers in both the formal and informal sectors, and cover not only the office or factory floor but also work-related travel, accommodation, commuting and online communication.\n\nSecond, every employer above a reasonable size should be legally required to adopt a written anti-harassment policy, to establish an internal complaints committee that includes women and at least one independent member, and to investigate complaints within fixed time limits. Smaller employers and informal workers should have access to an external mechanism at the district level, as India\u2019s Local Committees provide, so that no woman is left without a door to knock on. Alongside this, we should create an independent, low-cost forum, whether an ombudsperson on the Pakistani model or a specialised tribunal on the Malaysian one, where complaints can be heard swiftly and without the expense of lawyers.\n\nThird, complainants and witnesses must be protected from retaliation. A woman who reports harassment and is then transferred, demoted or dismissed has been punished twice. The law should presume that adverse action taken against a complainant within a defined period is retaliatory unless the employer can prove otherwise.\n\nFourth, remedies must be meaningful. That means compensation for victims, proportionate disciplinary consequences for offenders and real penalties for employers who ignore their duties. A law without enforcement is a decoration, not a safeguard."
  },
  {
