    "language": "en",
    "md5": "68134f6eb8144f75cf4ce89e3586358f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "Fourth, remedies must be meaningful. That means compensation for victims, proportionate disciplinary consequences for offenders and real penalties for employers who ignore their duties. A law without enforcement is a decoration, not a safeguard.\n\nIt is tempting to treat workplace harassment as a \u2018women\u2019s issue\u2019, to be left to women\u2019s organisations and gender desks. That would be a mistake. Harassment drives talented people out of the workforce, depresses productivity, breeds mistrust and exposes institutions to legal and reputational damage. At a time when Sri Lanka is working hard to rebuild its economy, we simply cannot afford to lose the skills and energy of women who would otherwise work, lead and innovate. Increasing women\u2019s participation in the labour force is an economic necessity, and safe workplaces are a precondition for it.\n\nMen, too, have a vital part to play. Most men are not harassers, but silence from decent colleagues allows a minority to behave with impunity. Every man who challenges an offensive remark, supports a colleague who comes forward or insists that his organisation take complaints seriously makes our workplaces safer for his mother, his sister, his wife and his daughter.\n\nAt its heart, this is about dignity. Our Constitution guarantees equality before the law and freedom from discrimination on the ground of sex. Those guarantees ring hollow if a woman must choose between her livelihood and her self-respect. No woman should have to endure humiliation as the unspoken price of a pay packet.\n\nWe have taken the first step by joining the international community in committing to a world of work free from violence and harassment. The next step is ours to take at home. I urge the Government to bring forward comprehensive legislation without delay, and I call on employers, trade unions and civil society to work together to make it effective in practice. I, for my part, will support every sincere effort to that end."
  },
