    "md5": "3533319ad345bb0051620ff0954c6fa5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1836,
    "chunk_text": "Two German tourists, local guide arrested over alleged wildlife smuggling\n\nColombo, Nov. 5 (Daily Mirror) - Two German tourists and a local tourist guide have been arrested for allegedly collecting and attempting to transport live wild animals and parts of animals from the Babawa Uddakandara Forest Reserve, wildlife officials said.\n\nThe arrests were made following a special operation by wildlife officers after receiving information on November 2 that a group of individuals was collecting reptiles and insects in the area.\n\nWildlife officers, posing as tourists, conducted night-time surveillance at several locations before identifying the two foreign nationals and their local guide.\n\nAlthough an initial search of the suspects and their campsite, known as 'Yala Namba', did not reveal any illegal items, officers became suspicious when the local guide directed them to a different room as his accommodation.\n\nAfter consulting the establishment's manager, officers searched the guide's actual room and recovered a large collection of suspected wildlife specimens and equipment.\n\nThe seized items included live pythons, reptiles, porcupine quills, insects, fungi, a cow's tooth, deer bones, dragonflies, peacock feathers, chemicals, containers used to preserve animal specimens and equipment used to capture snakes and other animals.\n\nThe suspects and the seized items were produced before the Tissamaharama Magistrate's Court on Tuesday (3).\n\nThe Magistrate ordered that the live animals be handed over to the Dehiwala Zoological Gardens, while the case was scheduled to be taken up again on November 6.\n\nThe arrests come amid concerns over foreign nationals engaging in biopiracy through the illegal collection and removal of Sri Lanka's wildlife and biological specimens in violation of the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance."
  },
