    "md5": "f4bad71cb245c8ffad04dede363af761",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza\n\nBBC - An Italian chef sacked from a Brisbane restaurant had refused to put pineapple on a pizza because it was an \"insult to his culture\".\n\nDavide Tamburella was fired from the now-closed La Costa restaurant in May, a fortnight after a meeting with management who had a string of complaints about the chef's behaviour.\n\nAmong them were Tamburella's refusal to provide ham and pineapple pizza for guests at a function. At the time, the chef had stated that any direction from management \"need not be followed when it is offensive to his culture\".\n\nThe details came to light after the chef went to Australia's Fair Work Commission claiming he had been unfairly dismissed.\n\nIn its ruling released on Tuesday, the commission noted that Tamburella's refusal to put pineapple on the pizzas was because \"he considers, as an Italian, that putting pineapple on a pizza is an insult to his culture\".\n\nIt also noted that it was the third time Tamburella had refused to use the fruit on pizzas in the past four years.\n\nHe had previously requested his boss to \"check how important it is for Italians not to put pineapple on a pizza\".\n\nThe state of Queensland, where the restaurant is located, is considered Australia's pineapple heartland and produces all of the country's commercially-grown pineapples.\n\nOn the Sunshine Coast, visitors regularly stop to take photos next to The Big Pineapple, a heritage-listed 16m-tall fibreglass model of the fruit, which has become a local landmark.\n\nOther concerns were raised about Tamburella using foul language, his communication style and \"escalation of frustration in the workplace\".\n\nThe ruling also detailed an incident at work in April this year where Tamburella fractured bones in his right hand when he struck a metal hand rail.\n\nTamburella had been hoping the restaurant would sponsor a visa for him that allows skilled workers to be permanent residents."
