    "md5": "2fe1793b7c3d23ef50b5aff2b150d39c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Why oil prices plunged 8%: US midterm setback fuels bets on Trump ending Iran war\n\nFirstpost - Oil prices plunged more than 8 per cent on Thursday after US President Donald Trump's Republican Party suffered a sweeping defeat in the midterm elections, with Democrats winning control of both chambers of Congress. The result prompted traders to reassess the prospects of a prolonged US-Israeli war against Iran, raising expectations that Washington could pursue a diplomatic settlement rather than escalate the conflict.\n\nUS benchmark West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures fell $7.97, or 8.3 per cent, to settle at $88.12 a barrel. Brent crude futures, the global benchmark, declined $8.98, or 8.7 per cent, to $94.62 a barrel. Both benchmarks recorded their lowest closing levels since August 31.\n\nThe sharp fall came as markets increasingly priced in the possibility that Trump's political setback could accelerate efforts to end the eight-month conflict. The war has disrupted energy supplies from the Gulf and pushed up global oil prices, raising fuel costs for consumers and businesses.\n\nThe immediate political trigger came from Vice President JD Vance, who publicly blamed the Iran war for the Republican Party's electoral defeat.\n\n\"The reason we lost is the war,\" Vance said in a social media post on Wednesday, adding that Trump himself had acknowledged the impact of the conflict.\n\nHis remarks added to expectations that the White House could face growing pressure to find an exit from the conflict. Trump also acknowledged that the war had contributed to the election setback. He had told the Financial Times on Tuesday that the US had no immediate plans to strike Iran.\n\nThe election outcome has changed the political calculations surrounding the conflict.\n\nDemocrats are set to take control of the House of Representatives and the Senate when the new Congress convenes. This could make it more difficult for Trump to secure political and financial support for an extended military campaign."
  },
