    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "Democrats are set to take control of the House of Representatives and the Senate when the new Congress convenes. This could make it more difficult for Trump to secure political and financial support for an extended military campaign.\n\nSenate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer has already indicated that his party intends to push for an end to the conflict.\n\nThis has given oil traders a reason to reconsider the possibility of further US military escalation.\n\nThe market had previously been factoring in the risk of a wider conflict involving Iran, Israel and other countries in the Gulf. Any further escalation could threaten oil production, exports and shipments through the strategically important Strait of Hormuz.\n\nThe possibility of a diplomatic settlement has now prompted traders to unwind some of the geopolitical risk premium built into crude prices.\n\nA geopolitical risk premium is the additional amount traders are willing to pay for oil because they fear that a conflict could disrupt supplies. When the likelihood of escalation declines, that premium can fall even before actual oil shipments return to normal.\n\nNot necessarily. While markets are responding positively to signs of a possible diplomatic opening, Washington and Tehran remain far from reaching an agreement.\n\nQatar has stepped up mediation efforts to prevent another round of military escalation following the US elections. Iranian officials have indicated that communication through mediators is continuing, but negotiations remain complicated.\n\nA senior Iranian official told Reuters that the two sides were still far from a deal to end the war.\n\nThere are also concerns about the terms of any potential settlement. An agreement that allows Iran to present the outcome as a victory, while leaving Trump politically weakened, could create fresh instability rather than end the conflict.\n\nThe sharp fall in prices also needs to be viewed against the continuing disruption to global energy flows."
