    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "There are also concerns about the terms of any potential settlement. An agreement that allows Iran to present the outcome as a victory, while leaving Trump politically weakened, could create fresh instability rather than end the conflict.\n\nThe sharp fall in prices also needs to be viewed against the continuing disruption to global energy flows.\n\nThis was lower than the 18.9 million barrels per day recorded in September and represented only around three-quarters of pre-war levels.\n\nThe Strait of Hormuz remains a critical factor in the global oil supply equation. The narrow waterway connects the Persian Gulf with international shipping routes and is an important passage for energy exports from major Gulf producers.\n\nAny disruption to tanker movements through the strait can affect the availability of crude oil, increase shipping and insurance costs and push up prices across international markets.\n\nThe United Arab Emirates is also considering the construction of a third oil pipeline to transport crude outside the Strait of Hormuz, according to a Bloomberg News report.\n\nThere is another development worth noting. US crude inventories unexpectedly declined last week, according to the Energy Information Administration.\n\nCrude stocks fell by 865,000 barrels to 409.8 million barrels in the week ended October 30. Analysts had expected inventories to increase by 877,000 barrels.\n\nNormally, falling inventories can support oil prices because they indicate tighter supplies. But the sharp decline in crude prices suggests that expectations of a possible diplomatic settlement have outweighed this near-term supportive signal.\n\nThe decline in international crude prices could offer significant relief to India, one of the world's largest oil importers.\n\nIndia imported 246.6 million tonnes of crude oil in the financial year 2025-26, with its crude oil import dependence reaching 88.7 per cent, according to data compiled from the Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC)."
