    "chunk_text": "India imported 246.6 million tonnes of crude oil in the financial year 2025-26, with its crude oil import dependence reaching 88.7 per cent, according to data compiled from the Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC).\n\nHigher crude prices increase India's import bill, put pressure on the rupee and can add to inflationary pressures through transportation, manufacturing and fuel costs.\n\nAccording to estimates cited by The Indian Express, India imports around 1.8 billion to 2 billion barrels of crude oil annually. Every $1 increase in the price of a barrel can add up to $2 billion to the country's annual oil import bill if sustained.\n\nA sustained decline in crude prices could therefore improve India's external trade position, ease pressure on the current account and provide some relief to domestic industries.\n\nHowever, a fall in international crude prices does not automatically translate into an immediate reduction in petrol and diesel prices. Domestic fuel prices also depend on the rupee-dollar exchange rate, refining and marketing costs, taxes and the pricing decisions of oil marketing companies."
