    "chunk_text": "Prominent German politician found dead in Berlin hotel room hours before speech\n\nINDEPENDENT - German police have launched an investigation after a senior politician was found dead in a hotel room in Berlin on Wednesday evening.\n\nAuthorities are probing whether a third party was involved in the death of Hubert Aiwanger, the deputy premier of the state of Bavaria and leader of a small conservative party.\n\nMr Aiwanger, 55, who served as the economy minister in Germany\u2019s largest state, was due to give a speech at the opening of an office for his Free Voters party, but missed the occasion because he felt unwell, German outlet Bild reported.\n\n\"The aim of the inquest is to establish the specific cause of death and the circumstances surrounding it, and to rule out any possible third-party culpability,\" Berlin police and prosecutors said in a statement, giving no further details.\n\n\"The sudden death of Hubert Aiwanger is a terrible tragedy. We are all deeply shocked,\" Bavarian Premier Markus Soeder said in a statement published on the state government website.\n\n\u201cOur thoughts are first and foremost with his family and his children. We mourn with them and pray for them.\u201d\n\nA statement on the regional government\u2019s website said it \u201cmourns\u201d the loss of the 55-year-old.\n\nBild reported that he was found in the Hotel Neuer Fritz in central Berlin. Police cars and several state government cars were parked outside the hotel that evening.\n\nAfter he reported being unwell, staff members believed he might be suffering an infection, while members of the CSU parliamentary group said he had been experiencing gastrointesinal problems, the outlet reported.\n\nThe Berlin public prosecutor\u2019s office told Bild that there is \u201cno initial suspicion of a crime\u201d but that \u201cnothing can be ruled out\u201d. The outlet reported that Mr Aiwanger\u2019s body had no external injuries.\n\nThe Free Voters of Bavaria have been in a coalition with the conservative Christian Social Union in the state since 2018."
