    "chunk_size_bytes": 1652,
    "chunk_text": "Bomb alerts force flights to land in France, Bulgaria, Belgium\n\nReuters\u00a0- Bomb threats forced airliners \u200bto make unplanned landings across Europe on Thursday morning, \u200cthe latest in a series of threats targeting flights in Europe.\n\nAer Lingus flight EI402 from Dublin to Rome landed safely at Evreux airport, 100 \u200bkm west of Paris, after being diverted as a security \u200bprecaution, the Irish airline said in a statement.\n\nBelgian \u2060media reported that an airliner forced to land at Brussels' \u200bZaventem airport belonged to the Polish airline LOT.\n\nA Finnair \u200bplane from Helsinki was safely escorted to its scheduled landing in Rome by the Italian air force, a source close to the matter said.\n\nBulgarian \u200bofficials reported three aircraft making emergency landings because of bomb \u200bthreats.\n\nAirplanes flying from Copenhagen and Madrid landed in Sofia, the interior minister \u200csaid, \u2060according to broadcaster bTV.\n\nA third aircraft, from London, landed in Plovdiv, local media said.\n\nOn Tuesday, at least seven flights and a cruise ship received bomb threats, causing disruption at airports across Europe, \u200balthough no devices \u200bwere discovered.\n\nWhile \u2060no country has attributed blame for the threats, they come at a time when European officials \u200bhave been blaming both Russia and Iran for \u200bwhat they \u2060describe as \"hybrid attacks\" and acts of sabotage. Moscow and Tehran both deny any such campaign.\n\nThis week, unidentified hackers shut down fridges in supermarkets \u2060in \u200bseveral European countries. Some experts said \u200bhoax bomb threats could be part of a similar pattern of low-cost, deniable disruption \u200befforts."
