    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "People\u2019s Struggle Alliance warns Government against introducing \u2018repressive\u2019 laws under new names\n\nColombo, Nov. 5 (Daily Mirror) - The People\u2019s Struggle Alliance has warned the Government against introducing legislation that could reproduce the repressive powers of the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), saying a change of government does not make oppressive laws democratic.\n\nThe alliance made the remarks during a protest against the existing PTA, the proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and proposed legislation aimed at preventing organised crime.\n\nAddressing the issue, alliance Executive Committee member Rajkumar Rajeevkanth said the opposition was based on the need to protect human rights, democratic freedoms and the rule of law.\n\nHe said the proposed legislation could weaken judicial oversight and grant excessive powers to the police and security forces, creating opportunities for arbitrary arrests, prolonged detention and human rights violations.\n\nRecalling the consequences of the PTA, Rajeevkanth said many people detained under the law had spent years in prison without being convicted of any offence. Some were eventually released after losing valuable years of their lives, while their families, livelihoods and futures had been severely affected.\n\nHe acknowledged that national security and the prevention of organised crime were legitimate responsibilities of the State but stressed that such objectives should never be pursued at the expense of fundamental rights or judicial independence.\n\nHe also expressed concern that political groups which had previously opposed repressive legislation were now attempting to introduce alternative laws that could grant similar powers to the authorities.\n\nThe alliance called on the Government to repeal the PTA without replacing it with legislation containing similar provisions that allow arbitrary detention or excessive executive powers."
  },
