    "chunk_text": "He also expressed concern that political groups which had previously opposed repressive legislation were now attempting to introduce alternative laws that could grant similar powers to the authorities.\n\nThe alliance called on the Government to repeal the PTA without replacing it with legislation containing similar provisions that allow arbitrary detention or excessive executive powers.\n\nRegarding the proposed organised crime legislation, the alliance urged the Government to remove provisions that could enable arbitrary detention, disproportionate investigative powers or restrictions on fundamental freedoms without adequate judicial safeguards.\n\nThe alliance further called for guarantees of due process and independent judicial oversight, including ensuring that every arrested person is promptly produced before a judge, informed of the allegations and given access to legal representation.\n\nIt also demanded an urgent review of cases involving people held under the PTA for prolonged periods, calling for the release of those against whom there was no lawful basis for continued detention and timely judicial proceedings for others.\n\nRajeevkanth said independent and impartial investigations should also be conducted into allegations of torture, enforced disappearances, unlawful detention and other human rights violations to ensure accountability through due legal process.\n\nThe alliance further called for national security laws to be applied without discrimination based on ethnicity, religion, language or political affiliation, while insisting that proposed legislation be made available for public scrutiny and comply with the Constitution and Sri Lanka's international human rights obligations.\n\nDelivering a message to the Government, Rajeevkanth said those who had previously opposed oppressive laws should remember the principles they had defended before coming to power."
