    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "Government focuses on special needs preschool education\n\nColombo, Nov. 5 (Daily Mirror) - The Government is planning further measures to improve access to quality preschool education for children with special needs under its national early childhood education development programme, Deputy Minister of Women and Child Affairs Dr. Namal Sudarshana said.\n\nHe said special attention had been given to the educational requirements of children with special needs as part of the Government's efforts to improve the quality of preschool education across the country.\n\nAccording to the Deputy Minister, discussions are expected to be held with relevant institutions and officials to identify the necessary interventions, provide appropriate facilities and develop future programmes to ensure that these children receive quality early childhood education.\n\nHe said preschool education was a crucial stage in a child's development, as it laid the foundation for their future learning and educational progress.\n\nTherefore, improving the knowledge, skills and professional competencies of preschool teachers was essential to creating a better learning environment for young children, including those requiring additional educational support.\n\nThe Government is currently implementing a free national-level training programme for approximately 35,000 identified preschool teachers across Sri Lanka.\n\nThe programme is being jointly conducted by the Ministry of Education, Higher Education and Vocational Education and the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs, with the involvement of the National Secretariat for Early Childhood Development and the National Institute of Education.\n\nEach preschool teacher will receive 10 days of training under the programme, which also aims to introduce a structured national curriculum framework, strengthen preschool registration and improve professional standards in the sector."
