    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "New wave of bomb threats hits at least 25 passenger flights across Europe\n\nReuters\u00a0- A series of hoax bomb threats hit European airlines from Ireland \u200cto Bulgaria on Thursday morning for the second time this week, with some of at least 25 affected flights making unplanned landings or escorted by fighter jets.\n\nWhile no country has attributed blame for the threats, they come at a time when many European officials have accused Russia of masterminding sabotage and \"hybrid attacks\" \u2014 often cheap, easily deniable and not expected to trigger an armed response.\n\nDenmark, Poland and Romania, all affected \u200bby Thursday's disruptions, have previously accused Moscow directly, while Germany blamed Russia for an attack on Leipzig airport in August. Britain has blamed Russia in the past but \u200blinked Iran to a suspected plot to attack a US air base. Both Russia and Iran deny any involvement.\n\nEric Schouten, head of the \u2060aviation security group Dyami, said most of the countries affected supported Ukraine in its war against Russia's invasion.\n\n\"From a geopolitical view, I'm thinking, you always have to think about the \u200bscenario of hybrid warfare, where a country in this way \u2014 today we are looking at Russia, right? \u2014 is attacking us on multiple fronts, from railways to supermarkets to blood banks, \u200bmost recently. The energy sector, the internet sector, and the aviation sector are among those,\" he said.\n\nBulgaria's air force said its fighter jets had escorted three airliners to the ground. Officials said that Ryanair planes from Copenhagen and Madrid to Sofia landed as planned while a flight from London to Sofia was diverted to Plovdiv, Bulgaria's second city.\n\nSpanish authorities received three emailed \u200bbomb threats in under 20 minutes from around 9 a.m. (0800 GMT), involving two Air Europa departures from Madrid to Paris and four inbound Iberia flights from Rome and Milan, \u200bSpain's Guardia Civil said."
