    "chunk_text": "Spanish authorities received three emailed \u200bbomb threats in under 20 minutes from around 9 a.m. (0800 GMT), involving two Air Europa departures from Madrid to Paris and four inbound Iberia flights from Rome and Milan, \u200bSpain's Guardia Civil said.\n\nTwo Easyjet (EZJ.L) flights from Britain, one flight from Ireland and one from Greece landed \u200cas scheduled \u2060in Prague after receiving bomb threats. Czech police said they had assessed the credibility of the threats as low.\n\nItaly's air force escorted a Finnair plane coming from Helsinki to its scheduled landing in Rome, a person close to the matter said. Rome's airport operator said there had been no disruption to services.\n\nAnother Finnair flight from Helsinki, operated by Jettime, landed as normal in Vienna after receiving a bomb threat, Finnair said.\n\nThe Italian news agency ANSA said two planes had landed safely in Milan after receiving threats.\n\nA LOT flight from Warsaw landed \u200bas scheduled in Brussels after a security \u200bwarning, the Polish airline said. In \u2060Warsaw, a LOT flight to Paris was stopped as it was taxiing towards take-off and passengers were transferred to another aircraft as a precaution, it added.\n\nA Wizz Air (WIZZ.L) flight from Milan landed safely in Bucharest, Romania's intelligence agency said. Italian media \u200breported that a Wizz Air flight from Milan to Warsaw had been diverted to Vienna.\n\nAer Lingus flight EI402 from Dublin to \u200bRome landed safely on Thursday \u2060at Evreux airport, 100 km (62 miles) west of Paris, the Irish airline said.\n\nAnd in Denmark, a military bomb disposal unit searched two planes at Copenhagen airport, but did not find any devices, police said.\n\nEliran Almog, CEO of aviation cybersecurity firm Cyviation, said the repeated bomb threats could serve both to disrupt operations and to observe how airlines, airports and national authorities respond.\n\n\"These aviation \u2060threats are \u200balso occurring in the same period as cyberattacks on supermarket refrigeration systems across Europe,\" Almog said."
