    "md5": "6933f6ef0e9de343212b47e76cc2379c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "Midterms deliver  wake-up call to America\u2019s two major parties\n\nAbdul El-Sayed embraces his wife, Dr Sarah Jukaku, onstage after declaring victory at a midterm election night party in Detroit, Michigan. Pic AFP\n\nThe midterm election results were more of a wake-up call for both Democrats and Republicans than merely a means of sending new members to the US legislature. The American people have spoken, and they have spoken through the ballot box more loudly than the voices of dissent that erupted in Paris and other major European cities weeks ago.\n\nIn Paris, the youth-led protests reflected public anger against politicians and turned unruly. In the United States, however, it was a silent and orderly revolt against the political establishment, one that neither party can afford to ignore and must instead take stock of and learn from.\n\nFor the first time since the Vietnam War, during which the current billionaire President Donald Trump allegedly evaded the draft five times, US foreign policy had become so intricately linked to domestic issues in an election. The post-mortems say so loud and clear.\n\nJust as much as the Iran war and America\u2019s blind support for Israel, rising living costs, corruption, and the moral decadence of the presidency helped decide the election.\n\nRuing their defeat, Republican leaders, including Trump and Vice President J.D. Vance, have admitted that the Iran war was a major cause of their humiliating defeat. Even in states regarded as Republican strongholds, such as Kansas, Ohio, Texas, North Carolina, and Maine, some Republicans abandoned the Grand Old Party and voted for the Democrats.\n\nThe result was a blue tsunami that the Democratic leadership was scarcely prepared to handle. Voters will find out in the coming months whether the party respects the mandate and delivers on its promises."
  },
