    "md5": "4121ded44416a6f34b8b1ac4fbafdf5b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1923,
    "chunk_text": "In post-election statements, Trump claimed that the war was in the interest of the American people, even though they did not understand its long-term benefits. But the war served the billionaire class rather than ordinary Americans. In a display of indifference to the high grocery and fuel prices that people were paying, Trump bragged that the war had made American oil companies richer.\n\nNevertheless, the issue energised student protests across the United States and prompted many people to question successive American administrations\u2019 unwavering support for Israel despite allegations of war crimes. Perhaps it was in recognition of the importance of this issue that Trump, on the eve of the midterm elections, announced that his administration would stop sending aid to Israel. It was too little, too late.\n\nEasier said than done. To say that the influence of the Zionist lobby is widespread would be a gross understatement. The lobby includes wealthy business figures, industry leaders and evangelical groups whose support for Israel is so strong that they view Netanyahu\u2019s actions as serving a divine purpose. They fund election campaigns to help secure the victory of pro-Israel candidates who, in turn, are expected to advance Israeli interests rather than those of the American people who elected them.\n\nThe influence of the lobby remains a problem that US democracy has been unable to free itself from. The one who funds you controls you. Such is US politics.\n\nAIPAC remains as influential as ever. In a message posted on X, the group congratulated \u201cthe hundreds of pro-Israel Democrats and Republicans who won the election.\u201d It said that 234 candidates it supported had won. Among them were Democratic Party heavyweights Chuck Schumer and Hakeem Jeffries. The latter is expected to become Speaker of the House, the third-highest office in the line of succession after the President and Vice President."
  },
