    "md5": "248c6ac785e15de7c939c6f0c779a420",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "The outcome is a reminder that even the most powerful political leaders are not immune to the verdict of the electorate. Trump, who often projects himself as a leader capable of bending institutions to his will, has been confronted with the reality of the American constitutional system. Congress has an important role in overseeing the administration, controlling public expenditure and scrutinising decisions relating to war and peace.\n\nThe economic dimension of the election cannot be overlooked. Trump campaigned on the promise of making America affordable again. Yet, tariffs on imports, coupled with the economic repercussions of the war with Iran, undermined that promise. American consumers ultimately bear part of the cost of tariffs through higher prices. Likewise, disruptions to global energy supplies affect households and businesses far beyond the region where a conflict takes place. The electorate appears to have sent a message that foreign policy adventurism and economic nationalism cannot be pursued without consequences at home.\n\nThe implications are not confined to the United States. In an increasingly interconnected world, decisions taken in Washington can reverberate across continents. The conflict involving Iran, Israel and the United States demonstrated how quickly a regional war could turn into a global economic crisis. Disruptions to energy supplies and shipping through the Strait of Hormuz sent shockwaves through international markets. Countries dependent on imported fuel, including Sri Lanka, were particularly vulnerable. They had to contend with rising import costs, pressure on foreign exchange reserves and the prospect of renewed inflation, despite having little influence over the decisions that led to the crisis."
  },
