    "md5": "26c29af96249abfbb834c4399498449a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1867,
    "chunk_text": "Rasamanickam questions rooftop solar restrictions as Govt. targets 1,392 MW renewable capacity\n\nIlankai Tamil Arasu Katchi (ITAK) MP Shanakiyan Rasamanickam yesterday raised concerns over delays faced by household rooftop solar applicants, reduced incentives and changes to payment arrangements, despite the Government\u2019s ambitious renewable energy generation targets.\n\nRasamanickam questioned the Government\u2019s renewable energy policy during questions directed at Energy Minister Anura Karunathilaka, highlighting the difficulties faced by ordinary households and small-scale investors.\n\nHe questioned why large-scale renewable energy projects were receiving approvals while household rooftop solar applicants continued to face administrative hurdles.\n\nRasamanickam pointed out that individuals who had registered to generate renewable electricity last year were still awaiting the necessary approvals, raising concerns over unequal access to opportunities in the sector.\n\nHe questioned how the Government expected to achieve its renewable energy expansion targets while introducing restrictions and policy changes that could discourage private investment.\n\nResponding, the Minister outlined renewable energy capacity targets of 642 MW in 2027, 300 MW in 2028 and 450 MW in 2029, amounting to 1,392 MW over the three-year period.\n\nHowever, Rasamanickam questioned whether these targets were achievable under the prevailing policy framework, particularly given the uncertainty surrounding incentives and payment arrangements for solar electricity producers.\n\nHe also raised concerns over the suspension of previously available incentives and concessions, as well as changes linking payments to electricity generated during daytime hours. Such changes could affect producers\u2019 earnings and extend the period required to recover their initial investments, he warned."
  },
