    "chunk_text": "He also raised concerns over the suspension of previously available incentives and concessions, as well as changes linking payments to electricity generated during daytime hours. Such changes could affect producers\u2019 earnings and extend the period required to recover their initial investments, he warned.\n\nHe also questioned the delayed implementation of the household-level electricity generation framework announced in the Budget, saying outstanding issues affecting existing applicants needed to be resolved.\n\nSeparately, he sought details on Sri Lanka\u2019s electricity generation mix, the highest- and lowest-cost generation sources and measures being taken to reduce generation costs.\n\nHe also questioned the Government on electricity tariff revisions since the beginning of 2025.\n\nResponding, the Minister confirmed that electricity tariffs had been revised twice in 2025 and twice in 2026, while maintaining that consumers had not incurred additional charges as a result.\n\nThe Minister cited domestic electricity rates of Rs. 5 per unit for consumption up to 30 units, Rs. 9 for 31-60 units, Rs. 14 for 61-90 units, Rs. 20 for 91-120 units and Rs. 28 for 121-180 units.\n\nOn renewable energy policy, Karunathilaka said payment arrangements for producers would be revised and measures introduced to address existing difficulties in the sector.\n\nHe also indicated that new programs to encourage renewable energy development and special incentives for new investors would be introduced through the forthcoming Budget.\n\nRasamanickam stressed that resolving outstanding approvals, ensuring policy consistency and providing fair opportunities for household producers were essential if the Government was to meet its renewable energy ambitions."
