    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "2027 Budget: From economic recovery to prosperity \u2013 what the nation should expect",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/2027-Budget-From-economic-recovery-to-prosperity-what-the-nation-should-expect/4-799695",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793925971.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-0d8ad565-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0a8152eea4797561e8717b741428e041",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1654,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: From economic recovery to prosperity \u2013 what the nation should expect\n\nThe 12 November Budget must go beyond fiscal consolidation and revenue collection to deliver meaningful relief to households, strengthen businesses, stimulate investment and translate economic recovery into\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget arrives at a pivotal stage of economic recovery. Since the 2022 crisis, the country has restored macroeconomic stability, strengthened public finances and resumed growth. However, recovery remains incomplete, with households and businesses continuing to face financial pressures. Presented on 12 November, 2026, the Budget 2027 offers an opportunity to move beyond fiscal stabilisation towards inclusive growth, employment, productivity and improved living standards.\n\nThe challenge is to transform economic recovery into tangible prosperity for the nation.\n\nA national Budget is the Government\u2019s annual financial and economic policy statement, outlining revenue, expenditure, borrowing and development priorities. It reflects the Government\u2019s economic direction and commitment to national development. Through taxation, public spending, subsidies and investment, the Budget directly affects individuals, businesses and the wider economy\u2014shaping living costs, public services, investment, employment and economic growth. Ultimately, a Budget\u2019s success should be measured not merely by revenue collection or fiscal surpluses, but by its ability to transform public resources into tangible economic and social benefits.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s present circumstances demand a careful balance between economic discipline and public welfare."
  },
  {
