    "chunk_text": "Businesses continue to face higher taxation, compliance costs, financing constraints and operational uncertainties. Private-sector investment is vital for employment, exports, innovation and productivity. Budget 2027 should therefore promote a predictable, business-friendly environment that encourages investment, expansion, technological advancement and sustainable economic growth.\n\nThe Government\u2019s fiscal performance during 2025 provides an important foundation for assessing the expectations surrounding Budget 2027.\n\nAccording to the IMF\u2019s 2026 program review, Sri Lanka recorded the following fiscal outcomes in 2025:\n\nThe primary surplus reached 5.4% of GDP, significantly exceeding the 2.3% program target, while revenue and grants rose to 16.7% against the projected 14.7%. These achievements reflect improved fiscal management and revenue mobilisation (IMF, Sri Lanka Fifth and Sixth Reviews under the Extended Fund Facility, 2026).\n\nGovernment revenue improved through economic recovery, stronger tax compliance and increased motor vehicle imports in 2025, which contributed approximately 2.8% of GDP in revenue, according to the IMF. However, this exceptional revenue reflects pent-up demand following import restrictions and may not be sustainable. Budget 2027 must therefore strengthen long-term revenue sustainability through broader tax compliance, economic expansion and a more productive tax base, rather than excessive reliance on exceptional or consumption-driven taxation.\n\nThe Government has continued modernising tax administration through expanded taxpayer registration, improved compliance and digital revenue collection. RAMIS 2.0 and enhanced VAT compliance are important developments. However, their effectiveness depends on efficient implementation, taxpayer accessibility and improved service delivery. Budget 2027 should further strengthen a transparent, efficient and taxpayer-friendly system that encourages voluntary compliance, economic activity and public confidence."
