    "chunk_text": "The Government has continued modernising tax administration through expanded taxpayer registration, improved compliance and digital revenue collection. RAMIS 2.0 and enhanced VAT compliance are important developments. However, their effectiveness depends on efficient implementation, taxpayer accessibility and improved service delivery. Budget 2027 should further strengthen a transparent, efficient and taxpayer-friendly system that encourages voluntary compliance, economic activity and public confidence.\n\nPersonal income taxation remains an important concern for employees, professionals, consultants and self-employed individuals. Possible policy discussions may include:\n\nA carefully designed revision of personal income tax could improve disposable income and encourage greater formal economic participation. However, the extent of any revision will depend on the Government\u2019s revenue position and the policy commitments announced in the Budget.\n\nCorporate taxation is particularly important for businesses operating in manufacturing, exports, tourism, technology, agriculture and professional services. Businesses require a predictable tax environment in which investment decisions can be made with confidence. Potential areas for consideration include:\n\nTargeted tax incentives could stimulate investment, employment and taxable economic activity. Rather than granting indiscriminate concessions, the Government should consider incentives linked to measurable outcomes such as job creation, export growth, technological advancement and regional development.\n\nValue Added Tax (VAT), currently imposed at 18%, remains a major indirect tax affecting consumers and businesses. It influences consumer prices, business costs and working capital requirements. Budget 2027 is expected to address the following key VAT-related issues:\n\nReviewing the economic implications of VAT on essential goods and services."
