    "chunk_text": "Value Added Tax (VAT), currently imposed at 18%, remains a major indirect tax affecting consumers and businesses. It influences consumer prices, business costs and working capital requirements. Budget 2027 is expected to address the following key VAT-related issues:\n\nReviewing the economic implications of VAT on essential goods and services.\n\n4. Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL): A major business concern\n\nThe Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL), imposed at 2.5% on applicable turnover, represents a significant indirect tax burden on businesses. As it is generally based on turnover rather than profit, businesses operating on narrow margins may face considerable pressure, particularly across multiple stages of commercial activity. Budget 2027 could therefore consider:\n\nAssessing the cumulative effect of VAT and SSCL on business costs.\n\nA reduction or rationalisation of SSCL could improve business cash flow and competitiveness. Nevertheless, any substantial reduction would require careful consideration of revenue implications and the need to avoid creating further distortions within the taxation system.\n\nTax exemptions and concessions can promote economic development when properly designed. However, indiscriminate concessions may reduce revenue, create tax inequities and weaken the tax system. Budget 2027 offers an opportunity to review existing exemptions and establish a transparent, targeted framework focused on measurable economic and social benefits. Potential priorities include:\n\nThe emphasis should be on concessions that produce measurable economic benefits rather than exemptions granted without clear performance objectives.\n\nThe SME sector: The most important opportunity for Budget reform"
