    "chunk_text": "The emphasis should be on concessions that produce measurable economic benefits rather than exemptions granted without clear performance objectives.\n\nThe SME sector: The most important opportunity for Budget reform\n\nAn important consideration is the distinction between providing genuine assistance to small businesses and creating tax structures that encourage businesses to remain artificially below registration thresholds. The objective should be to encourage SMEs to grow, formalise and become more productive.\n\nBeyond taxation: What should the Budget deliver to the general public?\n\nThe Budget should examine the affordability of essential goods and services and the efficiency of existing social support mechanisms. Targeted support for low-income households, vulnerable groups and economically disadvantaged communities would be more sustainable than poorly targeted subsidies.\n\nAdequate allocations for healthcare, education and public infrastructure are essential for long-term development. The Government should prioritise improvements in service quality, accessibility and efficiency rather than focusing exclusively on increasing expenditure.\n\nSri Lanka requires employment opportunities that provide sustainable incomes and improve household economic security. The Budget should encourage private-sector investment, vocational training, entrepreneurship and productive employment opportunities.\n\nAgriculture remains essential for livelihoods, food security and rural development. Investment in irrigation, agricultural technology, storage facilities, value addition, logistics and market access could reduce wastage and improve farmer incomes.\n\nAffordable housing, transport infrastructure, urban development and regional connectivity are important components of inclusive growth. Well-designed infrastructure investment can create employment while improving the productive capacity of the economy."
