    "chunk_size_bytes": 1718,
    "chunk_text": "Affordable housing, transport infrastructure, urban development and regional connectivity are important components of inclusive growth. Well-designed infrastructure investment can create employment while improving the productive capacity of the economy.\n\nBusinesses need confidence that the tax and regulatory environment will remain stable. Frequent changes, unclear administrative interpretations and unpredictable enforcement can discourage investment even when headline tax rates appear attractive. The Budget should therefore communicate a clear medium-term economic policy framework.\n\nA Budget must balance revenue mobilisation with expenditure efficiency, transparency and accountability. According to the IMF, total expenditure and net lending reached 19.0% of GDP in 2025, while capital expenditure and net lending accounted for approximately 3.0%. Budget 2027 should therefore balance fiscal consolidation with essential development investment, prioritising:\n\nThe objective should be to ensure that every rupee of public expenditure contributes meaningfully to national development.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recovery depends on fiscal reforms and debt sustainability. The Government must secure adequate revenue to meet expenditure, service debt and maintain essential public services. However, excessive taxation can discourage investment, weaken consumption and encourage informality. Budget 2027 should therefore strengthen revenue quality and efficiency rather than rely solely on higher tax rates. A sustainable revenue strategy should emphasise:\n\nThe IMF has also highlighted the importance of sustaining compliance gains while carefully managing revenue risks as exceptional vehicle-related taxation normalises."
