    "md5": "90d6829048c0b792f494875d6705445c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery depends on fiscal reforms and debt sustainability. The Government must secure adequate revenue to meet expenditure, service debt and maintain essential public services. However, excessive taxation can discourage investment, weaken consumption and encourage informality. Budget 2027 should therefore strengthen revenue quality and efficiency rather than rely solely on higher tax rates. A sustainable revenue strategy should emphasise:\n\nThe IMF has also highlighted the importance of sustaining compliance gains while carefully managing revenue risks as exceptional vehicle-related taxation normalises.\n\nA successful Budget should be assessed against measurable outcomes rather than the number of proposals announced. The following indicators would provide a meaningful framework for evaluating its performance:\n\nEconomic growth: Sustaining growth through productive investment and private-sector expansion.\n\nHousehold welfare: Improving real incomes, purchasing power and access to essential services.\n\nPublic expenditure efficiency: Improving the quality and effectiveness of Government spending.\n\nExport competitiveness: Supporting diversification, value addition and foreign exchange earnings.\n\nSocial protection: Ensuring that vulnerable communities benefit from economic recovery.\n\nConclusion: A Budget that must deliver more than economic stability\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery provides an opportunity for national transformation. Budget 2027 must convert fiscal stability into tangible benefits through improved living standards, stronger public services, investment, employment and business growth. For citizens, the priority is affordability and economic security; for businesses, predictable taxation and investment opportunities; and for the nation, sustainable growth and prosperity.\n\nThe true success of Budget 2027 will be measured not by revenue collected, but by how effectively public resources are transformed into prosperity and opportunity for Sri Lanka."
