    "md5": "270e7930909ad1a2fa9c5e1330d27c51",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1577,
    "chunk_text": "The capital order and the political economy of PSTA and POCA\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Government has Gazetted two Bills ostensibly intended to replace the Prevention of Terrorism (Temporary) Act (PTA), enacted in 1978\u2014nearly five decades ago\u2014despite the word temporary in its title. The repeal of the PTA has long been regarded by local and international human rights and democratic activists as imperative for the establishment of democratic governance and the rule of law. Nevertheless, successive Governments since 1978 have refused to repeal it. Moreover, the PTA has been extensively deployed to suppress and oppress political opponents. The NPP Government, which came to power promising to repeal the Act, has itself resorted to its provisions over the past two years to harass potential political opponents.\n\nThe Government has now taken steps to fulfil its promise of repealing the PTA, only to propose replacing it with two equally if not more draconian pieces of legislation: the Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and the Prevention of Organised Crime Act (POCA).\n\nThese two Bills would provide the Government with greater coercive powers under the guise of protecting the state and preventing organised crime. Lawyers, democratic and human rights activists, and international organisations have demonstrated in considerable detail how the enactment of these bills would undermine citizens\u2019 rights. The focus of this note, however, is not on their legal ramifications. Rather, it seeks to examine their political-economic dimensions."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-bff712a5",
