    "md5": "21d6e6da18ce8a5171f49c90bc586dc7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1435,
    "chunk_text": "These two Bills would provide the Government with greater coercive powers under the guise of protecting the state and preventing organised crime. Lawyers, democratic and human rights activists, and international organisations have demonstrated in considerable detail how the enactment of these bills would undermine citizens\u2019 rights. The focus of this note, however, is not on their legal ramifications. Rather, it seeks to examine their political-economic dimensions.\n\nIn the current discourse, two principal, though closely related, explanations have emerged.\n\nThe first explanation draws on a foundational concept in the social sciences: the self-reinforcing feedback loop. This concept suggests that once an individual or collective acquires power, it also gains access to resources and institutional mechanisms that can be mobilised to accumulate still more power. Hence, the familiar saying that \u201cpower begets power.\u201d\n\nThe Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), the core political force within the NPP, was established approximately six decades ago. For the first time in its history, it has acquired the opportunity to exercise state power and control and manage resources and Governmental mechanisms that have long been affected by multiple forms of corruption. Historical experience, particularly in the Global South, provides ample evidence of the concentration of power and resources in the hands of a small political elite."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-bff712a5",
