    "md5": "e74b18ec9a17e70eca80c89a754a130f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "The Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), the core political force within the NPP, was established approximately six decades ago. For the first time in its history, it has acquired the opportunity to exercise state power and control and manage resources and Governmental mechanisms that have long been affected by multiple forms of corruption. Historical experience, particularly in the Global South, provides ample evidence of the concentration of power and resources in the hands of a small political elite.\n\nIn defending the Government\u2019s intentions, NPP leaders repeatedly emphasise that their Government has so far refrained from using excessive force in dealing with protests and demonstrations against it. They therefore present the proposed legislation as essentially precautionary measures rather than instruments intended for immediate use. However, two fundamental features must be present in any counterterrorism legislation if it is to possess legal and moral legitimacy.\n\nFirst, the legislation must be clear and precise, leaving little room for arbitrary interpretation or misuse. Yet the definition of terrorism in the proposed legislation is excessively broad.\n\nSecond, counterterrorism legislation must incorporate effective safeguards for freedom of expression, peaceful assembly, association, religion or belief, and participation in public affairs. Any restrictions on these rights must be lawful, necessary, and proportionate to a legitimate objective.\n\nThe two proposed bills appear to fail these tests. As international human rights organisations have demonstrated, the PSTA, together with the POCA, seeks to impose \u201cunjustified restrictions on freedoms of expression, including media freedom, peaceful assembly and association, despite the new safeguards in sections 3 (4), 8 (2), 10 (3) and 11.\u201d"
  },
