    "md5": "618f538cb6d8d22e061d992ae7466b8b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "The two proposed bills appear to fail these tests. As international human rights organisations have demonstrated, the PSTA, together with the POCA, seeks to impose \u201cunjustified restrictions on freedoms of expression, including media freedom, peaceful assembly and association, despite the new safeguards in sections 3 (4), 8 (2), 10 (3) and 11.\u201d\n\nThe Italian political economist Clara E. Mattei has drawn attention to the nexus between the concentration of political power and the imposition of austerity policies. In 2017, Mattei first developed this argument in her article, \u201cAusterity and Repressive Politics: Italian Economists in the Early Years of the Fascist Government,\u201d published in The European Journal of the History of Economic Thought. She subsequently elaborated on the argument in her book The Capital Order: How Economists Invented Austerity and Paved the Way to Fascism.\n\nMattei\u2019s central argument is that austerity is not merely a technical economic policy but also a political instrument for protecting capitalism and suppressing working-class challenges to the existing social order. To preserve and protect what she calls the capital order, painful measures must be imposed, supported by carefully designed instruments of political power.\n\nFor example, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe has reportedly argued that the \u201ccorrect\u201d policies should have been adopted even if they ran counter to the wishes of the people. This position resonates with the views expressed by former Chair of the US Federal Reserve System Jerome Powell. Powell argued that, in order to \u201crestore price stability,\u201d economic policymakers must use their instruments \u201cforcefully,\u201d acknowledging that doing so \u201cwill also bring some pain.\u201d The pain, in this formulation, is to be borne by those held responsible for inflation, particularly those who are consuming too much and working too little."
  },
