    "md5": "c7fa243cf13f2907f266c5ad5458568e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "For example, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe has reportedly argued that the \u201ccorrect\u201d policies should have been adopted even if they ran counter to the wishes of the people. This position resonates with the views expressed by former Chair of the US Federal Reserve System Jerome Powell. Powell argued that, in order to \u201crestore price stability,\u201d economic policymakers must use their instruments \u201cforcefully,\u201d acknowledging that doing so \u201cwill also bring some pain.\u201d The pain, in this formulation, is to be borne by those held responsible for inflation, particularly those who are consuming too much and working too little.\n\nBut what happens when such measures encounter resistance? If opposition emerges from the lower strata of society, the necessary political and institutional precautions must be taken to ensure their implementation.\n\nAuthoritarian political power can facilitate the implementation of austerity by suppressing resistance from workers and other popular groups. Mattei argues that, \u201cin the Italian case, Fascism and austerity reinforced one another: Fascism provided the coercive political conditions for austerity, while austerity helped restore capitalist social relations\u201d.\n\nAccording to Mattei, repressive legislation, an empowered executive, and a subservient judiciary are not necessarily isolated institutional developments. They can form part of a broader political project directed towards the implementation of austerity policies, including cuts in public expenditure on health and education, reductions in social security, and the dismantling of the remaining institutions of the social welfare order.\n\nFrom this perspective, the proposed PSTA and POCA should not be examined solely as counterterrorism or crime-prevention measures. They must also be situated within the broader political economy of the NPP Government\u2019s economic program designed, developed and backed by the International Monetary Fund."
