    "md5": "8f6a4ac83c9199246781d5bbbd249b9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1603,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget should strengthen external sector capacity\n\nThe Government is expected to present its Budget for the year 2027 on 12 November. This is going to be a totally different Budget, even though the Government is required to stick to certain IMF guidelines. The dynamics of the economy have changed in 2026. The Treasury has excessive savings. On the contrary, the external sector will be experiencing a deficit in 2026 and in 2027, according to IMF forecasts.\n\nAccordingly, I think this raises an important macroeconomic policy question: Should fiscal policy be determined primarily by the Government\u2019s revenue position, or should it also respond to the country\u2019s external-sector capacity?\n\nHow much additional Government expenditure can the economy accommodate without creating an unsustainable balance-of-payments problem?\n\nThe next Budget should be able to answer the above macroeconomic questions. The answer would require developing a fiscal expenditure framework strengthening the external sector. Or this Budget would present an External-Sector-Compatible Fiscal Expenditure Framework.\n\nThis does not mean that Sri Lanka can\u2019t increase Government expenditure even when the external sector is experiencing a deficit. It is also true that a Government Budget surplus is not a guarantee of a current account surplus, and a Government Budget deficit does not automatically produce a current account deficit.\n\nMy view is that these two phenomena should be examined separately.\n\nAccording to the IMF\u2019s May 2026 review (Country Report No. 26/111), actual economic performance is as follows."
