    "chunk_text": "This does not mean that Sri Lanka can\u2019t increase Government expenditure even when the external sector is experiencing a deficit. It is also true that a Government Budget surplus is not a guarantee of a current account surplus, and a Government Budget deficit does not automatically produce a current account deficit.\n\nMy view is that these two phenomena should be examined separately.\n\nAccording to the IMF\u2019s May 2026 review (Country Report No. 26/111), actual economic performance is as follows.\n\nConsider the following transmission mechanism. The Government increases expenditure. As a result, household disposable income and business revenue increase. And if there is no proper monetary policy or administrative control to contain private credit growth, the demand for imported goods increases, almost exponentially, creating pressure on the external sector.\n\nAlso, suppose the Government increases expenditure while commercial banks simultaneously expand private-sector credit.\n\nIf the private credit expansion finances vehicle imports, consumer goods and other imports, the external-sector pressure could become substantial.\n\nTherefore, fiscal expansion should be coordinated with monetary and credit policy.\n\nThe objective should not simply be to increase or decrease money supply. It should be to ensure that additional purchasing power is accompanied by an appropriate expansion of domestic productive capacity.\n\nHowever, not all Government expenditure produces the same external-sector consequences, if the Government is careful as to what expenditure should increase."
  },
