    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b8af5e6bc6589a0bf0a1f1d013662d09",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1545,
    "chunk_text": "The objective should not simply be to increase or decrease money supply. It should be to ensure that additional purchasing power is accompanied by an appropriate expansion of domestic productive capacity.\n\nHowever, not all Government expenditure produces the same external-sector consequences, if the Government is careful as to what expenditure should increase.\n\nThe Government should evaluate each strategic expenditure scenario mentioned above and select major expenditure programs according to its expected foreign exchange consequences.\n\nI would suggest introducing an expenditure policy based on increasing external-sector capacity.\n\nThe Government should not unnecessarily suppress productive Government expenditure merely to maintain an exceptionally high primary surplus when the external-sector constraints can be addressed through expenditure composition. The IMF itself recognised the need for fiscal easing in 2026 in response to economic shocks, while requiring a return to a 2.3% primary surplus from 2027 onwards.\n\nHowever, there is one important qualification in following the said approach. The Government must also consider interest payments, debt-service obligations and the availability of sustainable financing. The primary surplus alone does not measure the Government\u2019s complete fiscal capacity.\n\nSuch a framework could help Sri Lanka determine the appropriate level of Government expenditure in its Budget by examining its effects on domestic production, imports, exports, private credit and foreign exchange reserves."
