    "language": "en",
    "md5": "df57b17d5c5f2eb1e447a96a25573a4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "These experiences stayed with me as I continued learning and working with technology.\n\nToday, we have software that can carry out complicated tasks in seconds. We can communicate across countries, attend classes from home and access information that once required a visit to a library.\n\nWe tend to design systems around how we expect people to use them. When someone struggles, we may assume that the person needs more training or practice.\n\nA website with small text, confusing instructions or too many steps can create difficulties that need not exist.\n\nI began thinking about what might happen if we approached technology from the other direction.\n\nInstead of expecting every person to use a system in the same way, could we give people more choice in how they use it?\n\nThat thinking helped shape Rihazudin i\u00b3 \u2014 Idea \u00b7 Innovate \u00b7 Implement.\n\nOne of the projects I have been working on is an accessible digital reading platform.\n\nThe idea grew partly from my own difficulties with reading and concentrating.\n\nI wanted to explore whether a reader could adjust the way information appeared on a screen rather than having to accept a fixed layout.\n\nThe platform is being developed with options such as changing text size, adjusting colours, focusing on smaller sections of text and listening to written content.\n\nSome of these features are already available in the working demonstration. Others are still being developed and tested.\n\nA person with dyslexia may prefer a different reading layout. Someone with visual difficulty may need larger text or stronger contrast. Another reader may simply find it easier to listen.\n\nEven the same person may need different settings on different days.\n\nMy intention is to give readers more control over their experience.\n\nI am interested in how technology can make everyday activities easier in education, healthcare, workplaces and small businesses."
  },
