    "md5": "266e0084d44cfa69123c28e2195d380d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "Even the same person may need different settings on different days.\n\nMy intention is to give readers more control over their experience.\n\nI am interested in how technology can make everyday activities easier in education, healthcare, workplaces and small businesses.\n\nSometimes a small change in the way something works can make a considerable difference.\n\nI am also interested in how organisations can make better use of technology without creating unnecessary complexity or waste.\n\nThere is still much to learn, test and improve. I would rather develop something useful and demonstrate what it can do than make promises that have not yet been proven.\n\nMy memoir, 'If You Hug Me, I Will Fall', tells the story of my illness, the people who helped me and the years I spent trying to regain my independence.\n\nThere were times when I needed someone beside me, but not necessarily someone holding me up.\n\nIf I was to improve my balance, I needed the chance to stand and try, even when I was unsteady.\n\nGood support should not take away a person\u2019s independence. Where possible, it should help them do more for themselves.\n\nI see a connection between that lesson and the work I am trying to do through Rihazudin i\u00b3.\n\nFrom 6 to 8 November 2026, I will be taking part in INFOTEL at BMICH, Colombo. Visitors can find Rihazudin i\u00b3 in Hall B, Stall 20.\n\nI will be sharing the ideas behind the initiative, introducing the accessible reading platform and discussing how similar approaches might be useful in other areas.\n\nI would particularly welcome conversations with educators, healthcare professionals, software developers, businesses and people who have experienced difficulties using digital services.\n\nTheir experiences and suggestions can help shape what comes next.\n\nI am not presenting Rihazudin i\u00b3 as the answer to every problem. It is a beginning, built on personal experience, continued learning and the belief that we can make technology more considerate of the people using it."
