    "md5": "8f94614fecd69c4bffdc31a6f0241276",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1394,
    "chunk_text": "Audits, research, and economic progress \u2013 Our audited nemesis\n\nUniversity of the Future,' wrote this opening line: To the researcher and the academic in the developing world, the eagerness to contribute to institution and nation building, in whatever modest a way, often becomes almost as exciting and challenging as the dedication to study, investigation, experiment and discovery.\n\nI understand the value of that write-up, having been part of the national university system for over four decades. I may say we sacrificed a lot of potential research time to the former trying and crying. The University of the Future is yet to come, and our engagement with the nation and for the nation is still tenuous at best! Another statement I remember is a line from the address to the Malaysian Academy of Sciences by Prof Douglas Osheroff \u2013 1996 Nobel Laureate in Physics. I saw the liquid doing something unusual; that told me right away that some sort of ordering was going on in the liquid, and I wrote it in my lab book that night. It was 2.40 in the morning, by the way. I had discovered the BCS transition in liquid helium-3. That was how it was done. This research was done in a highly supportive environment, though it may not have been obvious at the time. An open lab at 2.40 a.m. The researcher\u2019s freedom is understood, and given all the support, not the nation but the world changes."
  },
