    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "I understand the value of that write-up, having been part of the national university system for over four decades. I may say we sacrificed a lot of potential research time to the former trying and crying. The University of the Future is yet to come, and our engagement with the nation and for the nation is still tenuous at best! Another statement I remember is a line from the address to the Malaysian Academy of Sciences by Prof Douglas Osheroff \u2013 1996 Nobel Laureate in Physics. I saw the liquid doing something unusual; that told me right away that some sort of ordering was going on in the liquid, and I wrote it in my lab book that night. It was 2.40 in the morning, by the way. I had discovered the BCS transition in liquid helium-3. That was how it was done. This research was done in a highly supportive environment, though it may not have been obvious at the time. An open lab at 2.40 a.m. The researcher\u2019s freedom is understood, and given all the support, not the nation but the world changes.\n\nWe never check, nor are we interested in, the return on investment of public money for research. If the assets and final report are present, and an evaluation report has been duly filed with some boxes appropriately ticked, that is identified, reported, and concluded as research completed and money spent and accounted for. In this manner, whether you spend 0.1% of your GDP on R&D or 2% of GDP on R&D is irrelevant. The State has no plan for the output, so meaningful outcomes never arise. Since no one sees tangible economic benefits, no one tries to allocate more rupees to research. An unproductive annual cycle of standard allocation and substandard progress continues. The administration is engaged in a meaningless pursuit of verification."
