    "md5": "bd6a7586c0ad002d167359b00cdc3c22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1431,
    "chunk_text": "The anti-establishment paradox: What America\u2019s midterm revolt reveals about Sri Lanka\u2019s NPP\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake with US President Donald Trump during the special dinner reception hosted by the latter at the Lotte New York Palace Hotel, for the heads of state who had arrived in New York to participate in the 80th Session of the United Nations General Assembly in September 2025\n\nFrom Washington to Colombo, movements elected to overturn the political establishment face a fundamental contradiction: the powers they once condemned become the instruments through which they govern. Their lasting significance depends not simply on how they exercise authority, but on whether they are willing to constrain it\n\nPolitical movements rarely discover the deepest contradictions of their revolutions while fighting for power. Those contradictions emerge after victory, when the institutions they promised to transform become the instruments through which they govern. The electorate united by rejection of the old establishment begins to divide over what should replace it, while political authority once condemned as excessive becomes increasingly convenient to those who inherit it. The defining challenge of an anti-establishment Government is therefore not merely retaining public support, but overcoming the institutional incentives that can turn yesterday\u2019s reformers into custodians of the system they opposed."
  },
