    "chunk_text": "Political movements rarely discover the deepest contradictions of their revolutions while fighting for power. Those contradictions emerge after victory, when the institutions they promised to transform become the instruments through which they govern. The electorate united by rejection of the old establishment begins to divide over what should replace it, while political authority once condemned as excessive becomes increasingly convenient to those who inherit it. The defining challenge of an anti-establishment Government is therefore not merely retaining public support, but overcoming the institutional incentives that can turn yesterday\u2019s reformers into custodians of the system they opposed.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the comparison raises a more consequential question. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Government emerged from the economic catastrophe of 2022 promising not merely different political leadership, but a transformation of the structures associated with corruption, impunity and economic mismanagement. Its historic mandate represented a profound rejection of conventional politics. Two years later, the challenge is whether that rejection can produce enduring institutional change, or whether the practical demands of governing will gradually reconcile reformers to the powers they once sought to dismantle.\n\nTrump and Dissanayake are not ideological equivalents. One leads a conservative nationalist movement in the world\u2019s largest economy; the other heads a left-leaning coalition governing a heavily indebted developing state. Their circumstances and constitutional systems differ fundamentally. What connects them is a structural dilemma: anti-establishment movements can acquire extraordinary electoral authority without acquiring an equally coherent mandate over how that authority should be exercised."
  },
