    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "Trump and Dissanayake are not ideological equivalents. One leads a conservative nationalist movement in the world\u2019s largest economy; the other heads a left-leaning coalition governing a heavily indebted developing state. Their circumstances and constitutional systems differ fundamentally. What connects them is a structural dilemma: anti-establishment movements can acquire extraordinary electoral authority without acquiring an equally coherent mandate over how that authority should be exercised.\n\nTheir agreement about what had failed did not necessarily constitute agreement about what should follow. Fiscal consolidation, taxation, public-sector reform and redistribution impose different burdens on different constituencies. Electoral movements can unite voters by postponing these disagreements; Governments must eventually confront them.\n\nThis distinction between a negative electoral consensus and a positive governing settlement explains an important vulnerability of anti-establishment politics. The former identifies what voters collectively reject; the latter requires Governments to reconcile competing interests. Political scientist V.O. Key\u2019s work on retrospective voting provides a useful framework: citizens assess incumbents partly through their experience of Government rather than their initial ideological commitments. Once elected, reformist movements gradually lose the ability to define themselves through their predecessors\u2019 failures.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s May 2025 local elections illustrated the conditional nature of electoral support. The NPP remained the leading political force, securing 4,503,930 votes and 43.26% of valid ballots, compared with 61.56% in the preceding parliamentary election. Differences in turnout, candidates and electoral incentives prevent the results from being treated as a direct measure of declining national support. Nevertheless, the extraordinary parliamentary coalition was not automatically reproducible."
