    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s May 2025 local elections illustrated the conditional nature of electoral support. The NPP remained the leading political force, securing 4,503,930 votes and 43.26% of valid ballots, compared with 61.56% in the preceding parliamentary election. Differences in turnout, candidates and electoral incentives prevent the results from being treated as a direct measure of declining national support. Nevertheless, the extraordinary parliamentary coalition was not automatically reproducible.\n\nEconomic conditions expose these contradictions with particular force. A Reuters/Ipsos survey conducted immediately before the American midterms found that 48% of registered voters identified living costs as their principal electoral concern, compared with 26% citing democratic norms and 13% immigration. Nearly half reported that their household finances had deteriorated since Trump\u2019s return. Even amid disputes concerning presidential authority and foreign policy, material insecurity remained central to electoral judgment.\n\nSri Lanka faces a different but equally revealing problem. The World Bank\u2019s October 2026 Sri Lanka Development Update reports economic growth of 4.7% during the first half of the year, with real GDP returning to pre-crisis levels. Growth is projected at 4.4% for 2026, reflecting macroeconomic stabilisation, fiscal consolidation and recovering economic activity. These are consequential achievements following the sovereign default and economic dislocation of 2022.\n\nYet the same assessment estimates poverty at 16.9% in 2025, substantially above pre-crisis levels. Household incomes and labour-market conditions have recovered more slowly than aggregate output, while renewed inflationary pressures have weakened purchasing power."
