    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Yet the same assessment estimates poverty at 16.9% in 2025, substantially above pre-crisis levels. Household incomes and labour-market conditions have recovered more slowly than aggregate output, while renewed inflationary pressures have weakened purchasing power.\n\nVerit\u00e9 Research\u2019s July 2026 Mood of the Nation survey illustrates this divergence. Government approval stood at approximately 50%, down from 65% in February. The proportion believing economic conditions were improving fell from 64% to 42%, while 56% described current conditions as poor. These findings do not establish an inevitable electoral trajectory, but demonstrate that macroeconomic improvement need not produce equivalent political confidence.\n\nThe NPP inherited exceptional fiscal constraints, and abandoning stabilisation for immediate popularity could undermine recovery itself. Nevertheless, economic inheritance cannot indefinitely determine political accountability. As Governments remain in office, citizens increasingly judge them through present conditions rather than the failures of previous administrations. Stabilisation may restore financial credibility; durable legitimacy requires productive employment, rising real incomes and economic opportunity.\n\nThe deeper contradiction concerns political authority itself. Anti-establishment movements gain legitimacy by condemning executive overreach, political privilege and institutions vulnerable to manipulation. Once elected, they inherit those same constitutional and administrative powers. Authority previously portrayed as an obstacle to democracy becomes a convenient instrument for implementing the Government\u2019s program.\n\nThis is the institutional inheritance trap: Governments elected to constrain political power may discover that retaining inherited authority makes governing easier than reforming it."
