    "chunk_size_bytes": 1779,
    "chunk_text": "The deeper contradiction concerns political authority itself. Anti-establishment movements gain legitimacy by condemning executive overreach, political privilege and institutions vulnerable to manipulation. Once elected, they inherit those same constitutional and administrative powers. Authority previously portrayed as an obstacle to democracy becomes a convenient instrument for implementing the Government\u2019s program.\n\nThis is the institutional inheritance trap: Governments elected to constrain political power may discover that retaining inherited authority makes governing easier than reforming it.\n\nYet this creates a dilemma. If concentrated authority is continually justified by the need to complete reforms, the promised transformation can become dependent on preserving precisely the powers it was intended to restrict. Temporary necessity risks evolving into permanent political convenience.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s executive presidency illustrates this tension. The NPP\u2019s 2024 manifesto promised a new constitution and abolition of the executive presidential system. The Government has reaffirmed that commitment, with Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya explaining that constitutional reform requires examining existing proposals, developing a new constitutional framework and undertaking public consultation. Nevertheless, abolition remains unrealised, and a definitive timetable has not been publicly established.\n\nIn September 2026, Parliament passed the 22nd Amendment by 158 votes to 63, increasing the retirement ages of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges and expanding the latter court. The amendment demonstrated the Government\u2019s capacity to advance constitutional legislation, although it did not address the abolition of executive presidential authority."
