    "chunk_size_bytes": 1663,
    "chunk_text": "In September 2026, Parliament passed the 22nd Amendment by 158 votes to 63, increasing the retirement ages of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges and expanding the latter court. The amendment demonstrated the Government\u2019s capacity to advance constitutional legislation, although it did not address the abolition of executive presidential authority.\n\nThe NPP\u2019s record also resists simplistic declarations of institutional failure. The Proceeds of Crime Act, amendments to the National Audit Act and repeal of statutory entitlements for former presidents represent concrete reforms. Their enduring significance, however, depends on consistent enforcement and institutions capable of holding officeholders accountable irrespective of political affiliation.\n\nCounterterrorism legislation presents a more contested test. The Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill, gazetted in September 2026 as a proposed replacement for the Prevention of Terrorism Act, contains certain procedural improvements. However, the Centre for Policy Alternatives has argued that provisions involving executive detention, broad definitions and restrictions on fundamental freedoms risk perpetuating weaknesses of the previous framework.\n\nThe disagreement concerns whether legislative reform meaningfully constrains coercive authority or merely modifies the procedures through which inherited powers operate. Changing officeholders alters who possesses authority; transforming institutions determines how that authority can be exercised, challenged and restrained.\n\nThe American comparison becomes most instructive through the differences between the two constitutional systems."
