    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "The disagreement concerns whether legislative reform meaningfully constrains coercive authority or merely modifies the procedures through which inherited powers operate. Changing officeholders alters who possesses authority; transforming institutions determines how that authority can be exercised, challenged and restrained.\n\nThe American comparison becomes most instructive through the differences between the two constitutional systems.\n\nSri Lanka possesses no equivalent nationwide congressional midterm mechanism. Its presidential and parliamentary mandates operate through different electoral arrangements, making judicial independence, parliamentary scrutiny, autonomous commissions and public auditing particularly important between national elections.\n\nNeither arrangement guarantees accountability. Divided Government can produce legislative paralysis, while concentrated parliamentary authority can facilitate necessary reforms. The distinction is that the two constitutional systems distribute incentives for institutional restraint differently.\n\nIn America, an electoral reversal can introduce additional checks on executive power during a presidential term. In Sri Lanka, where the NPP commands a two-thirds parliamentary majority, the Government\u2019s capacity to implement reform is accompanied by greater responsibility for establishing safeguards that restrain its own authority.\n\nThis produces the article\u2019s central constitutional paradox: the parliamentary strength that makes systemic reform achievable can simultaneously reduce the political pressure to undertake it. The more effectively a governing coalition consolidates authority, the less immediately necessary institutional self-restraint may appear."
  },
