    "chunk_text": "This produces the article\u2019s central constitutional paradox: the parliamentary strength that makes systemic reform achievable can simultaneously reduce the political pressure to undertake it. The more effectively a governing coalition consolidates authority, the less immediately necessary institutional self-restraint may appear.\n\nA further difference concerns Opposition politics. America\u2019s two-party competition provided dissatisfied voters with an established electoral vehicle capable of assuming congressional control. Sri Lanka\u2019s opposition remains fragmented, with prominent parties carrying associations with previous administrations and facing difficulties establishing a convincing alternative program.\n\nConsequently, dissatisfaction with the Government need not translate into support for its opponents. Where citizens remain unconvinced by available alternatives, disillusionment may instead manifest through abstention, political disengagement or declining confidence in representative institutions.\n\nThis matters particularly for the NPP because its original mandate emerged from a broader crisis of confidence in conventional politics. If expectations of institutional transformation remain unmet, the consequences could extend beyond one administration\u2019s popularity to public confidence in democratic change itself.\n\nAmerica\u2019s midterms do not predict Sri Lanka\u2019s political future. The countries possess fundamentally different political institutions, and the NPP retains substantial public support alongside identifiable economic and legislative achievements. The comparison instead illuminates a broader democratic problem: movements elected through rejection must eventually convert that rejection into a governing settlement capable of surviving changes in political leadership."
