    "chunk_text": "America\u2019s midterms do not predict Sri Lanka\u2019s political future. The countries possess fundamentally different political institutions, and the NPP retains substantial public support alongside identifiable economic and legislative achievements. The comparison instead illuminates a broader democratic problem: movements elected through rejection must eventually convert that rejection into a governing settlement capable of surviving changes in political leadership.\n\nThe lasting significance of the NPP\u2019s mandate should therefore be measured through more than electoral dominance, economic indicators or the personal integrity of present officeholders. It depends on whether institutional safeguards become sufficiently independent to restrain Governments that may not share the reformist commitments of those who established them.\n\nThe fundamental contradiction confronting anti-establishment Governments is that electoral victory transfers the very powers reformers once condemned. Their most enduring achievement would be to establish a political order in which accountability no longer depends on the character or intentions of those exercising authority.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s transformation will ultimately be measured not by how decisively the NPP displaced the previous establishment, but by how much less power any future establishment will be able to abuse.\n\n(The author is an International Relations student at Monash University whose writing explores politics, governance, and international affairs)"
