    "chunk_size_bytes": 1802,
    "chunk_text": "The furnishing business also recorded 16% growth, supported by a confirmed project pipeline of Rs. 561 million. Together, these results reflect continued growth beyond JAT\u2019s traditional wood coatings base, strengthening the Group\u2019s increasingly diversified revenue portfolio.\n\nVolt Charge continued to expand its technology capabilities during the period, invoicing 2,000 L2 chargers to John Keells CG Auto for BYD vehicles, completing engineering of a 200 kW commercial vehicle charger and increasing its Volt app user base to 25,000 active users. While revenue declined by 5% due to the timing of revenue recognition, these developments reflected continued progress in scaling both its hardware and digital charging ecosystem.\u00a0 The business also commenced a pilot project for electric two- and three-wheeler charging, with the first charger installed in Kottawa.\n\nInternationally, Bangladesh recorded 26% industrial sales growth, 42% retail sales growth and a 151% increase in CoatEX revenue, demonstrating broader-based growth across JAT\u2019s established industrial business and its expanding retail presence.\n\nMirotone New Zealand recorded month-on-month sales growth of 12% in August and 9% in September, indicating improving momentum following slower sales movement in Q1. The business reported a first-half loss of NZD 99,000 amid challenging market conditions. Initial sales to Fiji were also recorded during the period, marking an early step in expanding Mirotone\u2019s export reach from New Zealand.\n\nMirotone Australia continued to advance its market re-entry, with its local facility and colour-matching infrastructure now fully established. Product R&D and market testing progressed during the period, while initial shipments supported product familiarisation and commercial discussions."
