    "md5": "d0dc4de5904b3af5852610eb2b008d5a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "Protest against PTA, proposed anti terrorism and organised crime Laws\n\nThe People\u2019s Struggle Alliance has staged a protest against the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), the proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and proposed legislation to prevent organised crime, calling on the Government to withdraw laws that could threaten the people's fundamental rights.\n\nRajkumar Rajeevkanth, an executive committee member of the People\u2019s Struggle Alliance, said the protest was held to protect human rights, democratic freedoms and the rule of law.\n\nHe said the group opposed laws that could give excessive powers to the police and security forces while weakening the role of the judiciary.\n\nSuch laws could lead to arbitrary arrests, long periods of detention and human rights violations, he said.\n\n\u201cMany people detained under the PTA have spent years in prison without being found guilty of any crime. Some were later released after losing valuable years of their lives, while their families and livelihoods were badly affected,\u201d he said.\n\nRajeevkanth said protecting national security and preventing organised crime were important responsibilities of the Government. However, these goals should not be achieved by violating people's basic rights.\n\nHe also criticised the Government for attempting to introduce new laws that could give authorities similar powers to those provided under legislation it had previously opposed.\n\n\u201cPolitical positions may change when governments change, but our principles must remain consistent,\u201d he said.\n\nThe alliance called on the Government to repeal the PTA without introducing another law with similar powers.\n\nIt also demanded the withdrawal of the proposed PSTA in its current form and called for public consultation and an independent human rights review before introducing any new anti-terrorism law."
  },
