    "md5": "f24af4e8bafcc7a788818235bb30d901",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Paraguay President Santiago Pe\u00f1a resigns after bank collapse\n\nBBC - Paraguay's President Santiago Pe\u00f1a has announced his resignation following a political crisis sparked by the collapse of a bank linked to him.\n\n\"I want to apologise to the people if at any point I failed,\" he said in a video message on social media. \"It was never my intention to betray the trust you placed in me.\"\n\nThe crisis erupted after Ueno Bank entered an insolvency process, which froze all operations. Lawmakers accused Pe\u00f1a, who was a shareholder until last year in the company that owned the bank, of conflicts of interest and misconduct.\n\nPe\u00f1a rejected suggestions of impropriety. His resignation came as Paraguay's lower house of congress voted in favour of pursuing an impeachment trial.\n\nPhotos taken near Paraguay's Congress building on Thursday show police officers clashing with protesters demanding Pe\u00f1a be removed from office.\n\nParaguay's congress must approve the resignation of a president. But a special session convened on Thursday to address his resignation was adjourned in favour of continuing with the impeachment process.\n\nCongress has scheduled a special session for Friday, when Pe\u00f1a is expected to present his defence, according to Reuters news agency.\n\nIn a post on X, Pe\u00f1a confirmed he would appear for Senate impeachment proceedings, adding that he would do so to \"exercise my right to a defence\".\n\nHe wrote: \"I will provide the information requested of me. Citizens deserve to know the truth, without cover-ups and without anyone being above the law.\"\n\nPe\u00f1a earlier said his resignation was to protect stability, social peace and democratic institutions.\n\nUeno Bank has said it has about three million customers, equivalent to about 60% of Paraguay's adult population.\n\nIt held pensions and some other public funds and, after its collapse earlier this week, millions could not access their accounts."
  },
