    "md5": "43b431e568b3b093b2d75b9aa06a2ae3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Raid Amazones returns to Sri Lanka after two-year break\n\nSri Lanka is hosting the world-renowned women-only adventure event, Raid Amazones, after a two-year break, with around 300 French participants taking part in a series of sporting activities in the country's southern region.\n\nThe event, which began on November 2, will continue until November 10, with Kalutara selected as the main destination for the 2026 edition. It aims to promote Sri Lanka's cultural heritage, beaches, natural landscapes and community life among international visitors.\n\nThe programme includes trail running, canoeing, cycling, running and treasure hunts, testing participants' endurance and physical strength.\n\nA canoeing competition was held on November 5, starting and ending at Malli's Seafood Restaurant. Minister of Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism Vijitha Herath attended the event and flagged off the competition, encouraging the participants.\n\nThe 2026 edition brings together around 300 French female contestants and 55 international staff members. Connaissance de Ceylan (Pvt) Ltd is the official ground-handling partner.\n\nThe delegation arrived at Bandaranaike International Airport on November 2 and was welcomed by officials of the Sri Lanka Tourism Information Centre with traditional dance performances. The organisers also highlighted that the flight carrying the delegation was operated entirely by female staff, reflecting the event's focus on women's empowerment.\n\nRaid Amazones is a women-only multi-sport adventure challenge founded in 2001 by French entrepreneur Alexandre Debanne of ZBO France. It brings together teams of women who compete in activities such as orienteering, mountain biking, canoeing, horse riding and running, while experiencing the culture and natural attractions of the host country.\n\nSri Lanka has previously hosted the event in 2004, 2009, 2018, 2019, 2022 and 2023, with each edition attracting more than 250 international participants."
  },
