    "md5": "593581dd7cc1b9c11add00d09ce294cb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "West Asian crisis lands cash strapped SriLankan Airlines in further turbulence\n\nColombo, November 6 (Daily Mirror)- The West Asian crisis, coupled with the resulting surge in fuel prices, has placed the cash-strapped national carrier, SriLankan Airlines (SLA), under further strain, with the authorities compiling a report on financial losses caused by the conflict alone, an official said.\n\nThe company recorded a loss before tax of Rs. 35.6 billion during the first quarter of the 2026/27 financial year, according to the mid-year financial report submitted by the Finance Ministry.\n\nAccording to the unaudited management accounts submitted by the company, total revenue during the period amounted to Rs. 82.8 billion, compared to Rs. 62.6 billion in the corresponding period of 2025/26.\n\nDuring the period under review, the escalation of the conflict in the Middle East disrupted air connectivity, with SriLankan Airlines suspending flights to several destinations in the region and subsequently resuming services as airspace restrictions were eased.\n\nThe disruption posed a significant risk to passenger and cargo revenue, particularly given the importance of Middle Eastern destinations to the airline's network. At the same time, the temporary diversion and rerouting of regional air traffic to Sri Lanka generated some additional aviation activity and passenger movements, partially mitigating the adverse impact on the sector.\n\nDespite the increase in revenue, the company continued to incur losses due to its operating deficit, high fuel and maintenance costs, foreign exchange losses and finance costs. These factors continued to constrain its ability to generate sufficient internal cash flows to meet operational and financial obligations.\n\nHowever, owing to an improved net income of Rs. 3.4 billion from SriLankan Catering, the net loss recorded by the group was reduced to Rs. 32.2 billion."
  },
