    "md5": "f0f216375bd975991157f38bfef878dc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "Despite the increase in revenue, the company continued to incur losses due to its operating deficit, high fuel and maintenance costs, foreign exchange losses and finance costs. These factors continued to constrain its ability to generate sufficient internal cash flows to meet operational and financial obligations.\n\nHowever, owing to an improved net income of Rs. 3.4 billion from SriLankan Catering, the net loss recorded by the group was reduced to Rs. 32.2 billion.\n\nDuring the first six months of 2026, the Government provided financial support to service the first and second instalments of selected Bank of Ceylon and People's Bank loans under the relevant tripartite arrangements. In addition, US$30 million was released to meet the airline's short-term funding requirements.\n\nFurther, following the restructuring of the US$175 million guaranteed bonds, the restructured bond value of US$126.5 million was absorbed into the Government's debt stock with effect from March 20, 2026.\n\nIn June 2026, the Cabinet approved the establishment of a Strategy and Restructuring Committee to undertake a comprehensive review of SriLankan Airlines, with the objective of strengthening its financial sustainability.\n\nAsked for comment, SriLankan Airlines Chief Executive Officer Vipul Misra said the West Asian crisis had also resulted in reduced availability of aviation fuel.\n\n\u201cWhen crude oil prices increase, refineries prioritise turning out more and more petrol and diesel. There is less focus on aviation fuel. When aviation fuel availability is reduced, it creates further price hikes, affecting the industry. It is not merely the rise in crude oil prices that has an impact,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said an assessment of the losses arising solely from the Middle East crisis had been compiled, and his office was awaiting its vetting."
  },
