    "language": "en",
    "md5": "221fdc8963816f121750df755e14bf30",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1549,
    "chunk_text": "CMTA calls for budget action to plug Rs. 120bn revenue leakage linked to 15% depreciation\n\nFrom left: Lakmal De Silva, Mahen Thambiah, Gahanath Pandithage, Andrew Perera, Nalin Welgama, Charaka Perera, Tarindra Kaluperuma, and Jawahar Ganesh\n\nThe Ceylon Motor Traders\u2019 Association (CMTA) is urging the government to take decisive action in the upcoming national budget to abolish the 15 percent depreciation concession granted on used vehicle imports, in order to address significant government revenue losses and establish a unified tax structure across the automotive sector.\n\nDespite the issue being raised through previous budget proposals submitted via the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce, the necessary policy change remains outstanding. With the upcoming budget presenting another opportunity to address this long-standing concern, the CMTA stresses that the government urges to prioritise the matter this year to safeguard public revenue, eliminate existing market distortions and ensure a fairer, more transparent vehicle taxation system in the country.\n\nThe Association estimates that government revenue leakage arising from the current depreciation mechanism amounted to approximately Rs. 40 billion in 2025, rising to an estimated Rs. 120 billion by September 2026. This substantial increase highlights the urgency of addressing the issue, as continued revenue losses place further pressure on public finances at a time when government revenue is critical to supporting economic recovery, national development and essential public services."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailymirrorlk-55f00072",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-55f00072",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "CMTA calls for budget action to plug Rs. 120bn revenue leakage linked to 15% depreciation",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/CMTA-calls-for-budget-action-to-plug-Rs-120bn-revenue-leakage-linked-to-15-depreciation/215-354326",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793948216.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailymirrorlk-55f00072-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "412bf75fa296acbbbe255956906a35e5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "The Association estimates that government revenue leakage arising from the current depreciation mechanism amounted to approximately Rs. 40 billion in 2025, rising to an estimated Rs. 120 billion by September 2026. This substantial increase highlights the urgency of addressing the issue, as continued revenue losses place further pressure on public finances at a time when government revenue is critical to supporting economic recovery, national development and essential public services.\n\nThe CMTA maintains that abolishing this concession should form part of a broader effort to establish a unified and equitable tax structure for vehicle imports. A consistent approach to taxation would help eliminate preferential treatment between market segments, reduce opportunities for misuse and create a more transparent and predictable operating environment for importers, consumers and the Government.\n\nThe Association also calls on the authorities to strengthen oversight and take decisive action against those exploiting the existing framework for personal gain. Closing loopholes and addressing practices that undermine the intended application of import duties are essential to protecting government revenue and maintaining the integrity of Sri Lanka\u2019s automotive trade.\n\nWhile recognising the importance of vehicle affordability, the CMTA emphasises that this objective should not be pursued through selective tax concessions that distort the market and impose a significant cost on the Treasury. If reducing the cost of vehicle ownership is a policy priority, the Association believes that any such measures should be considered within a broader, consistent taxation framework rather than through benefits that favour one category of vehicle imports over another."
  },
