    "md5": "d709928a673d65a6c1960bfdf5927e77",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1827,
    "chunk_text": "While recognising the importance of vehicle affordability, the CMTA emphasises that this objective should not be pursued through selective tax concessions that distort the market and impose a significant cost on the Treasury. If reducing the cost of vehicle ownership is a policy priority, the Association believes that any such measures should be considered within a broader, consistent taxation framework rather than through benefits that favour one category of vehicle imports over another.\n\nThe CMTA has previously advocated a more structured approach to depreciation, including a framework proposed as far back as 2013 and then continuously proposing same to every government up to date, under which depreciation would be determined according to vehicle age rather than applied as a flat-rate concession. The proposal envisaged a defined scale with depreciation capped at a maximum of 10%providing a more transparent and equitable basis for calculating vehicle import duties.\n\nWhile the Association continues to advocate the abolition of the existing 15 percent concession, it maintains that, should the Government decide to retain a depreciation mechanism, it must be based on a clearly defined and consistently applied framework that reflects vehicle age and is subject to a reasonable cap. Such an approach would help minimise arbitrary tax advantages while improving transparency and protecting state revenue.\n\nAs the Government prepares its upcoming budget, the CMTA reiterates that this issue requires urgent attention and can no longer be deferred. Addressing the depreciation concession and establishing a unified vehicle taxation structure would represent an important step towards reducing revenue leakages, strengthening public finances and ensuring a level playing field across Sri Lanka\u2019s automotive sector."
