    "md5": "38b32fb7f2aeec55716eb7696e5a6e53",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1773,
    "chunk_text": "Fitch revises SLT to \u2018AA+(lka)\u2019 on national scale recalibration\n\nSri Lanka Telecom PLC (SLT) has been revised two notches higher to \u2018AA+(lka)\u2019 from \u2018AA-(lka)\u2019 with a Stable Outlook, as the recalibration of Sri Lanka\u2019s national rating scale lifted the telecom operator, whose rating remains tied to the sovereign, Fitch Ratings said.\n\nThe recalibration followed Fitch\u2019s upgrade of Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign Long-Term Local-Currency Issuer Default Rating to \u2018B-\u2019 from \u2018CCC+\u2019 on 22 September. The agency expects no other Fitch-rated corporate to see a rating change on account of the recalibration, making SLT the only one to move on the exercise.\n\nFitch also resolved the Rating Watch Positive on SLT after the state decided not to proceed with the sale of its stake in the company, a plan by the previous administration that was formally abandoned following a Supreme Court ruling in April. The agency believes this reflects a strategic shift by the state to \u201cretain and strengthen SLT as a key national asset\u201d rather than privatise it.\n\nHowever, SLT\u2019s ratings remain constrained by the weaker credit profile of the state, its parent, Fitch noted, even though the company\u2019s standalone credit profile is stronger than that of the state. The agency attributes SLT\u2019s rating, a notch below Dialog Axiata PLC\u2019s \u2018AAA(lka)\u2019, mainly to this sovereign drag.\n\nMeanwhile, Fitch forecasts SLT\u2019s EBITDA margin to widen to 38 percent in 2026 from 34 percent in 2025, on revenue growth it expects to accelerate to 9 percent from 2 percent, along with cost controls. EBITDA net leverage is projected to fall to around 0.9x from 1.6x, while cash interest expenses are forecast to ease by about 10 percent to Rs.5.2 billion in 2026 from Rs.5.8 billion."
  },
