    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "JKH sees variable casino rent kicking in from next year\n\nRental and other income bring in Rs.364mn as hotel posts Rs.24mn EBITDA loss\n\nCity of Dreams pre-tax loss narrows 65% to Rs.Rs.1.19bn on lower interest and exchange gain\n\nJohn Keells Holdings (JKH) believes the variable rent component from the City of Dreams Sri Lanka casino could become payable starting next year, as earnings from the integrated resort\u2019s rental and other components more than offset an EBITDA loss at its hotel during the September quarter, according to its top management.\n\nSpeaking at the group\u2019s investor webinar on Wednesday, JKH Deputy Chairperson and Group Finance Director Gihan Cooray declined to disclose the performance threshold that triggers the variable payment, but indicated that gaming operations were approaching that point.\n\n\u201cIf you look at the run rate or the momentum that we are seeing in our current gaming operations, we are coming into that point where we are seeing good momentum. It\u2019s reached a certain scale and if that momentum continues, there\u2019s no reason to believe why from maybe next year onwards we could potentially start seeing that,\u201d Cooray remarked, cautioning that giving an exact view was difficult.\n\nJKH, through its subsidiary Waterfront Properties (Private) Limited, leases the casino space to Melco Resorts & Entertainment and has recognised only the fixed rental since the casino opened in August 2025.\n\nCity of Dreams Sri Lanka recorded an EBITDA of Rs.340 million for the quarter ended 30 September, against a loss of Rs.64 million a year earlier, according to JKH\u2019s interim financial statements filed with the Colombo Stock Exchange. However, the figure was 12.6 percent lower than the Rs.389 million posted in the June quarter. For the first half, the resort\u2019s EBITDA stood at Rs.729 million, a turnaround of Rs.1.79 billion from the Rs.1.06 billion loss recorded in the same period last year."
