    "chunk_text": "City of Dreams Sri Lanka recorded an EBITDA of Rs.340 million for the quarter ended 30 September, against a loss of Rs.64 million a year earlier, according to JKH\u2019s interim financial statements filed with the Colombo Stock Exchange. However, the figure was 12.6 percent lower than the Rs.389 million posted in the June quarter. For the first half, the resort\u2019s EBITDA stood at Rs.729 million, a turnaround of Rs.1.79 billion from the Rs.1.06 billion loss recorded in the same period last year.\n\nHotel occupancy edged up to 45 percent from 44 percent in the June quarter, while the average room rate rose 7.4 percent to US$ 116 from US$ 108. Even so, the hotel\u2019s EBITDA margin slipped to negative 0.7 percent from 2.4 percent, which JKH attributed to higher electricity and ramp-up costs.\n\nCooray also pointed to one-off charges, including online travel agent commissions and IT costs.\n\n\u201cIt was marginally negative at that number, so a slight increase in either occupancy or ARR will bring it to a point where we can break even,\u201d Cooray explained, adding that occupancy of 50 to 60 percent would turn the hotel into a positive contributor. The hotel had recorded 61 percent occupancy in the March quarter, before the full impact of the Middle East conflict was felt.\n\nMeanwhile, Melco\u2019s results for the same three months showed its Colombo operations generating revenue of US$ 17.6 million, nearly three times the US$ 6.1 million recorded a year earlier, when the casino had been open for only two months. Revenue was also 4.1 percent higher than the US$ 16.9 million in the previous quarter. Adjusted EBITDA before rent rose 14.3 percent quarter-on-quarter to US$ 4.0 million. The rent expense Melco booked for the Colombo casino stood at US$ 1.83 million, up just 2.2 percent from US$ 1.79 million a year earlier, which is consistent with only the fixed component being paid."
