    "chunk_size_bytes": 1741,
    "chunk_text": "Democrats\u2019 sweep cools oil, opening window for rupee and rate relief:\u00a0 FCR\n\nBrent falls about 20% to US$ 93/bbl from late-October peak of US$ 117\n\nLower fuel import bill seen narrowing trade deficit, easing fuel and power prices\n\nOutlook hinges on progress in US-Iran talks over Strait of Hormuz\n\nThe slide in oil prices after the Democrats\u2019 sweep of the US Congress and a hung parliament in Israel is set to trim Sri Lanka\u2019s fuel import bill and ease pressure on the rupee and interest rates, provided progress towards reopening the Strait of Hormuz holds, First Capital Research (FCR) said yesterday.\n\nFollowing the escalation of US-Israel operations against Iran earlier this year, \u201c2026 has become a year in which geopolitics has driven the pricing of risk,\u201d the research arm of Janashakthi Group-owned First Capital Holdings PLC noted in a flash note.\n\nBrent crude has fallen by about US$ 24 a barrel, or roughly 20.5 percent, from its late-October peak of nearly US$ 117 to US$ 93, a move the research house attributed to markets pricing in a lower probability of further escalation in the Middle East. On Wednesday alone, Brent futures shed 8.7 percent to settle at US$ 94.62 a barrel. Even so, the benchmark remains around 40 percent above the mid-US$ 60s level at which it began the year, before strikes on Iran in late February and the temporary closure of the Strait of Hormuz drove it past US$ 100.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, a sustained decline in Brent would reduce fuel import costs, narrow the trade deficit and ease pressure on domestic fuel and electricity prices, while lending support to the rupee, which the research house pointed out has weakened 6.8 percent this year, largely on account of higher energy import costs."
