    "chunk_text": "For Sri Lanka, a sustained decline in Brent would reduce fuel import costs, narrow the trade deficit and ease pressure on domestic fuel and electricity prices, while lending support to the rupee, which the research house pointed out has weakened 6.8 percent this year, largely on account of higher energy import costs.\n\nLower energy prices would also ease inflation risks and \u201cstrengthen the case for reduced pressure on interest rates,\u201d the research house added. The Central Bank raised its Overnight Policy Rate by 100 basis points to 8.75 percent in May, while inflation reached a three-year high of 8 percent in August. The note flagged the Monetary Policy Review on 20 November as the next domestic development to watch.\n\nFirst Capital Research is maintaining its \u201cstay short, add the belly\u201d fixed-income strategy, which favours short-dated securities while adding exposure to the middle of the yield curve, and continues to prefer sectors most leveraged to lower interest rates and energy costs.\n\nThe Colombo bourse rallied yesterday amid optimism over the US election outcome. The All Share Price Index gained 589.18 points, or 2.87 percent, to close above the 21,000 mark at 21,130.30, while the S&P SL20 rose 3.34 percent to 6,029.88, as per CSE data. Turnover reached Rs.5.43 billion, nearly six times the Rs.921 million recorded on Monday. The rupee, by contrast, closed marginally weaker at Rs.332.75/80 against the US dollar on Wednesday, according to dealers."
