    "chunk_text": "The Colombo bourse rallied yesterday amid optimism over the US election outcome. The All Share Price Index gained 589.18 points, or 2.87 percent, to close above the 21,000 mark at 21,130.30, while the S&P SL20 rose 3.34 percent to 6,029.88, as per CSE data. Turnover reached Rs.5.43 billion, nearly six times the Rs.921 million recorded on Monday. The rupee, by contrast, closed marginally weaker at Rs.332.75/80 against the US dollar on Wednesday, according to dealers.\n\nIn the US, the Democrats\u2019 capture of both the House and the Senate at the 3 November midterms enhances congressional oversight of Middle East policy, which, the note observed, could make it \u201cmore difficult for the administration to escalate tensions with Iran without congressional scrutiny or support.\u201d\n\nIsrael\u2019s 27 October election, meanwhile, left Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu\u2019s bloc with 53 seats, down 11 from 64 in 2022 and eight short of the 61 required to govern. First Capital Research anticipates protracted coalition formation, reinforcing expectations of a cautious approach to Iran.\n\nHowever, the note cautioned that \u201cthe de-escalation narrative remains contingent on progress in Hormuz negotiations,\u201d with the Qatar- and Pakistan-mediated US-Iran talks seen as the key determinant of Brent\u2019s trajectory.\n\nA prolonged Israeli coalition impasse, renewed friction between the White House and Congress or delays to a Hormuz agreement could keep geopolitical risk premia elevated, it warned.\n\n\u201cA reopening of the Strait would remove a significant geopolitical risk premium from oil markets,\u201d First Capital Research stated. It projected that this could push Brent towards US$ 90 a barrel or below over the medium term, a level only around 3 percent beneath current prices. (NF)"
