    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Israel president to announce Sunday who will form new government\n\nAl Jazeera, Oct 6- Israel\u2019s President Isaac Herzog has said he will name a candidate to form the country\u2019s next government by Sunday, after a majority of lawmakers backed opposition leader Gadi Eisenkot over Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.\n\nHerzog made the announcement on Thursday after holding marathon talks with parties elected to the 120-seat parliament, known as the Knesset.\n\nSome 67 lawmakers endorsed Eisenkot, a former military chief, while 46 backed Netanyahu.\n\nIn televised remarks at the end of the talks, Herzog said that although the \u201cresults of the consultations may appear clear\u201d, there were \u201cmutual vetoes between the blocs and within the blocs themselves\u201d that make it difficult to say who has the best chance of assembling a governing coalition.\n\nHe said he would hold \u201cpersonal talks with some of the party leaders\u201d over the Israeli weekend, which runs Friday-Saturday, and make a pick on Sunday.\n\nIsrael\u2019s October 27 election left neither Netanyahu nor Eisenkot with a clear path to a parliamentary majority, handing Herzog the difficult task of choosing a candidate in a deeply divided country.\n\nNetanyahu\u2019s Likud won the most seats of any single party with 27 in the 120-seat Knesset, \u2060but his wider religious-nationalist coalition garnered only 53 in total.\n\nEisenkot\u2019s alliance of four Jewish-led parties, which spans the political spectrum from centre-left to right, won 48 seats despite polls that had predicted a close race.\n\nTwo parties representing Israel\u2019s Palestinian minority won an unprecedented 19 seats.\n\nEisenkot, like Netanyahu, has ruled out forming a government with the Arab-led parties. Both of those parties nonetheless recommended him, giving him the support of a comfortable majority of lawmakers."
  },
