    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "Eisenkot, like Netanyahu, has ruled out forming a government with the Arab-led parties. Both of those parties nonetheless recommended him, giving him the support of a comfortable majority of lawmakers.\n\nEisenkot\u2019s party, Yashar, said his 67 recommendations were the most any candidate had received since 2015, and urged Herzog to give him the mandate \u201cas soon as possible\u201d so he could form a national emergency government.\n\nSeparately, Netanyahu suffered a setback on Thursday when United Torah Judaism, one of the two main ultra-Orthodox parties and a longtime ally, declined to recommend any candidate.\n\nThe party broke with Netanyahu over his coalition\u2019s failure to pass legislation exempting ultra-Orthodox men from military service, The Times of Israel reported.\n\nYariv Oppenheimer, a member of the Democrats, a party in Eisenkot\u2019s alliance, told Al Jazeera that he saw \u201cno reason\u201d Herzog would not choose Eisenkot on Sunday.\n\nHe said United Torah Judaism had signalled that its support was available for the right price, meaning that a good enough offer from Eisenkot could give him the numbers he needed.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s very clear that Netanyahu doesn\u2019t have a chance to establish a government at all,\u201d he added.\n\nOnce nominated by Herzog, the candidate will have 28 days to try to forge a government, with a possible 14-day extension.\n\nNetanyahu will remain prime minister in a caretaker government until a new coalition is formed, or through another election that must be held if no \u2060candidate succeeds.\n\nNetanyahu, at risk of losing power, has seized on an investigation by election officials into possible vote tampering to cast doubt on the results, accusing Arab-led parties of stealing the vote.\n\nCritics accuse Netanyahu and his partners of trying to undermine the results, which he denies."
