    "chunk_size_bytes": 1310,
    "chunk_text": "India amends visa rules for Pakistan, Sri Lanka\n\nIndia has introduced new passport and visa rules for foreigners. The changes cover select Pakistani citizens, minority refugees from Sri Lankan Tamils, Tibetans, Afghanistan, Bangladesh and Pakistan.\n\nSeparate travel relaxations have been issued for Nepalese and Bhutanese citizens. The new amendments provide relief to those who cannot renew their travel documents or were allowed to enter India under certain circumstances.\n\nThe renewed provisions are part of the Immigration and Foreigners (Exemption) (Amendment) Order, 2026, published by the Union Home Ministry on November 5.\n\nAccording to a PTI report, the exemptions apply to specific conditions, and several categories of people will still need to obtain a relevant visa if they want to continue their stay in India.\n\nRegistered Sri Lankan Tamil citizens who sought refuge in India on or before January 9, 2015, have also been granted relief from the standard passport and visa requirements under the amended order.\n\nThese include Tibetans who entered India with permits issued by the Indian embassy in Kathmandu between 1959 and May 30, 2003.\n\nThe relief also covers those who arrived after that date using special entry permits through designated immigration checkpoints along the India-Nepal border."
