    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Yes. Colombo averages 345.8mm of rain for the whole of November (1991 to 2020 average). Friday evening delivered 37% of that in a few hours, and about 5% of the city's average yearly rainfall of 2,397mm.\n\nSpeed matters more than the total. If the 129mm fell over three hours, that is an average of 43mm an hour, and the heaviest bursts would have been stronger.\n\nOne 100-square-metre roof sheds close to 13,000 litres in an evening like Friday's. Add every roof on a lane, plus the lane itself, and the drain at the bottom of the road is dealing with a swimming pool's worth of water in a few hours.\n\nThe Lanka Rain Water Harvesting Forum's calculator estimates a roof's harvest with this formula: runoff coefficient \u00d7 roof area (m\u00b2) \u00d7 rainfall (mm) \u00f7 1,000 = cubic metres of water. The runoff coefficient is the share of rain that reaches the gutter, and it changes with the roof type. Taking 80% as a typical figure, a 100 square metre roof would have collected about 10,300 litres on Friday.\n\nThe same calculator puts basic needs at 25 litres per person a day: 4 litres for drinking, 1 for cooking and 20 for sanitation. A family of four uses 100 litres a day on that basis, so one evening's rain from one roof would cover them for about 103 days.\n\nYou do not need to store all of it. A single 1,000-litre tank fills from the first 13mm or so of a storm like this. That is ten days of basic water for a family of four, and 1,000 litres kept off the road outside.\n\nWork out your own number. Measure your roof (length \u00d7 width in metres) and multiply by the rainfall figure in the news. That is how many litres your home sends to the drain.\n\nHold the water back. A gutter feeding a tank, a barrel or a soakage pit keeps water off the road at the peak of the storm.\n\nClear your stretch of drain. Polythene, leaves and building sand reduce how much water a roadside drain can carry.\n\nLeave some ground open. A strip of grass or gravel lets part of the rain soak in where it falls."
