    "chunk_text": "Updated:129mm of rain in a few hours: what it really means for Colombo\n\nNov 6 (Daily Mirror) - Colombo received 129mm of rainfall within a few hours on Friday evening (November 6), according to the Department of Meteorology. Several roads flooded, and traffic across the capital slowed to a crawl during the evening rush hour. The 129mm is a reading from the Met Department's Colombo station, so not every part of the city would have received the same amount; some areas will have had more rain and others less.\n\nThe figure is easy to read out on the news and hard to picture. Put simply, 129mm of rain means 129 litres of water landed on every square metre of the city. That is six and a half 20-litre water cans emptied onto a patch the size of a small bathroom floor.\n\nThe Disaster Management Centre (DMC) has released rainfall figures for the period ending at 6 am today. Thimbirigasyaya recorded the highest rainfall in Colombo, 146.6mm, which was also the highest reading in the DMC\u2019s report for the whole country. Mattakkuliya followed with 125.5mm. Piliyandala recorded 68mm in the same period.\n\nThat means every square metre in Thimbirigasyaya received 146.6 litres of water, more than seven 20-litre water cans. A 100 m\u00b2 house roof there took 14,660 litres, about 7,900 litres more than the same roof in Piliyandala. The figures confirm that the rain did not fall evenly across Colombo.\n\nMattakkuliya recorded the second-highest rainfall of 125.5 mm, followed by Bopagama with 117.6 mm.\n\nOne millimetre of rain is one litre of water on one square metre of ground. Rainfall is measured as depth: how high the water would stand on flat ground if none of it drained away or soaked in.\n\nThat gives you a rule you can use any time the Met Department issues a figure:\n\nOn Friday, 129mm would have stood 12.9cm deep on any flat surface that could not drain, about the height of a tea mug."
