    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-9c0f574c",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9c0f574c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "Is AKD running faster than the winds that buffet him?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Is-AKD-running-faster-than-the-winds-that-buffet-him/4-799740",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793985888.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-9c0f574c-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9e08e5ce2f0acfa3e6c28b22cf022870",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "Is AKD running faster than the winds that buffet him?\n\nPresident Dissanayake still has an opportunity to demonstrate that the NPP\u2019s original commitments were more than an electoral promise. The NPP came to power promising to dismantle a system characterised by concentrated political power, weakened accountability and institutional privilege. It must now decide whether it intends to transform that system or merely occupy it. The greatest danger for the NPP is not necessarily losing power at the next election. It is losing the democratic purpose and moral credibility that brought it to power in the first place\n\nThe paradox of political renewal, coercive power and an unfinished economic recovery\n\nThe late Dr. Colvin R. de Silva, the distinguished constitutional lawyer, Marxist politician and political thinker, once made a memorable observation about his contemporary and former classmate, J.R. Jayewardene, architect of Sri Lanka\u2019s Executive Presidency and the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA): \u201cThe problem with Dickie is that often he runs faster than the winds that buffet him.\u201d Veteran journalist Sarath De Alwis recently recalled this observation in the Daily FT.\n\nWatching President Anura Kumara Dissanayake (AKD) navigate the complexities of governance, one is tempted to ask the same question: Is AKD running faster than the winds that buffet him?\n\nThere is an uncomfortable irony in Sri Lankan politics. The National People\u2019s Power (NPP), elected in 2024 on an extraordinary mandate for democratic renewal, institutional accountability and the abolition of oppressive legislation, is now proposing two controversial Bills: the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill (PSTA) and the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCA). Both have generated serious concerns among constitutional lawyers, human rights organisations and civil society."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-9c0f574c",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9c0f574c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "Is AKD running faster than the winds that buffet him?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Is-AKD-running-faster-than-the-winds-that-buffet-him/4-799740",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793985888.0,
