    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "38905abb012effdd34c6e63c954f285a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1856,
    "chunk_text": "There is an uncomfortable irony in Sri Lankan politics. The National People\u2019s Power (NPP), elected in 2024 on an extraordinary mandate for democratic renewal, institutional accountability and the abolition of oppressive legislation, is now proposing two controversial Bills: the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill (PSTA) and the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCA). Both have generated serious concerns among constitutional lawyers, human rights organisations and civil society.\n\nFrom abolishing oppressive laws to creating new instruments of coercion\n\nThe NPP\u2019s electoral appeal rested substantially on breaking with the political practices of the past. Its manifesto promised to abolish oppressive legislation, protect fundamental rights and establish accountable democratic governance.\n\nThe PTA, introduced in 1979 as a temporary measure, became one of the most controversial instruments of State power in Sri Lanka\u2019s modern history. Successive administrations used it in circumstances involving arbitrary arrests, prolonged detention and the suppression of dissent. The JVP itself experienced the consequences of State repression and emergency legislation.\n\nYet the proposed replacement legislation raises an uncomfortable question: has the Government genuinely reconsidered the architecture of coercive State power, or is it simply seeking to administer that architecture under different statutory names?\n\nThe Centre for Policy Alternatives and the Bar Association of Sri Lanka have raised serious concerns about the Bills, particularly their broad provisions, detention powers and potential restrictions on fundamental freedoms. On 30 October 2026, the UN Human Rights Office called for substantive redrafting of the PSTA, warning of provisions that could reproduce or expand weaknesses associated with the previous legislation."
  },
