    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3b7a756bd36a3dc29ce41a25be325c4a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1943,
    "chunk_text": "The Centre for Policy Alternatives and the Bar Association of Sri Lanka have raised serious concerns about the Bills, particularly their broad provisions, detention powers and potential restrictions on fundamental freedoms. On 30 October 2026, the UN Human Rights Office called for substantive redrafting of the PSTA, warning of provisions that could reproduce or expand weaknesses associated with the previous legislation.\n\nA democratic Government cannot rely merely on assurances that extraordinary powers will not be abused. It must establish safeguards that prevent their misuse by this administration or any Government that follows.\n\nThe contradiction extends beyond security legislation. The JVP and NPP spent years condemning the concentration of executive authority under the presidential system and promising its abolition. Yet, the present administration continues to exercise the considerable powers of that very institution, reinforced by its parliamentary supermajority.\n\nConstitutional reform requires careful preparation and public consultation. Delay alone does not establish abandonment of a commitment. Nevertheless, there is a fundamental difference between taking time to design a better constitutional order and allowing inherited executive authority to become politically convenient.\n\nThe real test of democratic renewal is not how effectively a Government exercises power when it commands a parliamentary majority, but whether it is prepared to establish institutions capable of restraining its own authority.\n\nPolitical movements can gradually become protective of the very institutional privileges they once challenged. The danger is that the practical demands of governing may turn temporary concentrations of power into permanent political conveniences.\n\nThere is, however, an equally important question that deserves greater attention: what is happening to the economic transformation that brought the NPP to power?"
