    "md5": "a1f01d7770413b9ff9f52dbcf71aa377",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "Political movements can gradually become protective of the very institutional privileges they once challenged. The danger is that the practical demands of governing may turn temporary concentrations of power into permanent political conveniences.\n\nThere is, however, an equally important question that deserves greater attention: what is happening to the economic transformation that brought the NPP to power?\n\nTo be fair, the Government has maintained important elements of macroeconomic stabilisation. According to the World Bank\u2019s October 2026 Sri Lanka Development Update, the economy expanded by 4.7% in the first half of 2026, with projected growth of 4.4% in 2026 and 4.2% in 2027. Real GDP has returned to pre-crisis levels.\n\nThese achievements deserve recognition. Nevertheless, the same assessment estimates poverty at 16.9% in 2025, substantially above pre-crisis levels. Household welfare, real incomes and employment opportunities continue to lag behind the broader recovery.\n\nThis is the distinction the Government must confront: macroeconomic stabilisation is not synonymous with economic prosperity.\n\nA fiscal surplus does not automatically translate into better hospitals, functioning schools or completed infrastructure. Higher tax revenue does not necessarily create employment. Debt restructuring does not, by itself, restore household purchasing power.\n\nThe 2027 Budget, scheduled for presentation on 12 November, is therefore a critical test. Sri Lanka needs more than revenue mobilisation and fiscal consolidation. It needs productive public investment, stronger exports, private-sector confidence, technological advancement, employment generation and meaningful relief for households struggling with living costs.\n\nEconomic discipline must be a foundation for development, not an end in itself."
