    "chunk_text": "The 2027 Budget, scheduled for presentation on 12 November, is therefore a critical test. Sri Lanka needs more than revenue mobilisation and fiscal consolidation. It needs productive public investment, stronger exports, private-sector confidence, technological advancement, employment generation and meaningful relief for households struggling with living costs.\n\nEconomic discipline must be a foundation for development, not an end in itself.\n\nEmeritus Professor of Economics at the University of Peradeniya, Sumanasiri Liyanage, recently explored this question in the Daily FT,\n\ndrawing upon Italian political economist Clara E. Mattei\u2019s influential book, The Capital Order: How Economists Invented Austerity and Paved the Way to Fascism.\n\nMattei\u2019s historical analysis examines how austerity policies and coercive political institutions can reinforce one another when economic restructuring generates resistance. Her argument is not that every program of fiscal consolidation is authoritarian, but that economic policies imposing unequal social costs can, under certain political circumstances, be supported by increasingly restrictive instruments of State power.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s circumstances are not identical to those of Fascist Italy, and simplistic historical comparisons would be intellectually careless. Nevertheless, the underlying institutional question deserves attention.\n\nWorkers must retain their right to organise, trade unions must be free to negotiate, journalists must be able to investigate, and citizens must be able to protest peacefully. The State must distinguish genuine terrorism and organised criminality from democratic opposition, industrial action and legitimate public criticism.\n\nIf security legislation creates an environment in which resistance to economic policies becomes increasingly difficult, the consequences would extend far beyond individual civil liberties. They would undermine the democratic foundations upon which economic and social reform must rest."
