    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "The new age of ecologically empathetic design\n\nFor Sri Lankans, design inspiration is easily sourced, as every remaining precolonial complex is an example of ecologically empathetic design. The only obstacle is our own mental conditioning; unfortunately, even after three quarters of a century of colonial absence, for the majority of Sri Lankans, Western design is their default style\n\nThe Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award, represents a significant shift from its previous stream, of celebrating independent architects. This big and vital step, will recognise the contribution of multiple skills and knowledge to achieve ecological empathy\n\nSince the dawn of civilisation, humans have been talking about developing a better future. Yet, in the memory of every human living today, the quality of life on this planet has been plummeting quite rapidly. The foreground for today\u2019s global decline is an environmental crisis, birthed by our skewed definition of development. If anything is tagged \u2018Development\u2019 we blindly destroy the environment; confidently disrupt rivers, carve hillsides and obliterate entire ecosystems. Despite two centuries of practicing development designs intended to beat the environment into submission, we are yet to arrive at this ephemeral \u2018better future\u2019.\n\nPerhaps, if we borrow a design page from the past\u2026 and marry it to our sophisticated technology, we will find a hybrid solution for a \u2018better future\u2019. A new design ethos that humbly, acknowledges the tremendous power of Nature, is long overdue. Recognition of an ecologically empathetic approach to designing every type of built environment, and the policies that enforce them, is needed. Where better to seed this revised approach to built environments than, through the Trust of the doyen of Sri Lankan architecture, the late Geoffrey Bawa. The Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award hopes to inspire this alternate approach to design, from policy to buildings and infrastructure."
