    "chunk_text": "Perhaps, if we borrow a design page from the past\u2026 and marry it to our sophisticated technology, we will find a hybrid solution for a \u2018better future\u2019. A new design ethos that humbly, acknowledges the tremendous power of Nature, is long overdue. Recognition of an ecologically empathetic approach to designing every type of built environment, and the policies that enforce them, is needed. Where better to seed this revised approach to built environments than, through the Trust of the doyen of Sri Lankan architecture, the late Geoffrey Bawa. The Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award hopes to inspire this alternate approach to design, from policy to buildings and infrastructure.\n\nAncient Sri Lanka\u2019s elegant pavilions, rainwater-harvesting ponds and multiple courtyards were purposefully woven into the canvas of natural environments. Our ancients comprehended nature, beyond the superficial; forests were catchments, ground water was a feeder for cascading reservoirs, rock outcrops were soil stabilisers and prefab hard builds. Ponds were the monsoon\u2019s sponges, urban heat islands were defeated by open paving, courtyards and wooded parklands, water flowed unimpeded beneath raised pavilions\u2026. This is the language of design that we have lost. We also forgot that natural elements are most beneficial, if they are allowed to remain intact or even enhanced."
