    "chunk_text": "Ancient Sri Lanka\u2019s elegant pavilions, rainwater-harvesting ponds and multiple courtyards were purposefully woven into the canvas of natural environments. Our ancients comprehended nature, beyond the superficial; forests were catchments, ground water was a feeder for cascading reservoirs, rock outcrops were soil stabilisers and prefab hard builds. Ponds were the monsoon\u2019s sponges, urban heat islands were defeated by open paving, courtyards and wooded parklands, water flowed unimpeded beneath raised pavilions\u2026. This is the language of design that we have lost. We also forgot that natural elements are most beneficial, if they are allowed to remain intact or even enhanced.\n\nThe Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award, also represents a significant shift from its previous stream, of celebrating independent architects. This big and vital step, will recognise the contribution of multiple skills and knowledge to achieve ecological empathy. The Turkish Nobel laureate, Orhan Parmuk, in My Name is Red, writes of a philosophical conflict between painters of the Ottoman court where fundamentalists condemn the West\u2019s emphasis on individual style. This is the discrepancy, which widened between Eastern and Western perspectives. Eventually, as Western civilisations over-ran older cultures, the celebration of individuals, the soloists who created monumental architecture, rather than deep design through a pooling of knowledge and skills, became the norm."
