    "chunk_text": "The Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award, also represents a significant shift from its previous stream, of celebrating independent architects. This big and vital step, will recognise the contribution of multiple skills and knowledge to achieve ecological empathy. The Turkish Nobel laureate, Orhan Parmuk, in My Name is Red, writes of a philosophical conflict between painters of the Ottoman court where fundamentalists condemn the West\u2019s emphasis on individual style. This is the discrepancy, which widened between Eastern and Western perspectives. Eventually, as Western civilisations over-ran older cultures, the celebration of individuals, the soloists who created monumental architecture, rather than deep design through a pooling of knowledge and skills, became the norm.\n\nWho will succeed, will be the designers and developers who interpose rather than impose on landscapes. Advocates of the environment and team players, who read the land and accept that no single individual has the comprehensive knowledge and skills to successfully design and achieve ecological harmony. What is required by architects and developers today, is an understanding that we are now in the age of ecologically empathetic design. This will be the future of global design.\n\n(The author\u2019s\u00a0 design projects include; Jetwing Vil Uyana, Jetwing Kaduruketha, Teardrop\u2019s Camellia Hills etc. She was the first Chair of the Federation of Environmental Organisations, is a member of the Steering Committee of the Geoffrey Bawa Moonamal Award and the author of The Line of Lanka and Ravana\u2019s Lanka. Now retired, she lives in her rewilded monsoon forest, in Central Sri Lanka)"
