    "md5": "c14e4143fcd8ff4f98af94058f8f5b77",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "The Monk, UNP and Eightfold Path\n\nUltimately, the question is not simply whether we support or oppose a particular pardon. It is whether we apply the same standards of compassion, fairness, accountability and lawful conduct when the individual concerned is someone we admire, someone we oppose, or someone whose cause offers political advantage. That is a test not only for the monk or the politician, but for all of us. It is also an opportunity to demonstrate that Buddhist ethical principles can inform public life without displacing the rule of law\n\nA monk\u2019s robe should not confer immunity from accountability. Equally, a monk\u2019s conviction should not justify hostility towards Buddhism or the Sangha as a whole. Political advocacy for clemency is legitimate within the democratic process, but its ethical quality depends on the principles and methods employed. Any Presidential decision must, in turn, satisfy the applicable constitutional requirements\n\nMy article, Beyond the Robe: Sangha, Rule of Law and Monastic Accountability, (https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beyond-the-robe-Sangha-rule-of-law-and-monastic-accountability/4-799100), published on\u00a0 Saturday, 24 October examined the relationship between Buddhist monastic discipline, institutional responsibility and the rule of law. Subsequently the Daily FT reported that the United National Party (UNP) has \"demanded\" an immediate Presidential pardon for Ven. Galagodaaththe Gnanasara Thera\u00a0 (https://www.ft.lk/news/UNP-demands-immediate-presidential-pardon-for-Gnanasara-Thera/56-799219). This would be the second Presidential pardon, that the UNP will be associated with. However, it is about a previous pardon that has been invalidated by the Supreme Court. This presents a further opportunity to elaborate upon what I referred to in the previous article. That is: How might the ethical principles of Buddhism guide political advocacy and the exercise of public power?"
  },
