    "md5": "740bc641dc8384a182a180d751ab16db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "My article, Beyond the Robe: Sangha, Rule of Law and Monastic Accountability, (https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beyond-the-robe-Sangha-rule-of-law-and-monastic-accountability/4-799100), published on\u00a0 Saturday, 24 October examined the relationship between Buddhist monastic discipline, institutional responsibility and the rule of law. Subsequently the Daily FT reported that the United National Party (UNP) has \"demanded\" an immediate Presidential pardon for Ven. Galagodaaththe Gnanasara Thera\u00a0 (https://www.ft.lk/news/UNP-demands-immediate-presidential-pardon-for-Gnanasara-Thera/56-799219). This would be the second Presidential pardon, that the UNP will be associated with. However, it is about a previous pardon that has been invalidated by the Supreme Court. This presents a further opportunity to elaborate upon what I referred to in the previous article. That is: How might the ethical principles of Buddhism guide political advocacy and the exercise of public power?\n\nAs for my thoughts of Saturday, 24 October, while I did not think they required further elaboration, perhaps they do. Thus this writing. But first let me reproduce several key points I articulated in my previous article. These were that:\n\nThe issue is bigger than one monk: The controversy is not merely about one individual, one prison decision or one robe; it concerns the relationship between the State, the Sangha, the Constitution and the administration of justice.\n\nThe robe deserves respect, but it does not confer immunity: The robe represents renunciation, discipline and commitment. Its significance calls for sensitivity, not exemption from the law.\n\nCriminal liability and monastic status are different matters: A criminal court determines guilt and imposes punishment; the appropriate ecclesiastical authority determines questions of monastic discipline and status."
  },
