    "md5": "ea9a3b146d6e41efeaade0bd0f4748d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1585,
    "chunk_text": "Criminal liability and monastic status are different matters: A criminal court determines guilt and imposes punishment; the appropriate ecclesiastical authority determines questions of monastic discipline and status.\n\nThe autonomy of the Sangha must be respected: Questions of monastic discipline and status belong within the appropriate ecclesiastical processes, not automatically to political authorities or prison administrators.\n\nAutonomy must go hand in hand with accountability: Religious institutions, like professional bodies and other institutions exercising authority, need credible procedures to investigate allegations, consider evidence and reach fair decisions.\n\nAllegations must be investigated, not presumed: A monk should not be prejudged, but neither should serious allegations of misconduct through words or deeds be dismissed merely because the person wears the robe.\n\nAdvocacy must not become intimidation or interference: Religious leaders and public officials may make representations and express reasoned views, but public pressure should never become a substitute for lawful processes or judicial proceedings.\n\nInstitutional independence requires safeguards: Intimidation, improper influence and conflicts of interest are legitimate governance risks to examine. Their existence, however, must be established by evidence, not assumed.\n\nRespect and accountability strengthen each other: The dignity of the Sangha is best protected through integrity, compassion, restraint and credible self-discipline\u2014not by placing individuals or institutions beyond scrutiny."
