    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "Respect and accountability strengthen each other: The dignity of the Sangha is best protected through integrity, compassion, restraint and credible self-discipline\u2014not by placing individuals or institutions beyond scrutiny.\n\nTraditionally, this means understanding the Four Noble Truths. In public life, it also calls for an informed and considered appreciation of the facts, the law and the likely consequences of a demand or decision.\n\nRight Intention \u2013 Traditionally, this encompasses renunciation, freedom from ill will and harmlessness. Applied to public conduct, it requires principled motives guided by compassion, fairness and goodwill rather than vengeance, self-interest, political bias or the pursuit of partisan advantage.\n\nRight Speech \u2013 This requires abstaining from false, divisive and harsh speech, as well as irresponsible or idle talk. In public life, it calls for truthfulness, restraint and care over the consequences of our words.\n\nRight Action \u2013 Traditionally, this requires abstaining from taking life, taking what is not given and sexual misconduct. More broadly, it reminds us that our conduct must be ethically responsible and respectful of the rights of others.\n\nRight Livelihood \u2013 This calls for earning one's living through honest and ethical means. Applied to public life, it reminds us that professional and public responsibilities should be exercised with integrity.\n\nRight Effort \u2013 This involves preventing unwholesome qualities from arising, abandoning those that have arisen, cultivating wholesome qualities and sustaining their development. In public life, it encourages the consistent practice of ethical conduct and the correction of harmful behaviour.\n\nTraditionally, this involves mindful awareness of the body, feelings, mind and mental phenomena. In public conduct, it also encourages awareness of our emotions, prejudices and impulses before they influence our decisions and actions."
  },
