    "chunk_size_bytes": 1605,
    "chunk_text": "Traditionally, this involves mindful awareness of the body, feelings, mind and mental phenomena. In public conduct, it also encourages awareness of our emotions, prejudices and impulses before they influence our decisions and actions.\n\nThese are contemporary explanations intended to make the principles accessible to public discussion, not substitutes for their traditional Buddhist meanings. Their application to politics is nevertheless instructive: ethical consistency must be judged not merely by the cause one supports, but also by the motives, words and methods through which that cause is pursued.\n\nAs I mentioned in my previous writing, the robe carries moral significance because of the religious vocation and discipline it represents. That significance is sustained by conduct, not by appearance alone. The relevant Nikaya and its governing authorities have an important responsibility to address allegations of monastic misconduct, whether arising from words or deeds, through appropriate, impartial and credible procedures consistent with applicable Vinaya principles and institutional rules.\n\nI referred also to the importance of responsible advocacy in my article of Saturday, 24 October. Let me now expand on this. In a democracy, political parties are entitled to advocate clemency, make representations to the President and question decisions they consider unjust. Demands for a further presidential pardon should therefore be assessed with care and without assuming, in the absence of evidence, that the demand itself constitutes unlawful interference or establishes an improper motive."
