    "chunk_text": "Right Speech is equally relevant. The medium, tone and methods of advocacy should be consistent with the Buddhist values being invoked: truthfulness, restraint, non-harm, compassion and respect for lawful institutions. Advocacy should inform rather than inflame, persuade rather than intimidate, and challenge decisions through responsible argument rather than personal vilification. These standards apply equally to political parties, religious authorities, public officials and citizens. They do not prohibit disagreement or compassionate appeals. They ask that the means adopted should be ethically consistent with the ends sought.\n\nThe demand for a second pardon also raises a question distinct from the ethical merits of political advocacy: what does the Constitution and the relevant judicial determination permit?\n\nHere is an opportunity for wider Buddhist reflection, I thought. I noticed a news item about a preparatory meeting being held at the Presidential Secretariat on 26 October, chaired by senior representatives of the World Buddhist Sangha Council and the Secretary to the President, to review arrangements for its 60th anniversary celebrations and 11th General Conference, to be hosted in Sri-Lanka. The conference will take place from 28 November to 2 December 2026, commencing at the Nagananda International Institute for Buddhist Studies in Kelaniya, under the patronage of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake. Events are also planned for Anuradhapura and Kandy. More than 600 delegates are expected, including Buddhist monks from around the world, approximately 250 Sri Lankans living overseas and 250 Sri Lankan Buddhist monks."
