    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aa8165036ed86889d949cee4c6f478f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "John Keells Properties and John Keells  Stock Brokers bring real estate and capital markets together at \u2018The Investment Playbook\u2019\n\nJohn Keells Properties, in collaboration with John Keells Stock Brokers, hosted \u2018The Investment Playbook\u2019, an exclusive, invitation-only investment forum that brought together perspectives from real estate, equity markets and wealth management to explore how investors can build long-term wealth through a more diversified approach to investing.\n\nHeld on 29 October 2026 at The Residences at Cinnamon Life under the theme \u201cThe Investment Playbook - Rethinking Wealth Across Markets\u201d, the forum challenged the conventional view of real estate and equities as competing investment choices, exploring instead how both asset classes can complement one another within a well-structured portfolio. Leading voices from the financial services and real estate sectors examined the economic forces shaping investment decisions, emerging opportunities within Sri Lanka\u2019s capital markets, and the enduring role of property in long-term wealth creation.\n\nThe evening opened with a regional market outlook from Maybank Investment Banking Group Head of Research and Regional Head of Financials Thilan Wickramasinghe. Drawing on over two decades of experience across international financial institutions, Wickramasinghe offered a broader perspective on regional economic developments, interest rates and capital flows, and their implications for investors in Sri Lanka.\n\nJohn Keells Stock Brokers Head of Research Navin Ratnayake then turned to the relationship between equities and underlying real estate assets, examining the significance of land and property holdings within publicly listed companies and the importance of understanding underlying asset values when evaluating investment opportunities. His segment highlighted how investors can gain exposure to real estate both through direct property ownership and through the equity market."
  },
