    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "International partnership to strengthen hydrogen higher education in Sri Lanka\n\nEU-funded GREEN-HET project to be officially launched in Colombo on 10 November 2026\n\nBuilding on a decade-long partnership in Higher Education and\u00a0 Research on Clean Energy Technologies (HRNCET) between the University of Jaffna, Sri\u00a0 Lanka, and Western Norway University of Applied Sciences (HVL), Norway, a new\u00a0 international initiative will further strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s capacity in hydrogen education\u00a0 and research.\n\nThe Green Hydrogen Enabling Technologies (GREEN-HET) project will be formally\u00a0 launched on 10 November 2026 at Jetwing Colombo Seven Hotel, under the patronage of Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya, who will grace the\u00a0 occasion as the Chief Guest. The event will bring together international academic\u00a0 partners and key stakeholders from government, industry, the diplomatic community and\u00a0 wider society.\n\nFunded by the European Union (EU) through its Erasmus+ Capacity Building in Higher\u00a0 Education (CBHE) program, GREEN-HET is a three-year initiative aimed at\u00a0 developing a joint Master\u2019s degree program in Hydrogen Enabling Technologies across\u00a0 four Sri Lankan universities, while strengthening national expertise, higher education\u00a0 capacity and institutional collaboration in the emerging hydrogen sector.\n\nThe Master\u2019s program will be jointly developed and delivered under the national\u00a0 coordination of the University of Jaffna, with the participation of the Universities of\u00a0 Moratuwa, Peradeniya and Ruhuna. The European consortium comprises HVL, Norway;\u00a0 the University of Groningen, the Netherlands; OTH Regensburg \u2013 Technical University of\u00a0 Applied Sciences, Germany; and Virumaa College of Tallinn University of Technology,\u00a0 Estonia."
  },
