    "chunk_size_bytes": 1558,
    "chunk_text": "The Bay of Bengal region, one of the world\u2019s most densely populated and economically dynamic areas, continues to play a pivotal role in global trade and regional development. Approximately a quarter of global maritime commerce passes through its waters, while its considerable natural and human resources offer significant opportunities for sustainable economic growth.\n\nThe key thematic priorities included advancing regional consensus on collaborative maritime governance, establishing coordinated mechanisms to strengthen maritime communications, and addressing illegal, unreported and unregulated (IUU) fishing. The Dialogue also sought to deepen understanding of the Bay of Bengal as a distinct sub-region within the broader Indo-Pacific, where geopolitical, economic and ecological interests increasingly converge.\n\nThe Sixth Dialogue further incorporated cross-cutting priorities, particularly inclusive policymaking, recognising the importance of grounding maritime security policies in local realities and strengthening scientific collaboration.\n\nWelcome remarks were delivered by Ambassador (Retd.) Bernard Goonetilleke, Chairman of the Pathfinder Foundation, and Ambassador (Retd.) Venu Rajamony, Senior Adviser for South Asia at the Centre for Humanitarian Dialogue.\n\nThe two-day event brought together local and international participants, including representatives of diplomatic missions based in Colombo, among them ambassadors and high commissioners, as well as experts and stakeholders engaged in maritime affairs and regional cooperation."
