    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-2f98efec-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d65540a8579bbf46cb2ff8632e4c622a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1446,
    "chunk_text": "RCSS study says Middle East war exposed South Asia\u2019s deep dependence\n\nA Policy Study, authored by RCSS Researcher Thedini Herath, has found that the 2026 Middle East war did not create, but dramatically exposed how heavily South Asian states depend on a single region.\n\nIt noted that the war hit all five pillars of dependence at once: soaring oil prices raised fuel import bills and strained foreign reserves; the closure of the Strait of Hormuz disrupted the region\u2019s key trade routes; the loss of jobs in the Gulf threatened the livelihoods of tens of millions of South Asian workers; Gulf financing tightened as sovereign wealth funds turned inward; and long-standing politico-strategic partnerships with countries on different sides of the conflict were tested.\n\nThe study called for reducing this over-concentration in order to build long-term resilience in the relationship.\n\nThe study titled South Asia\u2019s \u2018Middle East Dependency Trap\u2019: How the 2026 Iran War Exposed the Region\u2019s Politico-Economic Vulnerabilities, was presented by the author at the RCSS-MIR joint webinar titled \u2018Evolving South Asia\u2013Middle East Relations: Opportunities for Cooperation in a Changing Geopolitical Landscape\u2019, held on 29 October 2026, with a live audience at RCSS, Colombo, and participants joining virtually from the UAE and representatives from Sri Lanka\u2019s diplomatic missions in the Middle East region and scholars across the region."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-2f98efec",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2f98efec",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "RCSS study says Middle East war exposed South Asia\u2019s deep dependence",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/RCSS-study-says-Middle-East-war-exposed-South-Asia-s-deep-dependence/34-799764",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793987622.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-2f98efec-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a955d68039e20683910e809c6d95cd39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1671,
    "chunk_text": "The study titled South Asia\u2019s \u2018Middle East Dependency Trap\u2019: How the 2026 Iran War Exposed the Region\u2019s Politico-Economic Vulnerabilities, was presented by the author at the RCSS-MIR joint webinar titled \u2018Evolving South Asia\u2013Middle East Relations: Opportunities for Cooperation in a Changing Geopolitical Landscape\u2019, held on 29 October 2026, with a live audience at RCSS, Colombo, and participants joining virtually from the UAE and representatives from Sri Lanka\u2019s diplomatic missions in the Middle East region and scholars across the region.\n\nViewing current developments through the prism of Robert Keohane and Joseph Nye\u2019s concept of \u2018complex interdependence\u2019, Herath noted that the study shows that the imbalance in the relationship is a source of vulnerability. However, while Keohane and Nye focus mainly on economic and transnational ties, the study adds \u2018politico-strategic partnerships\u2019 as a distinct pillar of interdependence. In a further theoretical contribution, the study emphasises how the present war also points to an additional dimension of vulnerability that may need to be integrated into the interdependence framework.\n\nWhile Keohane and Nye\u2019s analysis saw military force as playing only a minor role, and as something the governments in an interdependent relationship might use, whether against each other or against outside powers, rather than treating strategic ties as a channel of interdependence in its own right, South Asia\u2019s interdependence with the Middle East was disrupted by an extraneous force - in a war between the United States, Israel and Iran - in which none of the South Asian countries had any part."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-2f98efec",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2f98efec",
