    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a9764cd0bcd10657a30b0d71e79869e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1621,
    "chunk_text": "While Keohane and Nye\u2019s analysis saw military force as playing only a minor role, and as something the governments in an interdependent relationship might use, whether against each other or against outside powers, rather than treating strategic ties as a channel of interdependence in its own right, South Asia\u2019s interdependence with the Middle East was disrupted by an extraneous force - in a war between the United States, Israel and Iran - in which none of the South Asian countries had any part.\n\nIt was also noted that while cooperation through multilateral fora between the two regions as a whole remains important, both South Asian and Middle Eastern countries must also work hard to safeguard their respective bilateral relationships, rather than letting them be absorbed into the larger regional relationship, so that the close bilateral relations built over decades are not lost. Asked whether South Asia\u2019s dependence amounted to neo-colonialism, Herath noted that while colonial history helped create the early links, the current dependence was not imposed by a coloniser but was largely built through South Asia\u2019s own choices, which is why the study describes the relationship as asymmetric interdependence. On the options open to small states, she described non-alignment as part of a wider hedging strategy, pointing to Sri Lanka\u2019s own response during the current war: when the Iranian vessel IRIS Dena was sunk off its southern coast, Sri Lanka rescued and assisted the survivors, while also declining both a US request to land warplanes at Mattala and Iranian requests for naval goodwill visits."
  },
