    "md5": "935a88e6186ed1a71dc9f11de3b02ed6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1927,
    "chunk_text": "Who has made the effort to seize drugs worth nearly Rs. 220 billion and to confiscate such large quantities of firearms? It is the Police and the three armed forces. Our Government will therefore give due recognition to the work they have done in this operation. We have also taken steps to provide bonuses and incentives to many officers who took part in operations in the recent past, and we will consider further measures in this regard.\n\nIn addition, with the involvement of our Department of Health and a large number of relevant institutions, 259,000 community awareness programs on prevention have been organised across the country. We have conducted a large number of such programs to educate the public and children.\n\nWe must suppress this menace within the framework of the law. We have absolutely no intention of stepping outside the legal framework in the process of defeating it. We have laws in place, and we operate under those laws. If the law is inadequate, we will make new laws. There was a major issue concerning the destruction of seized drugs. There was widespread public suspicion: large quantities of drugs were being shown as seized, but were these drugs subsequently finding their way back on to the market?\n\nUnder the legal framework that existed, we had no authority to destroy seized items until a case filed in court had concluded and the court had issued an order to destroy the items concerned. We have, however, enacted the legislation required to enable their destruction.\n\nNext, there was no law to confiscate wealth and assets acquired through drugs \u2013 in other words, proceeds and assets acquired unlawfully. Therefore, in 2025, we passed the law on the confiscation of unlawfully acquired assets. It is under this confiscation law that assets worth Rs. 4.5 billion have now been confiscated from criminals. Had that law not existed, we would have had no opportunity to seize those assets."
