    "md5": "c8ded8d282643623b86efbadbf3a0443",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1659,
    "chunk_text": "Next, there was no law to confiscate wealth and assets acquired through drugs \u2013 in other words, proceeds and assets acquired unlawfully. Therefore, in 2025, we passed the law on the confiscation of unlawfully acquired assets. It is under this confiscation law that assets worth Rs. 4.5 billion have now been confiscated from criminals. Had that law not existed, we would have had no opportunity to seize those assets.\n\nWe intend to substantially weaken the PTA \u2013 the Prevention of Terrorism Act. This is because we do not currently face terrorist activity on a day-to-day basis. We will make it a much weaker Act. There is a discussion on this, and we are talking about it. However, we need strong legislation to prevent organised crime. This is the law required in this era to free the nation from this menace.\n\nThe law is constantly evolving. New laws are enacted in response to new challenges. There was a time when there were no environmental laws. However, when the environment began to suffer serious damage, laws were enacted to protect it. There was a time when there were no money-laundering laws. But when the accumulation of illicit wealth became a major threat, the Money Laundering Act was introduced. There was a time when rulers did not destroy public property. But when rulers began destroying public property, legislation was introduced to protect it. Organised crime has now grown. Therefore, an Act to prevent organised crime is necessary in this era.\n\nThe law is not eternal. A law enacted today will not remain in force for all time. New challenges emerge in society. When new challenges arise, strong laws are needed to address them."
