    "chunk_text": "The law is constantly evolving. New laws are enacted in response to new challenges. There was a time when there were no environmental laws. However, when the environment began to suffer serious damage, laws were enacted to protect it. There was a time when there were no money-laundering laws. But when the accumulation of illicit wealth became a major threat, the Money Laundering Act was introduced. There was a time when rulers did not destroy public property. But when rulers began destroying public property, legislation was introduced to protect it. Organised crime has now grown. Therefore, an Act to prevent organised crime is necessary in this era.\n\nThe law is not eternal. A law enacted today will not remain in force for all time. New challenges emerge in society. When new challenges arise, strong laws are needed to address them.\n\nToday, we need strong legislation to prevent organised crime. We have now presented the Bill to Parliament. There are two groups opposing this legislation. One is quite clear: those who receive privileges and protection from organised criminals. We do not take that group into consideration. But there is another group \u2013 those who speak about democracy and the fundamental rights of the people of our country. We are ready to discuss the matter with that group.\n\nOur intention is in no way to suppress the people or deprive them of their rights. We need legislation to combat organised crime, and we are prepared to enact it. The Bill is currently before the Supreme Court and is under consideration. Those who have challenged the Bill before the Court have, at this stage, raised three main issues."
