    "md5": "56814715c7db0d71bce5e91f4636ba6a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1588,
    "chunk_text": "Our intention is in no way to suppress the people or deprive them of their rights. We need legislation to combat organised crime, and we are prepared to enact it. The Bill is currently before the Supreme Court and is under consideration. Those who have challenged the Bill before the Court have, at this stage, raised three main issues.\n\nThere is another provision stating that offences committed under the Excise Ordinance can also be dealt with under the Organised Crime Act. We will remove that provision too, making it clear that offences under the Excise Ordinance will not be dealt with under the Organised Crime Act. There is no problem with that. These are the three main issues in the Bill currently being discussed before the Supreme Court. If there are any others, we are ready to consider them.\n\nThis is in no way about obtaining undue powers for me, the Government, the police or our departments. Why do we need these powers? No one holds power forever. Whenever we exercise power, we must do so with the mindset that we should be ready to relinquish it. When we exercise power, we must always consider the laws we enact by asking ourselves: what would these laws mean when we are no longer in power? We are not acting with the intention of retaining power for the long term or with the mindset that we will never give it up.\n\nWe act knowing that the people have entrusted power to us, and that they can take it back at any time \u2013 they can do so through an election. We know that. Therefore, in exercising power, we have no agenda involving me, my children or my relatives."
  },
