    "md5": "3f242d67c8c4c9b2fabbd842875ad61c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1746,
    "chunk_text": "Normally, only around 3,000 reports were completed a month. We deployed additional staff and provided new allowances. Our Navy also assisted with some infrastructure requirements. What did we do by providing assistance with infrastructure?\n\nWe must explain to parents what happens when their children\u2019s futures are destroyed. What happens when someone becomes addicted to drugs? The mind becomes confused, the body becomes emaciated, self-respect is lost, personality breaks down, the person is marginalised by society and, ultimately, the death penalty may be imposed under the law. If we strengthen this law, the ultimate penalty will be death \u2013 that is what the law provides. Parents who love their children must understand this: a child may initially become attracted to drugs, but what is the eventual outcome? The death penalty under the law.\n\nParents therefore have a responsibility to prevent their beloved children from falling victim to this menace. I know that when a family faces such a tragedy, mothers and fathers rush to tell the Venerable Thero: \u2018Our Venerable Thero, we never imagined that my younger son or my elder son would end up like this.\u2019 They say, \u2018We never thought this would happen to my husband.\u2019 Otherwise, they go to their church, their priest or their Kurukkal. We know that, at the outset, parents do not come directly to the police.\n\nMothers and fathers are more open with religious leaders or with doctors. They do speak to doctors to some extent. However, they are more likely to open up to our Buddhist monks and other religious clergy. Therefore, the first to hear about and witness the distressing consequences of this menace in a village are often the monks and religious clergy of that village."
  },
