    "chunk_size_bytes": 1798,
    "chunk_text": "Mothers and fathers are more open with religious leaders or with doctors. They do speak to doctors to some extent. However, they are more likely to open up to our Buddhist monks and other religious clergy. Therefore, the first to hear about and witness the distressing consequences of this menace in a village are often the monks and religious clergy of that village.\n\nHow many people do you meet in a day, a month or a year? How many sermons do you deliver? How many times do you advise children and parents? How often do you listen to their grief and suffering? I believe this is where our first line of defence lies. Our Venerable monks, together with the other religious clergy, are the principal force in providing the social leadership needed to defeat this menace.\n\nMany people have gathered here today. I believe this is one of the largest gatherings of Buddhist monks in one place in Sri Lanka in recent times. A large number of our Maulavis are also attending. Our priests and Kurukkals have come here from Jaffna. Some of the Kurukkals who left Jaffna yesterday have arrived here today. What does that demonstrate? It shows us your commitment. But what is needed now? I repeat: if the State alone could do this, we would not be holding a conference of this nature. We would simply summon State officials and speak to them.\n\nThe State cannot do this alone. Everyone\u2019s contribution and organised involvement are necessary. We will give this priority: we will enact the necessary laws, strengthen institutions, ensure that courts are equipped to hear cases, make the police and armed forces effective, and establish our rehabilitation authority and institutions. That is the Government\u2019s responsibility. However, the greatest role in defeating this menace belongs to the general public."
