    "md5": "0629b52bf12a64db1f6b72623766f288",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "Taxation, fairness and the public purse\n\nThe true measure of a successful fiscal policy is not how much money a Government manages to collect or borrow, but how effectively it converts public resources into national prosperity, economic opportunity and social well-being\n\nLessons from ancient statecraft and the responsibilities of modern governments\n\nTaxation is an instrument of governance, not an entitlement to extract\n\nAmong the many\u00a0 expectations placed on a national Budget, taxation policy occupies a particularly important position. This consideration assumes even greater significance in a country where the prevailing tax rates are already relatively\u00a0 high, placing a considerable burden on individuals, households and business alike.\n\nThe legitimacy of taxation rests not merely on the authority of a Government to impose taxes, but on the reasoning, fairness and public purpose behind such decisions.\n\nGovernments require revenue to maintain public services, protect citizens, develop infrastructure and fulfil their responsibilities. No responsible society can dispute that necessity. However, the more important question is whether the methods adopted to raise revenue are economically sensible, socially equitable and consistent with the long-term interests of the nation.\n\nThis is a question that deserves particular attention in modern economies, where governments increasingly rely on taxation, borrowing and elaborate Budgetary projections to finance their commitments.\n\nIt is interesting to revisit the principles advocated by Kautilya, also known as Chanakya, the celebrated Indian strategist and author of the Arthashastra.\n\nKautilya recognised the importance of a strong treasury as an essential foundation of state power. Yet his approach to revenue collection was not simply one of maximising the State\u2019s income."
  },
