    "md5": "3fe5ecf4b6fd725d6c0e36db7f40f33f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "It is interesting to revisit the principles advocated by Kautilya, also known as Chanakya, the celebrated Indian strategist and author of the Arthashastra.\n\nKautilya recognised the importance of a strong treasury as an essential foundation of state power. Yet his approach to revenue collection was not simply one of maximising the State\u2019s income.\n\nThe underlying message is particularly important: a ruler must not weaken the very economic foundation from which public revenue is generated.\n\nThe same principle can be applied to modern taxation. Excessive or poorly designed taxation can discourage investment, reduce consumption, weaken enterprise, drive economic activity into informality and ultimately erode the very tax base that the Government seeks to expand.\n\nA Government must therefore understand the difference between collecting more revenue and creating greater national wealth.\n\nTaxation should be guided by the ability-to-pay principle, economic capacity and the equitable distribution of the national burden.\n\nThose with greater financial capacity may reasonably be expected to contribute more. However, fairness cannot be measured simply by the nominal tax rate imposed on different categories of taxpayers.\n\nThe combined impact of direct taxes, indirect taxes, levies and other charges must also be considered.\n\nA salaried employee paying income tax, a small entrepreneur struggling with operating costs, and a low-income household paying consumption taxes experience the tax system in very different ways.\n\nIndirect taxation can be particularly burdensome on ordinary households because a greater proportion of their limited income is spent on essential goods and services.\n\nIt is therefore insufficient to claim that a tax system is equitable merely because it applies the same rate to everyone within a particular category.\n\nThe real test is whether the overall burden is reasonable, proportionate and sensitive to the economic circumstances of those who bear it."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-416cbfa2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-416cbfa2",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "Taxation, fairness and the public purse",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Taxation-fairness-and-the-public-purse/4-799766",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
