    "chunk_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-416cbfa2-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b1fbf565f9ac8e7924ef6ed47e7df940",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1924,
    "chunk_text": "It is therefore insufficient to claim that a tax system is equitable merely because it applies the same rate to everyone within a particular category.\n\nThe real test is whether the overall burden is reasonable, proportionate and sensitive to the economic circumstances of those who bear it.\n\nYet modern Budget-making can sometimes become excessively dependent on projections and assumptions.\n\nExpected increases in revenue, anticipated improvements in economic activity, projected investment inflows and estimated savings are often incorporated into financial plans before their actual realisation.\n\nThere is nothing inherently wrong with forecasting. Every Budget must look ahead. The danger arises when optimistic assumptions are treated as dependable financial resources and expenditure commitments are built around them.\n\nA Budget should distinguish clearly between revenue already secured, revenue reasonably expected and revenue that remains conditional upon future economic developments.\n\nWhere the assumptions fail to materialise, the consequences cannot simply be transferred to the public through additional taxation or indiscriminate borrowing.\n\nOne of the most important distinctions in responsible public finance is the difference between revenue and borrowed money.\n\nTax revenue represents a contribution collected by the State under law. Borrowing, on the other hand, creates an obligation that must ultimately be serviced and repaid, or refinanced at a future cost.\n\nBorrowing is not necessarily undesirable. Governments may legitimately borrow to finance economically productive infrastructure, investments that generate future returns, or extraordinary circumstances requiring temporary financial support.\n\nHowever, borrowing to sustain recurring expenditure, meet unrealistic Budgetary commitments or compensate indefinitely for deficiencies in revenue generation is a fundamentally different proposition."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-06-dailyftlk-416cbfa2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-416cbfa2",
    "date_str": "2026-11-06",
    "description": "Taxation, fairness and the public purse",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Taxation-fairness-and-the-public-purse/4-799766",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
