    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8af3782475db37d7967732ceca1151ac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1795,
    "chunk_text": "However, borrowing to sustain recurring expenditure, meet unrealistic Budgetary commitments or compensate indefinitely for deficiencies in revenue generation is a fundamentally different proposition.\n\nThere is no unlimited reservoir of borrowing. Every additional liability carries interest costs, repayment obligations, exchange-rate exposure where foreign currency is involved, and a corresponding reduction in the fiscal room available to future governments.\n\nA Budget cannot be considered balanced in any meaningful economic sense merely because a financing gap has been filled through additional borrowing.\n\nA particularly important issue is the manner in which governments reconcile their expenditure commitments with anticipated revenue and borrowing requirements.\n\nIf projected revenue is uncertain, expenditure estimates are insufficiently disciplined and the resulting deficit is covered through borrowing, the apparent financial equilibrium may be more a product of accounting assumptions than genuine economic strength.\n\nThe question is not simply whether the Treasury can raise the required funds today. It is whether the economy can sustain the resulting obligations tomorrow.\n\nResponsible Budget-making must therefore incorporate alternative scenarios, sensitivity analysis, debt-servicing capacity and an assessment of the risks associated with revenue shortfalls.\n\nThe International Monetary Fund\u2019s principles of fiscal transparency similarly emphasise realistic Budgets, medium-term fiscal frameworks and disclosure of fiscal risks.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recent economic experience makes these principles especially relevant.\n\nThe country has undergone a severe economic and debt crisis, followed by difficult fiscal adjustments, revenue-enhancement measures and debt restructuring."
  },
  {
