    "md5": "0d1acff628c57e76d896de044d4e8619",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s recent economic experience makes these principles especially relevant.\n\nThe country has undergone a severe economic and debt crisis, followed by difficult fiscal adjustments, revenue-enhancement measures and debt restructuring.\n\nThese measures recognise that public finance cannot be managed indefinitely through discretionary decisions and short-term Budgetary expedients.\n\nNevertheless, legislation and numerical targets alone cannot guarantee responsible financial management. Their effectiveness ultimately depends on the quality of the assumptions, the discipline exercised in implementing them, transparency in reporting and the willingness to correct policies when circumstances change.\n\nSri Lanka must also recognise that fiscal consolidation cannot be viewed exclusively through the revenue collected by the Treasury. The ability of households to sustain consumption, businesses to invest, industries to expand and exporters to compete is equally important.\n\nAn economy cannot tax its way into prosperity if the productive capacity required to generate future income and employment is weakened in the process.\n\nThe responsibility of a Government is not simply to balance the figures presented in an annual Budget. It is to protect and expand the nation\u2019s economic capacity while ensuring that the burden of financing the State is distributed fairly.\n\nEvery major tax measure should therefore be examined against several fundamental questions:\n\nIs there a clearly established public necessity for the tax?\n\nIs the burden proportionate to the capacity of those who pay it?\n\nHas its effect on investment, employment, production and household purchasing power been assessed?\n\nAre borrowed funds being directed towards purposes that justify the future liabilities?\n\nIs the public receiving transparent information on the actual results achieved?\n\nThese are not unreasonable demands from taxpayers. They are essential requirements of an accountable Government."
  },
