    "md5": "222fe4671dcdc558fe7d6be031b1b809",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "FSP challenges AKD\u2019s proposed amendments to Organised Crime Act\n\nThe Frontline Socialist Party (FSP) has challenged President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s commitment to amend the proposed Organised Crime Act, arguing that the changes he outlined are merely superficial and fail to address the fundamental threats the legislation poses to democratic rights and civil liberties.\n\nAddressing a media briefing yesterday, FSP Education Secretary Pubudu Jayagoda said the President\u2019s remarks at a meeting with religious leaders at Temple Trees on Thursday (5) had focused on three specific provisions, while ignoring the broader concerns raised by trade unions, human rights organisations, professionals and civil society groups.\n\nThe President had reportedly described the legislation as non-repressive and formulated in accordance with international standards, while alleging that those opposing it were acting in the interests of underworld groups and criminals.\n\nJayagoda said the Government had been compelled to reconsider its position following widespread public opposition, including the 4 November protest near Parliament.\n\n\u201cAn administration that previously defended the Bill with such rhetoric has reached this level of flexibility only because an entire society raised its voice against this repressive Act,\u201d he said.\n\nHe identified three areas addressed by the President and explained why the proposed amendments would not resolve the fundamental problems.\n\nCiting FSP\u2019s first concern as \u2018reducing detention from 72 to 48 hours\u2019, Jayagoda said the President\u2019s proposal to reduce the period of police detention from 72 to 48 hours failed to address the extensive powers granted to law enforcement authorities.\n\nHe pointed out that the legislation provides for arrests on suspicion of association with criminal gangs, detention orders extending up to one year without judicial review and detention facilities without adequate judicial oversight."
