    "chunk_text": "He pointed out that the legislation provides for arrests on suspicion of association with criminal gangs, detention orders extending up to one year without judicial review and detention facilities without adequate judicial oversight.\n\nHe also criticised provisions that, in his view, reduce magistrates to rubber stamps and transfer judicial authority to the executive.\n\n\u201cDo not bargain over the number of hours like a street vendor. These laws concern the democratic rights of the people,\u201d Jayagoda said.\n\nResponding to the President Dissanayake\u2019s indication that the provision identifying criminal gang members through tattoos would be changed, Jayagoda said the issue went far beyond tattoos.\n\nHe argued that the legislation could make individuals suspects based on their physical appearance and social associations rather than evidence of their involvement in criminal activity.\n\n\u201cThe tattoo provision is merely a symbol of the ridiculous nature of this Act,\u201d he said, adding that the legislation undermined the fundamental legal principle that criminal liability should be based on an individual\u2019s involvement in an offence.\n\nJayagoda also questioned the President\u2019s proposal to remove offences under the Excise Ordinance, asking why offences under other laws, including the Betting and Gaming Ordinance, Forest and Flora Ordinance, Wildlife Ordinance and Archaeology Act, were not also being reconsidered.\n\nHe warned that even people participating in protests could potentially be classified as organised criminals through provisions relating to obstructing public officers in the performance of their duties.\n\nJagoda further questioned why the President had not addressed provisions concerning police-obtained confessions, access to private communications and electronic devices, and the criminalisation of past associations with suspected organised criminals."
