    "chunk_text": "Energy Ministry seeks legal advice on private fuel operators\u2019 pricing agreements\n\nThe Energy Ministry is seeking legal advice on long-term agreements with private fuel operators, which allow them to revise certain fuel prices to avoid losses and could require the Government to compensate them if the prescribed selling prices result in losses, Ceylon Petroleum Corporation (CPC) Chairman D. J. Rajakaruna said.\n\nHe said the CPC had secured sufficient fuel supplies and stocks to ensure uninterrupted distribution well into the middle of next year, assuring the public that there was no fuel shortage or supply issue in the country.\n\nRajakaruna said the CPC had no difficulties maintaining a steady fuel supply, while recent queues reported at certain filling stations were mainly due to distribution constraints faced by some private petroleum companies.\n\nAccording to him, private operators, including Sinopec and RM Parks, had reduced their fuel distribution volumes due to financial losses under the existing pricing structure. This had resulted in temporary supply disruptions in certain areas, particularly where private filling stations were the main fuel providers.\n\nHe said discussions had been held between the Ministry, the CPC and the private operators to address the situation, with the companies instructed to prioritise fuel distribution in areas where supply constraints had been reported, despite lower profit margins.\n\nThe CPC also plans to establish 75 additional filling stations to improve fuel distribution and accessibility across the country, he said.\n\nExplaining the difficulties faced by private fuel operators, Rajakaruna said the issue was linked to long-term agreements signed for a period of 20 years, which included provisions relating to a ceiling price and the applicable pricing formula.\n\nUnder these agreements, the Government or the relevant Ministry could be required to compensate private operators if the prescribed selling prices resulted in losses, he said."
