    "chunk_text": "Explaining the difficulties faced by private fuel operators, Rajakaruna said the issue was linked to long-term agreements signed for a period of 20 years, which included provisions relating to a ceiling price and the applicable pricing formula.\n\nUnder these agreements, the Government or the relevant Ministry could be required to compensate private operators if the prescribed selling prices resulted in losses, he said.\n\nCommenting on international oil prices, Rajakaruna said global crude oil and refined petroleum product prices had increased significantly compared with February, with some increases estimated at between 80 per cent and 90 per cent.\n\nHowever, local fuel prices had increased by only around 40 to 43 per cent, as the Government had absorbed a considerable portion of the additional cost to minimise the impact on consumers, he said.\n\nHe added that international market conditions had shown some signs of stabilisation amid developments in conflict-affected regions.\n\nAny benefits arising from a reduction in international fuel prices would be passed on to consumers at the earliest opportunity, Rajakaruna assured."
