    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "Paraguay\u2019s President Santiago Pe\u00f1a impeached and arrested\n\nBBC:\u00a0Paraguay's President Santiago Pe\u00f1a has been impeached and placed under house arrest.\n\nPe\u00f1a was arrested on Friday minutes after lawmakers, including from his own party, voted to remove him from office due to a crisis triggered by the collapse of Ueno Bank earlier this week.\n\nHe had offered to step down on Thursday, but the move to impeach him and charge him with misconduct rather than accept his resignation meant he was stripped of his lifetime Senate seat and the immunity granted to ex-presidents.\n\nPe\u00f1a, who until last year held shares in the company that owned the bank, has denied any wrongdoing.\n\nThe demise of the digital bank has left more than half of Paraguay's population without access to their savings after it was seized by Paraguay's central bank.\n\nAn estimated three million depositors remain unable to access their savings and pensions prompting widespread protests particularly in the capital, Asunci\u00f3n.\n\nThe impeachment charges that led to Pe\u00f1a's removal alleged he had conflicts of interest, manipulated regulatory oversight, diverted pension funds, and participated in an institutional cover-up involving the central bank.\n\nPe\u00f1a is due to be questioned on Saturday over allegations of breach of trust, influence peddling, and misappropriation of funds, prosecutor Silvia Margarita Gonzalez Vester said.\n\nPe\u00f1a's impeachment passed with 44 out of 45 senators voting in favour of it. One lawmaker was absent.\n\nAfterwards, senate president Basilio N\u00fa\u00f1ez said Pe\u00f1a was found guilty of \"dereliction of duty\".\n\nParaguay's Vice-President Pedro Alliana was then sworn in as interim President.\n\n\"Compatriots who are now going through a difficult time as a result of the financial collapse will be fully compensated,\" said Alliana, who will serve as President until 2028."
