    "description": "Asia Foundation-Sri Lanka Police launch national cybersecurity awareness campaign",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Asia-Foundation-Sri-Lanka-Police-launch-national-cybersecurity-awareness-campaign/215-354388",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794035617.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-07-dailymirrorlk-4cdafb59-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b533c9c041736b65444a386da0ce14ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "Asia Foundation-Sri Lanka Police launch national cybersecurity awareness campaign\n\nFom left: Bureau for the Investigation of Abuse of Children and Women Deputy Inspector General Darshika Kumari, Assistant Superintendent of Police and Sri Lanka Police Media Spokesperson F.U. Wootler, The Asia Foundation Senior Programme Manager and Asia Policy Lead Rukshanie Vidyaratne and University of Peradeniya Senior Lecturer in Computer Engineering and Lanka Education and Research Network CEO Prof. Roshan Ragel\n\nThe Asia Foundation, in partnership with the Sri Lanka Police, has launched a public awareness campaign to strengthen cybersecurity awareness and encourage prevention of cybercrime among the micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) and wider public.\n\nDelivered through The Asia Foundation\u2019s Asia-Pacific Cybersecurity Fund (ACF) project, the campaign extends digital safety guidance to communities nationwide through television, police social media channels and public spaces. It seeks to help individuals and businesses recognise cyber threats, safeguard information and prevent cybercrimes.\n\nThe campaign features six infomercials, with broadcasts scheduled on TV Derana during November, alongside dissemination through Sri Lanka Police social media channels.\n\nSinhala and Tamil awareness pennants will be displayed at police stations nationwide, Bandaranaike International Airport and other public establishments. Public transport stickers and laptop stickers distributed through the Sri Lanka Police will reinforce these messages in everyday settings.\n\nCentral to the campaign are straightforward precautions that support safer digital practices. These include using strong, unique passwords, enabling two-factor authentication wherever possible, verifying suspicious links, attachments and messages before responding and never sharing one-time passwords with anyone. Together, these messages encourage vigilance in daily digital interactions."
  },
