    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "Fairway sets sights beyond Colombo as Kandy, Habarana and Yala join 500-key push\n\nKandy, Habarana and Yala resorts to add 80 keys to portfolio already past 250\n\nRobotics and self-check-in anchor lean operating model at Fairway Colombo\n\nFairway is turning its gaze beyond the capital, with resorts in Kandy, Habarana and Yala in the pipeline that would add 80 keys to a portfolio already lifted past 250 by the Fairway Trinco deal, carrying the group to two-thirds of its 500-key ambition, according to Fairway Leisure\u2019s top management.\n\nOver a luncheon with reporters in Colombo recently, Leisure Sector Chief Executive Officer Abdul Basheer Cader and Chief Operating Officer Gemunu Cooray outlined a model where the properties acquired by the group are handed to its leisure arm for operation.\n\nThe established operations account for over 210 keys, of which 181 sit at Fairway Colombo, the Fort hotel that has anchored the business since 2016. Across the wider group, Fairway Holdings employs around 600 staff.\n\nFairway Trinco brought a further 46 keys when the Trincomalee beachfront resort came under Fairway\u2019s management in September through a long-term agreement, under which it is being lifted to a four-star standard. The addition took the portfolio past 250 keys, consistent with the group\u2019s own reckoning that it stands roughly halfway to its goal.\n\nThe next leg, mapped out at the luncheon, takes in 33 keys at Mount Randholee Resorts & Spa in Kandy, 24 at Sorowwa Resort and Spa in Habarana, in the heart of the Cultural Triangle and 23 at Tranquil Sanctuary near Yala. The management described these as \u201call in the making\u201d, noting that growth would come largely through management contracts, with some properties to be acquired outright. Dambulla and Anuradhapura also feature on the group\u2019s radar."
