    "chunk_text": "The next leg, mapped out at the luncheon, takes in 33 keys at Mount Randholee Resorts & Spa in Kandy, 24 at Sorowwa Resort and Spa in Habarana, in the heart of the Cultural Triangle and 23 at Tranquil Sanctuary near Yala. The management described these as \u201call in the making\u201d, noting that growth would come largely through management contracts, with some properties to be acquired outright. Dambulla and Anuradhapura also feature on the group\u2019s radar.\n\nBeyond the city, the group\u2019s existing stays are more intimate in scale, led by The Planter\u2019s Legacy, three cottages overlooking Gregory Lake in Nuwara Eliya, where Fairway also runs a park of eight static caravans sleeping two, four or six guests, each fitted with a compact kitchen, having retired its mobile motor homes.\n\nIn Galle, Fairway Sunset offers eight two and three-bedroom serviced apartments, while Galkanda Manor in Matale is managed rather than owned.\n\nHowever, Fairway Colombo was not spared a dip in March, reliant as it is on India and other Asian markets for volume. A deep bench of corporate clients helped steady occupancy, which the officials pointed out \u201cdidn\u2019t drop below 85 percent\u201d.\n\nThe arrangement cuts both ways, they explained, with corporates filling rooms in the off-season on the understanding that peak-season rates cannot be extended to them, a balance that has reshaped the hotel\u2019s guest mix.\n\nThat resilience, the management argued, rests on a lean operating model in which most services are outsourced and only the essentials are kept in-house. Fairway Colombo\u2019s delivery robot, introduced in 2024, is an extension of that thinking, handling in-room orders that the officials conceded staff did not relish.\n\nIt moves within the hotel\u2019s floor-level access control, which confines guests to their own floors and politely asks anyone in its path to step aside. The management noted that the hotel was recognised in 2025 as Sri Lanka\u2019s most technologically advanced."
