    "md5": "9380c91beaae95ebc8bdff682398fc57",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "First Capital bets on \u2018Bunty Loves Chocolates\u2019 to raise a money-smart generation\n\nFrom left: Global Unites Founder Prashan De Visser, First Capital Holdings Vice President Deshani Ratnayake, AMW Capital Leasing and Finance Chairman Indrajith Boyagoda, Dinesha Samaranayake and Good Life X COO Christina Gunasinghe\n\nFirst Capital Holdings PLC has partnered with \u2018Bunty Loves Chocolates\u2019, a new children\u2019s book that introduces young readers to saving, budgeting and the difference between wants and needs, in a bid to help Sri Lankan families build healthy money habits from an early age, the company said in a statement.\n\nMore than four in ten Sri Lankan adults are still not financially literate, as per the Central Bank data, with the Central Bank\u2019s latest survey, carried out in 2021, putting the country\u2019s financial literacy rate at just 57.9 percent. That was up from 35 percent in a 2014 global survey by S&P Global Ratings, although the two studies followed different methodologies. The Central Bank survey also covered only adults, offering no measure of money skills among children, the very readers the book is written for.\n\nThe book is written by Dinesha Samaranayake, a banking professional with over a decade of experience across the World Bank Group, Standard Chartered and HSBC and draws on both her financial expertise and her experience as a mother of two. Its stories are built around saving for emergencies, budgeting and working towards financial goals and it includes interactive activities that children can complete with their parents.\n\nSamaranayake lives in Singapore and serves on the management team of Aidha, a Singaporean charity that promotes financial literacy and communication skills among migrant domestic workers and lower-income women."
