    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Samaranayake lives in Singapore and serves on the management team of Aidha, a Singaporean charity that promotes financial literacy and communication skills among migrant domestic workers and lower-income women.\n\nFirst Capital, a JXG (Janashakthi Group) company, was the event\u2019s Financial Literacy Partner. The company noted that its support formed part of a commitment to democratising financial knowledge and encouraging responsible investing and that, in its view, financial literacy goes beyond saving and is about building a healthy relationship with money from an early age.\n\nThe launch featured a panel discussion titled \u2018Raising a Money-Smart Generation\u2019, moderated by Good Life X COO Christina Gunasinghe. First Capital Holdings PLC Vice President Deshani Ratnayake urged parents not to pass their own fears about money and investing on to their children, encouraging them instead to let their children explore and learn through practical experiences, including small business ventures.\n\nGlobal Unites Founder Prashan De Visser highlighted the financial pressures facing young people and the need for early financial education. He drew on his own experience of investing in his children\u2019s Happy Waffles business to show how entrepreneurship can teach children about profit, money management and delayed gratification.\n\nAMW Capital Leasing and Finance PLC Chairman Indrajith Boyagoda encouraged parents to use pocket money, saving incentives and everyday conversations to help children practice financial responsibility, stressing the importance of leading by example at home. Meanwhile, the partnership adds to a string of financial literacy efforts by First Capital and the wider Janashakthi Group.\n\nThe company\u2019s Invest-ED programme reached more than 1,000 university students in 2025 through seminars at SLIIT and the Universities of Kelaniya, Jaffna and Colombo, while the group ran a \u2018Future Funders\u2019 workshop for children aged eight to 12 on the World Children\u2019s Day last year."
