    "md5": "30056ecba3e15c734fa487abcc169cc7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "EPF builds 10% stakes in Tokyo Cement and ACL Cables; discloses months later\n\nEPF now holds 10% of TKYO\u2019s voting and non-voting shares as well as 10% of ACL Cables\n\nThresholds crossed between February and July but disclosed to CSE only on November 5\n\nFund added Rs.Rs.12.8bn to listed equities in June quarter, with JKH and CTC taking over half\n\nThe Employees\u2019 Provident Fund (EPF) has built up 10 percent stakes in Tokyo Cement Company (Lanka) PLC (TKYO) and ACL Cables PLC through a series of transactions, crossing the thresholds between February and July this year, though the milestones were made public only this week through filings with the Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE).\n\nIn its letter to the CSE, the EPF stated that it holds 29,403,000 voting shares and 14,701,500 non-voting shares in TKYO, representing 10 percent of each class, having reached the non-voting threshold on February 17 and the voting threshold on April 8.\n\nA separate filing disclosed that the EPF\u2019s holding in ACL Cables reached 71,872,416 shares or 10 percent of the company\u2019s issued shares, on July 22.\n\nBoth letters are dated November 5, which means the announcements trailed the threshold dates by nearly nine months for TKYO\u2019s non-voting shares, almost seven months for its voting shares and about three and a half months for ACL Cables.\n\nThe EPF\u2019s voting stake in TKYO now matches the 29,403,000 shares held by Ube Singapore Holdings, which has a nominee director on the cement maker\u2019s board, as per TKYO\u2019s interim accounts for the September quarter. The EPF did not feature among the company\u2019s 20 largest voting shareholders a year earlier.\n\nIt has also emerged as the largest holder of TKYO\u2019s non-voting shares, more than doubling its stake from 6.21 million shares or 4.22 percent, as at September 30, 2025."
