    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "The EPF\u2019s voting stake in TKYO now matches the 29,403,000 shares held by Ube Singapore Holdings, which has a nominee director on the cement maker\u2019s board, as per TKYO\u2019s interim accounts for the September quarter. The EPF did not feature among the company\u2019s 20 largest voting shareholders a year earlier.\n\nIt has also emerged as the largest holder of TKYO\u2019s non-voting shares, more than doubling its stake from 6.21 million shares or 4.22 percent, as at September 30, 2025.\n\nThe ACL Cables stake, in contrast, has proved far more profitable on paper. ACL\u2019s interim accounts placed the EPF as the company\u2019s third largest shareholder at end-June, with 68.86 million shares or 9.58 percent, behind Chairman U.G. Madanayake and Managing Director Suren Madanayake.\n\nThe holding had risen from 61.90 million shares or 8.6 percent, at end-March, on an outlay of Rs.652 million. A further 3.01 million shares acquired in July then took it to the 10 percent mark, which leaves the fund alone holding more than a quarter of the 272.54 million shares in public hands at end-June.\n\nA 17.6 percent rise in ACL\u2019s share price to Rs.100 during the June quarter pushed the market value of the holding to Rs.6.89 billion, more than twice its Rs.3.32 billion cost.\n\nMeanwhile, the two disclosures form part of a wider equity buying push by the country\u2019s largest superannuation fund. The purchase cost of its listed equity portfolio rose 14.7 percent to Rs.100.15 billion in the June quarter, from Rs.87.33 billion at end-March, while its market value climbed 11.95 percent to Rs.204.94 billion, from Rs.183.07 billion.\n\nJohn Keells Holdings (JKH) and Ceylon Tobacco Company (CTC) absorbed more than half of the roughly Rs.12.8 billion added. The EPF deployed Rs.3.43 billion to lift its JKH holding 2.5 times to 280.79 million shares and Rs.3.35 billion to more than triple its CTC holding to 2.68 million shares."
