    "md5": "e1856b9b9c76d9173948be7edddf4bad",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2008,
    "chunk_text": "Households left with just Rs.4,000 a month after spending, down from Rs.13,000 in 2019\n\nAverage household\u2019s monthly margin after spending narrows to Rs.4,075, from Rs.13,284\n\nMedian spending overtakes median income by Rs.18,175, reversing a 2019 margin of Rs.5,789\n\nEstate households spend Rs.16,133 a month, more than they earn\n\nThe average Sri Lankan household is left with only about Rs.4,000 a month, after meeting its expenses, down from more than Rs.13,000 in 2019, as spending has outpaced incomes, the first official household income and expenditure survey since the economic crisis shows.\n\nThe preliminary data from the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS) put the average monthly household income at Rs.120,266 in the first quarter of 2025, against the average monthly expenditure of Rs.116,191, leaving a margin of just Rs.4,075 or 3.4 percent of income.\n\nIn 2019, the average household earned Rs.76,414 and spent Rs.63,130, retaining Rs.13,284 or 17.4 percent, meaning the margin has narrowed by about 69 percent as spending rose 84 percent, against a 57 percent increase in income.\n\nThe median household income of Rs.53,333 exceeded the median spending of Rs.47,544 by Rs.5,789 in 2019 but the position has since reversed, with the median spending of Rs.94,057 overtaking the median income of Rs.75,882 by Rs.18,175 or about 24 percent, in 2025. Although the two medians need not describe the same household, the shift points to a larger share of families spending more than they report earning.\n\nAt the constant 2016 prices, the median household income fell 11.1 percent, from 2019 to Rs.41,623, a steeper decline than the 1.7 percent slip in average income to Rs.65,969, which the DCS said suggests that the erosion of purchasing power was \u201cmore pronounced for the household at the midpoint of the income distribution\u201d.\n\nHowever, the real spending moved the other way, with the average expenditure rising 15 percent to Rs.63,734 and the median expenditure 23.6 percent to Rs.51,593."
  },
