    "chunk_text": "At the constant 2016 prices, the median household income fell 11.1 percent, from 2019 to Rs.41,623, a steeper decline than the 1.7 percent slip in average income to Rs.65,969, which the DCS said suggests that the erosion of purchasing power was \u201cmore pronounced for the household at the midpoint of the income distribution\u201d.\n\nHowever, the real spending moved the other way, with the average expenditure rising 15 percent to Rs.63,734 and the median expenditure 23.6 percent to Rs.51,593.\n\nIn the rural sector, home to 2.93 million of the 3.42 million people below the poverty line, the households earned Rs.110,613 and spent Rs.108,918, leaving only Rs.1,695 or 1.5 percent.\n\nMeanwhile, the estate households spent Rs.88,691 a month against an average income of Rs.72,558, a shortfall of Rs.16,133 or more than a fifth of their income. The estate sector also recorded the highest incidence of poverty, at 23 percent, compared with 17.3 percent in rural areas and 7.8 percent in urban areas.\n\nThe preliminary results do not show how the households are covering these gaps, although the DCS counts withdrawals from savings, bank deposits and provident funds as income, meaning the gaps persist even after such drawdowns.\n\nThe figures on household debt, also collected in the 2025 survey, are not part of the preliminary release.\n\nThe DCS put the poverty headcount index, the share of the population living below the official poverty line, at 16 percent in the first quarter of 2025, up 1.7 percentage points, from 14.3 percent in 2019, a rise the DCS said \u201csuggests a reversal of the previous declining trend in poverty\u201d.\n\nThe poverty line used in the survey was Rs.16,417 per person a month, almost 2.4 times Rs.6,966 of 2019.\n\nThe official poverty line has since risen to Rs.17,742 per person a month in September 2026, as per the DCS data, 8.1 percent above the line used in the survey. Almost all of that increase has come in the past year, as the line stood at Rs.16,413 in September 2025."
