    "md5": "b394ec60dd9b82354f39d015e435467d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "Investigation into July 1983 -- eye wash or whitewash?\n\nIf media reports are to be believed, 43 years after the 1983 government-sponsored pogrom against the Tamil community in the country,\u00a0 a number of NPP MPs have called for the appointment of a Parliamentary Select Committee (PSC)\u00a0 \u2018to conduct a comprehensive investigation into the 1983 Black July riots and their impact\u2019.\n\nLet\u2019s hope the government MPs who sponsored the motion are not holding their breath until the Tamil community thanks them for the belated inquiry. First off, let\u2019s be clear. What happened in July 1983 was not a riot.\n\nMobs of the then government supporters who happened to be Sinhalese, armed with address lists of members of the Tamil community, went from house to house killing and\u00a0 assaulting Tamil citizens. They burned their homes, looted their goods and destroyed their businesses.\n\nThe police and armed forces of the country (whom MP Arunachalam wants president Dissanayake to refer to as heroes) stood by idly; more often than not, they egged the mobs on. The then President of the country did nothing to stop the unfolding violence on a section of his countrymen. When he did speak, he did not condemn the brutal anti-Tamil violence or offer sympathy to the victims.\n\nHe described the attacks on the Tamil community as a reaction of the Sinhalese people who had been \u201cprovoked\u201d, ann attempt to justify the pogrom by presenting the actions of organised mobs as a spontaneous, collective outburst of majority frustration.\n\nMost of the immediate victims of that pogrom are probably dead or are at death\u2019s door today. The original leaders of the organisations which took up arms to defend the victim Tamil community are also dead.\u00a0 Around a fourth of the then Tamil population fled the country and are now citizens of those country\u2019s which offered them succour."
  },
