    "md5": "6c9cfc22e1c4bd6025b32b3063ccc74e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "Education is the epicentre of Albania\u2019s student protests\n\nStudent protester throws stone at Education Ministry building (photo credit Al Jezeera/AP)\n\nThe student unrest which engulfed South Asia and Western Europe since 2022 has now spread to Albania.\n\nIt\u2019s a safe bet that many people haven\u2019t heard of Albania, or have any idea where it\u2019s located.\n\nAlbania is in the Balkans, sharing borders with Macedonia, Greece, Kosovo and Montenegro. With a population of just 2.74 Million people, nearly half of whom have fled abroad looking for work, it is also the last socialist state in Europe, and the poorest.\n\nThough called an upper middle income country with a per capita GDP of $12, 490, the average gross monthly salary is just US$1,050 ($840 Net). Cost of living is very high. People say rents in Tirana, the capital, costs up to 75% of their incomes.\n\nStudent protesters ranging from high school to university broke into the Education Ministry on October 14, demanding better conditions, lower tuition fees and jobs. Despite a very strong government crackdown and police action, the protests continue unabated.\n\nThese protests grew out of the broader Flamingo protest movement which began almost five months ago. Alongside economic relief, protesters have been demanding that Albanian President Edi Rama and opposition leader Sali Berisha should both resign due to overwhelming corruption, a cry that has been taken up by the current wave of student protesters.\n\nThey are also demanding a stop to the proposed luxury holiday resort in an environmentally sensitive area funded by US president Donald Trump\u2019s son in law Jared Kushner.\n\nThe irony is that, while Albania blessed by nature is fast becoming a top Balkans\u00a0 tourist destination, most of the population is struggling to make ends meet. Cost of renting houses and apartments was a major focal point of the protests. Food prices too, are very high. Protesters say food is cheaper in Germany than in Albania."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
