    "md5": "fa97d3d1d4d72fe9a6b4dcb8269f0324",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "The irony is that, while Albania blessed by nature is fast becoming a top Balkans\u00a0 tourist destination, most of the population is struggling to make ends meet. Cost of renting houses and apartments was a major focal point of the protests. Food prices too, are very high. Protesters say food is cheaper in Germany than in Albania.\n\nPrime Minister Rama, who has ruled for fourteen years, is accused of being increasingly authoritarian. Protest leaders turned down his request for negotiations, which he did by placing six chairs symbolically, as an invitation, in front of a ministry building.\n\nProtesters broke up one of the chairs, refusing talks with the unpopular\u00a0 PM. Just as it happened in Sri Lanka in April 2022, when \u2018Gota Go Home\u2019 became the deafening rallying cry, Albanian protesters are fed up with the system and want the entire government changed along with the head of state as well as opposition leader.\n\nUnlike in Sri Lanka, this could turn out to be a long-drawn struggle. In Serbia, long-standing Prime Minister Alexander Vucic was ousted by student protesters after a two-year struggle, bitter but without bloodshed,\u00a0 in October. But in Albania, PM Rama is grimly determined to hang on to power, and has introduced draconian new legislation to quell the protests.\n\nWhile the police have so far refrained from firing live bullets, they have been breaking up protests with rubber bullets and water cannon. Bulldozers have been tearing up paved streets in Tirana, making it difficult for protesters to move. But they have been using the cobblestones to attack the police.\n\nRama has promised bonuses to the police, and his new anti-protest legislation makes parents of under-18 student protesters liable for any damage caused with fines of 5,500 Euros, a minor fortune in a country with such low wages. They will also be stripped of free education and scholarships."
  },
  {
