    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cc074cbf65319c3f3981a4c532ff2d13",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1879,
    "chunk_text": "Rama has promised bonuses to the police, and his new anti-protest legislation makes parents of under-18 student protesters liable for any damage caused with fines of 5,500 Euros, a minor fortune in a country with such low wages. They will also be stripped of free education and scholarships.\n\nBut none of this has discouraged the protesters. Albania\u2019s experiment with democracy is relatively recent, holding its first multi-party elections in 1991 after half a century of Communist rule.\n\nLittle Albania was under Ottoman rule for more than four centuries. Next, it was occupied early in the 20th century by Italy. In 1944, communist partisans under Enver Hoxha liberated Albania from Italians.\n\nBut the country was liberated only to become a hermit kingdom with all borders closed to the outside world. Hoxha banned religion. After centuries of Turkish rule, about 50% of Albania\u2019s population are Muslims, devided into a Sunni majority and a Betakshi Muslim minority, a liberal Sufi mystical branch of Islam whose global headquarters are located in Tirana. Various branches of Christianity including Roman Catholics, Eastern Orthodox Church,\u00a0 Evangelicals and other sects bring up the rest. But Albania still remains a secular state.\n\nHoxha was paranoid, and cut off ties not just with the West but also with Soviet Russia and China. It\u2019s tempting to call Albania Europe\u2019s North Korea, but Hoxha\u2019s contemporary Kim Il Sung remained close to both Moscow and Beijing,\u00a0 while Hoxha made sure that Albania had no friends anywhere.\n\nAlbania under Hoxha didn\u2019t have forced labour camps like in Soviet Russia or North Korea. But Hoxha was, if anything, even more paranoid than Kim Il Sung. Even though Albania had no hostile countries along its borders, he ordered the construction of thousands of concrete pillbox shaped bunkers along the borders and inside the country."
  },
