    "md5": "1f845b7fd0b51660c546f9624d7ae369",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "Albania under Hoxha didn\u2019t have forced labour camps like in Soviet Russia or North Korea. But Hoxha was, if anything, even more paranoid than Kim Il Sung. Even though Albania had no hostile countries along its borders, he ordered the construction of thousands of concrete pillbox shaped bunkers along the borders and inside the country.\n\nBorder guards were ordered to shoot anyone coming near the border\u2019s bunkers, and they usually did. The total cost of construction is estimated to have exceeded that of France\u2019s Maginot Line, constructed after World War I to stop a German invasion (In 1940, the German army simply avoided it and invaded through Belgium).\n\nRadio and television were state-controlled. People listened to Italian radio using antennas, but TV was limited to two hours a night, mostly showing Hoxha cutting ribbons. He died in 1985, aged 76, and his communist party successors relaxed the rules a bit, but the borders still remained closed.\n\nFinally, students stormed Tirana in 1990. Hoxha\u2019s statue was torn down, and in 1992 the first-multi party elections were won by current opposition leader Sali Berisha, who finally opened the borders. Albania remained a socialist state but experimented with capitalism. Most Albanians invested their money in five finance companies.\n\nAll of them crashed in 1997, and people\u2019s savings vanished overnight. Chaos followed, with looting everywhere. Over 2000 people died in the violence. Finally, a UN force of 7000 arrived in 2000 to maintain order and distribute food.\n\nNormalcy returned, and Albania joined NATO in 2009, while talks opened to join the European Union in 2022. But most Albanians still remain poor as they always did, hence the current wave of student protests."
