    "chunk_text": "Elephants carry kilos of bullets in their bodies in Sri Lanka\n\nWith hundreds killed annually, gunshot wounds, improvised explosive devices and shrinking habitats continue to threaten the country\u2019s wild elephant population\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s wild elephants are carrying large amounts of metal fragments inside their bodies after being shot by poachers, with some suffering from serious injuries even years after being attacked.\n\nEnvironmental activist Nayanaka Ranwella has raised concerns, claiming that elephant Agbo had at least 75 kilograms of lead from bullets inside its body, while the famous blind Galgamuwa tusker had more than 25 kilograms following gunshot injuries.\n\nHe accused successive governments and wildlife authorities of failing to take effective action against poachers and illegal activities inside forests.\n\nAccording to Ranwella, poachers and some farmers use locally made firearms known as \u2018Gal Katas\u2019, containing ammunition made from iron balls, bucket hooks and scrap metal.\n\nWhen fired, these fragments penetrate different parts of an elephant\u2019s body, causing multiple injuries.\n\n\u201cCountless gunshot wounds can be seen all over the body of the famous wild tusker Agbo. At least 75 gunshot wounds can be seen within a single square-foot area of its body. Some of the wounds have healed, but the iron fragments remain embedded inside the body. Others are still open and have not healed,\u201d he said.\n\nA senior veterinary association official explained that metal fragments could remain inside elephants for years, causing chronic inflammation, recurring infections, abscesses, persistent pain and difficulty walking.\n\nRemoving fragments lodged deep inside the body or near vital organs is not always possible. Affected elephants may require prolonged veterinary treatment, while some suffer permanent damage."
  },
