    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "A senior veterinary association official explained that metal fragments could remain inside elephants for years, causing chronic inflammation, recurring infections, abscesses, persistent pain and difficulty walking.\n\nRemoving fragments lodged deep inside the body or near vital organs is not always possible. Affected elephants may require prolonged veterinary treatment, while some suffer permanent damage.\n\nHe claimed to have raised concerns about elephant poaching for nearly 15 years without effective action.\n\nRanwella said 14 routes used by poachers had been identified inside Yala National Park and questioned why drones and hidden cameras were not being deployed to monitor them.\n\nHe also referred to the killing of an elephant named Neela in the Kala Oya area, claiming that information about the suspected killer had been submitted to wildlife authorities without any arrest being made six months later.\n\nRanwella raised concerns over a proposed rice mill in Ampara, claiming it could obstruct an elephant corridor used by between 200 and 300 elephants.\n\nHe said radio-collar tracking of two elephants, Udani and Sugala, had identified the proposed site as part of an important elephant route.\n\nAccording to Ranwella, the migration map was presented to former President Mahinda Rajapaksa, who reportedly expressed concern about construction along the corridor. However, the land was subsequently allocated for the proposed rice mill.\n\nRanwella warned that blocking the corridor connecting the Ampara Sanctuary with Buddhangala could force elephants towards Ampara town, increasing human-elephant conflict.\n\n\u201cIf even a single elephant entering the town causes such a commotion, imagine what happens when 200 or 300 arrive!\u201d he said. He also demanded an investigation into Agbo\u2019s injuries and the release of Government Analyst reports concerning the incident."
