    "md5": "c8e2df302fbbf5e0b525a29ddfd6f8d3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIf even a single elephant entering the town causes such a commotion, imagine what happens when 200 or 300 arrive!\u201d he said. He also demanded an investigation into Agbo\u2019s injuries and the release of Government Analyst reports concerning the incident.\n\nGunshot injuries accounted for 42 deaths, while electrocution and improvised explosive devices known as \u2018hakka patas\u2019 caused another 34 deaths each.\n\nAnother 23 elephants were recorded after their bodies had become putrefied, while infected wounds caused 11 deaths. Other causes included accidents, drowning, poisoning and worm infestations.\n\nThe Eastern Wildlife Region recorded the highest number of deaths at 60, followed by Anuradhapura with 57 and Polonnaruwa with 42.\n\nBetween 2010 and October 23, 2026, Sri Lanka recorded 5,397 elephant deaths.\n\nPolonnaruwa accounted for 1,181 deaths, followed by the Eastern Wildlife Region with 1,023 and Anuradhapura with 799.\n\nThe highest annual death toll was recorded in 2023, when 488 elephants died, followed by 455 in 2025 and 439 in 2022. From the slaughter of the Panamure Tusker to today\u2019s indiscriminate poaching.\n\nSri Lanka has a long history of elephant exploitation. the Panamure Tusker, killed on August 10, 1950, during the country\u2019s last elephant kraal, remains a symbol of resistance against the capture of wild elephants.\n\nThe operation, organised by Sir Francis Molamure, involved driving elephants into a wooden enclosure. One powerful Tusker repeatedly resisted capture and was eventually shot.\n\nIts killing triggered public outrage and parliamentary debate, contributing to the eventual end of elephant kraals.\n\nDuring British colonial rule, elephant hunting was encouraged to clear plantation land. Major Thomas William Rogers, a colonial officer in Badulla, was estimated to have killed between 1,400 and 2,000 elephants.\n\nHe died after reportedly being struck by lightning in Haputale in June 1845, an incident that became part of local folklore."
