    "chunk_size_bytes": 1310,
    "chunk_text": "During British colonial rule, elephant hunting was encouraged to clear plantation land. Major Thomas William Rogers, a colonial officer in Badulla, was estimated to have killed between 1,400 and 2,000 elephants.\n\nHe died after reportedly being struck by lightning in Haputale in June 1845, an incident that became part of local folklore.\n\nHowever, habitat destruction, poaching and human-elephant conflict continue to threaten their survival.\n\nAmid growing concerns, the Government launched the \u2018Hasthi Sena\u2019 (Elephant Force) operation in Galkiriyagama on November 2.\n\nThe islandwide operation is jointly implemented by the Ministry of Environment, Armed Forces and Department of Wildlife Conservation.\n\nEnvironment Minister Dhammika Patabendi said the initial phase would focus on protecting 17 elephants facing threats.\n\nHowever, its success will depend on addressing illegal firearms, explosive traps, electrocution and the destruction of elephant corridors.\n\nRanwella said he had continued his campaign under four governments and would keep speaking out until effective action was taken.\n\nFor a country that takes pride in its elephants, the challenge is not only preventing their deaths but protecting survivors from years of suffering caused by bullets and metal fragments lodged inside their bodies."
