    "md5": "4486cb96f1c460bf21a81fd41dfc3b59",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1582,
    "chunk_text": "Independent foreign team sought for post-mortem on tusker Kawanthissa\u2019s death\n\nA wildlife photographer has requested the Galgamuwa Magistrate\u2019s Court to order an independent foreign team to conduct a post-mortem examination into the death of the tusker Kawanthissa.\n\nThe request was made by wildlife photographer Pathum Bandara, who submitted an affidavit and made several requests to court when the magisterial inquiry into the death of the tusker came up before the Galgamuwa Magistrate\u2019s Court on November 4, 2026, by way of a motion.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Upul Jayasuriya, appearing for Bandara, also requested the court to issue orders for an assessment of the monetary value of the tusker and to ensure the preservation of its remains and body parts.\n\nHe further sought an order bringing the investigation into the death of the tusker under the direct supervision of the Magistrate.\n\nThe Magistrate is scheduled to deliver the court\u2019s decision on these requests on November 18.\n\nKawanthissa was an iconic, 11-foot-tall wild Asian elephant and one of Sri Lanka\u2019s tallest and largest tuskers. It roamed the Galgamuwa forest range before dying on October 16, 2026. A gunshot injury to its right front leg was discovered, and the resulting impact is suspected to have contributed to its death.\n\nPresident's Counsel Upul Jayasuriya with Attorneys-at-Law Kanishka Hikkaduwa, Nipuni Dissanayaka, Nilanka Fernando, Vidath Devaraja, Savana Ranatunga, Gihan Siriwardena, Kanchana Samaradiwakara and Rohitha Rajakarunanayake appeared on behalf of the rights of Pathum Bandara."
  },
