    "chunk_text": "In the end, the vehicle launched in 2023, keeping its novel design - an angular shape with a body made of stainless steel.\n\nIn 2025, Tesla sold just 20,237 Cybertrucks, a decline from 38,965 a year earlier, according to figures from Kelley Blue Book.\n\nTesla fans praised its retro-futuristic look, which some said looked like something \"straight out of Blade Runner\".\n\nBut critics were far less kind, joking about its appearance and labelling it \"ugly as sin\".\n\nSeveral recalls - including in 2025 affecting more than 46,000 Cybertrucks over concerns part of its trim could fall off - would not have helped.\n\n\"We need to make it at a price that people can afford,\" Musk told Wall Street analysts that year - which ended up being $61,000 (then \u00a348,000) for all the \"bells and whistles\".\n\nIn 2026, the so-called \"Cyberbeast\" model comes in at $102,000."
