    "chunk_text": "After midterm rout, Vance tries to build his own 2028 brand\n\nReuters - Trump has called Americans\u2019 affordability concerns a Democratic \u201choax.\u201d He \u200blaunched a war against Iran following sustained lobbying from Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu. Trump went on to endorse Netanyahu in the Israeli election.\n\nThe post for the first time put Vance openly on the side of ending the war.\n\nThree people familiar with the White House and Vance's communications told Reuters that the vice president was not breaking with Trump and said White House chief of staff Susie Wiles knew in advance about Vance's post.\n\nOn Friday, Trump reshuffled senior White House leadership, replacing \u200bWiles with Stephen Miller in a move that suggests the White House plans to double down on some of Trump\u2019s more hardline policies.\n\nTrump has acknowledged the Iran war played a role in the Republican rout because of the \u200bresulting high gas prices but has continued to defend it. \"I would have done nothing different,\" he told reporters on Wednesday.\n\nReuters could not determine whether Trump was aware of the post in advance.\n\nDonald Trump Jr., the president's eldest son, endorsed Vance's remarks \u200con social media, \u2060along with some conservative allies.\n\nThe dialogue further elevated Vance as a potential post-Trump conservative standard-bearer, said Michael Ricci, a Republican strategist and former adviser to House Speakers John Boehner and Paul Ryan.\n\nHe said Vance\u2019s message forced conservatives to debate the merits of the war, and more broadly, what core tenets Republican presidential candidates should embrace in 2028.\n\n\"It\u2019s simplistic to say this about him looking for ways to differentiate himself from Trump,\" Ricci said. \"This is about him using his megaphone of the vice presidency to set the terms of debate.\""
  },
