    "chunk_text": "He said Vance\u2019s message forced conservatives to debate the merits of the war, and more broadly, what core tenets Republican presidential candidates should embrace in 2028.\n\n\"It\u2019s simplistic to say this about him looking for ways to differentiate himself from Trump,\" Ricci said. \"This is about him using his megaphone of the vice presidency to set the terms of debate.\"\n\nDemocrats are poised to \u200btie Vance \u2013 or any Republican nominee \u2013 to Trump \u2060in 2028, especially if Trump plays a large role in vetting conservative hopefuls.\n\n\"There\u2019s a real challenge of how you position yourself to be viable in a general election while winning a primary that still requires you to bow down and kiss the ring,\u201d said Igor Volsky, a Democratic strategist.\n\nVance\u2019s brand did not emerge from the midterms unscathed. \u200bRepublicans lost in states where he campaigned. Some of the strategies he endorsed \u2013 including an ad by Ohio gubernatorial candidate Vivek Ramaswamy that cast doubt on the \u200bsexual abuse suffered by the \u2060victorious Democratic candidate, Amy Acton \u2013 may have swung races away from Republicans.\n\nOther possible 2028 presidential contenders have offered their diagnoses of the midterm defeat. US Senator Ted Cruz of Texas said Republicans hadn't done enough on affordability. Florida Governor Ron DeSantis said losing candidates had focused too heavily on transgender issues and neglected voter concerns about data centers and artificial intelligence. Secretary of State Marco Rubio has stayed silent.\n\nVance's effort to shape the debate was visible during the closing days of \u2060the campaign.\n\nIn stump \u200bspeeches, he told voters to support Republicans because they would investigate fraud in federal health care and antipoverty programs, tying that to the \u200bWhite House's immigration agenda and cost of living. He almost entirely ignored the Iran war and gas prices, and seldom mentioned Trump by name."
