    "chunk_id": "2026-11-08-dailymirrorlk-65918e5f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2d26c34c70ca8c3bd1f134dc13f3b0fe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "Butterfly swarms across Sri Lanka linked to natural monsoon migration\n\nColombo, Nov 08 (Daily Mirror) - Large swarms of white and yellow butterflies spotted across several parts of Sri Lanka in recent days are part of a natural seasonal migration triggered by monsoon rains and the growth of host plants, experts have explained.\n\nThe sightings, reported in areas including Ratnapura, Galle and Colombo, have attracted considerable attention on social media, with some questioning whether the unusual movement signals an approaching natural disaster.\n\nHowever, research and expert observations indicate that the phenomenon is a recurring migration within the island, mainly involving butterflies of the Pieridae family.\n\nA field study conducted by the Department of Zoology at the University of Peradeniya identified two main migration periods in Sri Lanka: the principal migration season from October to December, during the Second Inter-Monsoon, and a smaller migration period from March to April during the First Inter-Monsoon.\n\nButterfly expert Himesh Jayasinghe explained that monsoon rains encourage the growth of host plants, providing fresh leaves for caterpillars and suitable conditions for butterflies to lay eggs. The resulting increase in butterfly populations prompts them to disperse in search of food and breeding grounds, reducing competition for resources.\n\nResearch conducted by Prof. Kapila Dissanayake and a team from Sabaragamuwa University, published in the Taprobanica Journal in 2024, identified three migratory species: the Lesser Albatross (Appias galene), Common Albatross (Appias albina) and Lemon Emigrant (Catopsilia pomona).\n\nBased on observations at 60 locations across the island, the researchers identified three main flight paths, with the principal migration route running from the southeast towards the northwest, from Kebiliththa in Kumana National Park towards Wilpattu."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-08-dailymirrorlk-65918e5f",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-65918e5f",
    "date_str": "2026-11-08",
    "description": "Butterfly swarms across Sri Lanka linked to natural monsoon migration",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Butterfly-swarms-across-Sri-Lanka-linked-to-natural-monsoon-migration/108-354438",
