    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "18f4d0930677ff3053b981f6f1d03cff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Based on observations at 60 locations across the island, the researchers identified three main flight paths, with the principal migration route running from the southeast towards the northwest, from Kebiliththa in Kumana National Park towards Wilpattu.\n\nExperts said the traditional belief that butterflies migrate towards Sri Pada also has a scientific explanation. The behaviour, known as 'hill-topping', involves butterflies gathering at elevated locations such as Sri Pada and Kirigalpoththa, where changes in elevation and temperature help them locate potential mates.\n\nHowever, field studies indicate that most Albatross butterflies migrate towards the northwest rather than Sri Pada.\n\nExperts have also dismissed claims that large butterfly migrations are warnings of impending natural disasters, saying there is no scientific evidence to establish such a connection. Although butterflies are sensitive to atmospheric pressure, humidity, temperature and wind changes, their movements cannot be considered an official warning of cyclones or other disasters.\n\nMeanwhile, researchers have observed a decline in butterfly populations and migration activity compared with the past. Irregular monsoon patterns linked to climate change can disrupt breeding cycles, while invasive plants such as Guinea grass and Gandapana (Lantana camara) have affected native host plants, including Thora species.\n\nThe destruction of these plants threatens migratory butterfly species by reducing the availability of suitable breeding and feeding grounds.\n\nThe former president of the Butterfly Conservation Society of Sri Lanka said the October-November transition between the Southwest and Northeast monsoons creates favourable conditions for the growth of host plants in the dry zone, encouraging butterfly populations to increase and spread across the island.\n\nShe said the recent sightings were part of this natural internal migration, rather than an arrival of butterflies from overseas."
  },
