    "md5": "1b61347879ef3fb9157f9972406936a3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1866,
    "chunk_text": "Doctors to protest on Nov. 10 as Development Officers launch sick-note campaign tomorrow\n\nTwo major groups of public sector employees are preparing to step up trade union action over unresolved demands, with doctors planning a protest in Colombo on November 10 and Development Officers across the country set to report sick leave tomorrow (9).\n\nThe Government Medical Officers\u2019 Association (GMOA) announced that it would stage a protest outside the Colombo National Hospital on November 10, demanding solutions to doctors\u2019 salary and allowance issues and the shortage of medical professionals in the country.\n\nThe GMOA said it had repeatedly requested discussions with the Ministry of Health and other relevant authorities to resolve long-standing concerns but had yet to receive a satisfactory response.\n\nThe association said the cancellation of tax-concession vehicle import permits following the 2022 economic crisis, together with increased taxation on doctors\u2019 earnings and work-related allowances, had placed additional financial pressure on medical professionals.\n\nIt also pointed out that one-third of doctors\u2019 overtime and holiday allowances had been suspended under the 2025 public sector salary revision. The Government had reportedly promised to restore these payments once the country\u2019s economic situation improved.\n\nRaising concerns over staff shortages, the GMOA said fewer than 25,000 doctors were currently in active service under the Ministry of Health.\n\nIt said no scientific, evidence-based revision of approved staff cadre numbers across the health sector had been carried out since 2015, despite a significant expansion of healthcare services, institutions and units.\n\nAccording to the association, the failure to revise staff numbers had increased the workload of healthcare workers and threatened the quality of healthcare services."
