    "md5": "ab2d016647f7ff946e80112ac8d3ac58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo launches joint operation to tackle flooding; 60 drainage blockages identified\n\nA special joint operation involving several State institutions has been launched to address flooding and flash floods in Colombo and its suburbs, with 60 locations where water drainage is obstructed identified for urgent action.\n\nThe programme was launched today (8) under the leadership of Colombo Mayor Vrai Kelly Balthazar and Deputy Minister of Urban Development Eranga Gunasekara.\n\nThe initiative covers five electoral divisions under the Colombo Municipal Council (CMC), namely North Colombo, East Colombo, West Colombo, Borella and Central Colombo.\n\nAuthorities have begun clearing drainage blockages, cleaning canals and restoring drainage systems, giving priority to areas facing a high risk of flooding.\n\nInitial operations are being carried out at several locations, including Kirimandala Mawatha in Narahenpita, Haig Road in Bambalapitiya, Collingwood Place and Parakum Road in Wellawatte, the Cooray Urban Housing Scheme, Stafford Road in Kirulapone and the adjoining canal.\n\nOther locations include Heen Ela near Baseline Road, Aramaya Road in Dematagoda, the New Bazaar area in Central Colombo, De Mel Watta and Paradise Road.\n\nPersonnel from the Sri Lanka Army, Colombo Municipal Council, Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation, Urban Development Authority and Road Development Authority are participating in the operation.\n\nOfficials said the programme was not limited to removing immediate blockages but would also address long-standing drainage issues through repairs, regular maintenance and better coordination among State institutions.\n\nThe initiative comes after heavy rainfall on November 6 left several parts of Colombo and its suburbs inundated for hours, disrupting traffic and affecting daily activities.\n\nPettah, Dematagoda, Orugodawatte, Kollupitiya, Bambalapitiya and Kompanna Veediya were among the areas affected by the flooding."
