    "md5": "407c701c94da4100921f0b597e223d63",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "Former student Dilruha de Silva remembered his approachable nature and trademark humour: \u201cAn extraordinary man who took a genuine, personal interest in the school and the wellbeing of every student. He knew each of us by name and enforced his slightly strict rules with a unique blend of firmness, kindness and fairness. I still laugh remembering him walking around with his foot ruler during intervals and school socials, greeting everyone while ensuring couples maintained a strict 12-inch distance!\u201d\n\nThough a British educator, Sanders embraced Sri Lanka as his home in heart and spirit. When my wife Premani organised a Sil program for the CIS Junior School at Sambodhi Vihara, Sanders accompanied us to meet the late Ven. Daranagama Kusaladhamma Thero. Before any of us could remove our shoes, he had already removed his, entering the temple premises with profound respect. He named his home in Dorset \u201cSri Pada\u201d, and for his daughter\u2019s wedding in the UK, he personally brought a traditional Kukula Pahana, so that a symbol of Sri Lanka might shine at the ceremony.\n\nHis vitality was legendary. On annual expeditions to World\u2019s End and Sri Pada, he led from the front with energy to spare. On one descent from Sri Pada, he and the front group took a wrong turn towards Ratnapura \u2014 a seven-hour walking detour that became a cherished school legend. He was also a familiar figure across Colombo, leaving everyone in his dust as he jogged through the streets in the evenings.\n\nBeyond his academic standing, David possessed a rare personal warmth. He nurtured students not merely for examination papers, but for the world of work, entrepreneurship, and principled citizenship. The lives of my own children \u2014 and thousands of alumni globally \u2014 stand as enduring proof of his mentorship. At the conclusion of his funeral service in Dorset, he chose to play the CIS anthem as he departed: a final, poignant farewell to the school community he cherished."
