    "chunk_size_bytes": 1560,
    "chunk_text": "One voice, million hearts: Myriad reflections  of a maestro\n\nHaving watched \u201cRhythm of Guitar,\u201d which was like a Sri Lankan \u201cMamma Mia,\u201d I expected many to follow in the same genre. From Clarence to Milton, it is amazing how cinema can capture the versatility and vibrancy of valued celebrities. Today\u2019s column is not a conventional movie review but a concentration on \u201cOne Voice, One Million Hearts,\u201d from a managerial perspective.\n\n\u201cEda Raa\u201d (\u201cOne Voice, One Million Hearts\u201d), is a Sinhala musical movie that appeals to all masses. It is described as a biographical drama film that depicts the true life story of\u00a0 Milton Mallawarachchi, known as a musical legend with a \u201cmagical voice.\u201d Directed by Aruna Jayawardena and produced by Oneline Production and Entertainment in collaboration with Ranil Mallawarachchi, Dr. Arosha Fernando, and Chamaka Manjula, Channa Deshapriya covered the cinematography, with Shan Alvis in charge of editing and color grading. The movie has an amazing screenplay by Suneth Malinga Lokuheva and Priyanath Vidanapathirana, while Suresh Maliyedda provides the brilliant music as usual.\n\n\u201cEda Raa\u201d, like \u201cRhythm of Guitar\u201d reminds me of many memorable musical movies. Among such classics, \u201cSingin\u2019 in the Rain,\u201d \u201cThe Sound of Music,\u201d and \u201cThe Wizard of Oz\u201d are often regarded as the most popular. \u201cWest Side Story,\u201d \u201cCabaret,\u201d and \u201cMy Fair Lady\u201d also highlight the high impact created by musical movies. Mama Mia is one of the recent ones that created a music sensation."
  },
