    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cEda Raa\u201d, like \u201cRhythm of Guitar\u201d reminds me of many memorable musical movies. Among such classics, \u201cSingin\u2019 in the Rain,\u201d \u201cThe Sound of Music,\u201d and \u201cThe Wizard of Oz\u201d are often regarded as the most popular. \u201cWest Side Story,\u201d \u201cCabaret,\u201d and \u201cMy Fair Lady\u201d also highlight the high impact created by musical movies. Mama Mia is one of the recent ones that created a music sensation.\n\nLet us move from Mama Mia to Milton. It is indeed amazing to see a village lad who had a painful childhood rising to centerstage as a vibrant vocalist. He was born on 7 April 1944 in Kotte, Colombo. He completed his education at Ananda Sastralaya, Kotte, and got his first job as a bill clerk at the Welikada Co-Operative and later worked as a cashier at the Nawaloka business On 23 December 1973, he married Swarnamala Malathi Panangala, and was blessed with four children, Ranil, Harshini, Nadeeja, and Tharaka.\n\nAt early stages of his singing career, he entertained the audience with popular Hindi songs. He was the vocalist of a musical band called the \u201cSakyans\u201d, and thereafter the \u201cLa Ceylonians\u201d since 1965. He transitioned from not only Hindi songs to Sinhala originals but also from records to cassettes. He is acclaimed as the first Sinhala singer to produce a music cassette. It is interesting to see his mega musical journey from records to cassettes and then to CDs, aptly named as the \u201cPrince of Love Songs.\u201d\n\nMilton, like many other artists, was much into liquor and cigarettes. Whilst finding solace amidst pressure, he was slowly digging his own grave by sheer neglect of taking care of himself. The movie portrays him as a person who had plenty of fun with his friends, having moved from \u201crags to riches.\u201d Yet, he had a price to pay. With a brief illness, he\u00a0 passed away on 10 March 1998 at the age of 53."
