    "chunk_text": "Milton, like many other artists, was much into liquor and cigarettes. Whilst finding solace amidst pressure, he was slowly digging his own grave by sheer neglect of taking care of himself. The movie portrays him as a person who had plenty of fun with his friends, having moved from \u201crags to riches.\u201d Yet, he had a price to pay. With a brief illness, he\u00a0 passed away on 10 March 1998 at the age of 53.\n\nIn the movie, Roshan Ravindra portrays the complex character of Milton in a magnificent manner. The magic of Milton\u2019s music was aptly blended with the equal magic of Roshan\u2019s acting. Selecting the right person for the right reason is amply demonstrated here. He is not a stranger to both Cinema and television, with his previous powerful performances. The way he demonstrates the simplicity and seriousness of Milton at the same time from an innocent rural lover to an intense, mature music maestro was simply amazing.\n\nApart from Roshan with his ravaging role as Milton, Nethmi Nisheka Roshel Rogers as Swarna, Senali Fonseka as Annette,\u00a0 and Sheshadrie Priyasad as Shirani have done justice to the assigned roles as the three beautiful women surrounding the life of Milton. Further Biyanka Amarasinghe as Victor Rathnayake, Vishwa Kodikara as Vijeya Kumaratunga and Kalana Gunasekara as Premakeerthi de Alwis rekindle the visual memories we have about the golden times of artistic giants of Sri Lanka."
