    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan University Rankings: The free fall (Part 1)\n\nSame income as Indonesia and Vietnam, a fraction of their academic standing \u2013 and the gap widened again this year\n\nOn 4 November, Quacquarelli Symonds (QS), the London-based higher education analytics firm, released its Asia University Rankings for 2027. For Sri Lanka, the results made uncomfortable reading. The University of Colombo, still the country\u2019s best-ranked university, dropped 46 places to 364th in Asia. The University of Peradeniya fell to 389th and the University of Sri Jayewardenepura to 398th. Each of the three recorded its lowest placing in four years. Of the 12 Sri Lankan universities ranked, eight went down, two went up and two stayed where they were.\n\nTwo years ago, the University of Colombo was 275th in Asia. It has now lost 89 places in two editions. In the global QS World University Rankings, it slipped from the 951\u20131,000 band in 2025 to the 1,001\u20131,200 band, where it has stayed for two consecutive years.\n\nI wrote on this subject in these pages in March 2025, under the title \u201cAre Sri Lankan universities not ashamed of their poor global rankings?\u201d That article argued that our universities sit well below where a country with our income and literacy levels should be. Twenty months later, the question is no longer whether we are behind. It is how fast we are falling further behind, and why so few people in positions of responsibility seem to mind.\n\nThis article updates the numbers and looks closely at the full QS Asia 2027 dataset \u2013 some 1,722 universities from 26 countries and territories \u2013 to see what it tells us about Sri Lanka compared with its natural peers: India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Indonesia, Malaysia, Vietnam, Thailand and the Philippines.\n\nUniversity rankings are sometimes dismissed as a vanity exercise for vice-chancellors. That would be a mistake. For a business audience, rankings matter in at least four practical ways."
  },
