    "md5": "f0e6fe72785aa9d0965d31f2b85b2d00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "This article updates the numbers and looks closely at the full QS Asia 2027 dataset \u2013 some 1,722 universities from 26 countries and territories \u2013 to see what it tells us about Sri Lanka compared with its natural peers: India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Indonesia, Malaysia, Vietnam, Thailand and the Philippines.\n\nUniversity rankings are sometimes dismissed as a vanity exercise for vice-chancellors. That would be a mistake. For a business audience, rankings matter in at least four practical ways.\n\nSecond, they influence where our brightest school-leavers go. Families with means increasingly send their children to India, Malaysia, Australia or the UK. Every such student is foreign exchange leaving the country, and often a future professional who does not return.\n\nThird, they affect investment. Foreign companies choosing a location for a technology centre or a back office look for a deep pool of well-trained graduates and for universities they can partner with on research. A weak university brand is a weak national talent brand.\n\nFourth, higher education is itself an export industry. Malaysia has publicly set a target of 250,000 international students by 2030. Sri Lanka, with a strategic location, low costs and a largely English-literate professional class, barely competes.\n\nIn short, rankings are a proxy for how the rest of the world values the human capital we produce. That is a business issue, not just an academic one.\n\nBefore going further, a caveat. Rankings are imperfect. Half of the QS Asia score comes from reputation surveys of academics and employers, which favour large, well-known and English-speaking institutions. The weightings are a matter of judgement, and a university that serves its national mission well can still rank modestly. QS has also expanded its tables so quickly that year-to-year rank changes need careful reading."
