    "chunk_size_bytes": 2015,
    "chunk_text": "Before going further, a caveat. Rankings are imperfect. Half of the QS Asia score comes from reputation surveys of academics and employers, which favour large, well-known and English-speaking institutions. The weightings are a matter of judgement, and a university that serves its national mission well can still rank modestly. QS has also expanded its tables so quickly that year-to-year rank changes need careful reading.\n\nThe most damning way to look at Sri Lanka\u2019s position is against countries with similar or lower incomes. According to the International Monetary Fund\u2019s World Economic Outlook (April 2026), Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP per capita in 2025 was about $ 5,000. That is almost exactly the level of Indonesia ($ 5,082) and Vietnam\n\n($ 4,829). It is nearly double that of India ($ 2,675) and Bangladesh ($ 2,636) and three times that of Pakistan ($ 1,696).\n\nIf university standing simply followed national income, Sri Lanka would sit alongside Indonesia and Vietnam, and well ahead of India, Bangladesh and Pakistan. It does neither. Table I, which updates the first table of my 2025 article, sets out the global picture.\n\nThe pattern is stark. Every country in the table, rich or poor, has a flagship university ranked in the top 600 in the world. Sri Lanka\u2019s best is outside the top 1,000. Pakistan, with one-third of our per capita income, has 18 universities in the global list, and its best, Quaid-i-Azam University, is at 381st. Bangladesh has 13, led by the University of Dhaka at 600th. India has 52, and IIT Delhi is at 118th. Indonesia, at our income level, has 20 ranked universities with Universitas Indonesia at 191st.\n\nTo be fair, not every neighbour is rising. Pakistan\u2019s and Bangladesh\u2019s flagships have slipped in the global table over the last two years, as have those of the Philippines and Vietnam. And Sri Lanka did add one university, Moratuwa, to the global list. But slipping from 315th to 381st, as Quaid-i-Azam did, is a very different problem from being stuck beyond 1,000."
