    "chunk_text": "To be fair, not every neighbour is rising. Pakistan\u2019s and Bangladesh\u2019s flagships have slipped in the global table over the last two years, as have those of the Philippines and Vietnam. And Sri Lanka did add one university, Moratuwa, to the global list. But slipping from 315th to 381st, as Quaid-i-Azam did, is a very different problem from being stuck beyond 1,000.\n\nIt would be convenient to blame the decline on QS adding hundreds of new universities to its tables. The Asia list has indeed grown rapidly \u2013 from roughly 1,000 institutions in the 2025 edition to 1,529 in 2026 and 1,722 this year. A bigger field means more competitors, and more ways to be overtaken.\n\nHowever, QS itself has rejected that explanation for Sri Lanka\u2019s latest fall. According to QS data shared with the Daily Mirror, only two newcomers ranked above Colombo or Sri Jayewardenepura this year, accounting for about two places of each university\u2019s decline. The main reason is that regional rivals improved while Sri Lankan scores stood still. A typical university in the table improved its overall score by about 1.7 points; Colombo\u2019s slipped from 39.4 to 39.0 and Sri Jayewardenepura\u2019s from 37.8 to 37.5. In a race, standing still is the same as going backwards.\n\nIt is also notable that Sri Lanka had no new entrant at all in 2027. Of the 195 universities that entered the Asia rankings for the first time this year, India contributed 34, Indonesia 22, Vietnam eight, Pakistan seven and Bangladesh six. Sri Lanka contributed none. Our neighbours are widening the base of their systems; we are not.\n\nTable II, which replaces the second table of my previous article, shows how each of the 12 Sri Lankan universities fared this year."
