    "chunk_text": "It is also notable that Sri Lanka had no new entrant at all in 2027. Of the 195 universities that entered the Asia rankings for the first time this year, India contributed 34, Indonesia 22, Vietnam eight, Pakistan seven and Bangladesh six. Sri Lanka contributed none. Our neighbours are widening the base of their systems; we are not.\n\nTable II, which replaces the second table of my previous article, shows how each of the 12 Sri Lankan universities fared this year.\n\nThe longer view is even more sobering. In the 2019 QS Asia rankings, the University of Peradeniya was 174th and Colombo 188th, albeit in a much smaller field. Both are now well below 350th. In 2022, five Sri Lankan universities were in Asia\u2019s top 200 for employer reputation. Today only Colombo remains, at 192nd.\n\nIn the second part of this article, I will look inside the QS data to see exactly where Sri Lankan universities lose their marks, what our peers did differently, and what policymakers, university leaders and the business community can do to reverse the fall.\n\n(The author is a policy researcher cum academic. AI tools including Claude.ai, Perplexity AI and Grok.com have been used in the compilation of the article. The author has validated the facts and takes full responsibility. The ideas expressed are personal. The author can be reached at [email\u00a0protected].)"
