    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "Rooftop solar: A national asset or a burden on national electricity grid?\n\nDespite benefits, the rapid growth of solar power presents significant operational challenges for electricity utilities, particularly in developing countries where electricity grids are often relatively weak, with limited flexibility and modern control systems\n\nSri Lanka must recognise the economic and environmental benefits of solar power while addressing the growing technical and financial challenges faced by electricity utilities.\n\nSri Lanka's electricity sector is undergoing a significant transformation. What began as a modest rooftop solar programme has developed into one of the country's most important sources of renewable electricity. Thousands of households, commercial establishments and industries have invested their own funds to generate electricity, thereby reducing dependence on conventional power plants.\n\nHowever, this rapid expansion has also created significant challenges for electricity utilities, mainly due to the intermittent nature of solar power generation. When clouds pass over solar installations, electricity generation can drop suddenly and substantially, requiring the utility to immediately compensate for the shortfall to maintain a stable electricity supply. To manage these unpredictable fluctuations, the utility must maintain sufficient spinning reserves, ready to increase generation at short notice, adding to the cost of operating the electricity system. Furthermore, during very sunny weekends, when electricity demand is relatively low, conventional generation has to be reduced to accommodate the high solar output. This can significantly reduce the system's inertia, increasing the risk of frequency instability and making it more challenging to maintain the stability and reliability of the national power system."
