    "chunk_size_bytes": 1583,
    "chunk_text": "However, this rapid expansion has also created significant challenges for electricity utilities, mainly due to the intermittent nature of solar power generation. When clouds pass over solar installations, electricity generation can drop suddenly and substantially, requiring the utility to immediately compensate for the shortfall to maintain a stable electricity supply. To manage these unpredictable fluctuations, the utility must maintain sufficient spinning reserves, ready to increase generation at short notice, adding to the cost of operating the electricity system. Furthermore, during very sunny weekends, when electricity demand is relatively low, conventional generation has to be reduced to accommodate the high solar output. This can significantly reduce the system's inertia, increasing the risk of frequency instability and making it more challenging to maintain the stability and reliability of the national power system.\n\nThe answer requires a comprehensive assessment of both its benefits and its challenges.\n\nAccording to information released by the National System Operator (NSO) in September 2026, Sri Lanka\u2019s total installed solar power capacity exceeded 3,070 MW, comprising approximately 2,542 MW of rooftop solar and 534 MW of ground-mounted solar installations.\n\nDepending on weather conditions, solar generation now contributes approximately 15\u201325% of the country\u2019s daily electricity requirements. This is a remarkable achievement for a country that remains dependent on imported fossil fuels for a considerable portion of its electricity generation."
