    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Depending on weather conditions, solar generation now contributes approximately 15\u201325% of the country\u2019s daily electricity requirements. This is a remarkable achievement for a country that remains dependent on imported fossil fuels for a considerable portion of its electricity generation.\n\nThese figures represent a substantial contribution to national electricity supply. Furthermore, rooftop solar electricity consumed directly within households and commercial premises reduces the electricity that must be supplied through the grid, even where this self-consumption is not fully reflected in conventional generation statistics.\n\nIt is important to distinguish installed capacity from actual generation. A 1,000 MW solar installation does not generate at full capacity throughout the day, as its output depends on sunlight and weather conditions. With a typical capacity factor (plant factor) of 15\u201320%, it produces the same amount of electricity over a year as a 150\u2013200 MW power plant operating continuously at full capacity.\n\nNevertheless, the contribution of solar electricity during daylight hours has become sufficiently significant that the electricity system would face a very different operating and financial situation without it.\n\nSri Lanka should not approach rooftop solar development as a conflict between electricity utilities and solar investors. Both have an essential role in the country's energy future\n\nOne of the greatest benefits of solar electricity is its ability to reduce the amount of electricity generated from conventional thermal power plants.\n\nEvery unit of solar electricity that displaces oil- or coal-based generation can reduce fuel consumption. This is especially important for Sri Lanka, where imported fossil fuels create a substantial foreign-exchange burden.\n\nHowever, the actual economic benefit depends on which generating plant is displaced."
