    "chunk_size_bytes": 2000,
    "chunk_text": "Every unit of solar electricity that displaces oil- or coal-based generation can reduce fuel consumption. This is especially important for Sri Lanka, where imported fossil fuels create a substantial foreign-exchange burden.\n\nHowever, the actual economic benefit depends on which generating plant is displaced.\n\nThese figures are reported average costs(forecast) for different sources, not the marginal cost of generating an additional unit at any particular hour. They nevertheless demonstrate why the economic value of solar cannot be determined simply by comparing its purchase tariff with the overall average generation cost.\n\nWhen solar electricity replaces expensive oil-fired generation, the financial benefit can be considerable. Conversely, when it displaces comparatively inexpensive generation or is produced during periods of excess supply, its immediate economic value may be much lower.\n\nThe correct comparison is therefore between the full cost of solar electricity and the actual cost of the electricity that would otherwise have been generated at that time.\n\nThis is particularly relevant when evaluating claims that Net Metering creates financial losses for electricity utilities.\n\nAnother important benefit is the opportunity to conserve water stored in hydropower reservoirs.\n\nDuring periods when solar generation is available, it may be possible to reduce hydropower generation and retain water for use during evening peak-demand hours.\n\nThis effectively allows hydropower reservoirs to serve as a form of energy storage, although the benefit depends on reservoir levels, water management requirements and whether water would otherwise have been spilled. Such opportunities can reduce the need to operate expensive thermal plants during peak demand.\n\nSolar power also contributes to energy security. During periods of coal plant outages, fuel shortages or reduced hydropower availability, distributed solar generation can reduce daytime demand on conventional generating stations."
