    "chunk_size_bytes": 1848,
    "chunk_text": "Solar power also contributes to energy security. During periods of coal plant outages, fuel shortages or reduced hydropower availability, distributed solar generation can reduce daytime demand on conventional generating stations.\n\nDespite these benefits, the rapid growth of solar power presents significant operational challenges for electricity utilities, particularly in developing countries where electricity grids are often relatively weak, with limited flexibility and modern control systems. Utilities must continuously balance electricity generation with demand to maintain a stable and reliable power supply. Unlike conventional power plants, solar generation is highly dependent on sunlight and weather conditions, making its output variable and sometimes unpredictable.\n\nOne of the major challenges is the changing pattern of electricity demand throughout the day. As rooftop solar generation increases in the morning, demand for electricity from the national grid falls considerably. However, as the sun sets, solar generation declines rapidly while household electricity demand rises, creating what is commonly known as the duck curve. Utilities must therefore maintain sufficient conventional generating capacity to meet the evening peak, even when these power plants operate at reduced output during the day.\n\nFurthermore, sudden changes in solar generation, particularly due to passing clouds, require conventional power plants to increase or decrease their output rapidly. This demands flexible generating capacity, adequate spinning reserves and effective frequency control, all of which involve additional costs. These challenges are particularly serious in smaller and weaker power systems, where limited generation flexibility, low system inertia and inadequate grid infrastructure make it more difficult to maintain stability."
  },
