    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e55710af02f86de93bee0f10b273f681",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1810,
    "chunk_text": "Furthermore, sudden changes in solar generation, particularly due to passing clouds, require conventional power plants to increase or decrease their output rapidly. This demands flexible generating capacity, adequate spinning reserves and effective frequency control, all of which involve additional costs. These challenges are particularly serious in smaller and weaker power systems, where limited generation flexibility, low system inertia and inadequate grid infrastructure make it more difficult to maintain stability.\n\nThe second major challenge occurs in distribution networks. Traditionally, electricity flows from power stations through transmission and distribution systems towards consumers. Rooftop solar changes this arrangement by allowing consumers to export electricity back into the distribution network. When a large number of solar installations are connected to the same feeder, local generation may exceed local electricity consumption. This can result in reverse power flow, voltage rise, protection coordination difficulties and, in some cases, overloading of distribution transformers or feeders. Excessively high voltage can also cause solar inverters to disconnect automatically, reducing generation and creating further voltage fluctuations.\n\nThese are not merely theoretical concerns. The Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka (PUCSL), in its analysis of rooftop solar integration, identified distribution network hosting capacity and inadequate real-time monitoring as major obstacles to further solar expansion.\n\nFollowing the nationwide blackout in February 2025, additional attention was given to inverter protection settings and grid-support capabilities. The regulator subsequently reported that compliance with updated inverter protection requirements remained inadequate."
  },
