    "language": "en",
    "md5": "27fca8abe53d479adca08fb9710d5394",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1578,
    "chunk_text": "Following the nationwide blackout in February 2025, additional attention was given to inverter protection settings and grid-support capabilities. The regulator subsequently reported that compliance with updated inverter protection requirements remained inadequate.\n\nThe financial challenges: Are net metering and net accounting really causing losses?\n\nPerhaps the most controversial issue is the financial impact of Net Metering and Net Accounting on electricity utilities. Under conventional Net Metering or Net Accounting, electricity exported to the grid can offset electricity imported by the consumer on a unit-for-unit basis. However, electricity exported during the daytime does not necessarily have the same economic value as electricity imported during the evening peak.\n\nA consumer may export solar electricity when relatively inexpensive generation is available, but consume electricity at night when the utility must operate more expensive generating plants. Furthermore, electricity tariffs recover more than the direct cost of energy generation. They also contribute towards transmission and distribution infrastructure, maintenance, system operation and other fixed costs.\n\nAs more consumers reduce their net electricity purchases through solar generation, the utility may recover less revenue towards these costs. This can create financial pressure and potentially transfer costs to consumers who do not own solar installations. The concern is legitimate, particularly from the perspective of lower-income households that may not be able to afford rooftop solar."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-c109b5a2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c109b5a2",
