    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-c109b5a2-0007",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fae25bf29dc3a31a81bee2f9731c3117",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "As more consumers reduce their net electricity purchases through solar generation, the utility may recover less revenue towards these costs. This can create financial pressure and potentially transfer costs to consumers who do not own solar installations. The concern is legitimate, particularly from the perspective of lower-income households that may not be able to afford rooftop solar.\n\nOn the other hand, solar generation does not automatically eliminate existing capacity payments, network maintenance costs or the need to retain generating capacity for periods without sunlight. Therefore, the actual financial impact can only be determined by considering all these factors together.\n\nIn September 2026, the Ministry of Energy directed that new rooftop solar connections and extensions of existing agreements should operate under the Net Plus scheme, rather than the traditional Net Metering and Net Accounting arrangements.\n\nUnder Net Plus, electricity generation and electricity consumption are accounted for separately, allowing solar generation to receive an approved payment while electricity purchased from the grid is billed under the applicable consumer tariff.\n\nThe Government has justified this change partly on the grounds that electricity exported during the day should not automatically compensate for higher-cost electricity consumed at night. However, the policy has generated considerable concern among solar investors and industry participants.\n\nThere is also a regulatory difference of interpretation. In a September 2026 clarification, PUCSL maintained that separate measurement of electricity imports and exports could be implemented without necessarily discontinuing the existing commercial arrangements under Net Metering and Net Accounting."
  },
