    "md5": "c6a2b93839d2abee88e7e0332b3514aa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1913,
    "chunk_text": "The National System Operator (NSO) has outlined plans to introduce approximately 1,240 MW of battery storage capacity, with an energy storage capability of 4,660 MWh, during 2027\u20132029. However, the location of these storage facilities is equally important. While large batteries connected to grid substations can support overall power system stability, they may not resolve local voltage fluctuations and transformer overloading caused by high rooftop solar penetration. Therefore, a coordinated approach involving grid-scale storage, strategically located distribution-level batteries and consumer-owned storage systems is essential.\n\nSri Lanka should not approach rooftop solar development as a conflict between electricity utilities and solar investors. Both have an essential role in the country\u2019s energy future.\n\nElectricity utilities must remain financially sustainable and maintain a reliable electricity supply. At the same time, renewable energy investors should receive fair compensation for electricity that provides genuine economic value to the national grid.\n\nA balanced policy should therefore begin with an independent and transparent assessment of solar power\u2019s actual contribution. Such an assessment should compare the hourly operating costs of the electricity system with rooftop solar and without rooftop solar, considering avoided fuel expenditure, hydropower reservoir management, network losses, fixed generation costs, capacity payments, energy storage requirements and grid stability.\n\nIt should also examine whether existing electricity tariff structures distribute system costs fairly among solar and non-solar consumers.\n\nThe longer-term solution requires modernising the distribution network, improving real-time monitoring, enforcing smart-inverter standards and introducing more dynamic tariff arrangements that recognise the changing value of electricity throughout the day."
  },
  {
