    "md5": "cca20b81689e0bea950dbd42f4b5da6b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1667,
    "chunk_text": "The longer-term solution requires modernising the distribution network, improving real-time monitoring, enforcing smart-inverter standards and introducing more dynamic tariff arrangements that recognise the changing value of electricity throughout the day.\n\nFinally, any changes to electricity tariffs or connection schemes should be implemented through a transparent, consultative process that maintains investor confidence and protects consumers.\n\nRooftop solar has become a valuable national energy resource, making a substantial contribution to Sri Lanka\u2019s electricity supply, reducing dependence on imported fuels, conserving hydropower resources under suitable conditions and supporting the country\u2019s transition towards cleaner energy. Equally important, much of this generation capacity has been developed through private investment by households and businesses, reducing the need for government and utility capital expenditure on additional daytime generation.\n\nAt the same time, the growing contribution of solar power presents genuine technical and financial challenges for electricity utilities. Its intermittent nature, the need for costly spinning reserves, reduced system inertia during periods of high solar generation, evening peak-demand requirements and limitations in existing distribution networks all require careful attention. The financial sustainability of electricity utilities and the fair recovery of network costs must also be ensured. These challenges, however, should be addressed through appropriate grid development, modern technologies and sound regulatory policies rather than by discouraging further investment in rooftop solar."
  },
