    "md5": "ee4850a6426a9244fa483798ea08cb9b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "At the same time, the growing contribution of solar power presents genuine technical and financial challenges for electricity utilities. Its intermittent nature, the need for costly spinning reserves, reduced system inertia during periods of high solar generation, evening peak-demand requirements and limitations in existing distribution networks all require careful attention. The financial sustainability of electricity utilities and the fair recovery of network costs must also be ensured. These challenges, however, should be addressed through appropriate grid development, modern technologies and sound regulatory policies rather than by discouraging further investment in rooftop solar.\n\nBefore making fundamental changes to Net Metering and Net Accounting arrangements, it would be prudent for the authorities to undertake and publish a comprehensive, transparent cost-benefit assessment of rooftop solar generation. Such an assessment should establish its true economic value to the national electricity system and provide a sound basis for fair and sustainable tariff policies, while protecting existing contractual commitments and investor confidence.\n\nSri Lanka has already made considerable progress in developing rooftop solar through the participation of its citizens and businesses. Preserving this achievement while addressing the legitimate concerns of electricity utilities requires a balanced, long-term approach. The priority should be to strengthen and modernise the electricity grid, introduce appropriate energy storage and encourage better utilisation of solar electricity through suitable tariff incentives.\n\nThe challenge before Sri Lanka is not to restrict the growth of solar power, but to develop an electricity system capable of accommodating it safely, reliably and economically. With proper planning and balanced policies, rooftop solar can continue to benefit consumers, electricity utilities and the national economy alike."
