    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "36db2e688d167d57d410fd547c769e77",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1528,
    "chunk_text": "State and your hard-earned money: Moral contract  AKD cannot afford to ignore\n\nPresident and Finance Minister delivering his second Budget in November 2025. His third Budget will be read in Parliament this Thursday (12) amid growing concern over high taxes and poor public services\n\nThe third Budget\u2019s ultimate test will be neither the enthusiasm surrounding revenue achievements nor the generosity of promised concessions. It will be whether ordinary citizens believe they are treated fairly and their money is used responsibly. Failure to honour this moral contract could deepen dissent and undermine even worthwhile policies, including good-governance and anti-corruption programs\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s third Budget is approaching amid growing concern over high taxes and poor public services. The AKD Government, closely following International Monetary Fund (IMF) prescriptions, takes pride in exceeding revenue targets. Yet its satisfaction is not shared by many taxpayers, who question whether they receive value for the money they surrender to the State. Fiscal success means little to citizens unless it translates into better services and improved living conditions.\n\nWith the Opposition capitalising on these grievances, pressure for tax relief is mounting. AKD and his senior ministers have promised reductions in the forthcoming Budget without compromising revenue targets. The challenge is to turn these assurances into meaningful relief while preserving the hard-won gains in fiscal stability."
  },
