    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d8a98b757607aa4d4ad88aa8d9ba6acd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "With the Opposition capitalising on these grievances, pressure for tax relief is mounting. AKD and his senior ministers have promised reductions in the forthcoming Budget without compromising revenue targets. The challenge is to turn these assurances into meaningful relief while preserving the hard-won gains in fiscal stability.\n\nThis is more than a book about taxation and expenditure. It examines the moral relationship between the State and its citizens, urging policymakers to look beyond fiscal targets and consider fairness, integrity and public purpose. Those preparing Sri Lanka\u2019s next Budget should keep these principles firmly in view.\n\nMauro begins with a fundamental question: what is the right thing to do when a Government collects and spends public money?\n\nTaxation is usually debated in terms of revenue, deficits, debt sustainability and growth. Governments need resources to provide services, maintain infrastructure and meet social obligations. The need for taxation is rarely disputed; the harder questions are how much to collect, from whom and for what purposes.\n\nYet taxation is also a moral issue. Every rupee collected comes from income that individuals or businesses could otherwise spend, save or invest in education and better living standards. The State must therefore offer a convincing public justification for taking part of those earnings.\n\nCitizens have a duty to contribute to the common good, and a functioning State requires adequate resources. But this obligation is reciprocal: the State\u2019s authority to tax carries a corresponding responsibility to serve its people.\n\nThis is more than a book about taxation and expenditure. It examines the moral relationship between the State and its citizens, urging policymakers to look beyond fiscal targets and consider fairness, integrity and public purpose\n\nMauro explores the growing concern over fairness in taxation and the distribution of income and wealth."
  },
  {
