    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5d5188496702da667c5335aead57c3e3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "This is more than a book about taxation and expenditure. It examines the moral relationship between the State and its citizens, urging policymakers to look beyond fiscal targets and consider fairness, integrity and public purpose\n\nMauro explores the growing concern over fairness in taxation and the distribution of income and wealth.\n\nThese principles need not conflict. A fair tax system should recognise individual effort without placing a disproportionate burden on those least able to finance public services. Fairness requires balancing these concerns.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, taxes affect groups differently. Salaried employees taxed at source have little scope to avoid or defer payment, while small businesses face substantial compliance costs. Consumption taxes weigh especially heavily on lower-income households, which spend a larger share of their earnings on essentials. These differences matter when assessing who bears the burden of taxation and whether the system treats citizens fairly.\n\nThe test is not merely whether the Government raises enough revenue, but whether the burden is shared fairly. Fairness does not require cutting every tax or shielding wealthy individuals and profitable businesses from higher contributions. It requires a system citizens can understand and accept.\n\nAKD\u2019s forthcoming Budget should therefore target genuine inequities and ease disproportionate burdens, rather than use tax relief to reward selected groups for political advantage. Fairness, not favouritism, should guide relief.\n\nMauro examines the State\u2019s expanding role. Beyond law and order and basic administration, modern governments are responsible for education, healthcare, transport, infrastructure and social protection.\n\nThese obligations require more revenue, but also demand greater efficiency and accountability. A larger State must demonstrate that it uses public money well."
  },
