    "md5": "d65a8a52a5a58864ca949956a41a4ac9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1879,
    "chunk_text": "Mauro examines the State\u2019s expanding role. Beyond law and order and basic administration, modern governments are responsible for education, healthcare, transport, infrastructure and social protection.\n\nThese obligations require more revenue, but also demand greater efficiency and accountability. A larger State must demonstrate that it uses public money well.\n\nNot all these failures originated with the present Government. It inherited serious economic and institutional problems, limited fiscal space, heavy debt obligations and competing demands. Nevertheless, its performance must be judged by the services it delivers, not simply the revenue it collects.\n\nFiscal consolidation cannot indefinitely justify high taxes while the quality of public services remains inadequate. Taxpayers deserve tangible benefits in return for their contributions.\n\nThe next Budget must therefore be assessed not only against revenue targets and the IMF\u2019s primary-balance requirement, but also against visible improvements in daily life. The State must learn to spend as carefully as it collects: fiscal discipline and better services must advance together.\n\nCorruption is among the most important issues Mauro examines, particularly for Sri Lanka. He explores why it persists and how institutional opportunities and incentives enable public resources to be diverted for private gain.\n\nThe damage extends beyond missing money: corruption undermines the relationship between the State and its citizens.\n\nAn ordinary taxpayer works hard, pays taxes and expects that money to serve the public. Inflated contracts, wasteful spending, political patronage and outright theft betray that expectation. The sense of injustice deepens when those responsible escape accountability. Taxpayers are entitled to feel cheated when their contributions enrich private interests rather than meet public needs."
  },
