    "md5": "9f96d64076d819992a8581ab294f6462",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1884,
    "chunk_text": "An ordinary taxpayer works hard, pays taxes and expects that money to serve the public. Inflated contracts, wasteful spending, political patronage and outright theft betray that expectation. The sense of injustice deepens when those responsible escape accountability. Taxpayers are entitled to feel cheated when their contributions enrich private interests rather than meet public needs.\n\nFighting corruption is therefore not separate from sound public finance. It is essential to sustaining a tax system that citizens trust and are willing to support.\n\nFor the AKD Government, elected on a strong mandate for good governance and ending corruption, the stakes are particularly high. Its anti-corruption program is more than a political pledge: it is essential to restoring public trust in the State.\n\nCitizens asked to sacrifice for economic recovery must see public officials held equally accountable. The Government must uphold one law for everyone, award contracts transparently and act against those who misuse public funds, without political interference or favouritism.\n\nOnce taxpayers lose confidence, securing cooperation for necessary reforms becomes much harder. Public trust is therefore not an optional benefit of sound fiscal policy; it is vital to its success. Mauro\u2019s lesson is simple: the State must earn the trust of those whose money it collects.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s IMF program is central to its economic recovery. Following the crisis, restoring fiscal discipline, rebuilding foreign reserves and reducing public debt became unavoidable priorities.\n\nThe Government deserves credit for maintaining discipline and exceeding programme targets. According to the IMF\u2019s 2026 review, total revenue reached 16.6% of GDP in 2025, against an estimate of 14.6%. Tax revenue was 15.4%, compared with a 13.6% target. The primary surplus reached 5.4%, well above the 2.3% target."
  },
