    "md5": "aa831d22b5a0986bafbe703be7492eb7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1444,
    "chunk_text": "The Government deserves credit for maintaining discipline and exceeding programme targets. According to the IMF\u2019s 2026 review, total revenue reached 16.6% of GDP in 2025, against an estimate of 14.6%. Tax revenue was 15.4%, compared with a 13.6% target. The primary surplus reached 5.4%, well above the 2.3% target.\n\nExceptional import receipts do not necessarily signal a lasting improvement in the tax system. Nor does a surplus achieved partly by underspending on capital projects demonstrate better financial management. Collecting more while postponing essential infrastructure and development investment cannot secure long-term prosperity. Fiscal results must therefore be assessed for their durability and the expenditure choices behind them, not celebrated solely for exceeding targets.\n\nThe IMF recommends stronger tax administration, a broader tax base, rationalised exemptions and a medium-term revenue strategy to improve efficiency and equity. These reforms provide a framework for recovery, not a substitute for the Government\u2019s judgement. Within that framework, elected leaders must make choices reflecting Sri Lanka\u2019s social and moral priorities. They remain responsible for explaining unpopular decisions and ensuring that public money serves citizens. The IMF should neither become a blanket excuse for fiscal decisions nor a scapegoat for domestic failures in administration, expenditure management or service delivery."
  },
