    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "This is the moral contract between the State and the citizen. Citizens contribute to common objectives; in return, the State provides security, essential services, equal treatment, economic opportunities and governance that protects the public interest. Neither side\u2019s responsibilities can be considered in isolation. The contract is more than an abstract ideal: it should shape budget priorities, spending decisions and everyday administration. It links the citizen\u2019s duty to pay with the Government\u2019s duty to justify the use of that money. Revenue collection and public service are thus reciprocal obligations, not separate exercises.\n\nThe next Budget gives AKD an opportunity to move beyond celebrating revenue targets and address citizens\u2019 legitimate concerns through six priorities.\n\nFirst, make tax relief fair. Assess how existing taxes affect different income groups and correct inequities, paying particular attention to lower- and middle-income households, salaried employees and small businesses.\n\nSecond, improve spending quality. Require every ministry to demonstrate the public value of its expenditure. Curb waste, unnecessary administrative costs and politically motivated projects, while protecting capital investment that supports long-term growth from short-term fiscal pressures.\n\nThird, deliver visible improvements in hospitals, schools, public transport, local infrastructure and other essential services. Set measurable service-delivery targets and publish progress regularly so citizens can judge results.\n\nFourth, strengthen anti-corruption measures. Make transparent procurement, independent audits, proper investigation of allegations and timely recovery of misappropriated assets integral to fiscal management.\n\nFifth, communicate clearly with taxpayers. Publish a simple, accessible annual account showing how much revenue is collected, where it is spent and what benefits it delivers. Openness about public finances would help rebuild confidence."
