    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "Fifth, communicate clearly with taxpayers. Publish a simple, accessible annual account showing how much revenue is collected, where it is spent and what benefits it delivers. Openness about public finances would help rebuild confidence.\n\nFiscal discipline and social justice need not conflict. A well-designed Budget should advance both, making public finances sustainable while ensuring that citizens receive fair treatment and tangible value for their contributions.\n\nThe forthcoming Budget gives AKD an opportunity to turn that principle into practice. He should seize it before frustration becomes a deeper crisis of confidence, placing responsible stewardship at the heart of fiscal policy. The State may have the power to collect our hard-earned money. But it has a moral obligation to earn the right to spend it\n\nMauro\u2019s book reminds us that successful public finance cannot be measured by revenue alone. A Government may meet fiscal targets, record a primary surplus and satisfy international creditors without winning its citizens\u2019 confidence. Economic recovery must ultimately improve people\u2019s lives. Fiscal stability is essential to that goal, but it is a means, not an end. The distinction matters when judging a Budget that asks citizens to make sacrifices.\n\nAKD can use the forthcoming Budget to renew the relationship between the State and the taxpayer through fair taxation, efficient spending, integrity and accountability. Tax relief should not be a political concession to silence the Opposition, but part of a broader effort to make Government more responsive and responsible. Citizens, too, must recognise that taxation sustains essential services, supports recovery and protects future generations from excessive debt. The goal is not a State without taxes, but one that justifies what it collects. Both sides must honour their obligations if this relationship is to endure."
