    "description": "Are tax concessions really best way to attract investment to Sri Lanka?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Are-tax-concessions-really-best-way-to-attract-investment-to-Sri-Lanka/14-799800",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794178813.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-39113184-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "88e427206019ae1e4e8abc1e21c7142b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "Are tax concessions really best way to attract investment to Sri Lanka?\n\nA 25-year exemption by the Port City Economic Commission for a modest initial investment requires clear justification, and the resulting domestic employment, investment and economic activity must be proportionate to the revenue forgone\n\nAttracting foreign investment is a priority for Sri Lanka as it seeks to strengthen growth, create jobs and expand exports. Yet the growing reliance on tax concessions deserves reconsideration. Preferential tax treatment can encourage some investments, but it carries a fiscal cost, creates disparities between businesses and does not guarantee the benefits Governments expect.\n\nTax concessions are revenue foregone that could fund public services and infrastructure or reduce borrowing. They also tilt the playing field towards selected businesses. The central question is not how much investment a concession attracts, but whether that investment would have come anyway and whether the benefits justify the revenue forgone.\n\nThe Colombo Port City illustrates these concerns. It already enjoys significant locational, infrastructural and regulatory advantages, including proximity to Colombo\u2019s financial districts, modern infrastructure, planned airport expressway connectivity and a more flexible labour framework. Additional tax concessions on top of these raise questions about necessity and justification.\n\nConcessions to port terminal operators also warrant scrutiny. Sri Lanka\u2019s location on major East-West shipping routes is its \u2018oil in the ground\u2019, and the Port of Colombo has been an established maritime hub for over a century. Granting terminal operators tax holidays of up to 25 years raises the question of whether the country is capturing the full value of its strategic location."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-39113184",
    "num": "dailyftlk-39113184",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Are tax concessions really best way to attract investment to Sri Lanka?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Are-tax-concessions-really-best-way-to-attract-investment-to-Sri-Lanka/14-799800",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794178813.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-39113184-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "333891712259a962099f355cb94801ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1568,
    "chunk_text": "Concessions to port terminal operators also warrant scrutiny. Sri Lanka\u2019s location on major East-West shipping routes is its \u2018oil in the ground\u2019, and the Port of Colombo has been an established maritime hub for over a century. Granting terminal operators tax holidays of up to 25 years raises the question of whether the country is capturing the full value of its strategic location.\n\nTax concessions should not be granted simply because an investment is large. The Government must establish what additional benefits it will generate and whether they justify the fiscal cost.\n\nGenerous incentives granted under earlier regimes can create lasting advantages for beneficiary companies. Long-standing concessions under Section 17 of the Board of Investment (BOI) Law, with tax-free periods followed by extended preferential rates, illustrate this.\n\nAccording to their financial statements, Overseas Realty (Ceylon) PLC, Dialog Axiata PLC and Mobitel Ltd., received 15-year tax exemptions under BOI agreements signed in 2005, 1997 and 1994. After these expired in 2020, 2012 and 2009, each became eligible for a 2% tax on turnover for a further 15 years. These arrangements end in 2035 for Overseas Realty and 2027 for Dialog, while Mobitel\u2019s expired in 2024.\n\nSuch investments may generate wider benefits through employment and related activity, but these must be weighed against the scale and duration of the relief. Long-term concessions should not continue by default and should be reviewed periodically to confirm that the intended benefits have materialised."
  },
  {
