    "language": "en",
    "md5": "929d7c7f7babb80b104f57dde6f48228",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "Such investments may generate wider benefits through employment and related activity, but these must be weighed against the scale and duration of the relief. Long-term concessions should not continue by default and should be reviewed periodically to confirm that the intended benefits have materialised.\n\nThe concern is sharper, where incentives support luxury developments that largely cater to wealthier buyers and rely heavily on imported materials and fittings. With Sri Lanka needing to strengthen its external position, the Government should ask whether import-intensive property is the best use of tax concessions, judging cases by their employment, foreign exchange earnings and economic linkages rather than the scale of investment.\n\nMore broadly, differential tax treatment distorts resource allocation. Capital and labour should flow to where they generate the greatest returns to society, but preferential rates raise the after-tax returns of favoured activities relative to others, even when they are not the most productive. The wider the variation in effective tax rates, the greater the inefficiency.\n\nThe Port City BPO Ltd., received a 25-year tax holiday for an initial investment of $ 500,000 with a commitment to create 2,500 jobs within five years. Employment is an important justification, but it matters whether these jobs go to the domestic workforce. Recent reports that over 14,300 foreign worker visas have been issued under the Port City framework raise questions about who benefits. This highlights the need for greater transparency and monitoring by the Colombo Port City Economic Commission (CPEC) to ensure commitments are met. A 25-year exemption for a modest initial investment requires clear justification, and the resulting domestic employment, investment and economic activity must be proportionate to the revenue forgone."
  },
