    "md5": "38ec4659a7959bffd30d71df7eff9d11",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "The Port City BPO Ltd., received a 25-year tax holiday for an initial investment of $ 500,000 with a commitment to create 2,500 jobs within five years. Employment is an important justification, but it matters whether these jobs go to the domestic workforce. Recent reports that over 14,300 foreign worker visas have been issued under the Port City framework raise questions about who benefits. This highlights the need for greater transparency and monitoring by the Colombo Port City Economic Commission (CPEC) to ensure commitments are met. A 25-year exemption for a modest initial investment requires clear justification, and the resulting domestic employment, investment and economic activity must be proportionate to the revenue forgone.\n\nSri Lanka should move towards a more targeted framework that rewards measurable outcomes. For example, tax credits could encourage net job creation, employee training and the recruitment of underrepresented groups, particularly women, aligning incentives with broader development and labour market goals.\n\nChanges in the international tax environment strengthen the case for reform. Under Pillar Two of the OECD/G20 Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) framework, large multinational groups with annual consolidated revenue of at least \u20ac750 million face a 15% global minimum effective tax rate. Where eligible companies pay lower effective rates in countries such as Sri Lanka, another jurisdiction may collect the difference as additional tax. Some concessions may therefore no longer deliver their intended benefits to the countries offering them.\n\nSri Lanka should therefore compete on fundamentals: policy stability, efficient public services, reliable infrastructure, a skilled workforce and predictable regulation, rather than preferential tax treatment."
  },
  {
