    "md5": "96991ec7dcf434dfe186e1168d7917c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka should therefore compete on fundamentals: policy stability, efficient public services, reliable infrastructure, a skilled workforce and predictable regulation, rather than preferential tax treatment.\n\nSri Lanka also needs stronger institutional oversight of how incentives are designed, approved and monitored. The IMF\u2019s 2024 Governance Diagnostic Assessment recommends that the Department of Fiscal Policy play a central role in evaluating and guiding incentive design. Institutions such as the Board of Investment and the Colombo Port City Economic Commission may be better placed to assess commercial potential, but the fiscal implications of their decisions must be considered within the Government\u2019s broader revenue priorities.\n\nInternational experience offers examples. The Philippines has progressively reformed its framework through the Tax Incentives Management and Transparency Act of 2015, the CREATE Act of 2021 and the CREATE MORE Act of 2024. These provide central oversight through the Fiscal Incentives Review Board, cost-benefit analysis in approvals, and reporting requirements under which beneficiary firms disclose incentives received and outcomes such as investment, employment and exports. This makes fiscal costs and economic results visible and enables evaluation of whether incentives deliver.\n\nUltimately, success should not be measured by the volume of capital entering the country or the number of businesses receiving concessions, but by the quality of investment and its contribution to productive employment, productivity, technology transfer and broader economic activity. Sri Lanka needs an incentive framework that is transparent, targeted, time-bound and regularly evaluated, with clear evidence that the benefits outweigh the fiscal costs.\n\nThe objective should not be to offer investors the most generous tax concessions, but to create an environment in which investing in Sri Lanka is attractive even without them."
  },
