    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fb67dc56a808b98953d79b55ca581dd2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1960,
    "chunk_text": "The Court concluded that the plaintiff had not established sufficient grounds to prevent the defendants from continuing to use the Ride For Ceylon name and mark by way of the interim injunctions sought until the final determination of the case.\n\nThe Sri Lankan Court\u2019s order also considered the earlier UK Intellectual Property Office (UK IPO) proceedings concerning the Ride For Ceylon trademark.\n\nThe Sri Lankan Commercial High Court noted that the plaintiff had knowledge of the UK IPO proceedings but had failed to disclose their status in the present action.\n\nFOMH considers the UK IPO decision an important part of the documentary history establishing the charitable origins and ownership of the Ride For Ceylon brand in the UK.\n\nRide For Ceylon has brought together cyclists, sponsors, donors, and volunteers internationally to support the development of Green Memorial Hospital, a historic medical institution founded in 1848.\n\nFOMH recognises and values the contributions made by volunteers over many years.\n\nThe charity nevertheless considers it essential that intellectual property, goodwill, and fundraising initiatives developed for charitable purposes remain protected for their intended beneficiaries.\n\nFOMH will continue to act through its legal representatives to safeguard its interests and consider any further remedies advised by its legal team, including a possible claim in reconvention.\n\nLegal costs incurred in protecting the assets, as well as travel costs to attend Court hearings, are personally met by the Trustees, without dissipating the charity\u2019s funds.\n\nThe Board of Trustees of Friends of Manipay Hospital welcomes the Commercial High Court of Colombo\u2019s decision.\n\nThe Trustees reaffirm their commitment to protecting the charitable purpose of Ride For Ceylon, maintaining public confidence in international fundraising and ensuring that the initiative continues to support healthcare services at Green Memorial Hospital."
  },
