    "chunk_text": "Goonetilleke has also received several awards. Among them Sports Journalist of the year 2000 (Editors Guild of Sri Lanka), Olympic Award for Excellence in Sports Journalism 2024 (International Olympic Committee), Presidential Award 2015 for the most outstanding sports publication, Kreeda Sahithya Vedi (2020-Sabuddhi Sports Literary Awards) and Multi Literal Sports Literate Award (2022- Sabuddhi Sports Literary Awards).\n\nAlongside his professional career, he obtained a Diploma in Journalism from the University of Colombo.\n\n1994, was a water-shed year in his career in journalism, while in Lake House he became the Deputy Editor of a new evening Sinhala language newspaper - Colombo Puwath and later Associate Editor of the newspaper. He was later appointed Editor of Sarasaviya newspaper while being managing editor of periodical publications.\n\nRajaguru left Lake House in 2008 while serving as Associate Editor of ANCL\u2019s Training and Research Department. Rajaguru also worked as a relief announcer at the Sri Lanka Broadcasting Corporation (SLBC).\n\nHis contributions to creative writing earned him the Wimalasiri Perera Memorial Award for Creative Writing. He also completed a UNESCO course on writing for Children.\n\nChandra Fernando has over 45 years of experience in the media industry. She began her career in journalism in 1977, joining the Chinthamani and Thinapathi newspapers published by the Davasa Group as an Assistant Editor.\n\nAfter working under the leadership of S. T. Sivanayagam, she joined Sudamani, a newspaper launched by the Gunasena family, where she served as an Assistant Editor.\n\nShe subsequently served for a brief period as the person in charge of the Thinaththanthi newspaper published during that era."
