    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "After working under the leadership of S. T. Sivanayagam, she joined Sudamani, a newspaper launched by the Gunasena family, where she served as an Assistant Editor.\n\nShe subsequently served for a brief period as the person in charge of the Thinaththanthi newspaper published during that era.\n\nFernando has held several key editorial positions, including Editor-in-Chief, Assistant Editor and Deputy Editor. Her extensive experience and dedicated service have made her a notable figure in the field of journalism.\n\nRohan Sarath Abeywardena entered the journalism scene in 1980 with the now defunct Sun Newspaper of the Independent Newspaper Group as a trainee journalist (sub editor). Having got a good grounding there, he soon crossed over to the newly launched Upali Newspapers Ltd. in November 1981 when the UNL launched its first daily English publication The Island, where he quickly rose from sub editor, Shipping page editor, business page editor, Deputy News Editor, News Editor, Associate Editor and Consultant Editor.\n\nIn the run up to and after the signing of the Indo-Lanka Accord in July 1987, he manned the \u2018hot seat\u2019 of the News Editor in The Island, Sunday Island, The Sunday Times (twice). Abeywardena also had some memorable attachments with the Daily Mirror as Deputy Editor News at its launch and as the Associate Editor of the now defunct The Nation between 2003 and 2010. He also served as the editor of the Weekend Standard, when Tiran Alles launched that newspaper in 2006.\n\nHe never bothered about hogging the limelight with his byline, but he ensured that journalists serving under him got as much exposure as possible. Till he retired in 2011, he was always in the thick of the news while keeping his reporters on their toes in the interest of the country whatever the personal consequences.\n\nSince retirement he has continued to serve The Island online."
