    "chunk_text": "Shared responsibility key to protecting elections from disinformation\n\nRepresentatives of the Election Commission of Sri Lanka (ECSL), the media, and civil society met in Colombo on 30 October to discuss how to protect the integrity of election information. Democracy Reporting International (DRI) organised the event, Beyond the Algorithm, under the project Strengthening Resilience Against Disinformation in Sri Lanka. The European Union co-funds the project.\n\nOpening the event, DRI Executive Director Nicola Schmidt said that disinformation is a worldwide phenomenon.\n\n\u201cGenerative Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a big issue. Deepfakes and disinformation are being spread through different channels. Social media and different platforms are contributing to the speed of the spread of disinformation. We as DRI are looking into this globally, looking at what algorithms do. That\u2019s why the title of this conference is Beyond the Algorithm. It\u2019s not just the algorithms but their contributions to disinformation is very important when promoting digital rights and trying to hold tech platforms accountable for what they are doing,\u201d she said.\n\nAddressing the gathering, ECSL Member A.M. Faaiz said that algorithms decide what appears on our screen, but algorithms do not vote.\n\n\u201cBehind every screen sits a citizen and behind every ballot lies a judgement that citizen has formed from what he or she has read, heard, and come to believe. Our concern is with the quality of the judgement and with the conditions with which it was formed. Democracy rests on a simple promise; that citizens are free to choose and that their choice is an informed one,\u201d he said.\n\nFaaiz further said that with the support of DRI and Factum, the ECSL is formulating a strategic action plan on disinformation and harmful content and mapping digital capacities to shape a roadmap for the Commission\u2019s digital presence."
  },
