    "chunk_size_bytes": 1561,
    "chunk_text": "Faaiz further said that with the support of DRI and Factum, the ECSL is formulating a strategic action plan on disinformation and harmful content and mapping digital capacities to shape a roadmap for the Commission\u2019s digital presence.\n\nOne of the key components of the event included research-driven lessons on three recently concluded elections in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe results presented by Digital Democracy Research Officer Camila Weinmann and Digital Research Analyst Prihesh Ratnayake revealed that 66 accounts impersonated political parties and candidates on TikTok. 45.5% of accounts had been active during the 2024 Presidential Election and 21.2% of them remained active till the 2025 Local Council Election. In terms of action, following reports of these accounts made to TikTok and the ECSL, 51 of these accounts were removed.\n\nThe research also revealed that women politicians faced higher levels of technology facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV) during the 2024-2025 election cycle. The findings also revealed how chatbots spread inaccurate electoral information and how political advertisements kept running through the silence period during the Parliamentary Elections campaign. The research also shed light on Generative AI and risks to electoral integrity.\n\nThe event also featured two panel discussions with panellists representing the media, ECSL, advocacy groups, and civil society. The first panel discussion, titled \u2018The Election Information Ecosystem,\u2019 discussed disinformation, accountability, and challenges to electoral integrity."
