    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "Construction and heavy machinery became one of the foundations of the Group\u2019s growth. Machinery supplied and supported by SENOK has been deployed across roads, irrigation schemes, hydropower projects, precast construction projects, harbours, airports and other major infrastructure throughout Sri Lanka. Today, SENOK represents internationally recognised brands including Kobelco, JCB, HAMM and Airman, supported by extensive sales, service, spare parts and technical capabilities.\n\nSENOK was also the pioneer of wind power development in Sri Lanka, helping introduce commercial wind generation to the country long before renewable energy became a national priority. Its energy interests have since expanded into hydro and solar power, with capabilities covering project development, engineering, construction, commissioning, operation and maintenance.\n\nAviation represents another distinctive chapter in the Group\u2019s history. In 2012, SENOK expanded into aviation with the establishment of Senok Air, operating Airbus AS350 B3 multi-role helicopters across specialised aviation services including passenger transport, aerial filming, mapping and survey work and medical evacuations. Supported by its own engineering and maintenance capabilities and an experienced flying crew, Senok Air has developed a specialised helicopter operation serving both commercial and critical-response requirements across Sri Lanka.\n\nBeyond Sri Lanka, SENOK has established a growing international presence, including in the Maldives and East and West Africa, extending its experience and capabilities in construction, machinery, commercial vehicles and other sectors into overseas markets.\n\nWhile much of SENOK\u2019s growth has taken place away from the public eye, its businesses have been closely connected with Sri Lanka\u2019s development for decades \u2013 helping build infrastructure, pioneering renewable energy, introducing international technologies, developing local manufacturing capability and creating skilled employment."
