    "chunk_text": "Oil falls as Trump says new Iran attacks \u2019unlikely\u2019\n\nReuters - Oil prices fell at Monday's open after US President Donald Trump said he was highly unlikely to order new Iran \u200battacks while talks to end the conflict make progress, despite both nations' reports of attacks on ships in or near \u200cthe Strait of Hormuz.\n\nBrent was down 84 cents, or 0.85%, to $97.48 a barrel by 0138 GMT, while West Texas Intermediate fell 32 cents, or 0.35%, to $90.51 a barrel.\n\nThe fall reversed minor gains made on Friday\u2019s settlement \u200bas the market assessed the possibility of further US strikes on Iran, although \u200bBrent fell 17% last week, the steepest weekly drop since the start \u2060of the COVID pandemic and the biggest in absolute terms, according to LSEG data \u200bdating back to 1988.\n\n\"It depends on whether or not they sign a very strong agreement,\" \u200bTrump told CNN in a phone interview. \"The answer is, highly unlikely it's going to be necessary.\"\n\nTrump had hinted at renewing attacks on Iran after last week's US midterm elections, but there has been no sign \u200bof a large-scale US military operation. Iran has said it would respond by striking \u200bUS and Israeli targets.\n\nTim Waterer, chief analyst at KCM Trade, called Trump's comments \"more measured\" but said \"the market \u200cis \u2060still waiting to see whether the midterms have produced a lasting shift in Trump\u2019s approach to the conflict, or whether this is just another temporary change in tone.\"\n\nOil is headed for a glut again as recovering Middle Eastern oil flows, rising production from other countries \u200band weak demand have \u200bpushed global supply \u2060above consumption, JPMorgan said in a report on Friday, estimating a surplus of 1.1 million barrels per day in September, widening to 1.4 \u200bmillion bpd in October.\n\nOn Sunday, Iraq reached an understanding with the US \u200bto allow \u2060Iraqi oil exports through the Strait of Hormuz via a shipping lane near Oman, Foreign Minister Fuad Hussein said in an interview with Al Jazeera."
