    "md5": "3476cc16c8b1b8dfa4c4c24282714e39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1832,
    "chunk_text": "Strong 2Q lifts Hemas 1H revenue to Rs.66.6 bn as borrowings triple on Twiga acquisition\n\nHemas Holdings PLC grew its second-quarter revenue by 17 percent year-on-year (YoY) to Rs.37.8 billion, but earnings attributable to shareholders slipped 3.0 percent to Rs.2.0 billion as higher finance costs and a heavier tax charge absorbed the rebound in operating profit, a stock filing showed.\n\nHowever, the September quarter marked a sharp recovery from the June quarter, with EBITDA nearly doubling from Rs.2.3 billion to Rs.4.5 billion, up 10.5 percent YoY, and earnings more than doubling from Rs.937 million.\n\nFor the six months ended September, revenue grew 9.5 percent to Rs.66.6 billion and EBITDA edged up 0.9 percent to Rs.6.8 billion, reversing a 14.1 percent decline at the end of the first quarter. First-half earnings still fell 9.7 percent to Rs.2.9 billion, dragged by a weaker first quarter.\n\nThe group absorbed much of the increase in raw material, energy and freight costs triggered by the Middle East conflict to protect affordability and volumes, narrowing its gross margin by 3.1 percentage points to 29.7 percent. Nearly half of its revenue comes from businesses subject to regulated pricing.\n\n\u201cExperience has shown that volume erosion costs a business far more over the long term than a temporary adjustment to margins during a period of high inflation,\u201d Group CEO Ashish Chandra noted in the interim report.\n\nNet finance costs climbed to Rs.249 million in the quarter from around Rs.56 million a year earlier, as total borrowings, including bank overdrafts, more than tripled to Rs.25.2 billion by end-September from Rs.7.5 billion at end-March. The group, which still held net cash of around Rs.3.5 billion at end-June, closed the half with net debt of around Rs.11.9 billion and net gearing of 16.6 percent."
