    "chunk_text": "Net finance costs climbed to Rs.249 million in the quarter from around Rs.56 million a year earlier, as total borrowings, including bank overdrafts, more than tripled to Rs.25.2 billion by end-September from Rs.7.5 billion at end-March. The group, which still held net cash of around Rs.3.5 billion at end-June, closed the half with net debt of around Rs.11.9 billion and net gearing of 16.6 percent.\n\nAll four sectors grew their quarterly revenue. Hospitals led with 19.3 percent growth and a 26.9 percent rise in earnings to Rs.265 million. Consumer Brands revenue rose 18.3 percent to Rs.15.6 billion, including Twiga from 17 August, but earnings fell 30.6 percent to Rs.1.1 billion.\n\nLife Sciences earnings dropped 26.8 percent to Rs.623 million despite 16.2 percent revenue growth, as the business awaited approval of regulated price revisions.\n\nMeanwhile, Hemas paid Rs.5.46 billion through Atlas Axillia for its first overseas acquisition, a 75 percent stake in Kenya\u2019s Twiga Stationers & Printers Limited, recognising provisional goodwill of Rs.1.4 billion pending the valuation of Twiga\u2019s intangible assets. Atlas also holds a put and call option over the remaining 25 percent, allowing either party to trigger its sale to Atlas.\n\nThe Board declared an interim dividend of Rs.0.25 per share, amounting to around Rs.749 million, payable on or before 4 December. The Employees\u2019 Provident Fund (EPF) raised its stake to 5.39 percent from 4.18 percent at end-June, adding 36.3 million shares during the quarter to become the fifth largest shareholder."
