    "chunk_size_bytes": 1413,
    "chunk_text": "Fraud outpacing digital payments growth in Sri Lanka, warns Novus COO\n\nFraud up 198 percent as digital transactions grow 40 percent\n\nBanks told to share fraud data and use AI against AI-driven attacks\n\nFraud linked to digital payments in Sri Lanka grew almost five times faster than the transactions themselves last year, as scam syndicates now using artificial intelligence (AI) take advantage of banks that still manage fraud in isolation, according to a senior payments technology executive.\n\nDelivering the keynote address on cyber security and fraud prevention at the Sri Lanka FinTech Festival 2026 in Colombo, Novus Technologies Chief Operating Officer Thorsten Hellwig noted that digital transactions in the country grew by 40 percent last year. That was double the 20 percent growth in payments across the Asia Pacific region, while the number of merchants was increasing by 10 percent a year.\n\nHowever, citing central bank data, he pointed out that fraud in Sri Lanka had risen by 198 percent, nearly five times the growth in digital transactions. According to the figures he presented, Malaysia recorded fraud growth of around 400 percent and Singapore 150 to 160 percent.\n\n\u201cSo the damage grows even faster than the payments,\u201d Hellwig told the audience.\n\nHe added that the Maldives, which topped the regional comparison, should be treated as an outlier since its figure was based on a small sample."
  },
