    "md5": "bdf25eda16fca4f64ce04f20e7077919",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1722,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cSo the damage grows even faster than the payments,\u201d Hellwig told the audience.\n\nHe added that the Maldives, which topped the regional comparison, should be treated as an outlier since its figure was based on a small sample.\n\nTurning to Sri Lanka, Hellwig said the country was facing the same warning signs seen in Singapore, Kuala Lumpur and Manila. Sri Lanka CERT received more than 12,650 cyber and scam complaints in 2025. Meanwhile, over 1,000 foreign nationals were arrested at scam centres between January and May this year, more than twice the 430 arrests recorded in the whole of 2024, according to the Sri Lanka CERT and Police data he presented.\n\nHe also referred to the nearly US$ 2.5 million government payment meant for a debt repayment to Australia, which was diverted through a phishing email and later found by a parliamentary committee to be a cyber fraud. In another case, a licensed bank\u2019s balance grew fourfold over a four-day holiday, and it was a spot check, not a system, that picked it up.According to Hellwig, 80 to 90 percent of frauds begin with phishing outside the institution.\u201cSri Lanka is in the same storm as other countries. It is exposed to the same sophisticated types of AI-enabled, deepfake-driven ransomware attacks,\u201d he warned. He explained that individual attackers have given way to syndicates using the same AI tools as the industry, and that more than half of all fraud is now AI-generated. A phishing message written by an AI bot is four times more likely to be clicked on than one written by a person, he added.\n\nA 2024 academic study found that fully AI-written phishing emails had a click-through rate of 54 percent, against 12 percent for generic phishing emails."
