    "chunk_size_bytes": 1825,
    "chunk_text": "He also referred to the nearly US$ 2.5 million government payment meant for a debt repayment to Australia, which was diverted through a phishing email and later found by a parliamentary committee to be a cyber fraud. In another case, a licensed bank\u2019s balance grew fourfold over a four-day holiday, and it was a spot check, not a system, that picked it up.According to Hellwig, 80 to 90 percent of frauds begin with phishing outside the institution.\u201cSri Lanka is in the same storm as other countries. It is exposed to the same sophisticated types of AI-enabled, deepfake-driven ransomware attacks,\u201d he warned. He explained that individual attackers have given way to syndicates using the same AI tools as the industry, and that more than half of all fraud is now AI-generated. A phishing message written by an AI bot is four times more likely to be clicked on than one written by a person, he added.\n\nA 2024 academic study found that fully AI-written phishing emails had a click-through rate of 54 percent, against 12 percent for generic phishing emails.\n\nHe called on banks and fintechs to work together against scams. He described the central layer Sri Lanka is looking at as the most important part of its defences, and also the most underrepresented at present.\n\nEvery transaction should carry a real-time risk score along with an explanation, Hellwig stressed. He also called for graph network technology to detect mule account networks, and for banks to share device intelligence instead of assuming that a customer\u2019s device data is in order.\n\n\u201cSo the path is no longer detection, it is interception in real time,\u201d he noted.\n\nHe identified deepfake onboarding and card testing by bots as the most common forms of attack at present, adding that global card fraud is projected to reach US$ 40 billion by 2027."
