    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "JKH awaits govt. direction on SAGT beyond 2029 as Colombo Port nears capacity\n\nHandover, fresh tender or extension still on the table with no word from govt.: Balendra\n\nJKH\u2019s newer CWIT overtakes SAGT, handling 502,351 TEUs against 425,882 in the September quarter\n\nBalendra urges govt. to fast-track North Port as Colombo can take only one more terminal\n\nJohn Keells Holdings (JKH) is yet to receive direction from the government on how South Asia Gateway Terminals (SAGT) will be handled once its build-operate-transfer concession expires in 2029, with a handover, a fresh tender or an extension all still on the table, JKH Chairperson and CEO Krishan Balendra said.\n\n\u201cWe are waiting for the Ports Authority and the ministry and the government to get back to us on how they expect to address this,\u201d Balendra noted, speaking at the group\u2019s investor webinar last week.\n\nHe added that the 30-year concession, signed in 1999, would end around August or September 2029.\n\nBalendra outlined three options. Since SAGT operates under a build-operate-transfer arrangement, the terminal could revert to the government. Alternatively, the government could put it out to tender through a request for proposals, or the concession could be extended with the existing consortium.\n\n\u201cWe have not got direction from the government on how they intend to take this forward beyond 2029,\u201d he stressed.\n\nJKH holds a 42.19 percent stake in SAGT, while APM Terminals, the terminal arm of shipping giant Maersk, holds 32.81 percent. JKH\u2019s latest annual report states that the business will evaluate opportunities to extend the concession in line with the government\u2019s policy. Asked about the potential cost of securing the terminal again, JKH Deputy Chairperson and Group Finance Director Gihan Cooray pointed out that this was not something the company could know at this stage."
  },
