    "chunk_size_bytes": 1730,
    "chunk_text": "JKH holds a 42.19 percent stake in SAGT, while APM Terminals, the terminal arm of shipping giant Maersk, holds 32.81 percent. JKH\u2019s latest annual report states that the business will evaluate opportunities to extend the concession in line with the government\u2019s policy. Asked about the potential cost of securing the terminal again, JKH Deputy Chairperson and Group Finance Director Gihan Cooray pointed out that this was not something the company could know at this stage.\n\nAt 940 metres of quay and a 15-metre draft, SAGT is the smallest and shallowest of the port\u2019s three privately operated terminals. Colombo International Container Terminals (CICT) has 1,200 metres of quay and an 18-metre depth, while CWIT has 1,400 metres and 20 metres. JKH\u2019s annual report concedes that the continued upsizing of vessels \u201cpresents challenges to SAGT due to draft constraints\u201d, although it maintains that SAGT, positioned as a strategic feeder terminal, remains well placed to support rising transhipment volumes between India, the wider South Asian region and mainline routes. SAGT\u2019s share of the port\u2019s volumes fell to 21.6 percent in 2025/26 from 26.8 percent a year earlier, as its throughput declined 11 percent to 1.84 million TEUs while the port\u2019s total grew 10 percent to 8.52 million TEUs.\n\nPart of that decline stemmed from crane rail replacement works, which closed one of SAGT\u2019s berths during the second half of 2025/26. Cooray attributed the latest drop to vessel movements disrupted by the Middle East conflict, adding that momentum had recovered in October and \u201cthat should be the trend going forward.\u201d He also clarified that the disruption had a net positive impact on the Port of Colombo as a whole."
