    "chunk_text": "Part of that decline stemmed from crane rail replacement works, which closed one of SAGT\u2019s berths during the second half of 2025/26. Cooray attributed the latest drop to vessel movements disrupted by the Middle East conflict, adding that momentum had recovered in October and \u201cthat should be the trend going forward.\u201d He also clarified that the disruption had a net positive impact on the Port of Colombo as a whole.\n\nAdani Ports and Special Economic Zone (APSEZ), which leads the CWIT consortium, reported that revenue from its international ports business rose 71 percent to INR 18.39 billion in the September quarter, while EBITDA more than tripled to INR 8.78 billion, taking the margin to 47.7 percent. APSEZ attributed the performance to the continued ramp-up of CWIT and its Tanzania operations, along with a contribution from its Australian terminal. With phase 2 now doubling CWIT\u2019s capacity to 3.2 million TEUs, APSEZ expects the terminal to handle nearly 25 percent of the Port of Colombo\u2019s targeted capacity of 13 million TEUs by 2028.\n\nListed contractor Access Engineering PLC, which was awarded the construction of CWIT\u2019s terminal platform by APSEZ, has undertaken the ground and paving works, gantry crane rail foundations and support buildings at the 86-acre reclaimed site.\n\n\u201cThe Middle East crisis has also created some congestion in the Port of Colombo because there are some transhipment boxes that have got dropped off that have not got cleared and given that we don\u2019t have a lot of yard space, that\u2019s been one of the challenges,\u201d Cooray explained, adding that volume growth could otherwise have been higher.\n\n\u201cIf not for the CWIT capacity inducted this year, the Port of Colombo would have witnessed a negative growth,\u201d Sri Lanka Ports Authority (SLPA) Chairman Dr. Parakrama Dissanayake remarked at the launch of CWIT\u2019s second phase in late September."
