    "md5": "0746876d34848f54028dbd6fe3478638",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Crisis Monitoring Is Not a Back Office Function\n\nSri Lankan organisations still call their communications agencies in the middle of a crisis they could have seen coming. The tools have never been better. The gap is in how the monitoring work is valued, staffed and connected to the decisions it is meant to inform.\n\nTrust is thinner, and the information environment more crowded, than most organisations assume. Four recent sources make the case.\n\nThe Edelman Trust Barometer 2026 Asia Pacific report finds that 67% of respondents in the region hold an insular trust mindset, and only 33% are open to trusting someone who is different from them. Across 28 markets, social media is the least trusted sector at 50%, and 65% worry that other countries deliberately contaminate their media with falsehoods, up 11 points since 2021. In Malaysia and Indonesia, the figure is 73% and 72%.\n\nThe World Economic Forum\u2019s Global Risks Report 2026 ranks misinformation and disinformation second over the two year horizon, its highest position since 2023, and fourth over ten years. It warns that the problem \u201ccorrodes public discourse, weakens crisis responses and is propagated by technological advancements, such as in AI.\u201d\n\nAt home, DataReportal\u2019s Digital 2026 Sri Lanka counts 13.9 million internet users, 59.7% of the population, and 9.0 million social media user identities, 38.7% of the population. These are advertising reach estimates, not active users, but they show how far a conversation can travel before anyone at the top has read it.\n\nFinally, the State of Social Intelligence 2026, a survey of 330 social intelligence professionals, finds that 80% are often or always involved in interpreting meaning from social and internet data. Yet 64% name tool limitations or integration problems as the main thing holding back their impact, and only 12% say strategic planning is their most frequent use of the work.\u00a0 The first signal is usually small"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailymirrorlk-5cbf8840",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5cbf8840",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Crisis Monitoring Is Not a Back Office Function",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Crisis-Monitoring-Is-Not-a-Back-Office-Function/172-354488",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794208184.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailymirrorlk-5cbf8840-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1bc7a4f9862a74d825d7d2b85c83770d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Finally, the State of Social Intelligence 2026, a survey of 330 social intelligence professionals, finds that 80% are often or always involved in interpreting meaning from social and internet data. Yet 64% name tool limitations or integration problems as the main thing holding back their impact, and only 12% say strategic planning is their most frequent use of the work.\u00a0 The first signal is usually small\n\nEarly warning does not come only from screens, and print still matters. A critical piece in a feature section weighs differently from the same words on the front page.\n\nA story with modest volume, taken up by a credible tier one publication, can carry more risk than a viral post with no institutional weight. Reach, credibility and engagement have to be read together.\n\nExperienced practitioners can often feel the change in the room before the numbers confirm it. Instinct is not a substitute for data. It tells you which data to examine first.\n\nNot every signal warrants a response. Some low volume items are better tracked quietly, because a statement would turn a quiet item into a public one.\u00a0 The hours that matter\n\nCrises often break in the morning or late at night, and this is not a job with a clear off switch. A crisis desk that works only office hours is working on yesterday\u2019s conversation.\n\nWhen a client needs an answer within the hour, the person reading the data has to stay calm. That is a discipline, not a personality trait. Panic in the monitoring room reaches the client quickly.\n\nThe honest answer is usually the gist: where things stand, what is still being checked, and when a fuller picture will follow. Reassurance and time are legitimate tools. Making a call before the data is ready is not.\n\nInformation should reach the strategy team and the account lead before the client. Sending a raw item before anyone has assessed it is an error many practitioners learn from early."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
