    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b5ec3a8ac6dcc97480b0a03bdbd3053a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "The honest answer is usually the gist: where things stand, what is still being checked, and when a fuller picture will follow. Reassurance and time are legitimate tools. Making a call before the data is ready is not.\n\nInformation should reach the strategy team and the account lead before the client. Sending a raw item before anyone has assessed it is an error many practitioners learn from early.\n\nA good result often looks like a falling negative line, and not every situation needs a statement or a spokesperson.\n\nThe report is also a meeting. The monitoring lead has to sit with the strategy team and tell the story, not just display the data.\u00a0 Where the work breaks down\n\nThree problems recur, and none of them is really about software.\n\nThe first is information. Monitoring teams often learn about a client\u2019s activity only when it appears in print, with no word on whether it could be contentious.\n\nThe second is interpretation. A dashboard can show a spike in mentions, but only someone who knows the outlet, its audience and history can say whether it matters.\n\nThe third is people. Monitoring desks absorb theworst of public conversation every day, including abuse, grief and threats, largely out of sight.\u00a0 The cost on the people doing the work\n\nThe first rule is not to absorb everything. Short breaks, time away from screens and conversations that are not about work all help. When colleagues say it is too much, the workload is the problem, not the person.\n\nMany clients call for monitoring only in a crisis, but good times need tracking,\u00a0 too. A strong position makes a brand the first target when a regulation or labelling change arrives, so a market leader should worry more, not less.\n\nProactive monitoring is far cheaper than a crisis that arrives unseen, and a playbook written in advance saves time when it matters most.\u00a0 What machines cannot read"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailymirrorlk-5cbf8840",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5cbf8840",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Crisis Monitoring Is Not a Back Office Function",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Crisis-Monitoring-Is-Not-a-Back-Office-Function/172-354488",
