    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "49721f9f7d8b66fff1800efad5070387",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "Many clients call for monitoring only in a crisis, but good times need tracking,\u00a0 too. A strong position makes a brand the first target when a regulation or labelling change arrives, so a market leader should worry more, not less.\n\nProactive monitoring is far cheaper than a crisis that arrives unseen, and a playbook written in advance saves time when it matters most.\u00a0 What machines cannot read\n\nThe analyst draws the picture for the client, with judgement built over years of reading. That remains the step that matters most.\u00a0 What agencies and clients should do differently\n\nFour changes would make a measurable difference, and none requires new software. One, Brief monitoring before the announcement, not after it, with the context, the likely reaction and the people who could be affected.\n\nTwo, write the crisis playbook before the crisis, naming who decides, who speaks, who monitors and what triggers escalation Three,\u00a0 Protect the first hour. Let the monitoring lead give an incomplete picture honestly. Four, Staff for the work and recognise it, in performance reviews as well as the fee line.\n\nClients have a part to play too. Agencies that do this work well should be brought in early, not called after the damage is done.\u00a0 Advice for the next practitioner\n\nRead the data before you respond, especially when you are new, and understand the platforms and publications, not just the headlines. Ask your seniors, or whoever handles the client, when you are unsure. Early mistakes are common and recoverable.\n\nWhen something goes wrong, take a breather. The world is not falling apart.\n\n(Anjalie Munasinghe is Manager, PR and Social Intelligence at Mark and Comm, a boutique strategic communications agency in Colombo. She is Secretary of CIPR International, the international chapter of the Chartered Institute of Public Relations, and a Member of CIPR)."
  },
