    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailymirrorlk-5025d21a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a6315fc1d8c7c6a0ae9171ba03d53431",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1633,
    "chunk_text": "What America\u2019s Midterms Can Teach Us\n\nVoters react after Democrat Jina Hinojosa won the election for Texas governor\n\nOn Tuesday, American voters did something Sri Lankans can only watch from far away: they put a president to the test in the middle of his term. Democrats won both the House and the Senate. They took seats from Ohio to Texas to Kansas, which has not sent a Democrat to the Senate in almost a hundred years.\u00a0 Voters wanted limits on a president who had told them a month earlier, \u201cI can do whatever I want, if I want.\u201d\n\nThe point is not Donald Trump. It is how the system works. A midterm gives voters a fixed date to say \u201cwell done\u201d or \u201cnot good enough\u201d without waiting for the whole term to end. Congress is chosen on a different schedule from the White House, so people get a second chance to speak. Whatever you think of the result, the system did its job: Power was tested halfway through, in the open, at the ballot box.\n\nSri Lanka has no such moment, and its recent history shows why one is needed. In September 2024, Anura Kumara Dissanayake won the presidency with about 42 per cent of the votes in the first count. That was the biggest share of any candidate, but it was short of a majority, and he was declared the winner only after second-preference votes were counted. Two months later, his National People\u2019s Power won 159 of the 225 seats in Parliament, the first time a single party had won two-thirds of the seats under the PR.\u00a0 The government is now two years old, with three to go, and voters who have changed their minds have no set date to say so before the elections due in 2029."
  },
  {
