    "md5": "afc2088a5e5c684a9faca869e5a1ff63",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1774,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has no such moment, and its recent history shows why one is needed. In September 2024, Anura Kumara Dissanayake won the presidency with about 42 per cent of the votes in the first count. That was the biggest share of any candidate, but it was short of a majority, and he was declared the winner only after second-preference votes were counted. Two months later, his National People\u2019s Power won 159 of the 225 seats in Parliament, the first time a single party had won two-thirds of the seats under the PR.\u00a0 The government is now two years old, with three to go, and voters who have changed their minds have no set date to say so before the elections due in 2029.\n\nLocal council elections in May 2025 were a partial test, but local councils are small, and a country that votes in a big way only at the top gets one real verdict every five years. There is a better tool, and it has been left unused.\n\nThe nine provincial councils are long overdue for an election. They have had no elected members since 2018. The reasons have changed over the years, from a fight over how to redraw boundaries to gaps in the law, but the result is the same: a level of government set up under the Thirteenth Amendment sits idle. The government said it aimed to hold the polls in the first half of 2026.\n\nThat time has passed. In August the Cabinet spokesman said the government wants them held as soon as possible, once the legal steps are done. Statements have been mixed: in June a JVP leader was reported as saying there was no money for the polls, while the government has since said the money is set aside and only the legal framework is missing. The Centre for Policy Alternatives has warned that the delay hurts citizens\u2019 democratic rights and risks a party-state system."
  },
