    "md5": "2ff6b29a9f089a1be6e8d17f9ab023e1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1603,
    "chunk_text": "That time has passed. In August the Cabinet spokesman said the government wants them held as soon as possible, once the legal steps are done. Statements have been mixed: in June a JVP leader was reported as saying there was no money for the polls, while the government has since said the money is set aside and only the legal framework is missing. The Centre for Policy Alternatives has warned that the delay hurts citizens\u2019 democratic rights and risks a party-state system.\n\nNothing in this plan favours one party. A rule in the constitution would bind whoever governs in 2032, and after. That is the difference between a favour and a rule: between a government that allows an election when it feels safe, and one that faces voters whether it feels safe or not. The government has said it wants to hold provincial elections before bringing in constitutional changes. That is an argument for this very order. An early provincial vote would let voters judge the government\u2019s record, and its reform plans, before those plans become law.\n\nWhy provincial councils and not an early vote for Parliament? Because they give a verdict without a crisis. A provincial election cannot remove a president or bring down a cabinet. It cannot undo the calm a country still recovering from the 2022 collapse is trying to keep. But it covers the whole island, province by province, and it would let people in the north and east, who have had no provincial representatives for years, have a say in how they are governed. One SLMC Member of Parliament has said that having no councils weakens democratic representation."
  },
