    "language": "en",
    "md5": "57f0ed5fe407dfda90bc3d4080b40e10",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "Why provincial councils and not an early vote for Parliament? Because they give a verdict without a crisis. A provincial election cannot remove a president or bring down a cabinet. It cannot undo the calm a country still recovering from the 2022 collapse is trying to keep. But it covers the whole island, province by province, and it would let people in the north and east, who have had no provincial representatives for years, have a say in how they are governed. One SLMC Member of Parliament has said that having no councils weakens democratic representation.\n\nThere are fair objections. Some politicians remain cool to the provincial system, which grew out of the 1987 Indo-Lanka Accord and which some see as an unwelcome legacy. Councils with limited powers may be a poor measure of national feeling: Local issues and local personalities can drown out the message voters mean to send, and a government that does badly could simply say the vote was about something else. Running nine elections at once costs money, and the country is still repairing its public finances. The fight over boundaries that has blocked the polls is not easy to settle. Others will say that a real check on a two-thirds government must be built into the rules: A requirement that major constitutional changes go to a referendum, or a fixed mid-term vote for Parliament itself.\n\nStill, the choice is not between a midterm and a perfect check. It is between a midterm and silence. Provincial voters have waited long enough. Let the first midterm come early next year, and let the new constitution make it permanent."
