    "chunk_text": "I found Sarnie by chance on day five of my Dog Jog (October 13) as I had to change my route at the last minute due to reports of elephant attacks in the area. She didn\u2019t move as my support tuk drove past her, which was a huge red flag, and that\u2019s when I realised she was paralysed and couldn\u2019t use her back legs. She was super skinny and I knew if we left her there, she would end up starving to death by the side of the road. So we asked around if anyone knew her (they didn\u2019t) and took her with us. We were really far from the hospital at this stage. So we snuck Sarnie into our hotels for a few nights before managing to get her down to the WECare hospital for treatment. The X-rays showed that she had an old spinal fracture and an old femoral fracture as well as issues with her bladder.\n\nQ Your mission is to treat the many street dogs in Sri Lanka. But challenges are aplenty. What is the ultimate goal you plan to achieve through the Dog Jog?\n\nThe goal was to raise money to start building a new WECare street dog hospital, and raise awareness about why it\u2019s so desperately needed. We need \u00a31.5 million (LKR 671,270,850) to build our new street dog hospital, rehabilitation area, and veterinary training centre, which is a huge amount of money. So I knew I needed to do something crazy that would hopefully catch people\u2019s attention. There are so many amazing animal lovers in the world and I truly believe that we can achieve this dream together.\n\nI still can\u2019t quite believe that I managed to complete this run. While I\u2019m so proud of myself and incredibly thankful for all the amazing people who supported me, we still have a long way to go to reach our \u00a31.5 million (LKR 671,270,850) fundraising goal. We\u2019ve raised \u00a3260,000 (LKR 116,353,614) so far, which is incredible. But we need a lot more. So please continue to share, donate and support to give these street animals the hospital they not only need, but deserve."
