    "md5": "87f0f40d627bc6fce6adb959b056387f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "Our current hospital is in Gandara in the south of Sri Lanka and is the only fully-equipped vet hospital on the island that\u2019s dedicated to street dogs (and cats!). We have 27 permanent kennels for inpatients (plus many pop-up kennels), X-ray, ultrasound machines, an in-house laboratory, isolation facilities (for potentially infectious animals), and a surgical suite.\n\nThe new hospital will have a rehabilitation area for recovering animals. This will free up kennels in the main hospital, allowing us to treat an extra 3,000 patients per year, which will have an incredible impact on the street animal community. It will also have a veterinary training centre that will offer free courses for Sri Lankan vets, as well as Sri Lanka\u2019s first-ever veterinary nursing qualification. I\u2019m really excited about this as it\u2019s our way of extending our impact around the island, and ensuring community animals receive a fantastic level of care wherever they are in the country.\n\nWe also have an incredible CNVR (Catch-Neuter-Vaccinate-Release) outreach programme, with two neutering and vaccination lorries out in the community five days a week in Matara District. To date, we\u2019ve neutered 27,114 dogs and vaccinated 41,000 against rabies. CNVR is the only sustainable way of managing the street dog population and keeping the current dogs happy and healthy.\n\nI understand why people might be wary of street dogs and cats because rabies is\u00a0 a very real risk here. But they\u2019re just innocent souls trying to get through life the same way we are, and they deserve love, understanding, and compassion. One of WECare\u2019s areas of focus is rabies vaccination as vaccinating dogs is the only humane and sustainable way of eliminating the virus on the island.\u00a0 We\u2019ve vaccinated 41,000 animals against rabies since 2014. We also educate local communities about rabies prevention, to help people live more harmoniously with street animals."
  },
