    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e2e9402f20b0927b40db9b8bed9572de",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1903,
    "chunk_text": "When nearly one-quarter of a country\u2019s merchandise exports depend on a single foreign market, political uncertainty in that market becomes a national economic vulnerability.\n\nSri Lanka must therefore look beyond the electoral headlines and confront the economic realities.\n\nGLOBAL MARKETS SHAKEN \u2014 A WARNING AND AN OPPORTUNITY FOR SRI LANKA\n\nThe United States remains Sri Lanka\u2019s largest single-country export destination.\n\nIn 2025, Sri Lankan exports to America reached approximately US$3 billion, representing nearly 24% of merchandise exports.\n\nApparel dominates this trade, alongside rubber products, tea, coconut-based products and manufactured goods.\n\nThe economic arithmetic is alarming. A hypothetical 5% reduction in exports to America would translate into approximately US$150 million in lost annual export revenue. A 10% decline would represent US$300 million, assuming the 2025 export base. These are scenarios, not predictions. Nevertheless, they expose the magnitude of Sri Lanka\u2019s vulnerability. Behind these figures stand factory workers, suppliers, transport operators, financial institutions and families dependent on export-generated employment.\n\nA trade shock in Washington can become an employment crisis in Sri Lanka.\n\nThis is why protecting access to the American market must be treated as an economic priority, not simply a diplomatic responsibility.\n\nThe Democratic takeover of Congress could increase scrutiny of President Trump\u2019s tariff policies and create opportunities for renewed trade-policy debate. However, expecting an immediate removal of tariffs would be dangerously premature. The President retains substantial executive authority, while protectionist sentiment extends across both major American political parties. Sri Lanka\u2019s greatest concern should therefore be its competitiveness relative to Bangladesh, Vietnam, India and other exporting economies."
