    "chunk_text": "The Democratic takeover of Congress could increase scrutiny of President Trump\u2019s tariff policies and create opportunities for renewed trade-policy debate. However, expecting an immediate removal of tariffs would be dangerously premature. The President retains substantial executive authority, while protectionist sentiment extends across both major American political parties. Sri Lanka\u2019s greatest concern should therefore be its competitiveness relative to Bangladesh, Vietnam, India and other exporting economies.\n\nThe real danger is not merely losing preferential treatment. It is watching competitors secure commercial advantages while Sri Lanka remains unprepared. Colombo must pursue predictable trading arrangements and defend its exporters through sustained economic diplomacy.\n\nTariffs are only part of the challenge. American consumers, facing pressure from housing, healthcare, food and energy costs, ultimately determine demand for many Sri Lankan exports. When household purchasing power weakens, retailers reduce inventories, postpone orders and demand lower prices. Sri Lankan apparel manufacturers then confront shrinking margins, reduced production and intensified competition. A change in congressional control does not automatically restore consumer confidence or purchasing power. For Sri Lanka\u2019s garment industry, the spending decisions of American households may matter as much as the policy decisions of American politicians. Exporters must therefore strengthen productivity, develop higher-value products and reduce excessive dependence on individual buyers and markets.\n\nAmerican political developments also influence global financial expectations. Although the Federal Reserve operates independently, changes in US economic conditions can affect interest rates, capital movements and currency markets. For Sri Lanka, a stronger dollar can increase the rupee cost of imported fuel, essential commodities and dollar-denominated debt obligations."
