    "chunk_size_bytes": 1908,
    "chunk_text": "American political developments also influence global financial expectations. Although the Federal Reserve operates independently, changes in US economic conditions can affect interest rates, capital movements and currency markets. For Sri Lanka, a stronger dollar can increase the rupee cost of imported fuel, essential commodities and dollar-denominated debt obligations.\n\nThe country has already paid an enormous price for inadequate financial buffers. Repeating that mistake would be economically indefensible.\n\nThe conflict involving Iran has made energy security an even greater concern. Any sustained reduction in global petroleum prices could ease Sri Lanka\u2019s import bill, reduce transportation costs, moderate inflation and improve industrial competitiveness. Yet geopolitical expectations are not guarantees. Renewed military escalation or disruption to oil supplies could rapidly reverse those gains. Sri Lanka must use periods of favorable energy prices to rebuild reserves, improve fuel procurement and accelerate renewable-energy development rather than introduce financially unsustainable subsidies.\n\nEnergy security is economic security. A country permanently exposed to imported-fuel shocks cannot fully safeguard its economic independence.\n\nChanges in American trade policy could encourage multinational companies to reconsider manufacturing locations and international supply chains. Sri Lanka possesses genuine advantages skilled workers, established apparel expertise, maritime connectivity and longstanding commercial relationships. But investors also examine taxation, energy costs, regulatory stability, infrastructure and the speed of government approvals. Vietnam, India and Bangladesh continue competing aggressively for manufacturing investment. Sri Lanka cannot expect international companies to overlook structural weaknesses simply because the country needs foreign exchange."
