    "chunk_text": "Changes in American trade policy could encourage multinational companies to reconsider manufacturing locations and international supply chains. Sri Lanka possesses genuine advantages skilled workers, established apparel expertise, maritime connectivity and longstanding commercial relationships. But investors also examine taxation, energy costs, regulatory stability, infrastructure and the speed of government approvals. Vietnam, India and Bangladesh continue competing aggressively for manufacturing investment. Sri Lanka cannot expect international companies to overlook structural weaknesses simply because the country needs foreign exchange.\n\nThe government must urgently improve the investment environment and attract industries capable of generating sustainable export earnings.\n\nTHE IMF IS A STABILISATION PROGRAMME, NOT AN ECONOMIC MIRACLE\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s IMF-supported reforms have helped restore macroeconomic discipline and international confidence. But stabilization and economic transformation are not the same.Tax increases, expenditure restraint and debt restructuring cannot independently create the industrial expansion, export diversification and productive employment required for lasting prosperity. An economy cannot tax its way to prosperity, borrow its way to competitiveness or depend indefinitely on international financial assistance. Sri Lanka needs investment-led growth, technology adoption, higher productivity and stronger integration into regional and global supply chains. The American election reinforces the urgency of that transformation.\n\nSri Lanka needs an immediate, coordinated economic response built around five national priorities.\n\nFirst, launch a high-level trade-diplomacy offensive in Washington. Engage the White House, congressional leadership, trade committees, American retailers and industry associations. Protecting market access must become a measurable diplomatic objective."
