    "chunk_text": "First, launch a high-level trade-diplomacy offensive in Washington. Engage the White House, congressional leadership, trade committees, American retailers and industry associations. Protecting market access must become a measurable diplomatic objective.\n\nThird, diversify export markets aggressively. Accelerate commercial integration with India, including progress on the long-delayed Economic and Technology Cooperation Agreement. Expand practical trade initiatives across Africa, ASEAN and the Middle East.\n\nFourth, prioritise export-oriented foreign investment. Manufacturing, technology, logistics, renewable energy and value-added industries must receive focused investment promotion supported by transparent, time-bound approvals.\n\nFifth, strengthen financial resilience. Build external reserves, maintain debt discipline and prepare contingency measures against oil-price shocks, exchange-rate volatility and deteriorating international financial conditions.\n\nThese priorities require coordinated leadership from the Ministry of Finance, Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Central Bank, Export Development Board, Board of Investment and private sector.\n\nEconomic diplomacy must be measured by investment secured, markets opened, exports increased and jobs created \u2014 not by meetings held, photographs taken or declarations issued.\n\nThe Democratic victory has changed the political balance in Washington. Whether that produces tariff relief, greater economic predictability or prolonged political confrontation remains uncertain. But one reality is beyond dispute.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic vulnerabilities did not begin with this election, and they will not disappear because of it. Our dependence on limited export markets, inadequate diversification, energy insecurity and inconsistent investment policies are longstanding weaknesses demanding decisive action. The country must strengthen its American trade relationship while building the capacity to withstand shocks from any single market."
