    "md5": "a974072e4a485d4b30e7ac3943ed9c02",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1561,
    "chunk_text": "THE $6.3 BILLION REPUTATION TEST\n\nImagine discovering that hackers have stolen $6.3 billion from your company. Within minutes, your security teams are scrambling to stop the theft, customers are worried about their savings, and you have to explain what has happened before you have all the answers. That was the extraordinary situation facing Binance, the world's largest cryptocurrency exchange, last week. Hackers moved approximately $6.3 billion in digital assets from Binance's wallets during a sophisticated attack, triggering one of the largest security crises the cryptocurrency industry has faced. This week on our BBC show, When It Hits the Fan, my co-host David Yelland and I discussed Binance's response, revelations about Meta's efforts to challenge a prominent critic, and Norway's rather amusing answer to an insult from Donald Trump.\n\nWhen billions of dollars disappear from a financial platform, the immediate concern for customers is whether their money is safe. For Binance, that question became particularly urgent as news of the hack spread across social media. The company's leadership moved quickly. Co-chief executive Richard Teng used X to reassure customers that Binance would cover the losses from its own resources. He followed up with a video, written statements and more detailed explanations of the steps being taken to secure the platform. The video itself was hardly a polished corporate production. Teng appeared in a T-shirt, looking as though he had been awake all night, and some viewers mocked him for reading from a script."
  },
