    "md5": "4a1eeb1e9c9510c6dcaaf8622947a8f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "When billions of dollars disappear from a financial platform, the immediate concern for customers is whether their money is safe. For Binance, that question became particularly urgent as news of the hack spread across social media. The company's leadership moved quickly. Co-chief executive Richard Teng used X to reassure customers that Binance would cover the losses from its own resources. He followed up with a video, written statements and more detailed explanations of the steps being taken to secure the platform. The video itself was hardly a polished corporate production. Teng appeared in a T-shirt, looking as though he had been awake all night, and some viewers mocked him for reading from a script.\n\nThere is also something distinctive about communicating with the cryptocurrency community. Much of the conversation happens on X, where users expect frequent updates and technical detail. Binance's leadership responded in that environment, sharing information in formats that people could read, watch or examine more closely. A traditional financial institution might have approached the situation quite differently. But a carefully prepared statement issued several hours later would have offered little comfort to customers watching the story unfold in real time.\n\nNone of this diminishes the seriousness of the security failure. Binance temporarily suspended withdrawals, and customers subsequently moved billions of dollars off the platform. Its leadership still faces difficult questions about how such a substantial theft was possible. Binance's leaders gave customers clear assurances and backed them with a substantial financial commitment. But the security failure remains, and the company still has questions to answer. Its longer-term reputation will depend as much on those answers as on how quickly it responded."
  },
