    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "None of this diminishes the seriousness of the security failure. Binance temporarily suspended withdrawals, and customers subsequently moved billions of dollars off the platform. Its leadership still faces difficult questions about how such a substantial theft was possible. Binance's leaders gave customers clear assurances and backed them with a substantial financial commitment. But the security failure remains, and the company still has questions to answer. Its longer-term reputation will depend as much on those answers as on how quickly it responded.\n\nI wasn't particularly surprised to read about those discussions. In political communications, researching opponents and challenging their arguments have long been part of campaigning. Over the years, I've watched some of those tactics find their way into corporate communications, particularly when businesses face threats to their commercial interests. Meta is entitled to challenge Haidt's conclusions, which have attracted legitimate disagreement from other researchers. But questioning the evidence is very different from trying to discredit the person presenting it. Haidt has considerable influence of his own, although the resources available to an individual academic are hardly comparable to those of one of the world's largest technology companies. Communications advisers sometimes find themselves in uncomfortable conversations with senior executives who want to respond aggressively to criticism. Part of our responsibility is to question whether the proposed response is proportionate and whether it might ultimately attract more scrutiny than the original criticism. In Meta's case, the discussions themselves have now become a news story. We do not know whether the proposed approach was pursued, but the documents have invited fresh questions about how the company deals with those who challenge it."
