    "md5": "fda3d68349b5ddb52272761db5cd11ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Scientists predict 2026 hottest year on record as climate talks begin\n\nBBC - The world is on course for its hottest year on record as the planet is hit with warming from climate change and El Ni\u00f1o, scientists have told BBC News.\n\nThe warning comes as world leaders and negotiators gather in Turkey on Monday for the opening of COP31, the latest UN climate summit.\n\nBerkeley Earth, the independent US research group behind the forecast, said that even with two months of the year left it is 95% confident that 2026 will surpass the previous record set in 2024.\n\nThe impact of rising heat has been felt acutely in recent months by countries attending COP, with extensive wildfires across Europe and the sudden glacier collapse in Nepal which killed more than 1,000 people.\n\n\"We have had a run of record warm years,\" said Dr Robert Rohde, of Berkeley Earth. \"That is what we expect as global warming progresses\u2026 glaciers collapsing, record-breaking heatwaves and droughts.\"\n\nOn Friday, the EU's Copernicus climate service confirmed that October was the hottest on record.\n\nBut Rohde warned this was just the start. \"There is potential for much larger and much more dramatic changes down the line,\" he said.\n\nThe main driver of rising global temperatures has been the increasing concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, largely caused by burning fossil fuels.\n\nThe UN's World Meteorological Organization (WMO) published its own report on Monday, and although more cautious than Berkeley, it has forecast that 2026 will be among the four warmest years on record.\n\nIt said that 2027 will provide no let-up, as an unusually powerful El Ni\u00f1o will continue into next year, pushing temperatures higher.\n\nWMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo said it \"gives new meaning to the phrase off the charts\".\n\nThe findings underline the challenge facing negotiators gathering in the seaside Turkish city of Antalya for COP31."
  },
