    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo said it \"gives new meaning to the phrase off the charts\".\n\nThe findings underline the challenge facing negotiators gathering in the seaside Turkish city of Antalya for COP31.\n\n\"No single COP is a silver bullet. No single conference can bridge the chasm between the science and reality,\" Simon Stiell, the UN's climate chief, is expected to tell governments on the opening morning.\n\nBut he will add \"the urgency of this moment must drive us forward. Global heating is already costing the Earth.\"\n\nLast month the UK defence secretary warned the public and businesses to think about how they would cope with increasing natural disasters, including stockpiling food.\n\nWes Streeting pointed to wildfires this summer and record heat, which the Met Office has said was made 130 times more likely by climate change, as well as increased risk of flooding this winter as a result of El Ni\u00f1o.\n\nStephen Belcher, chief scientist at the Met Office, told BBC's Panorama: \"We're designed for a climate of the 20th century that we don't live in any longer, so it's going to change all aspects of our lives.\"\n\nThe additional greenhouse gases being added to the atmosphere have trapped additional heat and about 90% of that has been absorbed by the oceans.\n\nThat's where El Ni\u00f1o comes in, said Saulo. \"It doesn't add new heat to the planet. It releases heat from the ocean, which is the warmest ever measured. There is simply more heat available to release.\"\n\nThis natural climate pattern is pushing global temperatures even higher.\n\nThe world is currently on track for 2.8C of warming above pre-industrial levels by the end of the century - all but guaranteed to exceed the goal of 1.5C set under the Paris Agreement to avoid the worst effects of climate change.\n\nCountries will negotiate for two weeks with leaders, including Prime Minister Andy Burnham, attending the conference this week."
