    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "The world is currently on track for 2.8C of warming above pre-industrial levels by the end of the century - all but guaranteed to exceed the goal of 1.5C set under the Paris Agreement to avoid the worst effects of climate change.\n\nCountries will negotiate for two weeks with leaders, including Prime Minister Andy Burnham, attending the conference this week.\n\nAlthough emissions continue to increase, countries have also made significant progress in increasing renewable energy capacity. The International Energy Agency has predicted growth in the next six years will be 57% greater than the previous period.\n\nStiell is also expected to say that expanding renewable energy was both a climate and economic solution for recent energy shocks. \"Global renewables saved around half a trillion dollars last year.\"\n\nThe Turkish government says it wants discussions this year to focus on a new goal for electrification - moving energy systems away from fossil fuels towards electricity - and cutting waste levels.\n\nEarly drafts of the EU position seen by the BBC make clear they would only sign up to a goal on electrification if it did not allow countries to meet the target using gas.\n\n\"It is important that progress on electrification is underpinned by clean energy sources,\" it said in a memo to members.\n\nBut nations, such as Saudi Arabia and Russia, have previously pushed the interim use of gas as a \"cleaner\" fossil fuel alternative to coal.\n\nCeleste Saulo of the WMO said she could not offer an explanation as to why countries have previously not reached agreement on certain issues, but said \"what I see are the impacts of what this lack of consensus generates with more extreme events\".\n\nThe BBC understands that the UK government is reluctant to set any new goals at COP31 and thinks the focus should be on achieving existing agreements."
