    "md5": "111556eff47d8c356e05b867d6c24e37",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Dengue cases surpass 107,000 as Western Province accounts for more than half of infections\n\nColombo, Nov. 9 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has reported more than 107,000 dengue cases this year, with the Western Province alone accounting for over half of all reported infections, raising fresh questions about the effectiveness of dengue prevention and control measures.\n\nAccording to the latest National Dengue Surveillance System figures, a total of 107,651 dengue cases had been reported by midnight on November 7, while 81 deaths had been recorded during the year.\n\nThe Western Province has emerged as the country's biggest dengue hotspot, accounting for 55,734 cases, or 51.77 per cent of the national total.\n\nGampaha District recorded the highest number of infections with 22,465 cases, followed closely by Colombo with 20,771 cases. The Colombo Municipal Council area alone reported 4,413 cases.\n\nThe figures raise concerns over why the country's most densely populated and administratively developed province continues to carry such a heavy dengue burden.\n\nThe Southern Province recorded the second-highest number of cases at 15,228, accounting for 14.15 per cent of the total, followed by the Central Province with 11,062 cases (10.28 per cent).\n\nKandy reported 8,765 cases, while Kalutara recorded 6,691 and Matara 6,487.\n\nMeanwhile, 37 Medical Officer of Health (MOH) areas have been identified as high-risk areas during the 44th week of 2026. The average midnight dengue case count for the week stood at 472.\n\nAlthough the number of reported cases has declined from the July peak, the overall burden remains substantial.\n\nJuly recorded the highest monthly figure with 29,964 cases, followed by June with 21,533. August saw 10,155 cases, while September and October recorded 5,230 and 5,532 cases respectively.\n\nA further 1,399 cases were reported during the first seven days of November."
