    "language": "en",
    "md5": "21a92c1e946a7c0e9e0f9d3f8d9f0370",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "If Colombo\u2019s drainage infrastructure can only withstand 30mm to 50mm of rainfall, then surely the question should be why successive municipal administrations have failed to improve this capacity. More importantly, what has the present administration done during its months in office to address these longstanding shortcomings?\n\nThe problem is not merely the volume of rainfall. Blocked drains, neglected canals, unauthorised constructions, garbage accumulation and the destruction of natural waterways all contribute to urban flooding. When sewage systems overflow into residential neighbourhoods, the consequences extend beyond inconvenience. They create serious public health concerns, particularly for children, elderly residents and vulnerable communities.\n\nA properly functioning municipal council should have a systematic programme to inspect, clean and maintain every drainage channel, particularly before the monsoon seasons. Was this done adequately? Were the drainage networks inspected, and were blocked canals identified and cleared before the rains arrived? These are questions the Mayor must answer rather than simply pointing to the extraordinary volume of rainfall.\n\nOther South Asian cities offer important lessons, even though none is immune to flooding. Mumbai has repeatedly experienced devastating urban floods, including the 2024 monsoon floods. Yet the city has continued investing in stormwater drainage improvements and flood management infrastructure. Chennai, which experienced catastrophic flooding in 2015, has pursued an integrated urban flood management programme with support from the Asian Development Bank. Its approach includes improving drainage infrastructure, strengthening preparedness and ensuring long-term maintenance."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailymirrorlk-dc647e31",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-dc647e31",
