    "language": "en",
    "md5": "66ea07a0e5fc08471057b96e642bc28e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1898,
    "chunk_text": "Other South Asian cities offer important lessons, even though none is immune to flooding. Mumbai has repeatedly experienced devastating urban floods, including the 2024 monsoon floods. Yet the city has continued investing in stormwater drainage improvements and flood management infrastructure. Chennai, which experienced catastrophic flooding in 2015, has pursued an integrated urban flood management programme with support from the Asian Development Bank. Its approach includes improving drainage infrastructure, strengthening preparedness and ensuring long-term maintenance.\n\nThe solutions are not beyond our reach. First, the CMC must undertake a comprehensive audit of the entire drainage network, identifying blocked, damaged and undersized drains. A publicly available maintenance schedule should be introduced, with regular inspections and accountability for officials responsible for ensuring the work is completed.\n\nSecond, the city\u2019s canals, wetlands and natural waterways must be protected from illegal encroachments and indiscriminate dumping. Construction approvals must be scrutinised, while existing obstructions should be addressed through lawful procedures that also protect affected communities.\n\nThird, Colombo needs a long-term climate-resilient drainage master plan, developed with engineers, environmental experts and urban planners. This must include larger stormwater drains where necessary, additional pumping facilities, retention ponds and rainwater harvesting systems. Parks and green spaces should also be incorporated into urban planning to allow rainwater to seep naturally into the ground instead of immediately overwhelming drainage channels.\n\nTechnology must play a greater role too. Real-time rainfall monitoring, flood-risk mapping and early warning systems could help authorities identify vulnerable neighbourhoods and respond before roads become impassable."
  },
