    "chunk_size_bytes": 1827,
    "chunk_text": "Votes from workers, land for investors: TPA\u2019s Barath questions Govt. ahead of Budget\n\nColombo, Nov. 9 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Tamil Progressive Alliance (TPA) has urged the Government to put its plantation leasing decision on hold and first honour the housing and livelihood land commitments made to the Malaiyaha Tamil community through the Hatton Declaration.\n\nAhead of the 2027 Budget, DPF Vice President for International Affairs and Communications and TPA Politburo Member Barath Arullsamy said the community\u2019s land rights were \u201changing between the Hatton promise and the Cabinet\u2019s leasing decision\u201d.\n\n\u201cWe are asking for what you promised. The Hatton Declaration pledged land rights, dignified housing and the use of uncultivated plantation lands for our youth\u2019s livelihoods. Those assurances helped you win substantial support in the plantation region. Now honour them,\u201d he said.\n\nThe declaration, launched under Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s leadership in October 2023, committed to addressing land ownership and housing problems and using uncultivated and abandoned plantation lands to improve unemployed Malaiyaha youth\u2019s livelihoods.\n\nArullsamy contrasted this with the Cabinet\u2019s October 26 approval to lease 15 properties belonging to the Janatha Estates Development Board, Sri Lanka State Plantations Corporation and Elkaduwa Plantations Limited to investors.\n\n\u201cGovernement representatives describe the leasing programme as national policy. Then explain how that policy honours your own declaration. Secure housing land with legal deeds and livelihood land for our youth before signing commercial agreements,\u201d he said.\n\nHe stressed that the TPA welcomed private investment and economic development, but insisted that these must proceed after the community\u2019s promised land rights were secured."
