    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan University Rankings: The free fall (Part 2)\n\nClosed to the world, short of teachers and thin on research: what the data reveal, and how to reverse the slide\n\nIn the first part of this article, I showed that Sri Lanka\u2019s universities sit far below those of countries with similar or even lower incomes, and that the gap widened again in the QS Asia University Rankings 2027, released on 4 November. The University of Colombo, our best, is now 364th in Asia, down from 275th two years ago. Indonesia and Vietnam, at almost exactly our income level, have universities at 53rd and 159th. India, Bangladesh and Pakistan, with half or less of our per capita income, have theirs at 66th, 136th and 76th.\n\nThis second part asks the more useful question: why? The QS dataset \u2013 1,722 universities from 26 countries and territories \u2013 breaks each university\u2019s score into eleven indicators, showing exactly where we lose our marks.\n\nComparing Sri Lanka\u2019s top three universities with the top three of each peer country tells us exactly where we fall short. Table III summarises the scores (out of 100).\n\nnPattern 1: Sri Lanka is closed to the world. This is the single biggest gap. The average score of our top three universities on international faculty is 1.6 out of 100 and on international students 3.8. Inbound and outbound student exchange are similarly close to zero. Indonesia\u2019s flagships score close to 100 on international faculty and on both kinds of exchange; Malaysia\u2019s score 95 or more on all four. Even Bangladesh and Pakistan, hardly magnets for foreign academics, score ten to fifteen times higher than we do on international faculty. Across all 1,722 universities in Asia, the average international faculty score is 33.3. Ours is 1.8."
  },
