    "chunk_size_bytes": 1862,
    "chunk_text": "nPattern 1: Sri Lanka is closed to the world. This is the single biggest gap. The average score of our top three universities on international faculty is 1.6 out of 100 and on international students 3.8. Inbound and outbound student exchange are similarly close to zero. Indonesia\u2019s flagships score close to 100 on international faculty and on both kinds of exchange; Malaysia\u2019s score 95 or more on all four. Even Bangladesh and Pakistan, hardly magnets for foreign academics, score ten to fifteen times higher than we do on international faculty. Across all 1,722 universities in Asia, the average international faculty score is 33.3. Ours is 1.8.\n\nPattern 3: Thin research output and too few PhDs. On papers per faculty, our top three average 12.8 against 99.8 for India and 66.3 for Malaysia. On staff with a PhD, the averages are 41.5 for Sri Lanka against 100 for India and 99.7 for Malaysia. Every Sri Lankan university with data fell in rank on the PhD indicator this year, even where its own score improved slightly. Five Sri Lankan universities \u2013 Rajarata, Sabaragamuwa, South Eastern, Ruhuna and Wayamba \u2013 reported no PhD data at all, which itself costs points.\n\nPattern 4: Reputation is our relative strength \u2013 but it is fading. Sri Lanka still does respectably on academic reputation compared with the Asian average, and Sri Lankan research that is published is reasonably well cited. Sri Jayewardenepura is 194th in Asia for citations per paper, and Peradeniya is 274th for its international research network. Academic reputation was the only indicator where Sri Lanka gained ground this year. Yet the gap with peers\u2019 flagships remains wide: India\u2019s and Indonesia\u2019s top three average 98 on academic reputation, ours 41. And on employer reputation, both Colombo and Sri Jayewardenepura lost points this year while most of Asia gained."
