    "chunk_text": "Pattern 4: Reputation is our relative strength \u2013 but it is fading. Sri Lanka still does respectably on academic reputation compared with the Asian average, and Sri Lankan research that is published is reasonably well cited. Sri Jayewardenepura is 194th in Asia for citations per paper, and Peradeniya is 274th for its international research network. Academic reputation was the only indicator where Sri Lanka gained ground this year. Yet the gap with peers\u2019 flagships remains wide: India\u2019s and Indonesia\u2019s top three average 98 on academic reputation, ours 41. And on employer reputation, both Colombo and Sri Jayewardenepura lost points this year while most of Asia gained.\n\nTable IV compares Sri Lanka\u2019s overall presence in the 2027 Asia rankings with that of its peers. Two numbers stand out. First, Sri Lanka has no university in Asia\u2019s top 200; Vietnam and the Philippines have two each, Bangladesh three, Pakistan five and Indonesia twelve. Second, within South Asia alone \u2013 a subregion that QS ranks separately \u2013 the University of Colombo is only 83rd. In other words, 82 universities in India, Pakistan, Bangladesh and Iran are ahead of our best.\n\nAgain, honesty requires a caveat. This year was a difficult one for most of South Asia. About 71% of India\u2019s returning universities, 66% of Pakistan\u2019s and 59% of Bangladesh\u2019s fell in rank, compared with 67% of Sri Lanka\u2019s. IIT Delhi fell from 59th to 66th. The difference is the starting point: a slide from 59th to 66th still leaves IIT Delhi among Asia\u2019s elite; ours moves us further from relevance."
  },
