    "md5": "bbf362d8d6d460657a54c99b9eac2405",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1828,
    "chunk_text": "Again, honesty requires a caveat. This year was a difficult one for most of South Asia. About 71% of India\u2019s returning universities, 66% of Pakistan\u2019s and 59% of Bangladesh\u2019s fell in rank, compared with 67% of Sri Lanka\u2019s. IIT Delhi fell from 59th to 66th. The difference is the starting point: a slide from 59th to 66th still leaves IIT Delhi among Asia\u2019s elite; ours moves us further from relevance.\n\nRankings are a symptom. The causes are well known to anyone who has worked with our universities.\n\nThe first is money, and what happened to it after 2022. The economic crisis eroded academic salaries in real terms, cut research budgets and accelerated the departure of experienced lecturers abroad. The faculty-student and PhD numbers in the QS data are the statistical footprint of that exodus.\n\nThe second is insularity, and it is largely a policy choice. State universities have few incentives to recruit foreign staff or students, and the rules make it hard even when they try. As I pointed out in 2025, visa procedures for foreign academics and students are cumbersome and unpredictable.\n\nThe third is weak incentives for research. The research allowance paid to state university academics is largely automatic rather than linked to measurable output. Recruitment and promotion still give limited weight to publication impact. It should not surprise us that output per academic is low.\n\nThe fourth is that nobody owns the rankings. Most leading Asian universities have dedicated offices that manage data submissions to ranking agencies, track indicators, build relationships with employers and promote the institution abroad. Few Sri Lankan universities do. The absence of PhD data for five of our ranked universities is a small but telling example: points are being lost simply because data are not submitted."
  },
