    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "The fourth is that nobody owns the rankings. Most leading Asian universities have dedicated offices that manage data submissions to ranking agencies, track indicators, build relationships with employers and promote the institution abroad. Few Sri Lankan universities do. The absence of PhD data for five of our ranked universities is a small but telling example: points are being lost simply because data are not submitted.\n\nThe common thread is deliberate policy sustained over a decade or more, not money: none of these countries was richer per head than Sri Lanka when it started. What they had was a decision, made at the top that their universities would compete internationally.\n\nNone of this is beyond repair. Countries with fewer resources have moved faster. Here are six practical steps.\n\nOpen the doors. Set a national target for international students and staff, as Malaysia has done, and back it with a fast-track visa for foreign academics and students. Internationalisation indicators are where Sri Lanka\u2019s scores are closest to zero, so they offer the biggest gains for the smallest effort.\n\nFix the staff equation. Retaining and rebuilding the academic workforce should be a priority. That means competitive pay for research-active staff, funded PhD places linked to returning service, and allowing universities to hire experienced Sri Lankan academics from the diaspora on flexible terms.\n\nPay for performance. The University Grants Commission should link a portion of university funding to measurable improvement in research output, internationalisation and graduate outcomes. Research allowances should reward research.\n\nBring employers inside the tent. Employer reputation is worth 20% of the QS Asia score. Chambers of commerce, export associations and large employers should formalise partnerships with universities on curricula, internships and joint research."
