    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "546a9a18ba20120ffd9734682b66b075",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond Budget surpluses: Making public expenditure work for Sri Lanka\u2019s external sector\n\nSri Lanka\u203as economic recovery has restored a measure of fiscal stability, but its external-sector vulnerabilities remain a fundamental challenge. The 2027 Budget presents an opportunity to move beyond treating the primary surplus as the principal measure of fiscal success. While maintaining fiscal discipline and debt sustainability, public expenditure must increasingly be directed towards expanding productive capacity, strengthening export competitiveness, reducing avoidable import dependence and generating sustainable foreign exchange earnings\n\n\u201cOne of the great mistakes is to judge policies and programs by their intentions rather than their results\u201d\n\nSri Lanka has made considerable progress in restoring macroeconomic stability following its unprecedented economic crisis. Fiscal consolidation, improved revenue mobilisation, debt restructuring and the rebuilding of foreign exchange reserves have helped restore confidence in the economy. These achievements must be protected.\n\nHowever, economic stabilisation is not the same as economic transformation. A country can improve its fiscal accounts while continuing to face structural weaknesses in its external sector. Sri Lanka\u2019s challenge is particularly evident in the persistent gap between its foreign exchange earnings and its commitments to finance imports, service external obligations and meet other international payments.\n\nThis raises an important question for the 2027 Budget: How can public expenditure be transformed from an instrument primarily concerned with allocating financial resources into a strategic instrument for strengthening the country\u2019s capacity to earn foreign exchange?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Beyond Budget surpluses: Making public expenditure work for Sri Lanka\u2019s external sector",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beyond-Budget-surpluses-Making-public-expenditure-work-for-Sri-Lanka-s-external-sector/4-799840",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794248051.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "46483c1a5170937ff9c495e5d6c11988",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "This raises an important question for the 2027 Budget: How can public expenditure be transformed from an instrument primarily concerned with allocating financial resources into a strategic instrument for strengthening the country\u2019s capacity to earn foreign exchange?\n\nThe issue is not whether Sri Lanka should increase or reduce Government expenditure. It is whether the composition, quality and expected economic returns of that expenditure are appropriate for the country\u2019s development requirements.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should therefore mark a transition from expenditure discipline alone towards expenditure effectiveness, without compromising fiscal and debt sustainability.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s achievement of a primary surplus of 5.4% of GDP in 2025 demonstrated the progress made in fiscal consolidation. The IMF\u2019s May 2026 review projected a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP for 2027, reinforcing the importance of maintaining a credible fiscal anchor.\n\nHowever, a primary surplus measures Government revenue less non-interest expenditure. It does not, by itself, establish whether the economy is generating sufficient foreign exchange to meet its external commitments.\n\nSimilarly, a Government Budget surplus does not automatically produce a balance-of-payments surplus. The two accounts measure different economic relationships.\n\nThe external sector is influenced by merchandise exports and imports, tourism, other services, workers\u2019 remittances, investment income and external financing. Consequently, fiscal performance must be assessed alongside the country\u2019s capacity to generate and retain foreign exchange.\n\nThe policy implication is clear. Sri Lanka should maintain fiscal discipline, but it should not treat the achievement of a primary surplus as the ultimate objective of economic policy.\n\nThe more important question is whether the available fiscal resources are being used to strengthen the productive and foreign exchange-earning capacity of the economy."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Beyond Budget surpluses: Making public expenditure work for Sri Lanka\u2019s external sector",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beyond-Budget-surpluses-Making-public-expenditure-work-for-Sri-Lanka-s-external-sector/4-799840",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
