    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9f519d3079455e4c7ca1b30aca963f32",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "The policy implication is clear. Sri Lanka should maintain fiscal discipline, but it should not treat the achievement of a primary surplus as the ultimate objective of economic policy.\n\nThe more important question is whether the available fiscal resources are being used to strengthen the productive and foreign exchange-earning capacity of the economy.\n\nThese developments demonstrate that individual sources of foreign exchange earnings can move in different directions, while import expenditure can increase substantially.\n\nThe IMF\u203as May 2026 projections also indicated a current account deficit of $ 534 million for 2026 and $ 115 million for 2027.\n\nThese projections should not be interpreted as an inevitable outcome. They should instead serve as an early warning that the country\u203as external-sector capacity needs strengthening.\n\nSri Lanka must pay attention to both sides of the equation: increasing foreign exchange earnings and reducing avoidable foreign exchange expenditure.\n\nThis does not mean indiscriminately restricting imports. Imports of machinery, technology, energy, intermediate goods and essential inputs are necessary for economic growth. The objective should be to ensure that foreign exchange is used productively and that the economy develops a stronger capacity to generate the resources needed to finance these commitments.\n\nNot all Government expenditure produces the same economic returns. Some expenditure increases immediate domestic consumption, while other expenditure creates productive assets, improves efficiency and generates future income.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should therefore distinguish between three complementary expenditure priorities.\n\nInvestment in agriculture, fisheries, manufacturing, renewable energy, transport and logistics can reduce production costs, improve productivity and lower avoidable import dependence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7a140420",
    "date_str": "2026-11-09",
    "description": "Beyond Budget surpluses: Making public expenditure work for Sri Lanka\u2019s external sector",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beyond-Budget-surpluses-Making-public-expenditure-work-for-Sri-Lanka-s-external-sector/4-799840",
    "lang": "en",
