    "time_ut": 1794248051.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-09-dailyftlk-7a140420-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "47c8bae96b8bbc22ccf58d3a50bda87a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "The 2027 Budget should therefore distinguish between three complementary expenditure priorities.\n\nInvestment in agriculture, fisheries, manufacturing, renewable energy, transport and logistics can reduce production costs, improve productivity and lower avoidable import dependence.\n\nSimilarly, developing renewable energy and improving energy efficiency can reduce exposure to imported fossil fuels, strengthen energy security and release foreign exchange for other productive uses.\n\nHowever, import substitution should not become an excuse for protecting inefficient domestic production. Investments should be guided by productivity, quality, cost competitiveness and long-term economic viability.\n\nTraditional merchandise exports, including apparel, tea, rubber and coconut products, remain important. However, greater attention should be given to value addition, product diversification, market development and integration into international production networks.\n\nThere are also substantial opportunities in tourism, information technology, business-process outsourcing, professional services, maritime services, logistics, education and specialised knowledge-based services.\n\nPublic expenditure should support the infrastructure, skills, research, digital connectivity and institutional arrangements required to develop these activities.\n\nSri Lanka should not limit its ambitions to expanding existing industries. It should also identify emerging opportunities in artificial intelligence, digital services, advanced manufacturing, biotechnology, renewable-energy technologies and other knowledge-intensive activities.\n\nThis does not mean that the Government should attempt to manufacture every new technology domestically. Rather, it should facilitate partnerships between Sri Lankan entrepreneurs, universities, international investors and technology providers to identify commercially viable opportunities."
  },
