    "md5": "e9e732021b726de8910e8ff2739a3064",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "This does not mean that the Government should attempt to manufacture every new technology domestically. Rather, it should facilitate partnerships between Sri Lankan entrepreneurs, universities, international investors and technology providers to identify commercially viable opportunities.\n\nOne of the most important requirements is to ensure that public expenditure supports a coordinated institutional system.\n\nThe Board of Investment (BOI), Export Development Board (EDB) and National Business Facilitation (NBF) initiatives should operate as complementary components of a broader economic development strategy.\n\nThe BOI should concentrate on attracting and facilitating quality domestic and foreign investment, particularly investment that expands productive capacity, introduces technology, develops export industries and creates employment.\n\nThe EDB should strengthen export development by helping businesses identify international markets, improve product standards, diversify their markets, develop brands and integrate into global value chains. It should also give greater attention to services exports and emerging sources of foreign exchange earnings.\n\nThe National Business Facilitation initiative should help remove the administrative obstacles that delay investment and business activity. Simplifying approvals, improving inter-agency coordination, digitising procedures and developing an effective National Single Window can reduce the time and cost of doing business.\n\nThese institutions should not operate as separate administrative entities pursuing disconnected objectives. They should function as a coordinated system, with clearly defined responsibilities and measurable outcomes.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should support this coordination through appropriate institutional funding, performance targets and accountability mechanisms."
  },
