    "md5": "398d275ebeb1ff283aae96c0d9e04f45",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "These institutions should not operate as separate administrative entities pursuing disconnected objectives. They should function as a coordinated system, with clearly defined responsibilities and measurable outcomes.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should support this coordination through appropriate institutional funding, performance targets and accountability mechanisms.\n\nA major weakness in public expenditure management is the tendency to assess projects primarily through their financial allocations and physical completion rather than their wider economic returns.\n\nThe 2027 Budget provides an opportunity to strengthen project appraisal and monitoring.\n\nMajor public investment proposals should be evaluated not only by their construction costs and immediate outputs but also by their expected contribution to:\n\nA project that requires substantial imported equipment may initially increase foreign exchange expenditure. However, if it subsequently generates export earnings, reduces energy imports or improves national productivity, its longer-term economic contribution may be substantial.\n\nConversely, a project with relatively low initial foreign exchange costs may produce limited economic returns if it does not address a genuine productive need.\n\nThe Government should therefore introduce an external-sector impact assessment for major public investment projects.\n\nSuch an assessment should identify the project\u203as foreign exchange requirements, expected earnings or savings, implementation period, risks and long-term economic returns.\n\nIt should also distinguish between short-term costs and longer-term benefits. This would enable policymakers to make better-informed decisions rather than judging investments solely by their immediate impact on the balance of payments.\n\nAn increase in Government expenditure can stimulate household consumption and business activity. If domestic productive capacity does not expand correspondingly, additional demand may be directed towards imported goods and services."
