    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "An increase in Government expenditure can stimulate household consumption and business activity. If domestic productive capacity does not expand correspondingly, additional demand may be directed towards imported goods and services.\n\nThis is why fiscal policy must be appropriately coordinated with monetary, credit and trade policies.\n\nThe Central Bank should continue to safeguard price and financial stability, while allowing monetary and exchange-rate policies to respond to evolving economic conditions within its statutory mandate.\n\nCommercial banks should be encouraged to strengthen lending to viable productive activities, including export-oriented businesses, SMEs, technology-based enterprises and industries capable of improving domestic value addition.\n\nTrade policy should support competitive access to essential inputs, modern technology and international markets. Unnecessary administrative barriers and unpredictable policy changes can discourage investment and weaken export competitiveness.\n\nThe objective is not to control every import or direct every lending decision. It is to establish a policy environment in which fiscal resources, financial intermediation and international trade reinforce domestic production and sustainable foreign exchange generation.\n\nThe Government cannot finance the entire transformation required by Sri Lanka\u2019s economy through the national Budget.\n\nPublic resources are limited, and debt sustainability remains a critical consideration. Therefore, public expenditure should also be used strategically to mobilise private investment.\n\nWell-designed public-private partnerships can support infrastructure, renewable energy, logistics, industrial development and other commercially viable activities.\n\nHowever, such partnerships must be transparent, competitively structured and supported by appropriate risk-sharing arrangements. They should not create hidden fiscal liabilities or transfer unreasonable risks to the public."
  },
