    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5baa74211af095570cf7d81da8dc70aa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "However, such partnerships must be transparent, competitively structured and supported by appropriate risk-sharing arrangements. They should not create hidden fiscal liabilities or transfer unreasonable risks to the public.\n\nInvestment promotion, export market access, technology partnerships, development finance and participation in regional and global value chains should be treated as interconnected elements of economic strategy.\n\nThe objective should be to attract investment that contributes to production, exports, skills, technology and employment rather than investment measured merely by the amount of capital announced.\n\nI would therefore suggest that the Government consider introducing an External-Sector-Oriented Public Expenditure Framework as an integral part of its medium-term fiscal and development strategy.\n\nUnder this framework, major expenditure proposals would be assessed against their expected contributions to domestic productive capacity, export earnings, import efficiency, foreign exchange savings and private investment.\n\nThe Finance Ministry should provide the overall fiscal framework and ensure consistency with debt sustainability and medium-term expenditure limits.\n\nRelevant sectoral ministries should identify investment priorities and establish measurable performance indicators. The BOI, EDB and National Business Facilitation initiative should contribute to identifying investment opportunities, export potential and administrative constraints.\n\nThe Central Bank should provide independent analysis of external-sector developments and associated macroeconomic risks, consistent with its statutory responsibilities.\n\nAn annual public report could assess the outcomes of major expenditure programmes, identifying whether expected investments, exports, employment and foreign exchange benefits have materialised."
