    "md5": "c8ca5609ad09065ff3f40f93f1c26dca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "The Central Bank should provide independent analysis of external-sector developments and associated macroeconomic risks, consistent with its statutory responsibilities.\n\nAn annual public report could assess the outcomes of major expenditure programmes, identifying whether expected investments, exports, employment and foreign exchange benefits have materialised.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery has restored a measure of fiscal stability, but its external-sector vulnerabilities remain a fundamental challenge. The 2027 Budget presents an opportunity to move beyond treating the primary surplus as the principal measure of fiscal success. While maintaining fiscal discipline and debt sustainability, public expenditure must increasingly be directed towards expanding productive capacity, strengthening export competitiveness, reducing avoidable import dependence and generating sustainable foreign exchange earnings. This requires a strategic approach to public investment, stronger coordination between economic institutions, and a measurable framework for assessing the external-sector consequences of Government spending. Ultimately, the success of the Budget should be judged not only by how much the Government saves, but also by how effectively it helps the country earn.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget is an opportunity to consolidate the achievements of economic stabilisation while addressing the structural weaknesses that contributed to the country\u2019s external-sector vulnerability.\n\nMaintaining a credible primary surplus, controlling public debt and strengthening revenue mobilisation remain essential. However, these objectives should not be pursued independently of the country\u2019s productive and foreign exchange-earning capacity.\n\nThe fundamental challenge is to narrow the gap between what Sri Lanka earns in foreign exchange and what it must spend to sustain its economy and meet its international commitments."
  },
