    "chunk_size_bytes": 1435,
    "chunk_text": "Maintaining a credible primary surplus, controlling public debt and strengthening revenue mobilisation remain essential. However, these objectives should not be pursued independently of the country\u2019s productive and foreign exchange-earning capacity.\n\nThe fundamental challenge is to narrow the gap between what Sri Lanka earns in foreign exchange and what it must spend to sustain its economy and meet its international commitments.\n\nThe BOI, EDB and National Business Facilitation initiatives can play complementary roles in translating these priorities into practical economic outcomes.\n\nUltimately, the success of the 2027 Budget should not be judged solely by the primary surplus it achieves or the amount of public expenditure it contains. It should also be judged by the productive capacity it creates, the foreign exchange it helps generate and save, the investment it mobilises and the employment and prosperity it delivers.\n\nSri Lanka needs a Budget that does not merely manage scarcity, but builds the country\u2019s capacity to earn and save foreign exchange. That is the transition from economic stabilisation to sustainable economic transformation.\n\n(The author served as the Special Adviser to the Office of the President of Namibia from 2006 to 2012 and was a Senior Consultant with the UNDP for 20 years. He was a Senior Economist with the Central Bank of Sri Lanka [1972-1993]. He can be reached via [email\u00a0protected])"
