    "md5": "e700fc7b16d4efe93453daa06036a087",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Will Sri Lanka remain a tea plantation economy or become global capital of tea?\n\nAs Sri Lanka prepares for the National Budget on 12 November, the country faces a defining economic question. How do we generate the foreign exchange, investment and jobs needed to accelerate growth in an increasingly competitive global economy? For decades, tea has been one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most celebrated exports. Ceylon Tea remains one of the few truly global brands the country can proudly claim as its own. Yet while we continue to grow and export some of the world\u2019s finest tea, much of the industry\u2019s most profitable activities are happening elsewhere. The issue is no longer about producing better tea. The issue is whether Sri Lanka can capture more revenue share from the global value added tea business itself.\n\nThe tea industry deserves enormous credit for its contribution to the national economy even though things have been rough due the political turmoil/ war in the Middle East. During the first nine months of 2026, tea exports generated approximately $ 1.083 billion, compared with $ 1.163 billion during the corresponding period in 2025. Export volumes have declined by 4%, falling from 198.3 million kilograms to 189.4 million kilograms.\n\nWhile export earnings in Sri Lankan Rupees increased slightly from Rs. 347 billion to Rs. 350 billion, this improvement was largely driven by currency depreciation rather than stronger export performance. These figures reveal an important reality. Sri Lanka is earning less in foreign currency while exporting less tea. At a time when the country urgently needs foreign exchange, that trend should concern policymakers. This is not a criticism of the tea industry. Rather, it is recognition that the global tea business has fundamentally changed."
  },
