    "chunk_size_bytes": 1472,
    "chunk_text": "While export earnings in Sri Lankan Rupees increased slightly from Rs. 347 billion to Rs. 350 billion, this improvement was largely driven by currency depreciation rather than stronger export performance. These figures reveal an important reality. Sri Lanka is earning less in foreign currency while exporting less tea. At a time when the country urgently needs foreign exchange, that trend should concern policymakers. This is not a criticism of the tea industry. Rather, it is recognition that the global tea business has fundamentally changed.\n\nMarket dynamics are changing rapidly. Exports to Iraq, traditionally one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest tea destinations, declined sharply from 30 million kilograms to 18.4 million kilograms, a reduction of almost 39%. Shipments to Libya fell from 15.9 million kilograms to 6.6 million kilograms, representing a decline of nearly 60%. Exports to China, one of the world\u2019s largest consumer markets, declined by 10% to 7.1 million kilograms. Even Chile, a strong growth story in recent years, saw imports of Sri Lankan tea decline from 8.5 million kilograms to 6.3 million kilograms. Although exports to T\u00fcrkiye increased dramatically by 134%, rising from 15.2 million kilograms to 35.7 million kilograms, much of this reflects changing regional trade routes and geopolitical circumstances rather than long-term structural growth. The lesson is clear. Overdependence on a limited number of markets is becoming increasingly risky."
  },
