    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "Market dynamics are changing rapidly. Exports to Iraq, traditionally one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest tea destinations, declined sharply from 30 million kilograms to 18.4 million kilograms, a reduction of almost 39%. Shipments to Libya fell from 15.9 million kilograms to 6.6 million kilograms, representing a decline of nearly 60%. Exports to China, one of the world\u2019s largest consumer markets, declined by 10% to 7.1 million kilograms. Even Chile, a strong growth story in recent years, saw imports of Sri Lankan tea decline from 8.5 million kilograms to 6.3 million kilograms. Although exports to T\u00fcrkiye increased dramatically by 134%, rising from 15.2 million kilograms to 35.7 million kilograms, much of this reflects changing regional trade routes and geopolitical circumstances rather than long-term structural growth. The lesson is clear. Overdependence on a limited number of markets is becoming increasingly risky.\n\nSri Lanka effectively has three options. Option One: Defend the Current Model. We can continue investing heavily in promoting Pure Ceylon Tea and strengthening its premium image. This remains essential. The Lion Logo is one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most valuable national assets and must be protected at all costs. However, branding alone is unlikely to transform the sector from its current $ 1 billion export base into the multi-billion-dollar growth engine that the economy requires.\n\nOption Two: Pursue Better Market Access. Sri Lanka can continue negotiating trade agreements and seeking improved access for value-added exports. While worthwhile, many countries are unlikely to dismantle policies that support their own packaging and blending industries. Trade diplomacy should continue, but it is unlikely to become the breakthrough solution."
