    "md5": "33b4f14f60b7e4cc023847791841a88d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1525,
    "chunk_text": "Option Two: Pursue Better Market Access. Sri Lanka can continue negotiating trade agreements and seeking improved access for value-added exports. While worthwhile, many countries are unlikely to dismantle policies that support their own packaging and blending industries. Trade diplomacy should continue, but it is unlikely to become the breakthrough solution.\n\nThe biggest concern surrounding a Tea Hub is obvious. Will it damage the Ceylon Tea brand? The answer depends entirely on how the strategy is designed. These concerns are legitimate and deserve serious consideration. However, protecting Pure Ceylon Tea and developing a Tea Hub need not be mutually exclusive goals. The solution is a clear dual-brand architecture. Tier One: Pure Ceylon Tea-100% Sri Lankan origin, Lion Logo protected, Existing Tea Board standards maintained and Premium positioning preserved.\n\nTier Two: Sri Lanka Tea Hub Certified- Internationally blended products, Separate certification system, Full digital traceability and Distinct visual identity Consumers would know exactly what they are purchasing. The national brand would remain protected while a second engine of growth is created.\n\nBudget 2027 presents a rare opportunity to think bigger about tea. Instead of viewing the industry as an agricultural sector requiring periodic support, Sri Lanka should position tea as a national export competitiveness strategy. A dedicated Sri Lanka Tea Hub Authority should be established to coordinate policy, investment promotion and governance."
