    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 presents a rare opportunity to think bigger about tea. Instead of viewing the industry as an agricultural sector requiring periodic support, Sri Lanka should position tea as a national export competitiveness strategy. A dedicated Sri Lanka Tea Hub Authority should be established to coordinate policy, investment promotion and governance.\n\nTea remains one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest agricultural export industries, yet earnings have largely remained within the $ 1 billion to $ 1.5 billion range for many years. The real opportunity lies beyond production. A carefully designed Tea Hub strategy should target: $ 5 billion tea-sector value by 2035, over $ 500\n\nmillion in foreign direct investment, around 50,000 new direct and indirect jobs, significant expansion in packaging, logistics and export services and positioning Colombo emerging as South Asia\u2019s leading tea value-addition and trading centre. These are ambitious goals, but economic transformation has never been achieved through modest ambition.\n\nThe question before Budget 2027 is not whether Sri Lanka should protect Pure Ceylon Tea. That debate has already been settled. The real question is whether Sri Lanka is prepared to do the reforms and capture a larger share of the wealth generated by the global tea industry. A carefully designed Tea Hub can protect Brand Ceylon while attracting investment, creating employment, strengthening exports and generating new foreign exchange earnings. It can transform tea from a traditional export crop into a modern economic ecosystem. History rarely rewards countries that simply preserve yesterday\u2019s successes. It rewards nations that build tomorrow\u2019s opportunities on the foundations of their greatest strengths."
