    "chunk_text": "CMC moves against illegal structures as Colombo flood crisis sparks political backlash\n\nThe Colombo Municipal Council (CMC) has stepped up efforts to address recurring flooding in the capital, identifying 60 locations with drainage obstructions for urgent intervention and warning of legal action against unauthorised constructions blocking waterways.\n\nThe measures come amid mounting criticism over the Council\u2019s preparedness following heavy rainfall on 6 November, which left several parts of Colombo and its suburbs inundated for hours, disrupting traffic and daily activities.\n\nAccording to CMC, several unauthorised constructions identified along Sri Wickrama Road in Mattakkuliya and Bimelwatte Road had already been removed, while notices were being issued to other locations where similar obstructions had been identified. The CMC has warned that legal action would be taken against those responsible for unauthorised structures obstructing drainage systems and contributing to urban flooding.\n\nMeanwhile, a special joint operation involving several State institutions was launched on Sunday (8) under the leadership of Colombo Mayor Vraie Cally Balthazaar and Urban Development Deputy Minister Eranga Gunasekara to clear drainage blockages and address the city\u2019s vulnerability to flash floods.\n\nThe operation covers five electoral divisions within the CMC, North Colombo, East Colombo, West Colombo, Borella and Central Colombo, with priority given to locations facing a high risk of flooding.\n\nInitial work is being carried out at Kirimandala Mawatha in Narahenpita, Haig Road in Bambalapitiya, Collingwood Place and Parakum Road in Wellawatte, the Cooray Urban Housing Scheme, Stafford Road in Kirulapone and the adjoining canal.\n\nOther locations identified for intervention include Heen Ela near Baseline Road, Aramaya Road in Dematagoda, the New Bazaar area in Central Colombo, De Mel Watta and Paradise Road."
  },
