    "chunk_text": "The Government intends to establish a coordinated mechanism for the maintenance, rehabilitation and monitoring of drainage infrastructure, replacing the fragmented approach under which individual institutions have operated separately. The initiative is intended to provide immediate relief while establishing a longer-term framework to reduce Colombo\u2019s exposure to flash floods.\n\nOpposition MP S.M. Marikkar, meanwhile, called for Mayor Balthazar\u2019s resignation or removal, accusing the CMC of failing to clean drains and culverts despite advance warnings of heavy rainfall.\n\n\u201cColombo City would not have been flooded on Friday if the drains were cleaned on time. Either the Mayor should resign or President Anura Kumara Dissanayake should remove her from her post,\u201d he said.\n\nMarikar argued that Colombo had experienced heavier rainfall in the past without major public infrastructure being affected to the same extent. He cited rainfall exceeding 400 mm recorded in 1992, noting that important establishments such as the Colombo National Hospital had not been affected by flooding on that occasion.\n\nHe also pointed out that Jawatte, the ward represented by the Mayor, was among the areas inundated on Friday.\n\nCriticising the CMC\u2019s overall performance, Marikkar said the Council had failed to adequately serve Colombo\u2019s business community, including jewellers, despite being one of the wealthiest municipal councils in the region. He also praised the Council\u2019s performance under former Mayor Muhammed Uvais Emitiyas.\n\nMarikkar further alleged that neither the CMC Opposition Leader nor other Opposition councillors had been invited to participate in post-disaster discussions on the flooding."
