    "md5": "63a18f7ffca3ba319091107e2f61fe0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "Reflecting on the appointments, Page went on to say, \u201cThe appointments we are announcing are an important part of that journey. One of the greatest strengths of Cargills has been its people and our ability to develop leaders from within. These are individuals who have grown with the Group, understand our businesses and our values, and have demonstrated their ability to lead.\u201d\n\n\u201cAs the CT Holdings Group becomes larger and more complex, it is important that our businesses continue to be led by strong professional management, with clear accountability and the freedom and responsibility to take the Group forward,\u201d Page said.\n\nAlongside this evolution in management, the Group\u2019s controlling ownership is intended to remain stable and committed through Odeon Holdings, the parent company of CT Holdings. This long-term approach to ownership is also being shaped by a broader philosophy of stewardship and social responsibility.\n\nThe Chairman further commented, \u201cAt the same time, I want there to be equal clarity about the committed ownership of the Group. I believe that long-term committed ownership carries responsibilities that extend beyond the company itself.\u201d\n\nThrough the trust arrangements associated with Odeon Holdings, a significant part of the economic benefit derived from the Group is intended ultimately to support charitable causes in Sri Lanka. Elaborating on this commitment, Page said, \u201cIt is intended that the ownership held through Odeon Holdings will, through trusts, increasingly serve a wider social purpose. I am committed to contributing 50% of the economic interests in the capital and profits of Odeon Holdings, derived through its investments in CT Holdings PLC, towards charitable initiatives in Sri Lanka, with particular emphasis on education, supporting and advancing women\u2019s causes, and the development of art and theatre.\u201d"
  },
