    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Beyond promises to real progress\n\nWhat Sri Lanka must do to turn economic recovery into better living standards and lasting prosperity\n\nWhy better incomes, productive investment, effective public administration and responsible spending must become the measures of economic progress\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget Speech is scheduled for 12 November 2026. After years of economic hardship, public expectations are understandably high. Families want relief from living costs, better employment, reliable public services and greater confidence in the future.\n\nDiscussion has focused particularly on public sector salary increases. Fair remuneration matters, but a more fundamental question must guide the Budget debate: Will the government\u2019s decisions produce lasting improvements in people\u2019s lives?\n\nSince the 2022 crisis, Sri Lanka has restored a measure of stability. Growth has resumed, foreign exchange reserves have improved and public financial management has strengthened. According to the World Bank, the economy grew by 4.7% during the first half of 2026. Yet recovery in national statistics does not automatically translate into recovery for households.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should therefore be judged less by the size of its promises than by its capacity to increase real incomes, expand productive employment, attract investment and turn public resources into measurable results.\n\nPublic servants have faced considerable financial pressure, and higher salaries can provide welcome relief. But the increase expected in 2027 largely completes the three-stage salary revision announced in the 2025 Budget, rather than constituting an entirely new initiative.\n\nIt is also important to distinguish between nominal wages\u2014the money an employee receives\u2014and real wages\u2014what that money can purchase. If a worker earning Rs. 60,000 receives an additional Rs. 5,000, the nominal increase is about 8.3%. But if prices have risen by roughly 8% over the same period, the gain in purchasing power is small."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-10-dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "date_str": "2026-11-10",
    "description": "Budget 2027: Beyond promises to real progress",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Budget-2027-Beyond-promises-to-real-progress/4-799867",
    "lang": "en",
