    "language": "en",
    "md5": "76b3463d6628890d3b4620f95ffc222c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "It is also important to distinguish between nominal wages\u2014the money an employee receives\u2014and real wages\u2014what that money can purchase. If a worker earning Rs. 60,000 receives an additional Rs. 5,000, the nominal increase is about 8.3%. But if prices have risen by roughly 8% over the same period, the gain in purchasing power is small.\n\nRegular salary reviews are justified, but they must be financially sustainable and accompanied by efforts to improve productivity, contain living costs and raise earnings across the economy. The objective should be better living standards, not merely higher figures on payrolls.\n\nDespite the improvement in headline indicators, many Sri Lankans remain under financial strain. The Colombo Consumer Price Index recorded annual inflation of 8.3% in October 2026. The World Bank estimated poverty at 16.9% in 2025. Although overall unemployment stood at 4% in the second quarter of 2026, unemployment among people aged 15\u201324 was 17.8%, while labour force participation was only 50%.\n\nThe Health Ministry\u2019s 2025 Nutrition Month data also reported that 16.1% of children under five were underweight. These indicators remind us that economic hardship cannot be measured by GDP growth alone. Poverty, weak labour market participation and poor nutrition are not isolated social concerns: they reduce human potential and ultimately weaken the economy\u2019s productive capacity. Policies should therefore be evaluated for their effects on employment, opportunity and the distribution of gains, not growth alone.\n\nAnother distinction is often overlooked: lower inflation does not necessarily mean lower prices. If a food item rises from Rs. 100 to Rs. 200, a subsequent slowdown in inflation may simply mean that it becomes more expensive at a slower rate. Families still have to pay the higher price."
  },
