    "md5": "0be5ba85146b542f1d03575197d1848f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "Another distinction is often overlooked: lower inflation does not necessarily mean lower prices. If a food item rises from Rs. 100 to Rs. 200, a subsequent slowdown in inflation may simply mean that it becomes more expensive at a slower rate. Families still have to pay the higher price.\n\nSri Lanka cannot sustainably improve living standards through salary increases, subsidies and tax concessions alone. Those measures depend ultimately on the country\u2019s capacity to produce more goods and services, generate income and collect revenue without creating renewed fiscal instability.\n\nAgriculture, manufacturing, tourism, technology and export-oriented services all offer potential. Small and medium-sized enterprises are especially important because they create jobs and economic activity throughout the country. Yet, many struggle with expensive finance, outdated equipment, limited market access and cumbersome administrative procedures.\n\nFarmers need reliable irrigation, storage, transport and opportunities to add value to what they grow. Entrepreneurs need accessible finance, skilled workers and predictable rules. Exporters need productive infrastructure and efficient trade procedures.\n\nA stronger productive economy also depends on improving the quality of employment. Young Sri Lankans need pathways from education and training into rewarding work. This requires practical cooperation among universities, technical institutes, employers and regional businesses. Supporting enterprise outside Colombo would help distribute opportunities more fairly and reduce the pressure on young people to seek their future abroad.\n\nForeign exchange earnings deserve particular emphasis. Borrowing dollars is not the same as earning dollars. Loans can temporarily meet financing needs, but they bring repayment obligations. Sustainable foreign exchange comes from competitive exports, tourism, services and other productive activities, supported by formal workers\u2019 remittances."
  },
