    "md5": "71dadbcec4df78f78eb3be81322449e5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign exchange earnings deserve particular emphasis. Borrowing dollars is not the same as earning dollars. Loans can temporarily meet financing needs, but they bring repayment obligations. Sustainable foreign exchange comes from competitive exports, tourism, services and other productive activities, supported by formal workers\u2019 remittances.\n\nInvestment brings employment, technology, tax revenue and opportunities for growth. But investors commit capital when they believe policies will remain reasonably predictable and institutions will treat them fairly.\n\nSri Lanka must address delays in approvals, uncertainty over contracts, abrupt policy changes and inconsistent application of regulations. Existing investors also require timely, transparent remedies when legitimate concerns arise. Their experiences influence the decisions of potential new investors.\n\nInvestment promotion should consequently be judged by projects that actually begin operations, jobs created and exports generated\u2014not only by investment pledges or memoranda of understanding. A transparent, time-bound approval system, with clear channels for resolving disputes, could make a substantial difference. Equally important is consistent treatment of domestic enterprises, which are often the starting point for sustainable expansion.\n\nThe Government must, of course, investigate corruption and hold wrongdoers accountable. Strong institutions and the rule of law are essential to confidence. Yet public communication should also convey a positive economic direction: where investment opportunities lie, how obstacles will be removed and what policies businesses can reasonably expect.\n\nAccountability and investment promotion should reinforce rather than undermine one another. A credible government can investigate past misconduct while providing lawful, consistent and efficient conditions for future investment."
  },
