    "md5": "abacf870a9dd81f45f3a45134b2a3dcd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1769,
    "chunk_text": "Accountability and investment promotion should reinforce rather than undermine one another. A credible government can investigate past misconduct while providing lawful, consistent and efficient conditions for future investment.\n\nThere is concern that some officials hesitate to approve contracts, procurement decisions or projects because they fear later investigations. Such caution can delay development and discourage investors. But weakening anti-corruption institutions is not the answer.\n\nThe proper response is to establish clear procedures, transparent procurement standards, reliable documentation and fair review mechanisms. Deliberate misconduct must be investigated and punished. Officials acting honestly, reasonably and within the law should receive appropriate procedural protections.\n\nPolitical leaders and administrators must share responsibility for removing unnecessary complexity and improving coordination across agencies. Good governance means decisions that are both accountable and timely\u2014not the absence of decisions.\n\nThe public should not have to navigate overlapping offices to obtain routine approvals. Simplified procedures, better digital services and clearly assigned responsibilities would improve the experience of citizens as well as businesses. Administrative reform is therefore not a separate concern from economic policy. It is one of the conditions for translating policy into results.\n\nPublic investment in transport, energy, irrigation, education and healthcare can reduce business costs and expand opportunity. However, every major project should be assessed for economic benefits, financial affordability, environmental effects and long-term maintenance needs. Large expenditure is not, by itself, evidence of development."
  },
