    "md5": "407e56e6b79280be3c6049e884947918",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "Public investment in transport, energy, irrigation, education and healthcare can reduce business costs and expand opportunity. However, every major project should be assessed for economic benefits, financial affordability, environmental effects and long-term maintenance needs. Large expenditure is not, by itself, evidence of development.\n\nTax relief is understandably popular after years of hardship. Nevertheless, broad reductions without alternative revenue could weaken public finances and threaten recovery. A better approach is to widen compliance, reduce evasion, improve administration, limit waste and offer carefully targeted relief when affordable.\n\nThe Budget must confront these trade-offs directly. Social protection and economic development are both necessary, but they must be financed within a credible fiscal framework.\n\nThis means choosing among competing priorities rather than pretending all demands can be met immediately. Protecting essential healthcare and education, maintaining productive infrastructure and providing well-targeted assistance may yield greater benefits than highly visible but poorly prepared projects. Responsible budgeting is ultimately a question of priorities as much as accounting.\n\nPerhaps the clearest test of the 2027 Budget is whether allocated development funds become completed projects.\n\nDuring the first half of 2026, approximately Rs. 1,719 billion was allocated for capital expenditure and net lending for the full year, but only about Rs. 280 billion had been spent by the end of June\u2014just 16.3%. By comparison, total government expenditure had reached 39.2% of annual allocations and recurrent expenditure 45.9%. The comparable first-half capital expenditure and net lending rate in 2025 was 17.5%."
  },
