    "language": "en",
    "md5": "337d40c2893f1b580f489d640fd52c20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "During the first half of 2026, approximately Rs. 1,719 billion was allocated for capital expenditure and net lending for the full year, but only about Rs. 280 billion had been spent by the end of June\u2014just 16.3%. By comparison, total government expenditure had reached 39.2% of annual allocations and recurrent expenditure 45.9%. The comparable first-half capital expenditure and net lending rate in 2025 was 17.5%.\n\nDelayed expenditure has consequences beyond financial statements. If a hospital project stalls, patients wait for services. If a school building remains incomplete, students lose learning opportunities. Delayed irrigation systems affect farm output, while unfinished roads prolong transport costs.\n\nPostponed projects also reduce demand for construction workers, materials, machinery, transport and local suppliers. Thus, implementation failures can weaken both immediate economic activity and long-term productivity.\n\nThe causes are varied: inadequate preparation, land acquisition problems, procurement delays, environmental approvals, weak interagency coordination, shortages of technical expertise and difficulties releasing funds. Uncertainty among decision-makers may also contribute. No single official or institution can reasonably bear all the blame.\n\nA serious response must examine the complete project cycle\u2014from selection and preparation to procurement, funding, execution and evaluation\u2014rather than merely calling for faster spending.\n\nProjects should enter the Budget only when their feasibility, land requirements, approvals and implementation responsibilities are sufficiently clear. Otherwise, the government creates unrealistic expectations and later confronts predictable delays. Treasury funding plans and implementing agencies\u2019 work schedules also need to be aligned from the outset. More disciplined preparation could prevent many delays before they arise."
  },
