    "md5": "0d25aacc91d3a05187d12634fec83d9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "Projects should enter the Budget only when their feasibility, land requirements, approvals and implementation responsibilities are sufficiently clear. Otherwise, the government creates unrealistic expectations and later confronts predictable delays. Treasury funding plans and implementing agencies\u2019 work schedules also need to be aligned from the outset. More disciplined preparation could prevent many delays before they arise.\n\nThe central question is not how much the government spends, but what that spending achieves.\n\nThis is the value of performance-based budgeting. A road project should be assessed not only by its construction cost but by whether it reduces travel time, improves access to markets and benefits nearby communities. A hospital investment should be evaluated through service quality and patient access. Irrigation spending should be linked to agricultural productivity and farmers\u2019 incomes.\n\nEvery major publicly funded project should have a realistic timetable, a responsible institution, published financial and physical milestones, and measurable intended benefits. Quarterly progress reports should show what has been completed as well as what has been paid.\n\nProjects falling behind schedule need early diagnosis and corrective action. Officials should be held accountable for performance while receiving the resources, training and lawful protections needed to do their jobs. Government should also prioritise completing worthwhile existing projects before multiplying new announcements.\n\nIndependent evaluation has a role here. Citizens deserve to know not only whether a project was completed, but whether its promised benefits materialised. Publishing straightforward evaluations would help policymakers learn from mistakes, improve future project selection and strengthen public trust. The aim should be improvement, not simply assigning blame when targets are missed."
  },
