    "md5": "bd3d35d86dc238f593c598b835bdbb73",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Independent evaluation has a role here. Citizens deserve to know not only whether a project was completed, but whether its promised benefits materialised. Publishing straightforward evaluations would help policymakers learn from mistakes, improve future project selection and strengthen public trust. The aim should be improvement, not simply assigning blame when targets are missed.\n\nThe Budget should combine immediate relief with a credible plan for future prosperity. Public sector salary commitments and targeted assistance matter, but they should not overshadow private sector workers, self-employed people, farmers, pensioners, small businesses and unemployed youth.\n\nThe Government should explain how it will strengthen production, create productive jobs, expand exports, encourage domestic and foreign investment and improve essential services. Each priority needs a realistic target, adequate financing, a responsible agency and a public timetable.\n\nThis also requires an honest account of fiscal limits. Sri Lanka remains constrained by debt obligations and available revenue. Ambitious promises that cannot be funded or implemented may endanger hard-won stability.\n\nAbove all, ministries should be judged by outcomes, not the number of projects they announce. Parliament and the public need accessible information on costs, progress, delays and results. Transparency should support practical problem-solving rather than become another reporting exercise.\n\nA useful Budget should also distinguish clearly between measures that provide immediate relief and reforms whose benefits will emerge over several years. People can accept difficult choices more readily when the government explains the purpose, likely timing and costs of its decisions. This requires honest communication and consistent follow-through, not merely ambitious language on Budget day."
