    "md5": "705218e91142ce18421714cb9ec0ee0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1788,
    "chunk_text": "UN experts: Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed terrorism law must respect human rights\n\nUnited Nations - The Government of Sri Lanka must revise the proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA), to ensure the bill is consistent with international human rights law, UN experts* said today.\n\n\u201cWe welcome the proposed repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act of 1979, which enabled many serious human rights violations, including torture and enforced disappearances, committed before, during and since the horrific 26-year civil war in Sri Lanka, including abuse against minorities, human rights defenders, journalists and civil society,\u201d the experts said.\n\nThey noted the Sri Lankan Government had presented a much-improved latest draft of the PSTA in response to feedback, but emphasised that some vital concerns remain to be addressed.\n\nThe experts said detention orders must be subject to prompt and effective judicial control and oversight, including to test the legality, necessity and proportionality of the detention and order release.\n\n\u201cThe Independent Review Committee that hears detention appeals must be genuinely independent, and its decisions binding,\u201d they said.\n\n\u201cDetainees must be guaranteed access to legal counsel, interpretation, independent medical examination, legal aid, family contact and prompt visits by the Human Rights Commission of Sri Lanka,\u201d the experts said. \u201cTransfers from detention must be recorded and reported to prevent enforced disappearances and incommunicado detention.\u201d\n\nThey called for the definition of terrorism to be limited to acts intentionally causing death or serious injury, and to exclude mere property damage.\n\n\u201cExpression-related offences must involve a likelihood of causing terrorist violence,\u201d the experts said."
  },
