    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "They called for the definition of terrorism to be limited to acts intentionally causing death or serious injury, and to exclude mere property damage.\n\n\u201cExpression-related offences must involve a likelihood of causing terrorist violence,\u201d the experts said.\n\nThe experts called for stronger procedural protections. All evidence or statements obtained unlawfully, by torture or ill-treatment, must be excluded from proceedings. Due process concerning the non-disclosure of evidence on security grounds should be strengthened. Information relating to journalistic sources, legal privilege, professional confidentiality and human rights monitoring must be protected from disclosure.\n\n\u201cPresidential authority to direct how the law would be enforced must be limited, and the Prevention of Terrorism Act should not apply transitionally after the PSTA comes into force.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe Government must strengthen oversight, accountability and prompt and effective remedies for violations of human rights and of legal authority under the PSTA. This includes ensuring release from detention, exclusion of evidence, disciplinary or criminal liability of officials, and reparation for victims, such as compensation, rehabilitation, apology and guarantees of non-repetition,,\u201d the experts said.\n\n\u201cIt is unacceptable that the PSTA would confer immunities on officials for wrongful acts,\u201d they said.\n\n\u201cCountering terrorism is only effective if human rights are respected.\u201d\n\nThe experts addressed concerns about an earlier draft of the PSTA to the Government and called for consultation with stakeholders, including civil society. They also recently addressed related reforms, including bills regulating NGOs and on organised crime.\n\nThe experts offered technical assistance to the authorities to improve the bill."
