    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "Oil down more than $7 a barrel in post settlement trade as Trump signals restraint on Iran\n\nReuters\u00a0- Brent crude futures continued tumbling after settlement on Monday to lose more than $7 a barrel, hitting their lowest since late August, after US President Donald Trump downplayed the likelihood of renewed strikes on Iran.\n\nBrent \u200bcrude futures settled down $5.75, or 5.85%, at $92.57 a barrel. Prices continued falling after settlement, and were last down more than $7 a barrel.\n\nUS West Texas Intermediate futures \u200csettled down $3.98, or 4.38%, to $86.85. US crude futures also continued falling, last down more than $5 in post-settlement trade.\n\nThe Brent benchmark plunged by 16% last week, its steepest weekly drop since the start of the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nOn Sunday, Trump said he was highly unlikely to order fresh attacks on Iran as long as talks to end the conflict progressed. Still, both countries reported that shipping had been hit in or near the Strait of Hormuz.\n\n\"This seems to have \u200bencouraged sellers on the prospects for peace even while the actual fighting is not slowing down,\" TACenergy analysts said in a note.\n\nLast week, Trump had hinted at a \u200bresumption of attacks on Iran and Tehran vowed to respond by striking US and Israeli targets. But there has been no sign of a \u2060large-scale US military operation.\n\nA senior Iranian security official told the country's state media that Tehran has no plans to negotiate unless Washington takes practical steps to meet seven conditions set out in \u200ba June interim agreement.\n\nSaudi Arabia's foreign minister and his Omani counterpart stressed in a phone call on Monday the need to press ahead with diplomatic efforts to contain the regional crisis and safeguard \u200bmaritime security and sea lanes, Saudi state media reported.\n\nOn Monday, Trump will meet the prime minister of Qatar, which has been a mediator, though senior US and Gulf officials cast doubt on the chances of an imminent deal."
